books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md:29:Across the cut. Wall to wall, because the cut's walls were a stride and a half apart and a plane knows what it was told, and the walls were sheer and the plane went from one to the other without a hair of a gap, and from the second flight's top step to somewhere above a man's head, and it was, Cael saw — from two feet below it, looking up — the first thing he had ever watched Iron Wall do that the ground did not argue with. On a road the ground had said *go round*. Here the ground said *there is no round*. Three of the four at the top came down the first flight at a run and hit the plane as eight hounds had hit it, and did not go over each other, because they were men and men do not; they went into a thing that was not there and stopped as if it were the cut's own stone, and the crossbow, which had been coming up to a shoulder, went off into the plane a hand from the man's face, and the bolt did what the ice's bolts had done on a floor, which was nothing, and fell.
