books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-95-He stood with it. He stood in the lane with the kit's case on his shoulder and the stick in his hand and his face doing the thing a face does when a man has just found out that the reflex he is proudest of — the one that had ended every pause in his life, the fact that ended pauses — is not a tool any more but a blade with no handle, and that the only way he could help the four people in the lane with him was to be no one, and that he did not know how to be no one, and had not known that he did not know until a half-breath ago.
