books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-29-He sat back. He took both hands off the table.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-30-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:31:"A made thing's either a record or an instruction. It describes, or it commands. A ledger describes: this much, on this day, to this man. A recipe commands: do this, then this. They're built differently — the bones are different — and I can see which is which as you can see whether a wall's holding a roof or just standing there. These" — the sheets — "command. All fifty-two. Every run on every floor is built as an instruction's built, with the bearers where an instruction puts them. They're not the marks somebody cuts to say *we were here*, and they're not the marks somebody cuts to make a floor handsome. They're orders." He looked at the board. "This is an order too. Same make."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-32-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-33-Karis had not moved. Her pencil was on the notebook and had not touched it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:35:"And here's the limit, and I want it in your book in my words so nobody can say I claimed past it." He said it to her, and he said it slowly. "I can tell you these are orders. I can't tell you what they order. For that you need the language, and the language is in the text, not the stone. That's her work, not mine."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-36-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-37-He looked at Karis when he said *her*, and she wrote it down, in his words, and Cael watched her write it, and saw her underline *not the stone*, once, because that was where the limit fell and she wanted the line under it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:39:"Structure," Cleon said. "Not meaning. It works on a made thing — a carving, a wall, a document, a thing with a maker's choices in it. It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree, because nobody chose where the branches went. It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else. It's no use to anyone in a fight, which is why I'm Bronze at sixty and have never once minded. And it can't read. I want that understood. I've had this board ten years and I can tell you every bone in it and I cannot tell you one word it says, because it doesn't say words to me. It says *load*. The words are in the text, in a hand I can half read, and the text's your work, and I've been waiting thirty years for someone to walk in and do it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-40-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:41:Karis asked him one thing, then, in the voice for a finding. "The three floors. Same make, all three?"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-42-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:43:"Same make. Same bearers in the same places. The Hall's rows are longer and the Stair's are cut deeper, but the bones are the bones. Somebody cut three floors two days' walk apart in the same hand, or in three hands taught the same thing, and the thing they were taught is the thing on this board." He looked at the board. "I'll not say more than that. I've said more than I've said in thirty years."
