books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-109-That was all. Cael did not answer. He lay in the dark and had, from a man who said a thing once, the shape of the thing, which was as much as Seln ever gave, and the grit on a boot, which Cael had heard on the floor and not seen, said a roof: that Seln had gone up in the night — not to the crown; Seln did not stand beside a thing that read bodies; to somewhere under it, a roof in the district or the foot of a stair, with the cache on his back and the station's pale light above him — and had looked up at the door his old trade would have walked through, and had decided nothing, and had come back down, seen by nobody, and had let one person know that he had decided nothing, which was the nearest thing to trust that Seln had ever handed anyone, and was, Cael thought, a great deal.
