editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-87-*Six confirmed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:89:*One. Wind-adjacent. From Lira, at Ardenmere, the first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-90-*This year: six free on the sprung oak; the panel measured it on the twentieth. At the baseline, in the second month, it was four. The seventh is billed to the next morning, the eighth to the morning after.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-91-*Landing beat: the same length it has been since I first had it. Three years of trying to trim it, and nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-92-*The quarter-burst: once, on the fifth trial, I shut a burst while it was still being paid for, and the hip took the floor's share. I don't know how I did it. One point isn't a line, so it doesn't go in the column of things I can do.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-93-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:94:*Two. Pressure-adjacent. From Feryn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-95-*This year: held back at the plate both times. It's on the Ardenmere circuit record, not on this wing's, and to show it would have been a second surprise. Cost: one flat line on a semester note read in front of the Compact. Gault put his own name behind that flat line with a delegation listening, and never knew what was under it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-96-*No column for that. Bill to the shoulder, same as ever, four blows a sitting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-97-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:98:*Three. Iron-adjacent. From Brom.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-99-*This year: eleven clear of twelve at the fourth weight, called aloud, on its thinnest setting. Second month, third weight: seven clear of eight. Reserve frame, after the fault: eight of nine. The last two were the hip's, and I told them so unasked. Cold, at the end of three hours: twelve of twelve.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-100-*It tells me weight and direction, early, and nothing else. It has never lied to me. Because of it, a clerk's brown shoes never met the fifth weight. Cheapest thing I own.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-101-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:102:*Four. Compression-adjacent. From Reydan.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-103-*This year: nowhere on any record, because no record ever had it. Nine of eleven at the middle bag after a bad night, eleven of eleven rested, on three sandbags Brom sewed, in a wash-house, before the first bell. Nobody official has ever seen it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-104-*It's the one thing I own whose whole use is to stop the hurt reaching somebody else. Third inventory running I've written that line. Two years now, and no official eye has seen it work.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-105-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:106:*Five. Ember-adjacent. From Karis, with her consent, given in writing at Greyvane and asked for again on the night before I spent it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-107-*This year: four instants on the wing's copper pot, read off its glass straw in front of four Compact chairs. Gault's word: better than the Greyvane exhibit supports.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-108-*I said last year it would be worth more on paper than on a floor. It was. Karis has three words for it now, written one under another. She says the third is the last.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-109-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:110:*Six. Shadow-adjacent. Bronze. Presence-suppression; movement-masking. From a man who doesn't know, in the third hour of a bad night, on a stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:111:*Deployment: none. Held at nothing from waking, every day since. Held at nothing through three hours under the heaviest eyes I'll ever stand in front of. Never once surfaced.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-112-*All my year's work is in that line, and nobody will ever know the work was done.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-113-*Ledger: the two halves still won't sit together in my hands. To mask a moving body I have to read the room; to read the room I have to be the most awake thing in it; and being that awake is what switches the hiding off. He can do both at once. I can't yet. Brom says I'll hate it for a year and then be very good at it. I'm leaning on the first half.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-114-*Rent: from waking, spent or not. Drifts come in the gaps, never in the work. A whole chair-back of Lira's thumbnail marks on one bad night; two on the day of the sitting; fewer every week since. The falling count is the one line on this page that cheers me.*
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-117-He sat back. The window was black, and the lamp made a second room in the glass with a second boy in it, writing. Boy and reflection considered each other. Then he bent to the page again. Under the six went the entry that had stood in that spot two years running.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:119:*Anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-120-*Tonight I tried it again, as I try it every year: can I call it back, and can I say why it came at all? I can't do either. Two years and not a Tide practitioner within a day's ride. No long exposure. Nothing at stake. Every condition this book swears by was absent. It came all the same.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-121-*Six and an anomaly. It isn't a seventh anything. I've kept that sum honest for two years and I'll keep it honest now. The day I count it, I've decided a thing I don't know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md-122-*Still open. Still real. Patience.*
--
