     1	# Chapter 22 — The Grey Bridge
     2	
     3	The carters were gone before the two hundred and eighty-second day had any light in it, and they had left their fire raked out and a stack of thorn beside the ring for whoever came next.
     4	
     5	The six did the last three miles through river meadows, in a white mist that lay as high as Brom's belt. The road ran on a causeway there, with a ditch on either hand, and the town stood up out of the mist ahead of them like a thing set on a table. Cael kept his eyes on the foot of it. He had done so for a day now, and it had begun to be a habit of his neck.
     6	
     7	Then the causeway lifted, and they were at the river.
     8	
     9	It was wider than he had judged from the rise, and it ran brown and quick with the thaw of a whole moor in it. A bridge went over, long and low, with five piers standing in the water. He counted them twice, since Karis would ask. The roadway was only wide enough for one cart, with a bay built out over each pier where a person on foot could stand aside; and beyond the last pier there were two square towers with an arch between them, and the grey wall going away on both hands along the bank.
    10	
    11	Karis had stopped.
    12	
    13	She had stopped so short that he all but walked the box into her. She stood on the bank at the bridge's end, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, and she was not looking at the gate.
    14	
    15	"That is not the same stone," she said.
    16	
    17	Nobody answered, since she was plainly not speaking to any of them yet.
    18	
    19	"The wall is the bluff's stone. It is pale, and it lies in beds, and it has been laid in mortar, in courses, by people who were paid by the yard. I have seen forty such walls." She took three quick steps down the bank, to where she could see the first pier side on, and bent, with the calfskin sticking out behind her like a wing. "This is dark. It has no beds in it at all. And there is no mortar. Brom. Come and look at this, and tell me whether there is mortar."
    20	
    21	Brom set his box down. He went and stood beside her, with his head on one side, and studied the pier for some time, as he would have studied any pier.
    22	
    23	"There's none," he said. "It's cut to fit. You'd not get a knife in. Whoever laid that never meant to come back and point it."
    24	
    25	"And the tower. Look where the tower's foot comes down on the bridge's end." Her voice had gone up, and thin. "The courses do not meet. The mason has cut each of his pale stones to lie against the dark, one at a time, as a joiner cuts a shelf to fit a crooked wall. Nobody does that with a thing that he is building at the same time. He does it when the thing was standing there before he came, and he has been told to build up against it."
    26	
    27	"So the bridge was first," said Lira.
    28	
    29	"The bridge was first. The bridge was here when there was no wall for it to lead to." She straightened, and she had gone a little pink, which he had seen perhaps three times. "I can say that. I can say it on the evidence of my own eyes, on a river bank, without a catalogue. It is the first thing that I have been able to say about that town which I did not read on the spine of somebody else's book."
    30	
    31	"How much older?" said Cael.
    32	
    33	"I do not know. I should want to see the bed of the river, and I should want a week." She looked along the five piers, hungrily, as Brom looked at a pot. "The roadway is worn in two grooves, there, and there, and they are deeper than a wheel's rim. I do not know how long a wheel takes to do that to such a stone. I have not got a sheet for it."
    34	
    35	"You've not got a hand for a pencil, either," said Brom. "You've a hide and a flat. Look at it going over. It'll be here on the way out."
    36	
    37	She laughed. It was a short, startled sound, and Seln, on the first bay, turned his head to hear it.
    38	
    39	They went over in file, in the bays and out of them, as the carts came. *What does it carry?* asked the read, of the roadway under his boots, and he told it that it need not know. He kept his eyes on the parapet, which was as high as his hip and capped with long dark stones that a great many hands had polished; and when the parapet ended he looked at the water. A man who is keeping his eyes off the top of a thing learns a good deal about its bottom.
    40	
    41	The stick came over behind him, a stride at a time. On the third pier it stopped; and when he glanced round, the old man was standing in the bay, with one hand flat on the dark stone and a look that Cael had not seen on him before, which was simple curiosity.
    42	
    43	"I have ridden over this bridge on four occasions," said Vastin. "I could not have told you that it had piers."
