    47	The gate was open, and it was nothing like what he had been afraid of since a board at Lowmarch.
    48	
    49	He had made a picture of it in a back room at Lowmarch, from two sentences of Seln's: a gate, with a book in it. In his picture there had been a counter across the arch, and a man behind the counter with a pen, and a line of people giving their names one after another. He had carried that pen with him up the whole of a border road.
    50	
    51	There was a sill.
    52	
    53	It lay across the roadway under the arch, a single long stone, worn hollow in the middle, with a socket cut in each end where a bar had once dropped. He knew what it was before Seln's hand moved. He had stepped over its fellow going the other way, on another road, two hundred and eighty-two days ago, with four others; and he had begun a count that morning which he had kept ever since.
    54	
    55	*There?* asked Anchor, in its thin held note, of the two sockets and the road between them. He told it no.
    56	
    57	He stepped over. Nothing whatever happened, and he had not really supposed that anything would. It was a stone.
    58	
    59	The keeper sat on a high stool just within, in the shadow of the arch, where he could see everything that came off the bridge. He wore a long coat of grey wool that had never been dyed, and he had a frame on his knee with three wires across it and wooden beads on the wires. He did not look at faces. He looked at feet, and wheels, and hooves; and for each thing that came over the sill his thumb moved a bead.
    60	
    61	There was a book. It lay on a sloped desk against the tower's wall, a great thick thing with a strap round it, and a second man in grey sat beside it on a bench with a pen behind his ear. He had his eyes shut. Cael watched a cart of slates come through behind them, and the man woke, and undid the strap, and wrote, while the carter stood with his cap in his hand and told him a name and a load.
    62	
    63	Seln had explained it in the meadow, in the mist, walking backward so that all of them should hear.
    64	
    65	"There are two things at that gate, and they are kept by two men, for two purposes. The town wishes to know how many are within its wall at nightfall, since it must find water for them, and a watch. So one man counts. And it wishes to know who has come to sell, since it means to take its share. So another man writes. A load on wheels is the town's business. A load on a back is the back's." He had looked at the three boxes then, each in turn. "We have nothing to sell them, and we shall ask for nothing that is sold under a name. We shall walk. He will count six, and he will be right."
    66	
    67	The thumb moved. Cael heard the beads go along the wire, one after another, small and dry, like peas dropped in a cup: Seln, and Lira, and Brom, and a small pale woman with a hide. There was one for him.
    68	
    69	He had come back inside a Compact wall for the first time since he walked down a bluff with a house's certificate in his coat; and he had come as the fifth part of a movement of a stranger's thumb. Nobody had asked what he was. No pen had been lifted. He went on up the paved slope beyond the arch with his box on his shoulders, and his heart going as it had gone on the lip of a cut, and found that he could not have said whether he was relieved or insulted.
    70	
    71	It was Vastin whom he turned to see.
    72	
    73	The old man came over the sill on his stick, with the tarred case on his shoulder. The keeper's eyes were on the stick's foot. The thumb moved one bead, and stayed there, and the grey head did not lift.
    74	
    75	Cael saw the right hand leave the stick.
    76	
    77	It went a little way toward the breast of the brown coat, where a man carries his papers. It was the hand of somebody who had come to forty years of gates and had at each of them been met at the sill, by a person standing, with a book already open. It hung in the air for the space of one breath. Then it came back, and found the ash, and gripped it.
    78	
    79	"Six," said the keeper, to his frame.
    80	
    81	Vastin walked through the gate of Treswick as the sixth bead on a wire, and nobody stood up.
    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	The street beyond the arch was as wide as a field.
    86	
    87	It ran straight from the gate toward the bluff's foot, between tall houses of the pale stone, and it was full from wall to wall of things being moved. There were carts, and handcarts, and people bent under frames, and barrels rolled along by boys. There was a great noise of wheels on cobbles. After two hundred and eighty days of roads on which nobody spoke, it came against Cael's ears like a hand.
    88	
    89	"Stand out of it," said Seln, and put them into a doorway. "Look for a little, before you walk in it. It is not a crowd. It is a mechanism, and it will tell you how it works if you are civil enough to watch."
    90	
    91	There was a board at every door.
    92	
    93	Cael saw them when he had been shown. Each was a slab of black slate as tall as a child, hung beside the step, and each was ruled with chalk into the same columns. He read the nearest upside down by old habit, and then turned his head and read it the right way. *Wanted*, said its head. Under that, a load, or a number of hands. Then a place: *the landing*, or *the second wall*, or once, *the third*. Then a rate. And at the row's end there was a daub of colour, which was blue against the second wall and a pale buff against the third, and against the landing nothing at all.
    94	
    95	"Those are the brokers," said Seln. "Each house buys below and sells above. Nothing that is eaten or burned or worn inside the two upper walls is grown there. It comes in at that gate, and it stops in this street, and then it must be carried up a hill by somebody."
    96	
    97	"And the colour?" said Lira.
    98	
    99	"Is the pass that goes with the work. A person without the tier may not live above a wall. But the wall cannot do without him by day. So it lets him in by the piece. He has a slip, with a coloured edge, that says which door he is going to and how many he is; he is counted in at the gate above, with a pen, and he is counted out again before dusk."
   100	
   101	There was a long bench against the house across the way, and a dozen people sat on it, men and women, with carrying-frames between their knees. While the six watched, a clerk in an apron came out of the next door and rubbed a line off his slate. He chalked another. Four of the dozen got up without a word and went to him, and each was given a slip of card; and Cael saw the blue on its edge as it went into a cap.
   102	
   103	"It's day-labour," said Brom.
   104	
   105	"It is counted labour. There is a difference, and you will have it by tonight."
   106	
   107	Lira had read four boards while he spoke, with her lips going.
   108	
   109	"Everything up there is carried up by somebody down here," she said. "And then they're let out at dusk like hens."
   110	
   111	"That is very nearly how the code puts it," said Vastin.
   112	
   113	They walked in the mechanism after that, close along the house-fronts, and it took no more notice of them than a mill takes of a mouse. It was plain that the street had its own roads within it. Loads went up on the right hand and came down empty on the left. Things on wheels kept the crown of the cobbles. Nobody had painted a line, and nobody crossed one.
   114	
   115	Half way along, a lane went off toward the river, and there was a smell down it of tar and new hemp. Brom's head came round like a dog's.
   116	
   117	"That's a walk," he said. "I'll be as long as it takes to look."
   118	
   119	It was a shed, open all down one side, and Cael had never seen a building so long. It ran away along the inside of the town wall for three hundred yards. Down the middle of it, people were walking backward with bundles of hemp at their waists, paying out yarn between their fingers, while a wheel turned at the shed's head. Coils hung on pegs the whole length of the wall, tarred and white.
   120	
   121	Brom went along the pegs. He passed three-strand without a glance. He stopped at a coil as thick as his thumb, and took a fathom of it off the peg, and bent it double, and looked at the cut end as a person looks into a flower.
   122	
   123	"Four," he said. "Round a heart. That'd live in a block."
   124	
   125	A woman with tarred hands had come up beside him. He asked her the price of sixty yards. She named it, and it was a fair one, since he nodded as he nodded at a wall that stood plumb.
   126	
   127	"I'll take it going out," he said. "I'll not carry sixty yards of rope up a hill to carry it down again."
   128	
   129	"It'll be the same going out."
   130	
   131	"Then it'll keep. It's kept since the snow."
   132	
   133	He hung the fathom back on its peg, exactly as he had found it. Cael had watched that debt ride in a big man's head since before the snow, through a whole cold term, and over a river that wanted the rope and could not be paid in the wrong kind. It was the first time that he had seen Brom look at it without frowning.
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	The street ended in an open place, where the ground began to climb.
   138	
   139	There was a trough there, forty feet of hollowed stone with a pipe running into its upper end, and a row of beasts drinking at it with their carters beside them. One of the beasts was a mule. She was small, and brown, and she stood with her rump to the wind and her ears laid out sideways, entirely at her ease.
   140	
   141	Brom had gone half a dozen strides toward her before he stopped.
   142	
   143	He stood in the middle of that open place with a box on his shoulder. He looked at her feet, the near fore and the off, and then at the others.
   144	
   145	"It's not her," said Lira.
   146	
   147	"I know that it's not." He did not move. "This one's brown all the way down. Ours has the white foot."
   148	
   149	"He's counted bowls for a day and a night," said Lira, to nobody. "Now he's counting mules by the leg."
   150	
   151	"She's a day and a half north, with oats," said Cael.
   152	
   153	"I know where she is." Brom turned away from the trough at last. "I only looked."
   154	
   155	The smith's stood on the upper side of the open place, and it had two fires.
   156	
   157	Cael marked nothing in that at first; a busy smith might well keep two. It was Brom who slowed, and then stood, and then set his box down on its end and folded his arms on the top of it, as a man does who means to be some time. He had spent a good many nights of his boyhood on a heap of sacks in a shed where his father kept a forge for the pleasure of hitting something, and he looked at a fire as Karis looked at a seal.
   158	
   159	The left-hand forge was the kind that Cael knew. It had a hood, and a bed of charcoal, and a great leather bellows with a lad swinging on the pole. A smith in a scorched apron was fitting a shoe to a carter's cob, in a stink of burned hoof, and three more carters waited behind with their beasts on short ropes.
   160	
   161	The right-hand forge had no bellows.
   162	
   163	It had no lad, and no smoke. Its bed was white from edge to edge, with the quiet evenness of a thing that is not being fed; and beside it, well back, stood a woman in a long coat of dark blue with her hands in her apron pockets. She was not doing anything, as far as could be seen. She looked like somebody waiting for a ferry. A second smith worked at that fire, and the horse under his hand was a tall bay with a blue band braided into its forelock, held by a groom in a clean jerkin. Nothing else was waiting.
   164	
   165	"That is an Ember," said Karis, very low. She was looking at the woman's hands, through the apron. "She is holding the whole bed. Nine points, I should think, or ten, and she has held them since the bell. It is Rank Two work, at the most." She was silent. "It is steady. It is a great deal steadier than charcoal."
   166	
   167	"Is the shoe better?" said Lira.
   168	
   169	Brom did not answer at once. He was watching the two smiths' hands.
   170	
   171	"It's the same bar," he said. "Look. They're both drawing off the one rack, by the door. Same iron. Same nails; that's one keg between them. He's making the same shoe at both fires, and the white one costs him a woman's wages to keep." He scratched his jaw. "The horse can't tell. A hoof's a hoof."
   172	
   173	"Then what's the second fire for?"
   174	
   175	"For whoever owns the horse."
   176	
   177	He looked a moment longer, at the blue band in a bay's forelock, and at a carter's cob with a rope round its neck.
   178	
   179	"They shoe by the tier."
   180	
   181	He picked up his box. Nobody added anything, and Cael did not think that anything could have been added.
   182	
   183	Beyond the smith's the ground went up steeply, and one street climbed it. At its head, two hundred yards above them, stood the second wall, with an arch in it; and in the arch were two figures in long coats of that same dark blue. Cael looked at the coats. He did not look higher.
   184	
   185	Lira was looking at them too. Her right hand hung at her side, and her left was on the becket at her shoulder, and neither of them went near the breast of her coat.
   186	
   187	Seln found the inn.
   188	
   189	It was a long house one street back from the trough, with a carriage-arch through the middle of it. There were two ways in, and they were not ten strides apart. On the street side there was a painted door, with a step, and a lamp over it. It stood open; and just within, on a stand, lay a book with a pen beside it on a chain.
   190	
   191	The other was under the arch. It was a half-door in the side wall, with a ledge on top, and a woman leaned on the ledge with her sleeves rolled. Behind her were the yard, and a pump, and a long stable with a loft above it.
   192	
   193	"That one has a book," said Seln, of the first. "This one has a woman who wishes to be paid."
   194	
   195	"The long loft," the woman said, when Brom asked. She had counted them as the keeper had, by the feet. "It's over the horses, so it's warm. A week, the six of you: two marks, in front. That's the pot at dusk as well, a bowl a head."
   196	
   197	Brom counted two marks onto the ledge, and she swept them off it with the side of her hand.
   198	
   199	"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.
   200	
   201	"And a seventh bowl," he added.
   202	
   203	"There's six of you."
   204	
   205	"There's seven bowls."
   206	
   207	She looked at him as a person looks at a horse that may be going to kick, and then she shrugged with one shoulder.
   208	
   209	"It's your pot," she said.
   210	
   211	Vastin had stopped in the arch. He stood between the two doors, and his eyes were on the painted one, and on the step, and on the lamp. Cael saw the bound leg begin to turn toward it of its own accord, as a horse turns in at its own gate. Then the old man looked down at the leg, and brought it round, and came in by the yard.
   212	
   213	---
   214	
   215	The first of the five gates lay a day and a half north of the fork, under the hill-foot, and Oryn came to it at dusk on the second day with the mule's shadow going long in front of her.
   216	
   217	It was a small hold. It had a wall of field-stone, and a gate of oak bars, and a gate-house built against the wall's outer face, with a bench along it under a roof of turf. She had sat on that bench on thirty rounds. She had never once been through the gate, and it was a long while since anybody there had asked her to come in.
   218	
   219	There were five on the bench. Somebody had seen a mule on the hill road an hour ago, and they had come out and sat down.
   220	
   221	She did not eat first. She tied the mule, and washed to the elbow at the gate-house butt, and went along the five at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten. A child with a cough that was nearly gone. An old man's hip, which she could ease and not mend. A woman who was only tired, and needed to be told that she was allowed to be. A boy with a boil.
   222	
   223	The fifth was a carter, a big slow man with a red beard, and he was not of that hold. He sat at the bench's end, holding his right wrist in his left hand as a person holds a cup that is too full.
   224	
   225	"You're Oxhollow's," she said. "I've seen you on the ford with a grey pair."
   226	
   227	"I was. I'm here for the hay." He did not look at her. "You never came."
   228	
   229	"No. I was seventeen days late to your gate before the snow, and I'd a day there and no more; and then the cold shut the top road, and it's only now let me up it from this end."
   230	
   231	"I broke it a week after you'd gone by. Under a wheel, on the ice. The keeper's wife strapped it, at the gate, with a wet strap and two spoons. She did her best."
   232	
   233	"I don't doubt that she did. Wrist. May I?"
   234	
   235	He held it out to her, which was not an answer.
   236	
   237	"I'll have it said."
   238	
   239	"Yes, then."
   240	
   241	She took it in both hands, and counted, and went in.
   242	
   243	It was a river with a stone dropped in it. The arm's current came down to the joint, strong enough, a carter's; and at the joint it went round. It ought to have gone through. She followed it, as she would have followed a path round a fallen tree, and found the place: two ends of bone that had been laid a thumb's breadth off one another, and had done what bone does, and joined where they lay. It was good, sound, patient knitting. The body had made the best of what it was given, as the keeper's wife had.
   244	
   245	She came out, and laid the wrist back in his other hand.
   246	
   247	"It's joined," she said, "and it's joined wrong. It'll hold a spoon. It'll not hold a rein with a pair of mules on the end of it, and it'll ache worse every cold term till you're old."
   248	
   249	"Can you mend it?"
   250	
   251	"I can't mend a thing that's whole. It'd have to be parted again, where it's knit, and laid straight; and then I can knit it, and it'll be a wrist." She let him have that. She had never found a way of saying it that was easier to hear. "That's a hurt, and I'd be the one doing it, on purpose. I'll not do it tonight. I've walked a day and a half, and I don't put my hands on that sort of work tired. And I'll not do it at all unless you tell me to in the morning, when you've slept on it."
   252	
   253	"And if I say no?"
   254	
   255	"Then you've a wrist that pours soup, and I've four more gates."
   256	
   257	She saw to the other four, and ate what the gate-house woman brought out, and lay down in the lean-to beside the mule with her coat over her.
   258	
   259	She did not sleep at once. There had been a Gold, in a bed by a window, with a wrenched wrist. It had not even been broken. He had had six healers' attention on the first morning, and had gone home before noon; and a woman from the river sheds had waited three nights at the bottom of a heap. Oryn had walked out of a gate to be quit of those sums.
   260	
   261	A carter had waited a whole cold term, with a crooked wrist, for want of her.
   262	
   263	Nobody had sorted him to the bottom. There was no column out here. There was a top road, and snow on it, and one healer with one pair of feet; and the snow had done exactly what a board at a bed's foot would have done, and had not needed a clerk. She could not fault those sums, either. She had only liked them better because nobody had written them down.
   264	
   265	She turned over, and the mule shifted its feet.
   266	
   267	And there was a thing that she had not got to at all. She had walked away from it at a fork two days south, with her sleeve half pushed up, and had told a boy that it would keep three weeks. She had read a hundred bodies since she first laid hands on his. She knew a wrist with a stone in it when she met one. She had met something in him a long while back that everything went round, as that carter's current went round a bad join; and she had not been able to say then whether it was a join, or a stone, or a thing that had always lain there. She had told him to leave it be. Then she had walked north, owing him the one reading that might have told her.
   268	
   269	She did not like to owe. She had said so, and it had not made the road any shorter.
   270	
   271	He said yes in the morning, with his chin down. She had the hold's smith stand behind him, and gave him a strap to bite, and asked him once more, and had his answer. It took no longer than a breath. He made a sound like a man lifting a cart.
   272	
   273	Then she had her hands on it, and she was in, and she spent.
   274	
   275	It took an hour. She spent as she had not spent since a cut on a moor, laying the two ends true and holding them so while the body was coaxed into beginning again; and when she sat back, the yard had gone grey at its edges and somebody was holding a cup against her teeth.
   276	
   277	"Six weeks in that binding," she said, when she could. "Nothing lifted with it. And if you break yourself again where there's no healer, you tell whoever's by to bind it straight and wait. Straight, and wait. I can do a deal with straight."
   278	
   279	"What's owed?"
   280	
   281	"Oats for the mule. And tell that keeper's wife that she kept the hand alive. She'll have thought about it all the cold."
   282	
   283	She had meant to be on the road by sunrise. It was past noon before her legs would have it. Three weeks had been five gates and a ford, with one day in hand; and she had spent the day at the first gate, on a carter. She untied the mule, and turned its head along the hill-foot, and began to walk it off.

