     5	There were a dozen straw pallets along one side of it, and a rope to hang wet things on, and a single window in the gable at the far end. The window looked up the hill. Cael saw so much from the ladder's head, and no more, since he put his eyes on the floor; and by the time that he had his box off, Lira had crossed the boards, and taken a horse-blanket from a peg, and hung it over the glass on two nails.
     6	
     7	"For the draught," she said.
     8	
     9	"There's no draught," said Brom. "It's as tight as a cask in here."
    10	
    11	"There is now."
    12	
    13	Nobody argued with her. Brom looked at the blanket for a moment, and at Cael's back, and began to lay the three boxes in a row under the eaves with their beckets to the wall.
    14	
    15	Karis had not sat down. She stood in the middle of the loft with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as though she had been set there by somebody who would be back for her directly.
    16	
    17	"It is two hundred yards," she said. "I counted them from the trough. There is a gate, and a street, and another gate, and the thing that I have wanted for the whole of my life is under the second. I should like somebody to tell me why I am standing in a hayloft."
    18	
    19	"Because of the blue coats," said Brom. "I'm Copper. That's this street and the one behind it. I read the boards."
    20	
    21	"Iron walks through," said Lira. She was shaking out a pallet, and she spoke as she would have spoken of the weather. "That's what the blue is. It's not a pass at all, up there; it's what the place is for. You show the paper and you go by."
    22	
    23	She did not touch her coat. Cael marked that, since he had been listening for it. At a board in Lowmarch she had laid her hand on the pocket, and said what was in it, and that she had almost forgotten which side; she had spoken then of a certificate, and she spoke now of a wall.
    24	
    25	"I have Iron, of the third rank, on registry paper," said Karis. "She has it of the first. Either of us could be at that arch in the time that it takes to climb a street."
    26	
    27	"And be written," said Seln.
    28	
    29	He stood by the ladder's head, where he could hear the yard.
    30	
