    32	
    33	"And yours?" said Brom. "You said four. There's six of us."
    34	
    35	"Mine is not on that paper. I do not propose to offer it to a gate for all that. I should be asked where I had been employed."
    36	
    37	So that left one; and Cael felt the loft come round to it, as he had once felt a table take care not to look at himself.
    38	
    39	An old man leaned at the foot of the gable, under the blanket, with both hands on his stick. He had come in by a yard, and climbed a ladder on one leg with a healer's laths on the other, and had not yet been heard to say what he thought of either. There was a flatness under the breast of the brown coat, on the left side. Cael had taken it for a receipt.
    40	
    41	Vastin had seen them turn. He did not wait to be asked.
    42	
    43	"I have papers I will not show. I want that said once, so that nobody asks twice."
    44	
    45	It was said without heat. He looked at each of the five in turn, as he looked at a room, and gave every one of them the same length of time.
    46	
