   102	
   103	Cael took his shoulder off the butt.
   104	
   105	He had been afraid of that gate since a back room, and he saw now that he had been afraid of the wrong thing. He had pictured a machine that would catch him and grind. This one had not teeth of that kind. It sorted whatever it could see, by a colour, and what it could not sort it did not see at all.
   106	
   107	There was a colour for Copper, even. It was the street below, and the one behind.
   108	
   109	He had no tier of any sort. He stood by a water-butt, four strides from two men who read people for their living, with a box-strap's mark still red on his neck; and neither of them had so much as wondered what he was. There was no sieve in that town fine enough to hold him. He had fallen clean through the lowest.
