   111	---
   112	
   113	He went on round, and the road began to go down.
   114	
   115	It came out above the river on the bluff's far side, where the town wall ran along a quay, and there was a landing. He had not seen a flat boat since Lowmarch. There were six of them here, tied nose to tail, and a great wooden wheel on the quay with a man walking inside it, round and round, so that a sack went up on a rope at every dozen steps. A ferry lay at the landing's lower end, on a cable. He looked at the cable's blocks for Brom's sake, and could not have said what lay the rope was.
   116	
   117	The rope-walk came down to the quay there, and ended, in a yard full of tar barrels.
   118	
   119	There were children in the yard. They had a stick, which two of them held across the mouth of an alley at the height of a belt, and the others were coming up to it one at a time.
   120	
   121	"What are you?"
   122	
   123	"Iron!"
   124	
   125	"Show."
   126	
   127	A leaf was shown, with great ceremony.
   128	
   129	"Go by. What are you?"
   130	
   131	"Copper."
   132	
   133	"Round by the wall. You know that. You always try it."
   134	
   135	The smallest came last. It was a child of perhaps four, of no kind that Cael could determine, in a smock to its ankles, and it stood before the stick with its hands behind it.
   136	
   137	"What are you?"
   138	
   139	"Nothing."
   140	
   141	"You can't be nothing. You've to be something, or you can't play."
