   145	"Then I'm the gate," it said, and took hold of the middle of the stick with both fists.
   146	
   147	There was an uproar. He went on before it was settled, since a stranger who stands and watches children is marked in any town; but he carried it with him up the quay, and it was a little while before he could have said whether it had made him want to laugh.
   148	
   149	The road brought him round at last, by the back of the smith's, into the open place where the trough was. He had gone the whole circuit of the bluff, and he had not once lifted his eyes above a line of pale stone.
   150	
   151	He stopped at the foot of the street that climbed.
   152	
   153	It began there, between the smith's and a chandler's, at a kerb where the cobbles of the open place gave way to setts that had been laid aslant for the grip of hooves. It went straight up. He could follow it with his eyes on the ground: two hundred yards to the blue coats, and through, and on under slate roofs, growing narrower, to a second arch where the coats were a paler colour. Beyond that he did not follow it.
   154	
   155	An old woman sat on the kerb at his elbow, with a carrying-frame beside her and her boots off.
   156	
   157	"Boards are down the street," she said. She had looked at his neck, where the strap had been. "There's nothing going up before the second bell."
   158	
   159	"I'm not after work. I was looking for where the registry is."
   160	
   161	"That's the top." She pointed with a bare heel, without troubling to get up. "Straight up. Through the blue, and through the buff, and then you're in the square. You go across it. It's the door that faces the steps." She spat, neatly. "I've carried paper to it for thirty years. You'll not get that far in those boots without a card."
   162	
   163	"Across the square."
   164	
   165	"There's no other way to a front door, is there? It's a square. It's got the one thing on one side of it and the other thing on the other, and you walk between."
   166	
   167	He thanked her. She had shut her eyes already.
   168	
   169	So that was how it lay. He stood with the toes of his boots against the kerb, and looked at the first of the slanted setts, a hand's breadth in front of them.
   170	
   171	He had written two sentences in a book, at a fire, for a healer who would not take his word. They had been easy to write. The square had been two days off, in a town that he had never seen, and a thing which is two days off costs nothing to forgo. He had not known that it would stand at the head of the only street; that the record he had walked here for would be kept behind a door on its far side; or that the two of them would face one another across forty yards of paving, like a pair of people who have been set to keep each other honest.
   172	
   173	He could break it by walking. That was all that the promise was made of. There were four hundred yards of hill, and two gates that had not seen him, and a paved place.
   174	
   175	He did not put his foot on the sett.
   176	
   177	It was not a hard thing to refrain from, today. He had no card, and a blanket hung over a window for him in a hayloft. He thought that it would be harder on the day when he had a card. He turned his back on the street, and went down past the trough, where a brown mule that was not theirs lifted her head and looked through him.
   178	
   179	---
   180	
   181	Vastin had sat down.
   182	
   183	He had done it in the lower room of the inn, which opened off the yard: a long low place with one table down the middle of it and a bench on either side, a hearth at the far end, and a hatch through which bowls came. He had sat on the bench's end with the bound leg laid along it. There was a post behind him, and a strap hung from the post, and he had marked both before he let himself down. He would be able to get up by them. He had sat twice now in two days, and he noted, with the interest that he gave to any finding, that the second time had cost him less than the first.
   184	
   185	He had come in by a yard.
   186	
   187	He turned that over. In forty years he had entered some hundreds of houses on the seat's business, and he could not call to mind one in which he had seen the back of the kitchen door. He saw this one now. It had been mended with a board of a different wood, and somebody had cut a tally into the frame with a knife, in fives.
   188	
   189	Every district that he had ever inspected had been told that he was coming.
   190	
   191	He had known it, of course. An officer of his grade did not arrive; he was announced, by a form, four days before, and the form went to the very people whom he came to look at. So he had ridden for forty years through streets that had been swept the day before, past whitewashed kerbs, to offices where every book lay squared on its desk and a man stood at the sill with his cap in his hand. He had allowed for it, as a careful officer allows for the known error of a scale, and had supposed that he could subtract the whitewash and arrive at the street.
   192	
   193	He looked through the open door, and across the yard, and out under the carriage-arch, where a strip of the street went by in the sun.
   194	
   195	It was filthy. It was loud, and exact, and nobody in it had been told anything. A boy went past with a yoke of slops. Two porters were quarrelling over a card, with their foreheads almost touching. A woman had been turned away from the painted door ten minutes since; he had heard the whole of it, *that side's for names, mistress, you want the arch*, and had heard her come in by the arch, grumbling, and pay.
   196	
   197	It was the first honest district that he had ever been in.
   198	
   199	He had certified this town on four occasions. He had put his name to the soundness of its station, and the order of its gates, and the keeping of its books. He had ridden over a bridge without knowing that it had piers. It had taken a stick, and a brown coat, and a place in the count of a keeper's thumb, to show him what any carter saw from his seat.
   200	
   201	There was a keeper at the hearth.
   202	
   203	He was off his watch, by the unbuttoned collar; but he had kept the blue coat on, and he sat in the one chair that had a back. A bowl had come to him through the hatch before he asked for it. He had not paid. The carters along the bench had moved up for him as water moves for a boat, without looking round, and the bench had closed again behind him.
   204	
