   216	
   217	And he carried one thing that was not paper.
   218	
   219	A boy had given it to him on a bank, at dusk, across a yard of turf. He had it by heart, as he had always had a finding: there was a place, which a healer had found; it had been read twice in a day; nothing had come back. He did not know what had read it. A man of his training could set out the possibilities, in their order, and on the road that morning he had begun to; and he had reached the second, and seen that it was a draft, and stopped.
   220	
   221	He was not accustomed to holding a thing that he had not been given the whole of. He found that he could do it. It was like the leg.
   222	
   223	Out under the arch, a clerk in an apron had come to the bench across the way. Four people got up. A card went into a cap, and the four went off up the hill with frames on their backs, and not one of them had been asked so much as a name.
   224	
   225	He watched them out of sight. The lowest thing in that district went through its walls on the least ink. He set that beside the keeper's bowl, as a fact about a mechanism, and could not have said why he had troubled to keep it.
   226	
   227	A bowl came down on the table in front of him.
   228	
   229	It was the large young man who had set it there, and he had six others on a board. Vastin felt for a copper. He was told, as he had been told on four evenings running, to put it away.
   230	
   231	"I had not known," he said, "that a man could be fed for four days and still be owed nothing."
   232	
   233	"You've not been fed. You've been counted." Brom set the seventh bowl at the table's end, by itself. "It's different. Eat it while it's hot."
   234	
   235	---
   236	
   237	Seln came up the ladder after dark, with dust to the knees.
   238	
   239	Brom had begged a lantern of the woman at the half-door, and it hung from a rafter in the middle of the loft, lit at the kitchen fire with a spill. The six sat or lay in the round of it, and nobody had gone to sleep. Cael had Hesk's book open on his knee, with the day at its head and nothing under it yet.
   240	
   241	"I did not go above the trough," said Seln. He sat down on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung, which was as near as he came to resting. "I hired a frame for a copper, and I sat for three hours on the porters' bench at the top of the street, and was twice offered work. The man beside me has carried to the registry's yard for eleven years, and he likes to be listened to. I have four things from him, and one difficulty. I shall give them in his order."
   242	
   243	Karis had her pencil. She did not write; she held it.
   244	
   245	"The first is the place. The old town stands upon a hole: before there was a wall on that bluff, somebody had quarried the crown of it from the inside, by galleries, and had gone. The town that came afterward kept its records down there, since it was dry; and when the Compact took the town, it built its registry across the quarry's mouth, and has never troubled to bring the old records up. Its people call that the lower archive, and they do not go into it."
   246	
   247	"What stone?" said Karis. "Did he say what stone the galleries are cut in?"
   248	
   249	"He carries to a yard; he has not been below the door, and he knows nobody who has." He inclined his head to her, by a very little. "There is one door, in the undercroft beneath the yard, and it is of iron. He says that the registry's wall is built round its frame, and he meant it as a complaint against the masons."
   250	
   251	Her breath went in, and she said nothing more.
   252	
   253	"The second is the leave. I have seen that form, in another house: it is the oldest provision in the registry's book, and the shortest. A practitioner who is registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and he may have matter brought down to him for study. For the practitioner, that is the whole of it."
   254	
   255	"And there's one," said Cael.
   256	
   257	"There is one. He has gone down by that door in every week for longer than my porter has carried. The porter has never spoken to him."
   258	
