   263	The word was strange to Cael. He had stood on floors against a good many Paths, and had the pieces of ten of them in him, and he had never met that one on any list of bouts; it was not a thing that a person fought with, then. He turned it over, and it told him nothing.
   264	
   265	"He comes up at noon to eat," said Seln, "at the cook-house which is built against the wall, beside the blue gate; he stands at its counter, and goes down again within the hour. He has done so on every day that my porter can remember, and he is said to dislike being spoken to."
   266	
   267	"The third," said Brom, who had three fingers ready.
   268	
   269	"The third is the clerk. One man keeps the provision's book, at the yard door, and has kept it for many years, and in all that time there has been a single name in it. My porter does not know what he is called, and I did not ask."
   270	
   271	"And the fourth is how we get in," said Lira.
   272	
   273	"The fourth is what the form asks, when matter is brought." He drew his hands into his sleeves. "It was drawn a long while ago, when a scholar's bundles came with whoever happened to be carrying them, and nobody thought such people worth a line apiece. So it has a line for the matter, which is described. It has a line for the matter's bearer, who is one. And it has a line for hands, to carry, which takes a figure and no more; the figure may not be above four."
   274	
   275	The small pale woman on the pallet had turned her head. She was looking at the row under the eaves, where three boxes lay with their beckets to the wall and a tarred case leaned against the last; and her lips moved.
   276	
   277	Karis said, "Four hands."
   278	
   279	"Four," Seln said. "By count."
   280	
   281	Lira said, to nobody, "Four hands," and then, "I've two."
   282	
   283	She had turned her own over in the lantern-light, the right and the slow left, as though she had been asked to produce them.
   284	
   285	He had the pencil in his fingers already.
   286	
   287	Cael wrote: *Entered by count. That's the door.*
   288	
   289	Below them a horse blew through its nose, and stamped.
   290	
   291	"A hand's a person," said Brom, after some thought. "It's four of us. It's not four of those."
   292	
   293	"I know what a hand is. I was seeing what I'd got."
   294	
   295	She put the two of them away, and sat up, with her arms round her knees.
   296	
