   298	
   299	Cael had got there a breath before her, and it did not feel any better for being said aloud.
   300	
   301	The door did not ask for anything that he had spent his life refusing to give. It wanted to know how many of him there were.
   302	
   303	---
   304	
   305	The lantern had burned down a finger's breadth, and Brom had carried seven bowls back across the yard and come up again, before anybody asked for the rest.
   306	
   307	"There was a difficulty," said Karis.
   308	
   309	"It is the exposure. I cannot shorten it." Seln spoke as he would have priced a wall. "The form does not require the clerk to ask who a bearer is, or a hand, and it does not forbid him. There will be a moment at that yard door when a pen is held over a line, and a man is counting; and if he is a curious man, or only a careful one, he will say *and you are?*, to pass the time. I have known a hundred clerks. Eighty of them would."
   310	
   311	"And then?"
   312	
   313	"And then somebody must answer, with a name or a lie; and I will not have a lie told at a registry's door by anybody who means to go on standing on this side of the Line." His eyes went, briefly, to Lira, and to Brom. "We go in on a count, or we do not go in. I cannot tell you which of the hundred he is."
   314	
   315	"There's the other thing," said Cael.
   316	
   317	"The square."
   318	
   319	"A porter told me, at the trough. It goes across."
   320	
   321	"It is the only way to the front door, and a load does not go in at a front door. There is a yard, and a yard has a gate, and carts must reach it by some road which is not a paved square with a station's steps on it." Seln got up off the ladder's head. "I have not found that road. I shall find it tomorrow; and tomorrow at noon somebody will stand at a cook-house counter with a sheet in her hand that a certain man cannot look away from. That is as far as I have got."
   322	
   323	Karis laid her pencil down on the flat. Her hand was not quite steady.
   324	
   325	Brom turned the lantern low. The blanket hung over the gable, with a faint paleness round its edges that did not flicker; and Cael lay back, with the book shut on his chest and his eyes on the slates a yard above him.
   326	
   327	"Last time we walked into a place like this," Cael said, "it took four years and a hearing to walk out."
   328	
   329	"Last time we didn't know what we were looking for," Karis said.
   330	
   331	"Do we now?"
   332	
