    91	He had heard himself. That was the thing Cael catalogued, because it was the thing that cost: the man had heard the sentence come out of his own mouth — a name, a rank, the Compact's own title, spoken aloud in a lane behind a Compact registry in a Compact city on the Line by a man who had resigned from it, to a clerk, to smooth a door — and had heard it land. Not as authority. The clerk had not known the name. The rank was nothing at this level; the level had no column for it, and a rank that the form has no column for is a noise. And the third thing, the thing he had heard last and understood first: that a former Archmarshal invoking the Compact inside a Compact city, in front of a clerk who kept a book, with an unranked boy at his shoulder who had a piece of paper in an office in the west that said what he was, had just come within one careful man's question of closing every door on the hill, and had been stopped from it by a defector's tradecraft and a sentence that told the clerk nothing but what the form could hold.
    92	
    93	Seln had not lied. Cael entered that, because it was the kind of thing a ledger should carry: a man who could have said anything in that lane — a carter's man, a factor from up west, any story a clerk would have taken — had said only what was true and let the form do the rest, and the rest had been enough, and the not-lying had cost him a longer sentence and a longer half-breath of the clerk's eye, and he had paid both without a flicker.
    94	
    95	He stood with it. He stood in the lane with the kit's case on his shoulder and the stick in his hand and his face doing the thing a face does when a man has just found out that the reflex he is proudest of — the one that had ended every pause in his life, the fact that ended pauses — is not a tool any more but a blade with no handle, and that the only way he could help the four people in the lane with him was to be no one, and that he did not know how to be no one, and had not known that he did not know until a half-breath ago.
    96	
    97	"I'm sorry," he said. To the lane. In the ledger voice, which had never in Cael's hearing said that sentence before.
    98	
    99	"Don't be sorry," Seln said. "Be nobody. It's the only thing down here that's worth anything, and you can't buy it and you can't be given it. You have to stop being the other thing." He went back to the lane's mouth. "Down. She's waiting."
