    73	All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.
    74	
    75	- **The Deepstacks' ground.** Inside the iron door a bar of black iron as long as a man's arm, shot from within. A stair of the dark dry-laid stone, **twenty-seven steps in three flights of nine**, with two landings, low-roofed, unworn. At its foot a cross-way a dozen strides long, lined with the dark stone; off it **three galleries side by side**, each as broad as a cart, cut in the bluff's pale bedded rock with a pick. **The middle gallery** is shelved to the roof in bays divided by uprights of black oak, about a hundred and ten strides long; nothing on its shelves has a ticket (some bundles have tags on their under sides). From ninety strides the old town's sorting tables stand end to end down the middle; at the end, against the cut rock, a high sloped desk of black oak with a lamp in a glass chimney on an iron arm, and a shelf above it (a cup, a jar of water, a thick bundle tied both ways in brown tape, and the board in sacking). **The eastern gallery** holds the old town's ledgers; its tall books stand as high as the roof and are reached by ladders. **The western gallery** is the quarry's own: the board lay on its bottom shelf under quarrymen's wedges. The cold does not move; a flame stands straight; no sound comes down from the registry.
    76	- **The clerk's bell.** A small bell on a wire at the stair's foot, pulled by the clerk from the vault a quarter of an hour before he shuts his yard.
    77	- **Why the loads go up and down daily.** Cleon keeps on his tables only what he is reading: the fifty-two first copies lie there from the 284th, in three heaps as they lay on the floors (the Stair's twenty-nine in three rows, north toward the stair). The boxes go up at the bell and come down in the morning. Each day's card is written by Cleon the evening before.
    78	- **Karis on the stone (her stated conclusion):** the rock was cut first; the dark stone was laid against the cut; the town's mortared work is third. She puts no year to any.
    79	- **The clerk at the hatch (285th).** His voice: thin, dry, exact. His difficulty was his own form: matter entered on the 284th had gone up at his bell and come back, and his book would have said six boxes and two cases. He said that he must send up to **the under-registrar, who keeps the schedules**. After Seln spoke he wrote *The same*, the day, his mark and *four*, and has written *the same* each day since. He asked one thing ("The same?"). He looked at Seln "properly, from the cap to the boots".
    80	- **What Seln's truth cost.** He had been on the yard's bench since the first bell as a porter with a hired frame. By standing and speaking he showed his face to a registry's clerk and joined it to the four and the card. He watches from **the roof of the last building above the cut's head, which has a parapet**, from the 286th. His reason, said in the loft: his own rule of the 282nd, which he had not meant to except himself from.
    81	- **The lamp.** Low; a hand to the left of what is looked at; held on one knee. Lira on the stair; Brom ("You're a wall. I asked for a lamp."); from the third hour of the 285th, Cael. Cleon says *lamp*, *left*, *up a finger*; he has carried it himself for thirty years, "one hand for the light, and half an eye for where I'd put it". By the 289th Cael moves it before the word: all the looking runs from the low left corner of a thing upward to the right.
    82	- **The chairs.** One before the desk's slope, with a cushion in the shape of one man; one against the wall, heaped with bundles on the 284th, cleared and wiped on the 285th, and carrying his cushion on the 287th (his own chair has none). Karis is found sitting on it on the 288th.
    83	- **Cleon.** He missed his noon bowl on the 284th and has gone up for it on the other days. He does not touch paper with his skin: he points with a reed as long as a forearm. Three bowls of counters (white pebbles, black pebbles, red squares of broken tile). He does not blink while he looks; his eyes run; he rests them shut for an hour. To Cael at two feet the Path "made no sound at all". Brom said Karis's name in his hearing on the 286th ("I said no names." / "I never gave it to you. I gave it to her."). "It was the first thing I did when I came here": he has looked at the grey bridge twice. He made a sound "that was nearly a laugh" on the 290th.
    84	- **The board.** A plank of black oak as long as a man's arm and two hands broad, cut on one face with four rows of the floors' kinds of sign; found by Cleon ten years ago on the bottom shelf of the west gallery, with its face to the wall. At each row's end a count in figures (**a ring is five, a peck is one**) and after the figures a short group of signs. Rows of twelve, nine, twelve and seven signs. Its first row is the top row of the Hall's fourth sheet; its second is on the Stair's ninth; its third on the Ring's sixth; its fourth on the Stair's twenty-first.
    85	- **Bearer, carried, nothing.** "White" bears (take it out and the row comes down); "black" is carried (it hangs from the white); "red" is nothing (packing, so that a row fills its stone). **Of the forty-one kinds: seven bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing** (Karis's count on the 288th, when all fifty-two sheets had been laid). A bearer stands at the head of every run. Vastin: "To do a thing; to what; and how many times."
    86	- **Cleon's week.** A week of his, from the 287th; then every sheet goes up and the clerk writes that it is gone (he will not have matter lying a month under *the same*). Its seventh day is the 293rd; three days are left after the 290th.
    87	- **The bundle.** On the shelf beside the jar, tied across both ways in tape gone brown; it came off the same shelf as the board, behind it. "Paper. Not stone." He put two fingers on it and did not take it down. Nobody has untied it. Karis has not asked for it.
    88	- **The decipherment.** (1) The pecked figures equal the number of signs in each row: the cutter counted his own work. (2) The sign-group after each figure is the count in words. Karis tested it without looking at the board: from the oldest of the old town's tally-books (the eastern gallery, third bay on the left, the eleventh shelf) she wrote only the pattern of like and unlike letters in that hand's spelling of twelve, nine and seven, and then laid the pattern against the cutting. It held in every place. The tongue is the old town's "or that tongue's grandmother". **Eight letters** from the board. (3) The carried signs on the floors spell further numbers: *ten* on the Ring's fifth sheet and the number after ten on the Stair's fourteenth confirm the eight; two more letters from the Ring's ninth, confirmed on the Hall's eighth (**ten**, the 288th); three more from the Stair, confirmed on the Hall (**thirteen**, the 289th); one more from the Hall (**fourteen**, the 290th). Three signs are described: a hooked upright, a thing like a gate, a thing like a fork. **She has read no word that is not a number.** One group of six signs stands four times in the Stair's twenty-nine, always after a bearer; she has four of its letters and a word that she wishes to find there, and she has covered it and not written it.
    89	- **Brom's yard.** A broker's dray (two wheels as tall as Lira, one mule, a square of slate painted on its side) loaded with roofing slate shed its near wheel on the yard's slope; the slates slid and pinned the carter's lad by the boot against the dock's kerb. Brom stopped the yard-men's lever at the high side, took the low corner on his left shoulder with no grey, held while Lira drew the lad out by his belt, and was let down onto two split logs driven under the axle by Cael and a yard-man. The lad's five toes bend. **The broker's factor** (a good grey cloak, a broad hat, a shaved chin, clean hands, a tally; no name, no face): the house is the third on the brokers' street with the slate above its door; a guard's post at its store inside the second wall; a standing pass, sealed, by the month; a bed in the house above the wall; the house's coat; **four marks, and found**. His master "keeps eleven on his doors" who will not shift a keg until they have lit up, "and then it's a line in somebody's book". Brom: "I'd have taken it at sixteen and written home the same night."
    90	- **Seln's night (the 286th).** The district is empty at night; a watch's handcart goes its round with a lantern; the blue gate is shut, with a brazier in the arch. He sat on the leads of **the chandler's warehouse on the ring road, the tallest roof below the blue gate** (four floors, a hoist-beam, an outside stair with no door). The case weighs "something under three pounds"; its key is in his waistcoat. **Every registry on the Line has three doors**: the front for names; the yard for loads; and a returns hatch at the side, with a lamp and a night clerk, where a courier lays a sealed thing at any hour and neither may ask the other a question: "A registry enters what is laid on its ledge." Treswick's is down a passage off the square's eastern corner. He considered the hatch and put it by; he considered leaving the case on the Deepstacks' unticketed shelves and saw that for filing; he ruled nothing. He marked that a clerk had seen his face and that nothing had fallen.
    91	- **The watchers.** A man in the smith's doorway opposite the inn's arch on the night of the 286th; a porter on the kerb opposite the cut's head on the 288th, from the second bell to the third, with nothing on his frame; the same boots both times, a horseman's with the spur-rests cut away. On the night of the 290th the same boots, hooded, on the step of the painted door under its lamp, waiting until the five had come in under the arch.
    92	- **Vastin.** At the yard on the 286th he held the dray's mule by the cheek-strap with the left hand and gave no order. On the 287th he moved toward the lamp four times (Lira counted); on the 288th twice; on the 290th not at all. On the 288th he began to post a watch and stopped ("That's one"). On the 289th he held three tall books at Lira's word, qualifying it ("I cannot undertake to carry them to a table"). His three heads on the night of the 290th: the knee (he had Oryn's words in his mind as the stick came down, and asks that she be told that he heard her); what the six came for (they looked past him at Cael); and that he knows no more of what Cael is than the plate did.
    93	- **The alley.** The gate at the cut's foot can be barred from inside the cut. **An iron bar is let into the wall at the cut's mouth, beside the parapet, for people coming up under loads.** Six, hooded, each with a cloth over the lower face: two with shod staves from the yard; above, one kneeling with a crossbow on the highest landing, one with a stave beside him, one who came down to the plane, and one at the rail with a shuttered lantern. The plane stood from the lip of the ninth step, wall to wall and to the sky, seen for a breath as a bloom on the dusk. Two bolts broke on it (the second a hand from Cael's face); a palm and one shoulder were laid on it. Brom dropped his box across the fourth step, tipped up the loose third tread (a slab of dark stone a forearm long and a hand thick), lifted it to his thighs and let it go down the steps at the first stave. Lira came off the landing over the box and drove her knife a third of its length into the second stave's shaft; she took the wall on her right shoulder. Brom's boot put the second man down the steps; one of them then hauled on the box's becket, and Brom sat on the box. Karis's boot went from under her on the dished sixth step; her bare left hand opened on the lip of the step above. The hood on the landing lifted two fingers; the lantern was raised to the parapet; **Seln's cord (a clerk's page-weight: a pear of lead on a thin cord) took the arm to the rail** before the shutter opened. Two low notes whistled. They left at a walk: the bow unstrung, the cord cut in two places, the dead lantern taken up, the gate unbarred. Cael's count was eighty-one. **A stave was left** (a watchman's, shortened, its ferrule filed) and **two bolts** (plain, no fletcher's thread).
    94	- **Seln's finding.** They opened no Path, though the one on the landing stood like a man who has one: a Path opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it, and they took the same care as the six to have nothing of theirs entered. The bow was to make somebody open; the light was to tell somebody below. They went for a box and not for Brom: they were told to bring away what was carried. "What they would have been glad to find was on a roof above their heads." The four went two east and two west along the lane, hoods back before the first corner; the two below left by the carts' gate, which had been left on the latch for them.
    95	- **Karis's hand.** A straight cut across the heel of the left palm, from the root of the thumb to the wrist's other side; "not deep… only long". Bound in the cut with Brom's neckcloth; sewn in the loft with **seven stitches** of waxed thread on a curved roping needle held in the lantern's flame; the thread bitten; bound with a strip of his shirt. She said her fourteen letters over, one to a stitch. She writes with the right. Why no healer: in the district's house the entry on the sheet at a bed's foot (name, registry, tier) precedes the treatment, and after the fourth bell a card waits until those with names have been seen.
    96	- **Small things.** Brom keeps a twist of cloth under his box's lashing with a roping needle and waxed thread. A pot of horse liniment was begged from the stable for his left shoulder. Vastin mended the becket of Cael's box with the stable's awl. The old keeper at the third gate did not write the lateness against the card on the 290th. The third tread of the cut's bottom flight is now out of its bed.
    97	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the carter's lad; two yard-men; the broker's factor; the under-registrar (spoken of); the watch; the six in hoods.
    98	
    99	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   100	
   101	**Calendar.** The night of the **two hundred and ninetieth day past the Line**, in the long loft at Treswick. Days on the page: 284 (the Deepstacks), 285 (the hatch; the lamp), 286 (the dray; the board; Seln's roof at night), 287 (the morning log; the figures; four times; Cleon's week and close; the parapet), 288 (the test; ten letters; "We are looked at"), 289 (the loft paid; the ladders; thirteen), 290 (fourteen; the alley; the loft). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   102	
   103	**Where everyone is.** Six in the loft: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Vastin (on a pallet under the blanketed gable window, unable to stand), and Seln on the ladder's head. Cleon is under the registry with fifty-two sheets on three tables, the board against the third, and tomorrow's card written and given. The clerk has written *the same* on six days. Oryn and the brown mule are north on her round (not on the page in this movement). Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
   104	
   105	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Eleven fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He has stood unfiled in the middle gallery. He has been the lamp since the 285th and has knelt two feet from Rune in use for five days; nothing has come of that. He has begun to put the light where the looking will go. He wanted the Path tried on himself, wrote that he had not said so, and has not said so. He has the eleventh (Iron-Wall-adjacent), has read its lines, has told it no three times, and has not used it. **He has not told Vastin**, and holds that he cannot without telling the mechanism, which is the six's. He has not set foot on the square or its steps and has not looked at the crown; he has walked the lane with its four alleys fourteen times. He has ruled that the cut is not climbed at dusk again and has ruled nothing else for the morning. Body: sound; a tarred case struck his ear.
   106	
   107	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Arm: day twenty-five out of the wool on the 290th.** It held a lamp-ring, a ladder's rail and a belt; she has practised dropping off a ladder with it tucked (eleven times before the 289th) and came off a landing so in the alley. **Right shoulder bruised on a wall; the arm took nothing.** Her knife is sound. Her registry paper has not been touched. She keeps the square's side of Cael in the lane. She has read the 285th's log and the 290th's.
   108	
   109	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **No grey at any point.** Toes done on the 289th. **Left shoulder wrenched under the dray on the 286th and made worse by the slab on the 290th; he cannot raise either arm above the elbow.** He has carried his box on the bound right shoulder since the 286th and took two falling people on that arm on the 290th: owed to two healers at the mill, entered by Cael as his own debt as much as Brom's. He was offered the broker's post and has, in his own mind, turned it down (the factor has been told only "I'll think on it"). **The purse: a hundred and thirty-four.** He sewed Karis's hand. He means to buy the rope going out.
   110	
   111	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork. She has said "Older than [UNBOUND]" once, at Cleon's table. She has been believed. **Fourteen letters of twenty-three; she can read the cutter's figures and number-words and nothing else, and will not guess.** She knows that seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried and eleven are nothing; that a bearer heads every run; that the board and the floors carry the same rows. She has not asked for the bundle. **Vastin's page is in her coat, unshown; the title on it has not been spoken.** She holds the flat, the hide, the ledger, her notebook and the day's card. **Left palm: seven stitches, bound in a strip of Brom's shirt.** Three days of Cleon's week remain.
   112	
   113	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. A clerk of the Treswick registry knows his face and has heard him speak. He watches from the roof over the cut. He has looked at the registry, its three doors and the station's light from a roof, with the case on him, and has ruled nothing. **The case is on him, shut; its contents are not named in this movement.** He has the better half of his cord and the stave. He has seen the same boots three times.
   114	
   115	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **He stood a wall on the eleventh day of thirty.** The knee let go sideways when he released it; one lath cracked; Brom has bound it again with a slat; it is twice its size; he cannot stand or hold a bowl on the night of the 290th. He knows what Oryn said it would cost. He gave his name, rank and office at the yard hatch on the 285th and was not written. He has apologised to Seln and been told to be nobody. He has asked for the wall to be entered and has been told that it is. **He does not know** the mechanism, what Cael carries, that anything was taken from him, the sum, or what is under the bar; he does not know what the six in hoods hoped to see and has said that he does not ask. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. Whether he heard Seln speak of the roof is not known.
   116	
   117	**Cleon.** Rune Path, Bronze; shown from outside only. He has said that he knows the hand; has shown the board; has said what bears, what is carried and what is nothing; has given the limit in both its parts; has given a week from the 287th; has spoken his close; has Karis's name and has used it once. **He has taken no notice of Cael.** He does not know that anybody was set upon in the cut.
   118	
   119	**The clerk.** Unnamed. One question asked ("The same?"). He has heard a name, a rank and an office said at his hatch and has written none of them.
   120	
   121	**The faction.** Six, hooded, unnamed, faceless. It reached inside the third wall, opened no Path, took nobody and nothing, and saw a wall, a rock, a knife in a stave and a boy standing on a step. **It saw nothing of what Cael carries.** It saw Vastin's face uncovered and an Iron Wall. It knows the loft, and has shown that it knows.
   122	
   123	**Objects and money.** **134 marks** (136 less two for the loft's second week, paid on the 289th; the loft is theirs to the 295th). The reserve sealed in the third box. Three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide are in the loft. The fifty-two first copies are on Cleon's tables. In the loft besides: a shortened watchman's stave with a knife-cut in it; the card for the 291st. Two bolts were left lying in the cut. Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman.
   124	
   125	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; not Vastin, Cleon or the clerk. The eleventh: Cael and Lira (from the log). The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone. Cael's wish to be read: Cael and his book (Lira reads the book).
   126	
   127	**Open threads.** Five markers. A debt to a man who cannot be told. A knee that may have bought a stick. Two shoulders and a hand for two healers at a mill. A bundle that nobody has untied, and a page that has not been shown. Nine letters to find, and one word covered with a glove. A boy who knows which way a lamp will be wanted. A question that he has not asked, of a man who has not looked up. A case that has been looked at. A clerk who has heard a name. Boots under a lamp. A stair that will not be climbed at dusk, and no ruling yet on what is to be climbed instead.
   128	
   129	## Owner flags
   130	
   131	1. **The notice's typography and frame.** The protected text of fragment eleven names its source ("Vastin."), counts ("Eleventh.") and gives "Deployment: none", none of which the edition's earlier system blocks do (Book 7's reads `[unnamed]`, and Cael remarks there that it always does). I set it verbatim in a fenced block, as the edition sets system text, and framed it as the lines "put into the plain words in which he would have to write" them, so that nothing says the system itself has begun to name people. If it should be italic log text, or the frame cut, the wording is untouched either way.
   132	2. **How the eleventh came.** It is not reached for (unlike Tide in Book 7). It arrives, as "nine of the ten" did, from standing within a stride of a Path used in earnest, in the minute when he is refusing the other ten. Its question (*This far?*) is mine. Cael notes that he stood a count of twenty by the wall at the cut and a minute and some here, and draws no cause.
   133	3. **"I put the wall in front of it anyway."** The plan does not pin "it". I staged it as what Cael is, which Vastin could not read at four feet and cannot now (his "third head"). If the source's antecedent is otherwise (the healer's order, say), the three sentences before the line want re-composing; the knee and Oryn are his "first head", a few lines earlier.
   134	4. **Seln's case and Book 6.** The map and the packet call the case "unopened" and its contents "unknown". This edition's Book 6 opened it once, on a table above Ostrand, for five people, and Book 7 says only that it has been "carried shut". I wrote *shut* and never *unopened*; no content is named; Seln keeps his Book 6 rule of never describing it, even to himself; Cael's narration says that he knows what is in it and that it does not go into a book.
   135	5. **Who was in the yard on the 285th.** For Seln to answer the clerk he had to be within speech, though he kept off the 284th's count because a clerk might know his face. I put him on the hatch's own bench as a porter waiting for a return load, unseen by the five until he stood. What not lying cost him is that face.
   136	6. **The clerk's difficulty.** The plan gives Vastin's reflex and Seln's answer but not what was at stake at the hatch. Mine: an honest clerk who would not write six boxes for three and was about to send up for the under-registrar. He asks one question in this movement, of the boxes.
   137	7. **Cleon's week (from the 287th).** Minted so that the packet's "three days left of Cleon's week" on the 290th and §13's "seventh day" (the 293rd) are both true. Karis's own "Give me a week" was said to the crew on the 284th.
   138	8. **The purse: 134.** The loft's first week ran out on the 288th. The map's cash track (136, then 130 on the 292nd after a four-mark meal) needs two marks spent between; I paid a second week on the 289th.
   139	9. **The decipherment's method**, the board's figures (a ring for five, a peck for one), the counts **seven / twenty-three / eleven**, and **fourteen letters by the 290th**. The number-words are tested by the pattern of repeated letters, which is given in the story's own tongue as it is everywhere else (twelve, nine, seven; then ten, the number after ten, and four more). No word but a number is read; the bundle is not opened; Vastin's page is not shown and its title is not spoken. If Movement 6 needs her further on or further back, the figures in chapters 31 and 32 are the places.
   140	10. **The faction's aim and limit.** Mine: they wanted a declaration seen from the town (the shuttered lantern at the parapet) and a box carried off, and they opened no Path because they cannot be entered in that town's books any more than the six can. The plan's "the cache was on the roof the whole time" is Seln's one sentence about a parapet. Whether they knew Vastin is left open (they looked past him).
   141	11. **Vastin's wall was a declaration inside the third wall**, in a slot with no door or window. Nobody but the six in hoods saw it. I have not said whether anything on the crown felt it.
   142	12. **Iron Wall "has no tier" in the lines.** Cael reads "Tier equivalent unknown" against Vastin's remark at the shoulder hold that the Path's rank "would mean nothing on this road". No tier is stated for Vastin.
   143	13. **The rail at the cut's mouth** (BOOK_MAP §8 names "the rail"; Movement 4's cut has none). I added a single iron bar let into the wall at the top, beside the parapet.
   144	14. **Order on the stair in the alley:** Vastin, Cael, Lira, Karis, Brom. Movement 4 went down it Lira, Brom, Cael-below-Vastin, with Karis first into the yard; going up the slowest leads.
   145	15. **Lira's remark** that she fed Cael for a day "after a stair" rests on Book 7's bill for Shadow at the Stair (dead to the elbows). It is new as an event.
   146	16. **The under-registrar, the returns hatch and "three doors to every registry"** are new furniture of the Compact's houses.
   147	17. **A sequence lock, kept by a hair:** on the 289th Lira tells Vastin to hold three books and he does, having first qualified it. §13 says he takes no order without walking it back before Millrace; he walks this one back before he takes it.
   148	18. **Cael's ruling for the 291st is left open** (only that the cut is not climbed at dusk again), so that Movement 6 may send them by the carts' street in daylight, as the map has it, on its own terms.
   149	19. **Length:** 36,066 against 35,000 (about 330 of it marker text).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:Cael had his own stave, which was no longer than his forearm and hand, with a rag bound round the grip. He stood four strides off. Wind was in his feet, as it was at the start of everything; it leaned him away to the right, with a burst ready behind the lean, and he told it no and felt his own two legs take the weight instead. The read asked next, civilly, for the ground under that stave. He shut it. No Paths meant the ten, whatever he called them, and he had written so. They were not much. He had bought them on this grass, a touch at a time, from a seven-foot pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:51:"On my arm's lateness. Not at my arm. There's a difference, and you know what it is, because somebody did it to you with a pole all the cold term and you wrote it down." She put her left hand back on the stave, a yard below the right, and shut the fingers one at a time. "I'll keep it on. You stop minding it. That's a bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:71:It came out of her between her teeth. He had stopped already, and it was half a breath before he could make himself go on. In that half breath he watched her do the thing that she had come out there to do. The hand was shaking; she looked at it, once, as she might have looked at a dog that had run from a noise. The fingers closed again on the wood. They closed in their slow order, the first and then the next, and when the last was shut she heaved with the whole of that side, and the stave's lower end came round under his guard and thumped against his right ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:"That it's there. And that it didn't care for that." She opened the hand and shut it. "It's said so. I heard. Again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:It rained that night, for the first time since the snow, and in the morning Lira came to the board with her left arm held in against her and her mouth shut. A healer had told her that it would ache in the wet for a year. She did not speak of it. When Brom wanted the new hurdle lifted into its place between the shed's posts, she took one end of it in both hands before anybody else had stirred, and walked it round the drum with her jaw set, and lashed her end to its post herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:The old man stood at the gable, under the blanket, as he stood every night until the last of them was down. He had an awl in his fingers again. There was nothing left on the tarred case that wanted stitching, and he had begun on the becket of Cael's box, without asking, where the rope had started to fray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:Shadow had lifted at dusk, in the lane, between the second alley and the third. Cael had told it no while a bar of pale light went over his boots. It lay shut now, and the other nine had come back, each to its own place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:35:There was a case. It hung on a strap across that man's chest, and it had hung there on every road since Ostrand, shut. Cael knew what was in it. Five of them had gone through it on a table, by a shaded lamp, on a night above Ostrand; and he had written the next morning that it did not go into a book. It had not gone into this one either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:37:And they were inside a wall now. They were two hundred yards beneath the oldest registry on the Line; and that case had not been inside a Compact wall since five people went down a bluff with a cart. He did not know what a man did who brought such a thing back within reach of the people that it concerned. He did not think that Seln knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:51:The half-door was shut and barred at that hour, and the woman who leaned on it was asleep. He went out by the carriage-arch, past the painted door. Its lamp burned, and the book lay open on its stand within, with a pen on a chain, for whoever came late and had a name to give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:He had not seen it so before. He had gone up it once as a counted hand, with his eyes where a porter's eyes are, on the setts; and he had watched a yard from a bench, and a door from a parapet. From here it was laid out as a plan is. The second wall went round below his feet, with the blue gate in it, shut, and a brazier burning in the arch where two keepers stood their night. Above that the slate roofs climbed, close and dark. Above the slates ran the third wall, the old one, low and pale; and over the wall, like the back of a great book stood on a shelf, the registry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:77:He did not go over what was in it. He could have named every sheet, and the order in which they lay, and that was exactly the reason. He had told a boy once that a thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known, and he had kept that case for twelve years by never once describing it to anybody. He had found, a long while ago, that the rule held best if he did not except himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:79:It was shut. That was all that he needed to have about it tonight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:He had stood over a spring six days since, with a stolen sheet in his sleeve, and had gone through the three things that his training let him do with a fact. He had not been able to look at the case that morning. He had said to himself that a man cannot hold two closed things up to the light at once; and it had been true, and it had also been convenient.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:He had opened the one. He had set it between two hands on a rock, and gone to stand by the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:89:There were three doors to that building, as there are to every registry on the Line, and he had used the third for twelve years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:A line would be written. *One case, locked. By hand.* It would go up in the morning to a counter. Somebody would open it there, with a key or without one. He could not see past that. He had been able to see past it once; he had known the names of the men who read that sort of return, and what each of them wanted, and which would carry it up a stair and which would carry it down. An old officer had stood in a hayloft four days ago and said that he no longer knew who read this town's books at night. If that man did not know, a copying clerk could hardly pretend to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:There were four names, besides, which must not be written inside a wall. He had counted an hour for them, on a ladder. What was in the case had those names nowhere in it, and it led to them all the same, as a road leads to a house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:115:He found, when he had been still for a while, that one matter had changed, and it was not the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:It did not rule on anything. He set it beside the case, as a fact about doors which he had not possessed before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:129:He sat until the brazier in the blue gate had burned low. He had looked at the registry, and at its three doors, and at a pale light that did not flicker; and he had looked at the thing on his chest for the first time in twelve years without his hand going to the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:155:Cael did not know what had been looked at. It might have been a case. It might have been a door, or a light on a crown, or the whole of fifteen years. He found that he could not ask. He had been given three words by somebody who weighed them like coin, and it was a great many for that purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:There was a cushion on the second chair that morning. It had been flattened by a great many years into the shape of one person, and the chair that stood before the desk's slope had none. Brom looked at it, and then at the grey back bent over a page, and opened his mouth; and Lira trod on his right foot, which was very nearly well, and he shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:195:Karis stood up. She shut her notebook on the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:279:Karis had not moved in the chair. Her two gloves lay shut in her lap. Cael, at the table's end, could not see her face, and he thought that he ought not to try.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:37:Vastin had been standing at the gable all this while, under the blanket, with a strap of the tarred case in his hands. He had found a place where the stitching had begun to go, and he was working at it with an awl that he had borrowed of the stable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:53:He stood at the very end of it, by the yard door, where the draught was, with his back to the room and his face to the stone. He was not tall. He had grey hair, cut short and badly, as a person cuts his own; and a coat that had been brown and was now the colour of dust, with dust worked into every seam of it, paler than any that the street could have put there. His shoulders were round; and there was a bowl in front of him, from which he was eating steadily, with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:Cael looked at that for some time. The man did not blink them shut and open them, as someone does who is tired. He kept them closed, and found the bowl with his spoon by habit, and chewed, and rested them as a carter rests a horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:So this was Cleon. The name had been said at a spring, by a small pale woman with one glove off, and asked after once; nobody had said it since. It sat on a dusty coat and a pair of shut eyes no better and no worse than a name ever sits on a stranger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:71:It was the paper that he heard, Cael thought, or the breath of it settling. The grey head turned a little, and the eyes opened; and they were not as Cael had expected, since they were red at the rims, and wet, like the eyes of a man who has been all night over a small flame. They went to the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:He was silent, and he had not looked at her yet; he had looked at nothing in that room but a sheet of paper, since his eyes opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:145:"Three boxes. A surveyor's case. What I have under my arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Not a word. Not what they're for, and not who you reckon made them, and not how old. Not till I've looked." For the first time there was something in the rough voice besides weariness. "I've had half my life of people telling me what a thing is before I've seen how it's put together. And then I can't see it. I see what I was told. You'll keep your mouth shut on it till I say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:259:Nobody said who was to carry. There was a figure on that card, and three boxes had three backs under them; and there was a tarred case, and a man with a straw in his fingers who went ahead of every load and had never yet been counted with one. Cael marked it, as he marked anything that people took care to step round. It could be left until morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:279:Cael shut the book, and went and sat on the boards beside her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:77:The road opened there into a small paved place, where the street from the trough came up to meet the arch. There was a cook-house built against the wall on the near hand, with smoke going up from it, and a water-butt at its corner. Cael put his shoulder against the butt and stood. Nobody looks twice at a person who is waiting beside water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:87:There were strangers who came by right. He saw only one, a young man in travelling boots, who had the look of a courier. He took a folded paper from his coat without being asked, and the keeper with empty hands opened it and read it all, and read out a name and a district and a rank to his fellow; and the pen wrote for as long as it takes to say a line. The young man had his paper back and went up. He had been perfectly easy. He had been written in a book as a person is who has never had cause to mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:101:"I know they're fresh. They'll be fresh in the morning." He was not unkind about it. He gave her the card, and pointed down the hill with the pen; and she went and sat on the kerb with the basket on her knees, and looked at the arch as a person looks at a shop that has shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:167:He thanked her. She had shut her eyes already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:183:He had done it in the lower room of the inn, which opened off the yard: a long low place with one table down the middle of it and a bench on either side, a hearth at the far end, and a hatch through which bowls came. He had sat on the bench's end with the bound leg laid along it. There was a post behind him, and a strap hung from the post, and he had marked both before he let himself down. He would be able to get up by them. He had sat twice now in two days, and he noted, with the interest that he gave to any finding, that the second time had cost him less than the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:275:The small pale woman on the pallet had turned her head. She was looking at the row under the eaves, where three boxes lay with their beckets to the wall and a tarred case leaned against the last; and her lips moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:325:Brom turned the lantern low. The blanket hung over the gable, with a faint paleness round its edges that did not flicker; and Cael lay back, with the book shut on his chest and his eyes on the slates a yard above him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:5:They came to it at dusk on the two hundred and eightieth day. One pair of ruts went on past it to the west, and began at once to climb a long bare rise that shut out the sky on that side. The other turned north, along the moor's edge, toward the blue hills. Somebody had cut a hand into each face of the stone, with the fingers pointing, and a hundred cold terms had worn both hands nearly smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:61:She let them have that for the space of three breaths, and Cael did not need it explained. He had been a sheet with an empty column since his Kindling; and he understood now why a healer who had never asked him for a tier had once looked at his hand, on a mountain, as a person looks at a shut press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:87:She came round the fire then. She stopped at her stride and a half from Cael, and he saw her mouth open; and he knew the sentence before it came, not by its words but by its shape, since he had watched it arrive in her three times and be shut in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:89:This time she did not shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:He took the book out of his coat, and opened it on his knee, toward the fire. He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it. Then he wrote what he had been asked for, and it took him a good deal longer than the length of it deserved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:147:She stopped before the sill of the hearth, where a small grey man sat with a case on his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:209:Seln made no move toward the third, and nobody looked for him to. He went thirty yards ahead of any column with his hands empty, since a crew does not load the one person in it whose work is to see what is coming; and he had a case on his chest already that he had never yet set down. The third box stood on the frozen turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:There was not a great deal else to be done, since Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them off a landing. The tarred case that held a dead man's cord had come off the mule last of all, and it leaned against the foot of the fork-stone, where Brom had set it. The healer's pannier stayed on the mule, with her linen in it, and a bow hung beside it with its one bolt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:229:She did not say goodbye, since she had never said it to anybody, at any gate. She looked at each of them once, in no order, and at a bound knee last. Then she stood a moment before Cael, with her free hand shut, at her own distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:Nobody needed to be told. A tarred case leaned there by itself. She had a flat under one arm and a hide under the other; three backs had a box apiece; and the man who went in front carried nothing, as he had to. On the northern rut the bend of the moor had that moment taken a healer and a mule out of sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:283:The ruts went over a lip of bare rock and began to go down; and the whole country opened in front of him, as it had opened once from a shelf, on the second day past the Line, when it was the east that he was looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:297:It stood above the highest roofs, at the very top, where a town of that kind keeps the one building that reads. It was pale, and very small at that distance, and it did not flicker as a fire does. It burned as steadily in the full noon as a lamp burns in a shut room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:305:Vastin stood with both hands on its head, getting his breath, with the bound leg a little out and a tarred case on his shoulder. He looked at the town for some time. He had gone in at those gates, no doubt, on a horse, behind a clerk, with a seal; and he looked now as a man looks at a house that he once had the keys of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:313:She looked at him. Then she shifted the hide, and put her gloved hand into the breast of her coat, and brought out a square of oilcloth no bigger than a man's palm. She opened it on the top of Brom's box, along its own creases, and held down the corner that the wind wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:337:The stick was on his left. It came down on the turf at the rut's edge a little after each of his own strides, and a dead man's cord swung beside it in its tarred case. Nobody had told that man where to walk, on the first day of this road, and he had walked last, and a little too far back. Nobody had told him today. He had only not dropped behind when the file made up; and he was there, a shoulder off, on the ditch side, an old man who had been a marshal and was learning to be nobody.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/drafting/EDIT_DECISIONS.md:48:"eleven" appears 158×; target ≤3 per chapter. KEEP ONLY: the Kindling's eleven seconds (ch9, ch15 echoes), "eleven Paths", the eleven-pound response/case/truth motif (three uses), ch18's "eleven sittings" + "eleven administrative items", and ch4's deliberate "eleven seconds" door-log echo. RE-ROLL the rest to varied numbers (round numbers are allowed; vary shape: 8, a dozen, forty, two hundred, "a few"). Chapters over budget: ch4(8), ch5(7), ch9(10), ch11(9), ch12(8), ch13(18), ch14(11), ch15(10), ch18(14), ch20(10), ch21(12).
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/drafting/EDIT_DECISIONS.md:49:- Also: "nineteen" — keep Seln's nineteen postings anchor; re-roll incidental nineteens (leaves, seconds, lots...). "four hundred" — keep the code's four-hundred-year age; the 91-fix removes others; re-roll remaining incidentals. Seln's case documents "four hundred and ten" → "three hundred and eighty" everywhere (his REPORTS stay four hundred and ten). Halcenvane enrollees "four hundred and ten" → "four hundred and thirty". "sixty-one": Vastin becomes fifty-one; vary the lodge-man's and the filed Bronze's ages so no two share a figure.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:28:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (this book — the challenge):** The Compact resolves the threat-classification question Book 5's Ch24 convening opened by filing a formal classification challenge (served Chapter 1, sustained to full proceedings Chapter 8, ruled Chapter 18). The challenge, exactly per the bible: it would remove Cael's demonstration-provision enrollment and compel him to submit to a Compact re-evaluation — which he would fail, because the Compact cannot evaluate [SHATTERED] architecture, and their procedure for failed evaluations is expulsion and asset-restriction. The trap's anatomy is anatomized on the page (Chapter 2) and the B4 checkpoint's cross-check is honored explicitly: the falsification machinery lives in *Arbiter evaluation*; the assay clause bypasses Arbiter records entirely, which is precisely why it could hold Cael — and the challenge's entire design is to herd him out of the one legal stratum the machinery cannot reach and back into the stratum it owns. Karis says the first half of this in Chapter 2 without knowing why it's true. Chapter 16 pays the sentence off in full.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:29:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (this book — THE REVELATION; SERIES SECRET #2 DISCHARGED ON SCHEDULE):** Seln provides his intelligence cache — the files he collected during his years as a Compact operative, carried through nineteen postings in the locked case Book 4 established and Book 5 kept visible — opened on the page in Chapter 15, analyzed in Chapter 16. The files confirm: **the Guilds Compact has been falsifying Path classifications for two hundred years.** Practitioners who develop politically inconvenient abilities are reclassified into lesser Paths at their Kindling evaluation, via a modification built into the Arbiter evaluation protocol itself — a modification made so long ago that no current Compact official knows it's there. REACH discipline is absolute: the revelation is known at book's end to exactly six people — the inner circle (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis), Seln, Vastin (Chapter 17), and no one else. It is NOT published, NOT filed, NOT used in the proceedings, and the book argues why on the page (Chapter 19): evidence of the Arbiter protocol's corruption cannot be verified by any process that runs on Arbiters, and filing it burns Seln for nothing. The truth is carried out the door, not detonated. Its public exposure belongs to later books.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:30:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (the Level 4 payoff — the flag finally MEANS something):** The Suppression-Advisory Watch Level 4 flag — planted B2 Ch7/Ch15, carried by Havel's private note since Ardenmere and Ilsev's referrals since the Greyvane hearing, returned "no originating authority of record" (B4) and then "closed — no further inquiry authorized" (B5) — is connected, explicitly and on the page (Chapter 16), to the falsification machinery. The staging: the cache's protocol-era maintenance fragment documents the evaluation sub-layer's "advisory corrections" function — the falsification mechanism itself; and the sub-layer's *protection behavior* is what Ilsev's paper trail has been photographing for three years. When the Arbiter meets an architecture it cannot reclassify into a lesser Path — because [SHATTERED] has no lesser bin — the corrections function cannot fire, and what fires instead is the suppression-advisory flag: the machinery's contingency for the case it cannot falsify. The returns Ilsev received are the sub-layer defending itself — auto-closing inquiries no living official authorized because no living official runs that layer. Karis lays Ilsev's on-record testimony (Chapter 18 enters the returns' exact text into public record; Karis obtains the transcript) beside the cache's fragment and says the payoff line: "She's been interrogating the mechanism for three books and filing its answers. She just never knew what was answering." Ilsev and Havel do NOT learn the truth — their notes mean something to the READER and to the circle; the carriers themselves are left intact, unknowing, and available (see below). **One deliberate residue kept open for Book 8:** the sub-layer's base stratum is demonstrably OLDER than the two-hundred-year modification written into it — the cache documents the modification, not the layer; who built the layer, and when, is beyond the cache's reach and beyond this book's. Stated on the page, once, and locked.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:32:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (the walk-out):** Chapters 21–22, with the bible's four images staged verbatim: the companions leave Halcenvane together — not expelled, they **resign** (timed before the compelled re-evaluation date, converting the machine's expulsion into their refusal); **Karis forfeits her ranking** (the procedural act on the page — Iron-tier Rank 3 and her research standing, signed away at Bracken's counter); **Lira walks out of the last class she'll ever take at a formal institution** (mid-session, unhurried, the door closing on the Silver trajectory Book 5 built for exactly this cost); **Brom carries three boxes of Karis's compiled materials** (the Book 5 pending-advancement-review thread paid off here — see below — the review is what he's walking away from, and the boxes are what he chooses to carry instead); **Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning** (Chapter 22, staged in his register: no scene of decision, no goodbye, keys and files in perfect order, the man and the case gone).
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:41:- [ ] **NEW open threads (deliberate):** **The faceless faction** — the "people who want this to stop" from Vastin's B5 warning are the book's moving hand and are NEVER named, faced, or located: Procurator Jent is visibly their counsel and visibly not their face; the off-channel teams are their reach; the proceedings' presiding registrar is identified in the record by seal only (a deliberate structural choice — the book's title made procedural). Who they are individually remains open into Arc 3+. **The unnamed operative** — Cael now carries a fragment of a person with no name; filed in the log as an unpaid, unpayable account; open texture, available. **Havel's transfer** — after the ruling, Havel requests reassignment out of monitoring (Chapter 18); the private note, six entries deep, goes with him; carrier preserved, unknowing, available to later books. **Ilsev after the ruling** — she executes her office to the letter and is last seen filing the proceedings' record with a precision the chapter frames as its own kind of testimony; what a career textualist does after administering a forbidden question is left open. **Withrow's ledger** — "The record will say you left. I'll spend the rest of my tenure making the record say why" (Ch19, delivered at the walkout council; the gate farewell in Ch21 is "Your files are in order. All four."); available. **The sub-layer's base stratum** — see Level 4 payoff above; the Book 8 bridge, opened as a question and locked.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:50:- *The instrument:* a formal challenge to enrollment basis, filed at registry level against Halcenvane's assay-provision enrollment — the output of the threat-classification proceedings Book 5's convening opened. It does not allege fraud (Book 3 closed that door) and does not contest the clause's text (Vastin's B5 advisory closed that one). It asserts instead that an unclassifiable practitioner presents an *evaluation-integrity question* that the demonstration provision cannot answer, and petitions for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:111:Lira's Silver-bracket program opens: Rooke's training floor, continental-grade drills, the trajectory she declared at Norhold ("I want to become something they haven't seen yet") now an institutional program with her name on it — the first time in her life the system has built a ladder *toward* her. The chapter gives her one bout, one win, and one unguarded sentence to Cael at the rail: "Three years ago I'd have told you I didn't need any of this. I'm better now. I can admit I want it." Brom's review paperwork arrives: the mandatory advancement review, docketed for term's end, the continental Copper title making the outcome — everyone says quietly — a formality. His family's shadow crosses the page once: a second Velmere-sealed envelope, unopened for two days, then opened alone; the chapter shows nothing of its contents and one fact about his face afterward: it is the face of a man being offered his whole ledger back. Karis's standing renewed: research budget, archive privileges, a junior assistant assigned to her — the institution finally treating her the way Ternhall never did. Ephram, cohort captain now, training beside Brom like a man who has decided something and is waiting for the right term to say it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:172:The silence is Seln's, and the chapter makes it load-bearing: he attends the whole council and contributes one procedural correction and nothing else. His window, closing the chapter — the longest window Part 2 gives him before the hinge: fifteen years of product, four years of this boy, a locked case that has outlasted nineteen postings, and the sentence he has never permitted himself to finish, forming anyway: *the file's question was always the wrong question — and I am the only person alive holding the file that proves whose question it was.* The window does not open the case. It notes, in Seln's own audit-voice, the cost of the case staying closed: every session the boy fights inside the instrument, believing the instrument is merely broken. The window's last line: "He thinks he's arguing with a malfunction. I've read the maintenance records. Fifteen years I've been deciding to die with that sentence. The deciding has stopped being quiet."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:201:The hinge — Seln's decision completing, staged in the register Book 4 built for him: visible entirely in behavior, confessed never. The chapter opens on his window and stays there longer than the series has ever stayed: the escort order's forensics (he traced the paper stock; he knows which supply chain it walked out of; he has *run* operations on that stock), the pre-restriction inventory, the four grades of pressure he can name that come next — and against all of it, the boy's Chapter 9 sentence, quoted in Seln's own memory with professional distaste: *the text still works.* The window walks his logic one last time — the operative's catechism, fifteen years old: insurance is only insurance while it stays sealed; the moment the case opens, the man holding it stops being a keeper and becomes a witness, and witnesses do not retire — and then watches the logic lose, without drama, the way everything about Seln happens: he takes the case down. He does not open it. He carries it, for the first time in nineteen postings, out of his quarters and across the campus, and requests five minutes of Cael's evening.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:203:The conversation is four sentences long and the book stages it complete. Seln: "You said the text still works. I need to show you whose text it is. Not here — all five of us, somewhere I choose, this week." Cael, who has spent four books reading this man and knows the case by sight the way the reader does: "You're sure." Seln: "No. That's how you'll know it's true."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:205:*Close on:* the arrangements, in tradecraft — the venue Seln selects (a records annex he controls the sightlines of), the schedule gap he manufactures, the null report he files that week shown one last time in the old pattern, true and complete and empty — and one paragraph of Havel, on the proceedings' records rotation at Ostrand, logging exhibits late at night, adding a sixth entry to the private note; carrier intact, unknowing, converging. The chapter ends the night before the annex, on Cael's log: "Fifteen years he's kept whatever it is. He's spending it on us. Whatever's in that case, it's about to become the most expensive thing anyone has ever handed me. Sleep. Read in the morning."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:236:The birthday is staged against the term's darkening sky, and the book lets both be true at once: Lira instigates, one evening, the five of them in the residence common room with the proceedings' documents deliberately stacked face-down on the far table; Brom acquires food with quiet competence; Ephram is invited — the first non-circle guest at a birthday in the series, the invitation itself a statement the chapter does not underline; Seln sits at the table like a man who has been sitting at tables all his life, and only the reader and Cael know the case is already down from its shelf. Hesk's package, held ready as always: a travel-grade log satchel, instrument-maker's work, oiled against weather — the gift's meaning hiding in plain sight — and the note in full: *"Eighteen. A man's tools should fit the road he's actually on, not the one they promised him. Your grandmother would say I'm being unsubtle. She'd be right. Mind the shape. — H."* Cael is eighteen, on the page, witnessed, the date written into the log in his own hand.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:245:The revelation, first movement — the locked case opened, on the page, after two books of the reader watching it stay shut. The annex: Seln's chosen ground, sightlines controlled, one lamp, the five of them. He opens the case without preamble — a keeper's economy, no speech — and the chapter stages the contents physically before intellectually, because the series trusts objects: fifteen years of an intelligence officer's private duplicates. Case files with reclassification orders clipped to Kindling records that do not match them. Field reports on practitioners — some of them names Seln was assigned to watch — whose demonstrated architecture exceeded their filed Path by margins no honest evaluation survives. Registry memoranda, copies of copies, a lineage of administrative language two centuries deep, all orbiting one recurring phrase in the oldest stratum: *corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.* And the artifact at the case's floor, in a wax-paper sleeve older than everything above it: a protocol-era maintenance directive fragment — genuine, provenance documented in Seln's own cipher — referencing the Arbiter evaluation sub-layer's *advisory corrections function*.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:249:And then the chapter's second movement, the debt paid: Cael answers the case in kind. The mechanism — the Book 3 SECRET — disclosed to Seln in full, on the page, the circle's first expansion since Karis: the integration, the fragments, the notices, the log. And Shadow-adjacent: acquired during the records-office operation Seln let him watch, sealed ever since — through the tournament, through the banking's death, through four exchanges against a Gold — because exposing it would have burned its source. Seln looks at the boy who kept his cover at Gold-tier cost, for a long moment, and the book gives him his sentence (drafting note: under ten words, no warmth performed, all of it real). The keeper and the kept, each holding the other's file for years, both files now open on the same table.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:251:*Close on:* the log, that night, the arc's centre of gravity: "Two hundred years. It was never a malfunction. Karis has the case until morning. Tomorrow we find out what the truth weighs. Tonight I keep thinking about every Kindling I've ever heard described — Alis Trent's, Joren's, Daeva's, mine — and the room behind the room, correcting."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:260:**Finding one — the mechanism and the flag.** The maintenance fragment's *advisory corrections function* lives in the evaluation sub-layer; the corrections fire at Kindling, rewriting inconvenient architecture into lesser Paths before the classification ever reaches a record a human reads. And the case the function cannot handle: an architecture with no lesser bin. Karis lays the proceedings transcript beside the fragment — Ilsev's Chapter 18-bound testimony already foreshadowed in her empanelment scope, the returns' exact text from Books 4 and 5 (*no originating authority of record; designation predates file creation* — *closed; no further inquiry authorized*) — and delivers the payoff three books built: the Suppression-Advisory Watch Level 4 flag is the corrections function's contingency — what the machinery does when it meets the thing it cannot falsify. The returns are the sub-layer defending itself, auto-closing inquiries no living official authorized because no living official runs that layer. "She's been interrogating the mechanism for three years and filing its answers," Karis says, the transcript in one hand and the fragment in the other. "She just never knew what was answering." (Ilsev and Havel learn nothing; the payoff is the reader's and the circle's. The carriers stay intact.)
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:264:*Close on:* the second pole arriving, intellectually, in writing, eight chapters before it lands in the body: the Power Log, end of chapter: "I spent three years proving I could work within the system. Tonight I read the system's maintenance records. It isn't broken. It's *maintained*. The system was never designed to include me — it was designed, by name, before my name, to correct me at the door, and I'm only here because I'm the case the corrector couldn't parse. So the question was never how to win inside. The question is what the five of us do with one locked case of the truth. Karis says there's exactly one honest man left inside the machine. Tomorrow we find out what the truth does to him."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:288:The proceedings conclude — the chapter where the system finishes its sentence, staged as procedure throughout, because the book's thesis is that the pretense never needs to drop *inside the room*: the room IS the pretense. The final sessions at Ostrand, compressed and braided: Jent's closing submission (discretion, integrity, the unscorable tournament exhibit deployed with courteous efficiency); the defense's closing (Karis's masterwork, entered in full, and the chapter says plainly it is the best legal argument the series has staged — better than the Greyvane hearing — and that it changes nothing, which is the point); and the refused signature landing in the record as one procedural line — *evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority* — the Archmarshal's withheld name a silence every registry-literate person on the continent will read.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:303:*Close on:* Withrow, informed alone, in her office, by Cael — the respondent chancellor learning her academy's defense ends by resignation. Her registry-trained arithmetic runs it in front of him: the resignation protects Halcenvane, ends the proceedings as moot, and forfeits nothing that wasn't already forfeit. Then the other column, unperformed: "I spent my tenure building an institution worth measuring. You were the best case it ever held." A pause, and the line the book banks for later arcs: "The record will say you left. I'll spend the rest of my tenure making the record say why." The log: "Five weeks became five days. We leave before the date. All of us. Nobody gets corrected."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:343:Seln's exit and the machine's answer — the two-track chapter the series uses for Compact chapters, run one last time in this configuration, and the bible's fourth image staged in its exact grammar: **Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning.** The chapter opens in the assessment office, deliberately not in Seln's POV: the morning clerk finds the desk squared, the keys aligned on the blotter, every working file completed and cross-indexed to a standard the office will use as a template for years, and no teaching assistant. No note. No gap in the records except the man. The office does what offices do — waits a day, asks around, files a personnel query — and the query lands in the monitoring apparatus like a dropped instrument: fifteen years of product from a fully-vetted officer, review-flagged overnight, and somewhere above the file a very quiet, very expensive question beginning to be asked about what else the officer kept. The chapter gives the reader one window, brief, final for this book, Seln on the east road a day ahead of the others by tradecraft habit: the last null report, drafted in his head from pure fifteen-year reflex — *subject's activity: routine* — and, for the first time since Book 4, not filed anywhere, because there is no desk, no chain, no cover. Just a man with a case on his shoulder, walking toward the first honest employment of his adult life. The window notes that he does not look back, and that the not-looking is unpracticed.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:347:*Close on:* the east road, the second evening, the five of them rejoined at the crossroads camp Seln chose — the found family complete, employed by no one, enrolled in nothing — and the chapter's last image: the case and the three boxes stacked together under the same canvas, the two archives of the whole story riding in one cart. The log: "Five of us. No desks. He was at the crossroads before us with a fire already built, which I understand is the closest thing to a confession of relief we will ever get."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:381:- [ ] **The revelation staged per bible, exactly:** Seln provides his intelligence cache — the files collected during his years as a Compact operative, carried since he walked away from the job (his functional defection, B4, honored as the referent) — opened Ch15, analyzed Ch16. Contents: two hundred years of falsified classifications; politically inconvenient abilities reclassified into lesser Paths at Kindling; the mechanism a modification built into the Arbiter evaluation protocol itself, so old no current official knows it's there. The Level 4 flag payoff staged explicitly: the flag is the corrections function's contingency for the architecture it cannot falsify; Ilsev's referral returns are the sub-layer defending itself; her Ch18 testimony enters the returns' text into public record and Karis's Ch16 analysis names the connection for the reader ("filing its answers"). Ilsev and Havel remain unknowing carriers — their notes pay off for the READER, not to them; both preserved intact for later books (Ilsev filing to the letter; Havel transferred out, note in hand, six entries).
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:383:- [ ] **The walk-out staged per bible, exactly:** Not expelled — they RESIGN, before the compelled re-evaluation date, in good standing (Ch21), converting the machine's verb into their own. The four images verbatim: Karis forfeits her ranking (Iron R3 + standing, line-itemed at Bracken's counter, Ch21); Lira walks out of the last class she'll ever take at a formal institution (mid-session, on her feet, Ch21); Brom carries three boxes of Karis's compiled materials (three trips, help refused, Ch21 — with the B5 pending-advancement-review thread paid off in Ch19: advancement FOUND, guidance-note priced at Cael, never answered — the review is what the boxes cost him and the chapter stages the exchange rate in his own four-word arithmetic); Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning (Ch22, no decision scene, no goodbye, keys squared, the case gone). Withrow does not stop them and banks her thread ("make the record say why"). Ephram stays — his arc complete (Ch20), his staying framed as right for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:24:- **Withrow's ledger**: the charter file opened toward him; the blank remedy column; **"Make a note of the word *choosing*."**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"I've heard the case twice," he said. "By the fire, and by daylight the day after, and it's the same case. I've no quarrel with any line of it. What I've not heard is the price, and I can't rule on a thing till I've had its price in figures. Seln. You said a gate, and a book. Say what happens. Slowly, as you'd say it to a carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:81:"That is a gate. A man sits in it who is paid by the registry, and he has a book." He laid Karis's shut notebook beside the bowl. "He does not care who comes in, since he is not a guard. He asks each person for a name, and where the name is registered, and he writes down what he is told; and the person walks on into the town and buys bread. It is the mildest thing in the world; I have watched it done ten thousand times, and I never saw a hand laid on anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:93:Lira had put her hand into her coat. She brought out a square of stiff paper, folded once, and opened it on the board, where a seal showed at its foot in dull red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:125:Oryn's spoon had stopped, and Cael watched her mouth, since he had seen a sentence arrive in it three nights ago, and saw it come again and be shut in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:Cael read the slips from the left-hand post. The thaw had opened the holds, and the holds wanted hands. There was a mill-race to be cleared of ice, and a byre roof, and on the south road a bridge of two logs that the flood had carried a furlong. *Carts over, the first boat; hands on the landing; a copper the cart.* Under that one there were strokes already, in three different thumbs. Not one slip of the eight asked what a person was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:223:He laced it on himself, and shut the fist, and opened it, and shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:225:He did not try it on anything. Cael did not know what Path that man carried, since nobody on this bank had ever told him; he knew only that it lived in that wrist, and had been kept shut for a hundred and twenty-four days by an old woman's order. There was a post at Teague's elbow and a stone trough at his knee, and a boy would have struck one of them, to see. Teague looked at his own knuckles for a breath, and let the hand hang, and came down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:Nobody at that fire could dispute it, and Cael went over the offer as he went over any slip. Inside a line the other four were exactly what this man had said of his own; they had fought there as such, three times, with a bill and a knife and a borrowed bow, and two of them sat here bound up in consequence. He himself was another matter, which Teague did not know. But he had never once stood up against anybody with the ten fragments shut, and holding a thing down on a road was not the same as going without it in a fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:He knew a clerk's way through a rule when he was the one finding it. He had been on the wrong end of such ways since his Kindling, and he knew exactly what they were worth to the person on the other side. He shut the read, and kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:"May I read that?" he said. "The ankle. It's the one thing I've got that does nothing unless there's a hand on somebody. I can't shut it for you. I could say how it lies."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:169:"Leave it shut till the thaw, and I'll hand you a wrist," she said. "Use it on that road and I'll hand you this board, and you'll walk up to your bearing with your arm on a plank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:221:He did not go down the bank. A man who goes and looks through a hurdle at what he has been shut out of has told the other crew what he is worth. There was a bench by the lower inn's hearth, and the boy knew where Teague sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:The cold road paid. He had salt work from now to the thaw, and more of it if the pass stayed shut. The bearing paid nothing; the bow had said so twice, and had been right both times. But Teague had told fifty people at a hearth that his crew would come, and he had not said it for a price, and he would have held to it if he had said it to a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:307:He shut the book. From the block, in the half dark, a hand had begun to slap a knee: a quick beat, and a slow one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:39:They went over in her line: Brom at the rope's length, with the mule's cheek-strap in the same fist, and Karis on the upstream side with both hands shut on the pack-girth, and Cael, and Seln last. The river leaned on them the whole way, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it after the first breath. He kept his left boot in a groove that he could not see, and his eyes on the back of Lira's neck, and the read shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:103:It was as thin as a rail. It was white, and there were eight small marks along it in a row, as pale now as the skin about them. She lifted it, and it rose slowly, and shook, and the fingers opened and shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:123:She took her in to the plank. The door of the back room stood open, since that healer never shut a door on anything but a long reading, and from the well they could see a broad back, and a pair of elbows, and at the table's end a bare foot. They heard what she said first, as she said it to every person who lay down there: what she would do, and why, and what it would be like. It had knit as it lay in a river, crooked. She would have to open the way and hold it open, for the better part of an hour, and the girl would feel it going right, which was worse to feel than a thing going wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:149:"That's not my knot. A woman tied that who's kept shoulders shut for forty cold terms, and I can't read through her web without undoing it." She put two fingers under the lowest turn, and took them away. "She says when. I'll look at it beside her, when she's by, and you'll have what the pair of us make of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:"I am going to make a case," she said. "I have made it to one of you already, so that he should not hear it first in company. I shall say what I think, and what it rests on, and what it does not rest on; and I shall ask for nothing tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:245:She moved the finger to the kit's ledger, which lay shut at her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:257:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:259:"It has a gate, with a book in it," said Seln. He had not taken his eyes from the fire. "And a station on its crown. There is a paper of ours that executes when an enrolled name is written in a book inside a district; it must be served within the hour. I told you so on the ninth day, and nothing has changed it. And there is one among us whose file is not shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:263:It was Oryn whom he was watching. At the word *station* her head had come round, and her mouth had opened; he saw the sentence arrive in it, whole, as he had seen her sentences arrive since a mountain. She shut her mouth on it. She looked into her bowl, and turned the bowl a little in her fingers, and said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:295:*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:3:The third session at Ostrand was the full one, and the delegation went down the bluff road for it in the coach at first light with the whole term's argument packed in two cases, and the coach was quiet the way it had been quiet on the road to Norhold: nobody circling the thing, because the thing had stopped needing to be circled.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:5:Cael charted the ride anyway, because riding was cataloguing time. Lira with the Silver drill-book shut on her knee, the program's first road meet three weeks out and her whole attention on a trading floor instead. Brom with his forearms on his knees and the Velmere letter's second envelope in his kit — he had said nothing about it in four weeks and the table had said nothing back, per the arrangement, and the arrangement was beginning to cost the table something. Karis with the closing summary in her lap, not reading it, because she had written it and could not have improved it and knew both things. Seln at the coach's end with the office's cross-index and his eyes shut. The counsel with the privileged minute open, adding to it in her small exact hand as the river went by. And Withrow, in the registry grey, at the coach's head, looking at the road the way she had looked at the bluff road from a waystation the spring before, with the flinty enjoyment of a person whose worrying was done in advance — except that it was not enjoyment now; it was the same face with the enjoyment removed, and what was left was load-bearing.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:21:He catalogued the room again while the clerk opened the minute, because the room was the machine and the machine's parts were countable. Forty gallery benches, filled to the rails and standing at the back — the registry-literate of three districts, the correspondents at the front two benches with the folding writing-boards that marked continental papers, four of them with the flat cases of houses that could afford a courier standing outside. The petitioner's table, north-west of the seal, one man and one clerk. The respondent's table, north-east, four: the counsel, Bracken, Withrow, and the enrollee of record. The records table behind and below the seal's platform, two officers on rotation. The evaluation seat, west of the seal, at an angle to both tables — a chair, a lap-desk, and a low rail of its own — which at the first session had been empty and at the second had been empty and which today was not.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:33:Senior evaluator of record, in the registry's grey, files squared on the seat's lap-desk at angles that had not varied by a degree since the first counter she had ever read his case across — the woman who had read that case at the counter of his life and had been, in every recorded act since, exactly correct. She had come in with the working morning's traffic rather than with the officers, carrying her own files, and had set them down and squared them, and had spent the four minutes before the opening reading the topmost one with the specific attention of a person who has read it three times already and intends to read it a fourth.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:35:Empaneled. The clerk read the empanelment into the minute: *the evaluation seat constituted, the district's senior evaluator presiding, scope per the code's institutional provisions.* Ilsev inclined her head to the seal at the exact depth the registry taught, and opened the first of her files, and the room proceeded, and Cael sat with the reading the sight of her produced, which was not comfort and was not its opposite.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:141:The market street outside had four hours of afternoon left in it and the correspondents got their couriers away inside three minutes, which Cael timed from the top step, because the speed of a ruling's departure was a measurement and he had nothing else to do with the interval while the cases were brought down.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md:151:The counsel watched him go the length of two shopfronts. "Twenty-six years," she said, to nobody in particular, "and that is the first opponent I have ever had who tells me what I got right on the way out. He does it because it costs him nothing. It's still true." She took her case from Bracken. "Brace yourselves for the next one. He'll concede more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:"There is nothing to hunt it. Out there a declaration draws a thing with teeth. In here it draws a clerk. A Path that is opened on a town's ground is opened in somebody's district, under somebody's eye, and he is paid to write down what he saw and whose it was." The mild voice did not alter. "I should take the teeth, for choice. A hound does not send a copy up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:31:They went on between the hedges, and for half a mile nobody used the leave that he had been given. Cael found that he had a great deal to say and no notion of how a person began. He had not been silent all those days; they had talked at every fire, behind every wall, and through a whole cold term in a back room. It was only on a road that the mouth was shut, with the sigils in their pockets and his ten lying closed, and he had left off noticing it as a carter leaves off noticing his own wheels. He noticed it now, as it lifted. It was like setting down the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:Vastin went beside him on the ditch side, a shoulder off. The stick came down a little after each of Cael's strides, and the tarred case knocked softly against a brown coat, and neither of them spoke; of the six they were the two who found the leave hardest to use. Cael had kept one question from that man since a board at Lowmarch. He thought that the man was keeping something too. He walked like somebody carrying a full bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:167:Shadow he knew by what was missing. By day Shadow lay shut under its seal, and it made no sound whatever; he could tell where it was only as a person tells where a rug lies in a dark room, because the floor does not ring there. It would lift at dusk, as it lifted every dusk. He would tell it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:The stick had taken its place again on Cael's left, on the ditch side. It had not lost a stride on him in six miles. He could hear what that was costing, since the breath beside him came short at each planting of the bound leg, and the tarred case had been shifted twice to the other shoulder and twice back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:273:"Tonight," he said. "There'll be a halt. They'll shut those gates at dusk, and we'll not be at them by dusk, at this pace."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-006.md:13:The cold term, before the midyear recess [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: season words struck; audit A1]. Eighteen, eight fragments, the doorways quiet. The case has been down from its shelf for days. Five people will come to a stone room two floors below the library by three routes across an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-006.md:22:- **Two Hundred Years** — Karis's four findings, each landing on a carried thread: the mechanism and **reach, not strength**; the flag as the corrections function failing safe — *"She just never knew what was answering."* (Ilsev and Havel never told, in eleven words); **the strata** — [UNBOUND] said **once**, here only [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: this is the series' second voicing; B4 ch50 voiced the older word once, lower-case, at Halcenvane (Karis, one factual clause), and B5 carries it read-and-closed; this chapter builds on ch50 and does not discover it; audit B5]; *close the door, remove the exit, delete the name*; **the limit, verbatim**; Lira: "They *found* it… It isn't a long time compared to whatever's under it." (a Book 9 plant); the anomaly checked — *Not this either. Still open.*; the stratum note paid (re-evaluation as delivery mechanism); Seln: "A convenience cannot." It can't be filed. "It isn't broken. It's maintained." Lira on the stair: **"It's not evidence. It's a map."**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:They roped the boxes. Brom took his on the right shoulder, as he had for four days, and Lira got her arms through her beckets, the right and then the left. Cael felt the third box settle between his shoulder-blades, with a purse in it under five thumbs. The old man hooked the tarred case with the head of his stick, and brought it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:33:He had never seen it so. They had used the whole of the clerk's quarter-hour that evening, since a small pale woman would not leave a tally-book lying open; and the wagons were gone from the dock, and the pan had been hooked up on the weigh-beam, and the carts' gate at the bottom was shut and barred. The light had begun to go. It lay grey on the long worn trough of the paving, and there was not a soul on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:53:Behind them all, at the foot, the gate shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:One knelt on the highest landing, with a thing laid along his forearm that Cael knew before he could see it plainly. It was short, and it had a bow across its end. A second stood over him with a stave. A third was already coming down the flight below them, in the middle of the steps, as a man walks who knows that the ground is his. And at the very top, where the slot opened beside the parapet, the fourth stood by himself. He had one hand on a bar of iron that was let into the wall there for people coming up under loads. In the other he held a lantern, low, against his coat, with its shutter closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:101:Vastin made a sound, shut behind his teeth. Cael saw the bound leg take it. Whatever struck that plane came down through the man who was its foot, and it went into the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:103:His boots had not moved. They stood on the lip of the step, on the line, and they would stand there until he let the thing go, or until it left him. He could not lift a heel. He could not turn round. He had shut a stair with his own body, facing uphill, and whatever was done at his back was to be done without him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:117:The big man let his box go. He did not set it down; he had no arm that would have set it. He dropped his right shoulder, and the thing slid off the webbing and fell across the fourth step with its long side to the gate, and lay there. It shut one half of the slot. He stood in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:133:He let it go. He did not throw it. He opened his hands, with a heave of the hips behind them, and it went down the last two steps edge foremost, turning over, at the height of a shin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:169:She did not cry out. She looked at her palm, and then she shut it, and put it inside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:189:Wind was first, as it had always been, the dry hiss in his feet. *Away?* it said; and there was nowhere on that stair that was away. Under it the millstone went round, and told him the weight of a slab that he could not see, and which way it was falling. Ember ticked in his right hand, and showed him a cord, ten yards off, drawn back along a stock; a single point would have gone through that cord as through a thread. Pressure had drawn itself as tight as a held breath. A slot between two walls was the very thing that it was made for; it would have gone up that stair like a cork up the neck of a bottle. *There?* asked the thin held note, of the place by the parapet where the slot began; and a point set there would have shut the head of the cut like a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:197:He had been looking at it since he raised his eyes. A man stood at the top of that stair, with one hand on a rail, and a lantern held shut against his coat. He was not fighting. He had no stave. He was doing nothing whatever, and he was doing it with great attention, with his hood turned straight down the slot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:267:He raised the lantern. He brought it up from his coat to the height of the parapet, where the whole town lay below to see it, and his thumb went to the shutter. A wall would serve, it seemed, if the other thing was not to be had. A wall was a Path opened in a district. He did not doubt that somebody down there in the dusk had been told to watch for a light, and had a pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:273:The lantern jerked sideways. It struck the rail, and fell on the top step with its shutter still closed, and rolled, and went out inside itself with a small flat cough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:297:The old man shut his eyes. His hands opened on the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:299:There was no frost to fall. The place in front of him that the eye had slid off was only a stair again, with two bolts lying on it; and the roar went out of the slot as a fire goes out when the door is shut on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:303:It did not bend. It let go, sideways, all at once, as a prop is knocked from under a beam. The whole long weight of him came over backward, with the case and the stick, onto the step where Cael stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:He told it no. He took him as a porter would have. He got both arms round a brown coat, under the shoulders, and the box on his own back dragged him the way that they were both going. His left heel lost the tread, and found the next. They went down two steps so, the pair of them, and a tarred case came after and struck him on the ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:It was a road again as soon as the meadow was behind them, and so the hold came down on all five as it had on every road since the Line. Things lived along the roads of that country which hunted whatever a Path gave off, and nobody declared. The sigils stayed in their pockets, and Cael's ten fragments lay shut, and the read asked at every bend what the frozen ruts weighed and was told that it need not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:"He dug every hole before the ground shut," said the factor. "Forty-one of them. Stoned them over, and stuffed them, so the frost shouldn't get down. I never asked him to. He'd have had the posts in as well, only he went off south after somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:69:He went down to the gate, and opened the read, and gave it the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:71:It rang. He had never had it on earth frozen so deep, and the difference was that between striking a cushion and striking a board. Everything came back to him hard and plain: the ruts under the snow, and a buried stone as big as a bucket, and the gatepost's foot going down three feet into its packing. He held it for the length of a breath and shut it. Up the hill, on the sledge, a right hand stayed where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:73:He climbed beside Teague's posts and did it again at two hundred yards, and again at sixty, a long way beyond her, where he could see the lichen on the nearest animal's plates. At sixty the read told him nothing whatever about the herd, since its whole world was a pace and a half across. It told him about the slope under his boots. He shut it, and looked back along the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:105:Brom had been looking along the slope with one eye shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:173:It was the size of an ox, and broader. It had pitched forward onto the trapped shoulder, with its hind feet scrabbling on the frozen turf above, and it rolled a dark eye at him and heaved again. He stopped a pace and a half from its flank and opened the read on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:221:He shut the read. His own fingers, a foot from his face, were a blur; he could see a furlong well enough. Along the hill a small figure got down off a sledge by herself, and then sat in the snow beside it, which she had plainly not meant to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:235:He stood at the end of Teague's run before they lifted a stone, and looked back along the standing posts with one eye shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:247:Brom shut one eye and looked down the line. He nodded.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:15:Discretion, first: the registry's plenary authority, in an integrity matter, to compel re-evaluation regardless of an enrollment's validity. He read the provision. He read the two cases in which it had been exercised in the last forty years, both unglamorous, both uncontested, both entirely on point. He did not overstate them.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:27:Then Karis rose, and gave the best legal argument anyone in that room would hear in their working lives, and it changed nothing, and every person at the respondent's table knew both of those things before she opened her mouth.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:29:She spoke for fifty-one minutes. She had a folder in front of her and she opened it once. She argued the text one last time, whole, from the keel up — the assay clause's own language, the enabling instrument behind it, the four hundred years of practice that had made it what it was — and she did the thing she had done since the Greyvane hearing, which was to build every proposition on a document already in the seat's hands, so that not one sentence of her closing asked the seat to take her word for anything.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:43:Jent rose to reply and said, in full: "The petitioner has nothing to add. The submission the seat has just heard is the best statement of the respondent's case that could be made, and the registry does not dispute a syllable of it." Then he sat down, and that was the most damaging thing anyone said all day, and he had not meant it as damage. He had meant it as respect. Both things were true and the second did not soften the first.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:101:That evening he submitted a transfer request out of monitoring rotation. It cited career development and a preference for provincial posting and it was four sentences long and entirely true in every particular that could be checked. In his kit, in the flat case he had carried since Ardenmere, the private note was six entries deep and would not receive a seventh. He was done watching, and he did not say so anywhere that could be read, and the request would be granted inside the month, and he would carry the note to a posting with a view of nothing that had happened in this room, unknowing, preserved.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:115:The gallery emptied in nine minutes. The correspondents went out first and at speed, because a ruling is a dispatch and a dispatch has a coach to catch. The guild officers went out unhurried and talking. The ordinary curious went out saying the word *revoked* to each other in the tone of people who had come to see something happen and had seen it. Jent gathered his four documents, put them in his case, bowed to the seat, and crossed the twelve feet between the tables to say — to Withrow, not to the respondent, correctly — "Headmistress." Withrow said, "Procurator." And he went, and the chamber's arithmetic was finished, and the whole thing had taken a term and eleven sittings and had never once raised its voice.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:159:*So. Five weeks. Council tomorrow. Not to analyze the ruling. To make the decision the ruling was built to prevent us from making — the one Lira asked about in the first week, and Brom priced in three sentences, and Seln has been holding a locked case toward for fifteen years, and I have been refusing since Greyvane because I believed the text was ground.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:167:Brom was at the table with the ruling's copy-sets laid out along it — the seat's, the institution's, the delegation's, the four spares the clerk had run off at the close — and he was putting them into an order. Not reading them. Not filing them. Squaring each set to the one beneath it, heaviest to the bottom, the flat case's depth taken once between a thumb and a forefinger and not taken again: a farrier's son doing arithmetic with his hands about what a man can carry and how far.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:35:He told it no. He stood in a registry's yard, twenty feet from a hatch with its shutter up. A girl with eggs had been turned from a gate in that town for the wrong hour on a card; he did not care to learn what was written against a porter who was seen to know a thing that he had no way of knowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:61:Cael had priced that man a great many ways since a yard above Ostrand. He had priced him by what he could carry, and by what he would take on his own skin for somebody else, and by a figure that a board had given him and a house had declined to sign; and he saw now that every one of those sums had had the grey in it. He had never once asked what the man was worth with the Path shut. It appeared to be a dray of slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:Cael turned, and the mule was standing. It had been throwing itself about in the shafts a moment since, and every plunge had rocked the bed. It stood now with its head down and its sides going, and an old man had hold of its cheek-strap. He had the stick in his right hand. The left, which would not open flat, had shut on the leather; shutting was the one thing that it could still do well. He was looking at nobody; he had held a ferry's stern so once, in a flood, with a turn round a post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:221:Karis had risen from the chair, and did not seem to know that she had. She stood with a black pebble shut in her glove, and she had gone so white that Brom took a step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:305:*Brom: the left shoulder wrenched, under a dray in the registry's yard, with no Path opened. He is carrying on the right, in the old healer's knot, against Oryn's word at the fork. I did not tell him to put it down. I couldn't have made him. Owed at the mill, to two healers, by me as much as by him. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:9:**Read before drafting:** this movement has no source chapter of its own. Read the threads it carries: source `chapter-09.md` (the *fled* default; both roads; the first box; the carters), `chapter-14.md` (Lira's three bouts; the qualification, defined at BOOK_MAP §4a as the registry's Silver advancement evaluation [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: audit B9] [B5-reconciled 2026-10-06: on the page it is "the advancement evaluation" or "the sitting" (B5 ch20:231, ch21:63, ch60:145), never "qualification"; audit §F.3 B9]), `chapter-16.md` (the map), `chapter-18.md` (the year's documentary work; the counsel; Havel), `chapter-19.md` (the Greyvane request; the assistant; the docket; the second Velmere envelope unopened four weeks), `chapter-20.md` (the band Ephram charted), `chapter-22.md` (Seln east twice; water at forty paces; Brom checks). Context: `BOOK_MAP.md` §§2, 4a (M7 rows), 4d, 4e (#9–11), 6 (Lira, Havel, Vastin), 9; `STATE_LEDGER.md` (after M6).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:26:- **Brom**: the review's evidentiary schedule arrives; he prepares at the frames beside Ephram; **another Velmere envelope (the second to arrive this year) arrives about four weeks before the ruling and stays on the table, unopened** [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: no series ordinal; audit B11]. No guidance note yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:35:- Brom's guidance note does not appear before M8. The second Velmere envelope is never opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:23:He had the sheet itself this time, and daylight, and nobody at his shoulder. He read the technician's date a figure at a time, with his thumb moving under it, as he had not read anything since he was a junior: the year first, and then the day. He turned the sheet over, and looked at the brush's work on its back, and turned it again, and read the date a second time from its last figure to its first. Then he shut his eyes. He set the other date beside it, the one that he had copied twice, and went along the two of them together, as a clerk goes down a copy against its original.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:37:A fact could be filed. One kept it, and said nothing, and let it lie against the day when it should be useful; and he had kept a great many so. It was what a desk did. He had cut a hundred and forty-seven notches in two hazel wands to count what a desk would do with a clerk's packet, and had been wrong about the manner of it; but he had not been wrong about desks. There was an office somewhere that had held six years of sheets where nobody would receive them. He had been reading its work all morning. He did not care to find, at his age, that he had become a small copy of it, with a locked case for a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:He did not look down at the strap across his chest. He had carried that case for twelve years, shut, and it was shut now. He had found before today that he could not hold two closed things up to the light in one morning. He made no ruling upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:185:"A man who reaches for a pen that is not there has told the room what he was. So has one who classes a paper before he has read it, or says *it should be directed*." Seln's mild voice did not alter. "I knew you in a doorway for what you were before you had opened your mouth, and I was not the only person on this side of the Line who could have. There is a town at the end of that road. There is a gate in it, and a man in the gate who has been reading people for his living since before I entered the service. You will not need to tell him your name. You will only need to stand at his counter and begin to arrange it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:231:It was the first time that the town had been named aloud since a page was opened on a table, and the man who had carried the page was the one to do it. It lay on the moor now among the seven of them, like a thing set down. Cael turned the other name over. It was a short one, and he had never heard it. A man sat under that town with his hands on a language, and had a name, and had been waiting there, it seemed, for somebody to bring him three floors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:233:At the basin a square woman had stopped wringing linen. She stood with a wet cloth in her two fists and her head a little turned. She had heard a station spoken of twice at a board, and had shut her mouth on something each time; she had heard an archive spoken of today. He saw her put the cloth down on the stones, very deliberately, as she put down a thing that she meant to come back to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:45:"Then I shall read for what repeats," said Karis. "It is the only reading that I am good for, with a text whose words are shut. Seln, you will read for what is not there; you have told me a dozen times that it is the half of any paper. And you—" she had turned to the man who stood at the slab's end on his stick, with his bound leg a little out— "will tell me what the form is. I do not know this office. I do not know what a clerk in it is obliged to write, and so I cannot tell what he has chosen to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:85:She did not read them at once. She reached behind her, to the tarred case, and brought out the dead man's ledger, and laid it open beside the sheet; and she looked from the one to the other for as long as it took Brom to draw his foot out of the water. Then she shut the ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:155:"It should be directed that each row be taken up singly, and re-taped before the next is opened," he said, in a different voice, level and a little quick. "Under two hands. A schedule to be made of every sheet as it is turned, with its row and its place in the row, and the schedule to be signed by—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:185:Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of an iron stake that lay in a tarred case, three feet from her elbow, with its point still bright. It was on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:She took the dead man's ledger again, and opened it at its first page, and set it down above the four sheets, so that a small pressed stroke and bar in pencil lay over four in a clerk's ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:Vastin had drawn breath, and Cael saw him shut his mouth on it. It was the first time that he had done so of himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:213:"One thing more, before these are shut," said Karis. "I have a figure for every row, and I made each of them twice; but I made them both, and a count that one person has made is only a hope. I want a second hand on each. Take a row. Count its sheets, by the corners, and say nothing until you have the figure. Then I shall tell you mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:29:He spoke low. He was a stride behind, with the stick coming down on the cobbles and the case on his shoulder, and he had not looked down an alley either, though nobody had required it of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:59:"It's a very good view. I'd have given everything I had for it. I very nearly did." Her left hand had come out of its pocket, and she looked at that for a moment, the long thin fingers that had been in wool for the better part of half a year, and shut them. "I've carried fifty-two sheets up the inside of it, and seen who fetches its eggs. You can't want a place the same once you've been its porter. I don't think you can, anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:81:It opened in the wall of the last building, a slot a stride and a half wide, and it went down. Cael saw the first flight: five steps of dark worn stone, and a landing, and the head of the next. The walls went up on either hand, three times the height of a man, without a door or a window or so much as a drain. The sky was a strip at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:101:The grey eyes rested on him. Then Vastin shifted the case higher on his shoulder, and set the stick's foot on the first tread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:The load did not come. A raw left hand, which had not opened flat since a ferry's strap, struck the wall and held there; and the old man hung on it, with his shoulder against the stone and his breath gone, a foot above the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:129:It was a long paved place, sloping, shut in on three sides by sheds and on the fourth by the back of a very large building. A wagon of split logs was being unloaded at a dock. There was a beam on a post by the lower gate, with a pan hung from it and a man beside the pan holding a slate. Nobody looked round at four backs and a small pale woman coming out of a hole in the wall. Loads came into that yard all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:Brom lowered his box to the paving and himself beside it. Lira sat on hers. Vastin stood for a moment, measuring the bench with his eye, and the post at its end; and then he set his back to the post and let himself down, with the leg laid out in front of him, and shut his eyes. It was the third time. He had not asked anybody whether he might.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:169:He left it. He shut the book on the pencil, and sat with it between his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:175:The hatch's shutter moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:181:He was at the hatch. The shutter had gone up on a square of dimness, and a man sat within it, sideways to the yard, with a narrow book bound in green cloth lying open on the ledge under his hand; and he was writing in it before Cael had got to his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:183:He was a man of no particular age, in a grey coat with ink on its right cuff and none upon the left. Cael did not learn his name that day, or on any day after it. He had the card propped against his inkwell, and he did not look at the bench at all. He looked past it, at what stood on the paving. There were three boxes, which he counted with his eyes, and wrote. There were a tarred case, and a courier's flat, and a calfskin, and he wrote those. He did not ask what was in any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:The shutter came down. A key turned, and the low door beside the hatch opened inward, and the clerk stood on its sill with the card in his fingers and the green book shut under his arm. He blinked once at the daylight, as people do who are kept indoors. Then, having finished with the loads, he looked at what had carried them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:The clerk's mouth had opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:209:The clerk shut his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:217:Cael got his arms through the beckets. He went past a clerk of the oldest registry on the Line, into its foundations, as the quarter part of a figure at the edge of a leaf; and the man did not look at him as he went by. He was looking at the shut green book, with something very like satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:257:"Three," he said, in the voice like a hinge. "And the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:271:It shut with no more noise than a book makes. The clerk's lantern went out of the world, and the feet overhead, and the last grey thread of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:273:The tiers closed, and the dark opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:21:She had not had her gloves on. They were in her pocket, where they had been since the forenoon, since a person does not turn the leaves of a tally-book in kid. The stone's edge had gone across the heel of her palm, from the root of the thumb to the wrist's other side, as straight as a line ruled with her own brass; and she had it shut upon a fold of her skirt, and there was a good deal of red in the fold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:53:It was full dusk in the lane. The four alleys had no white in them. Each had, at its far end, the paleness that did not flicker, and it lay out along the cobbles in a bar. Cael went through all four with an old man's arm across his neck and a box dragging at his back, and he did not turn his head. It was not hard, tonight. He had too much on his shoulders to look at anything but the next sett; and a girl went by on his left each time, under two loads, with a tarred case knocking at her hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:65:"It's two streets from the blue gate. We've gone by it a dozen times. *Second bell: blue.* She's Iron, the third rank, and she's got it on paper." She had Karis's wrist in her right hand, and was holding the whole arm up above the pallet, as a healer had once told her to hold her own. "They'd have that shut in a quarter of an hour, with a Path. It'd not even leave a mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:113:There were seven stitches. She had come to the seventh letter by then, and she went on through the other seven with her eyes shut while he tied off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:123:Karis opened her eyes. She turned the bound hand over, and looked at the back of it, and then at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:131:Brom had brought the pot up, late, with seven on the board, and had set the seventh at the end by itself. The old man had been got up against the gable's foot, with a rolled coat behind him; and when he put out his right hand for the thing, the fingers would not shut upon its rim. The left, which had held a ferry's strap and a mule's head, lay open on the straw and did not stir.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:191:"The first is the knee. I was forbidden a wall on it, by the one person on this side of the Line who had the right to forbid me, and I stood one. I did not forget her; I had her in my mind as the stick came down, word for word." He shut his eyes. "I do not ask that it be excused. I ask only that she be told that I heard her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:201:"I do not know what they hoped to see, and I do not ask. I told you on a bank that I had measured a person and could not read him, and it is as true tonight. I sat four feet from you for three days, with a plate, and I have walked a shoulder off you since a ferry. I know that six people shut a stair to watch you do something, and that you went up a step into a crossbow's eye and did nothing whatever." He drew what breath he had. "I know no more of what you are than the brass did. That is the third head, and it is the one that I wished to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:203:He opened his eyes, and turned them, for the first time, to Cael's face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:253:"It is a watchman's, shortened, and the ferrule has been filed where a maker's mark would stand. The bolts are the same: a plain shaft, and no fletcher's thread." He put his hands into his sleeves. "Six people came into a stair in the oldest town on the Line, with their faces tied up, and not one of them opened a Path. They did not lack for them. I know how the one on the landing stood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:257:"Because a Path that is opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it; and they took the same care that we did to have nothing of theirs entered. They wished to have something of ours." His eyes went to Cael, and away. "The bow was to make one of you open, and the light was to tell somebody below that it had been done. When the light could not be shown, they had no reason to stay, and they went. That is their limit: they cannot be written down in this town any more than we can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:285:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:301:He turned the book round, and held it out across the gap between two pallets. Lira read it with her chin down, and she read the last two lines twice. She did not say anything at all. She shut the book over her thumb, and gave it back, and lay looking at the slates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:71:"I told you so last night. You said that you'd ask for hands, and I knew what you'd ask them for before you'd shut your mouth. You were never going to say the word. You were going to find a slip." She took her left hand out of her pocket. "I'm not complaining. It's how I'd have done it. I only want it known that I saw it first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:91:"I've a bench here that's done by noon, and it's had six mornings of me. The mill's my second stop. It's waited a whole cold term, and it can wait four days more while I go round by the top and come down on it from above; I've five gates up that way that I'd have had to climb to in any case." She threw the cloth over her shoulder. "I said once that somebody ought to stand at an edge with her sleeves up. I'm not having a girl with a fortnight's arm go into a cut behind something that takes carts, and hearing about it afterward from a carter. Write me. I'm a line on that slip. I'm not a name under theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:The broker turned back a corner of the sheet himself, and laid his hand on the nearest coil as a man lays his hand on a horse that he means to sell. Brom did not look at the tag; he took the rope's end between his finger and thumb, and bent it back on itself, and opened the lay with his nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:It was a road again as soon as the landing was out of hearing, and the hold came down on all of them as it had on every road in that country. The sigils stayed in their pockets. Cael's ten lay shut; the read asked at the first bend, civilly, what the wet ruts weighed, and was told that it need not know. Seln went ahead by thirty yards with his hands a little away from his sides. Lira came next, with the bow on her back. Brom had the mule's cheek-strap, with Karis at the off side of the load and Oryn at the near, and Cael walked behind the mule, as he always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:189:He showed them as they walked. A flat hand, with the palm to the ground, was *stand*: wherever a person was, and as he was. A shut fist was *seen*, and one looked where the fist went and did not ask. Two fingers lifted were *come on*; that was Seln's, mostly. And a hand turned over empty, as a girl had once turned hers over to him on a hillside, was *nothing*: the thing had gone, or it had never been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:61:There was a book. It lay on a sloped desk against the tower's wall, a great thick thing with a strap round it, and a second man in grey sat beside it on a bench with a pen behind his ear. He had his eyes shut. Cael watched a cart of slates come through behind them, and the man woke, and undid the strap, and wrote, while the carter stood with his cap in his hand and told him a name and a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:The old man came over the sill on his stick, with the tarred case on his shoulder. The keeper's eyes were on the stick's foot. The thumb moved one bead, and stayed there, and the grey head did not lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:157:Cael marked nothing in that at first; a busy smith might well keep two. It was Brom who slowed, and then stood, and then set his box down on its end and folded his arms on the top of it, as a man does who means to be some time. He had spent a good many nights of his boyhood on a heap of sacks in a shed where his father kept a forge for the pleasure of hitting something, and he looked at a fire as Karis looked at a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:229:"No. I was seventeen days late to your gate before the snow, and I'd a day there and no more; and then the cold shut the top road, and it's only now let me up it from this end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:She was a small brown mule, and she was not his. She belonged to that healer, who was two days off up a shut road, and who had lent her to five people at a hold's gate a long while ago without saying for how long. He led her by the cheek-strap across the landing's planks to the round shed where the ferry's gelding had walked his circle through all the dry months. The gelding was in the smith's stable now, under a blanket. The shed stood empty, with reed hurdles tied between its posts against the wind, and a great wooden drum in the middle of it that had nothing to wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:11:There was a stone trough inside the door, and the water in it had shut in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:17:The prices were what he knew best, since he had carried all ten down the roads of this country for a hundred and thirty days and used them as seldom as he could. He could have said to a grain what each fragment charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:23:The river had begun to shut. It ran dark and quick in the middle, as it always had, but the shelf along each bank had grown another yard since the ferry crossed. Beyond the shelf, on the slack water, a skin had formed in the night, grey and thin, with the snow lying on it in patches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:25:He opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:Cael had Hesk's book inside his coat, and he did not need to take it out, since every figure in it had stopped at this river. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:63:The kit's case lay open on the deal. It had come up out of a hole in the ground sixteen days ago, on Brom's good shoulder, and nobody had yet done more than look into it. There was the cord, coiled, with a knot at every fifth stride. There was the mallet. And there was the stake, which Karis had lifted out and was holding in both hands, as a person holds something that belongs to somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:71:"It has never been in ground." Karis laid it back in its place, and shut her fingers on nothing. "A man carried this down forty steps to drive it, and did not. It is the one thing in that case which is exactly as he left it, and I shall not be the person who alters that. I shall cut a peg out of hazel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:79:"I want to say what I'm doing with the cold term, since it'll want some of you. I wrote the inventory two nights ago. All ten fragments are in it, and nearly every line says what the thing costs me to keep shut." He had Hesk's book on his knee, and he did not open it. "That's half an inventory. I've carried some of them a long while and I couldn't tell you how far they go, or how they fail. I've a river that nothing can cross and five months. I mean to try them, one at a time, and write down what each does and what it bills."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:83:"The ferryman's shed, after dark. It has hurdles round it, and it's a hundred yards from the nearest window." He held up a finger for each. "Three rules. Nothing where anybody could watch who might carry what they saw over that river at the thaw. Shadow stays shut; I'll not spend what protects the person it came from. And nothing with a hand on anybody unless I've asked first and been told yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:189:It was full dark outside the hurdles by then, and the quietest of the ten fragments had come up with the dark, as it did at every dusk. Shadow lifted behind the rest, soft, as far as his knees. It was the only fragment that charged him for being shut, a little, from the minute he woke, like a lamp left burning in a room that nobody used. It could have made less of him. He could have stood in that lantern's light and not been there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:191:He held it where it was. It was a defector's craft, and in any hands but its owner's it would tell a trained eye exactly who had stood near enough to teach it; and the man who had stood near enough was ten feet off, with his back turned, keeping a door. Cael had opened it twice out here, where nobody could see who might carry the seeing west. He did not mean to open it a third time for the sake of a page in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:199:Pike brought his book out to the plank at noon on the hundred and thirty-third day, for the first time since the ferry shut, and there were three slips on twelve feet of oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:211:"That's a cold term." Pike set the horn pot down into its hole. "A roof, a well-house and a fence, and the fence is yours. The holds shut their gates and mend what they've got. I've had years with nothing on this oak from first snow to first boat but the nails."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:213:He opened the ledger at their heading and laid his stone on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"Standing." Pike considered him in his double fashion, and seemed to find the question a proper one. "That's the hearth's end you're lying at, which is the keeper's floor and my money, and I'll not explain that twice in one day. It's the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; he'll charge you for a second and he'll be right. And it's your names said to anybody who comes asking after a crew, which nobody will, the river being shut. That's three things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:253:He shut the ledger on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:291:*Shadow: came up at dusk as far as my knees. Held. Not opened, and it won't be.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:23:The fist opened and lay flat, with the palm to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:25:Six people and a mule stopped where they were, and stood as they were. Cael had done it a hundred times, and it had never once been easy, since all ten of the things that he carried woke at the first grey and spoke together. Wind leaned his feet toward the next rise with a whole burst ready. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, a breath at a time. The read asked to be let out for one pace and a half, to tell him what he knew already; and it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. He put them down, one after another, as a man goes round a house at night shutting doors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:63:They took the track. It was steep, and the mule went up it as if she had been born on a stair, and Vastin went up it a step at a time with his mouth shut and the stick doing the work of the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:117:The hollow was round, like a bowl, and it lay in the spur's shadow, where the day never came. There was ice on it. The ice was grey and dull, with water standing on it at the edges, and Cael knew that grey: the shelf at Lowmarch had gone that colour a week before the river took it. At one side, under a lip of rock, there was a black place where it had not shut, and he could hear water going down there, a long way, into the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:235:A seed of yellow stood on the tar. It grew for one breath to the size of a barley grain, and that was all he gave it. He shut Ember as he would have shut a hand. The heat that ought to have gone somewhere came home along his left arm instead, though he had been holding nothing, and the old weal across that palm woke and stung as though he had laid it on a kettle.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:3:The residence's small room was not a room anyone had chosen for councils. It had been the winter store when the residence was a farmhouse, and it kept the shape: one door, one shuttered window, a stone floor with a drain in the corner that had drained something else three hundred years ago, and a table too big for it that could not be got out again because the door had been narrowed at some point by somebody who did not think about tables. The delegation used it for the same reason Seln had approved of it the first time he was brought there, which was that its one window faced the wall of the stores range four feet away and its walls were three feet of stone, and a voice in it went nowhere at all.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:97:"You said the people are weather and the text is ground." She said it carefully. "I've been standing on ground I was sure of. It was a form with my name on it. Correctly filled, correctly stamped, correctly filed by a woman who wished me well. The ground was correct and it opened anyway." She looked at him. "That's not an argument against text. I know what the text has done for me; I've got a supersession because of it. It's an argument about what text is. It's not ground. It's a floor. And floors are held up by something, and I stopped being able to see what, about twelve days ago." She sat back. "I'm not saying leave. I'm saying: check the ground. That's all. Brom's three sentences. Both roads."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:115:She wrote both. Then she gathered the face-down papers without turning them over, and the five of them went up through a residence that had been asleep for an hour, and the small room's lamp went out, and the one line of light at the shutter's bad joint went out with it, and the stores range and the court were dark from the assessment office's window by the time the man who had sat in the bench's middle got there.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:138:The office at that hour was the shape his working life had always had. A counter with nobody at it. A window with a court beyond it and, across the court, the residence's dark bulk — and in it the small room's shuttered window, which for three hours that evening had let out one thin line of lamp at the shutter's bad joint, and which was dark now, and which he had noticed in his first week and had never mentioned to anyone, because mentioning it would have meant explaining why he had looked.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:144:And a locked case on a shelf in his quarters that had outlasted every one of the nineteen. It had traveled to Norhold and back unopened. It had ridden in a cart under the same canvas as three boxes of a researcher's compiled truth without either archive knowing the other's contents, and he had sat on the cart's bench for two days beside those boxes and had thought about the symmetry for most of the second day.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:160:He did not open the case. He did not go and look at it. He noted, in the audit-voice, exactly what the case staying closed cost, because the cost had been abstract for fifteen years and had stopped being abstract at a table three hours ago.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:166:He sat with the last line for a long time, in the dark office, with the cross-index under his hands and the case on its shelf across the campus and a dark window four buildings away where a line of lamp had shown for three hours, and the line was the closest thing to a decision the audit-voice had ever recorded, and it was not one yet, and he knew the difference exactly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:34:- Seln's method shown; his case not mentioned aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:42:The ferry crossing, one log line, and Seln with his eyes shut: "Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career." Next: Lira asks for the small room, by number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:43:Karis took up the lamp by its foot. She carried it down the length of the deal, past the tarred case and the three heaps that she had pushed aside, and the light went with her: it slid off the flat and its six rows and left them grey, and it came down the bare boards to the place where Cael's two hands lay on the table's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:49:It came out in a fold of oilcloth no bigger than a man's palm, and he opened the cloth on the deal as a person opens a thing that has ridden against his heart for a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:213:Brom opened the door for him. Oryn stood back against the hinges to let him by. She did not put out an arm, and her eyes went down to the knee, and stayed on it until it was through the door. Six people listened to a stick go across the tap-room boards and reach the foot of the keeper's stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:265:It was the third time. She shut it, as she had shut it at a board on the two hundred and sixty-eighth, and at the same board three days after; and the sentence, whatever it was, went back down with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:267:"I've a bench at first light," she said, and it was plainly not what she had been going to say. "And a knee that nobody's asked me about." She went out, and did not shut the door behind her, since she never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:291:"You have not asked me whether this alters my case," said Karis. She had not lifted her head from the notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:297:Cael took Hesk's book out of his coat with his left hand, and opened it beside the page, and wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:Karis laid the card on the ledge. The shutter was up already, and the clerk sat sideways within, with the green book open under his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:It was a small thing to see. The pen had gone down to the leaf, and it did not write. It hung a finger's breadth above a clean line, and the clerk looked at the three boxes on the paving, and at the tarred case, and then he laid his left hand on the book and turned it back by one leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:"Three boxes, corded. One surveyor's case, tarred. One courier's flat. One hide, rolled. Brought down for study on the two hundred and eighty-fourth." He read it from his own line, with his finger under it. Then he looked up, at the small pale woman on the other side of his ledge; and it was the first thing that he had ever asked any of them. "The same?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:"If I enter them today, my book will say that there are six boxes below, and two cases. There are three, and one." The pen had begun to tremble a very little. "It would not be so. I have kept this book for a great many years, and there is not a line in it that is not so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:"I shall have to send up. The under-registrar keeps the schedules, and he will know how it is to be put. He will want to see the matter, and the card, and whoever has brought them." He was reaching already for a slip of paper. "He is very thorough. I should not think that it would take him above an hour."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:They had come two hundred and eighty-five days to that hatch, and got through it once on a figure. It was going to be shut on them by an honest man, who would not write six for three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:91:The shutter came down, and a key turned in the low door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:95:Cael went over it, standing there with a box on his back, since he could not help going over a thing. Every word that the man had said would have stood under two hands. He had told a clerk what was true of a count and of a form. He had not said that an old porter was wandering in his wits, or that it was a carrier's joke, or that the fellow had once swept an Archmarshal's stair and liked to play at it; and any of the three would have shut that clerk's ears a great deal faster than what he did say. He had left the thing that the old man had put upon the ledge lying where it was put, and it was true, and a clerk of the registry now had it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The door had opened. The clerk stood on its sill, with the book shut under his arm, and he looked at Vastin once, as a man looks at an inkwell that has gone over and has by some mercy missed the page. Then he stood aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:115:Cael went below the stick, as he had the day before. The man in the dusty coat had counted three boxes and a case at the iron, with his eyes on the floor, and gone ahead of them into the dark; he had seen nothing in an old porter's face, since he did not look at faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:147:This man's name had been a key: it had opened every door on the continent for forty years, and at each of them somebody had been standing ready with a book. It opened nothing now; it was a knife that had lost its haft, and there was no way left of taking it up that did not cut whoever stood nearest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:199:He went down on his left knee, a hand to the left of the place where two red eyes were resting, and laid his right forearm along his thigh, and let the lamp hang from two fingers as a mason lets a plumb-bob hang. Then he did the one thing that he knew how to do in such a case: he let his breath go long. He counted it, as a healer had taught him on a plank, to ten and to ten again, and he gave over wanting to be anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:217:That was all that he gave her for it. He had asked how a thing was made, and been told, and what he had made of the telling went nowhere that Cael could see. Karis wrote nothing; she sat forward on a bare chair with her gloved hands shut, and watched him as she would have watched a pot that she was not allowed to stir.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:"I'm going up for an hour. Set it on the table's end. Touch nothing." He went by them toward the stair with his eyes already half shut. "There's a jar of water on the shelf. You may have what's in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:273:"There were things you could have said that'd have shut him up for good. That he was simple. That he'd been somebody's servant once and it took him that way. He'd have believed you; he wanted to. And he'd not have looked at you twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:309:"It's useful," said Cael; and he shut the book on the pencil, and found that he meant both.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:67:*There?* asked Anchor, of the post. It was a fair question. A point laid on that oak would have held it as a nail holds a board, for as long as he could hold the point. He had held one for sixty counts once, and a second time to thirty with his other hand shut on a drum to keep himself upright. While he held it he would have nothing else. And he would be kneeling at a post with his palm on it, working the registry's own craft, before forty people who meant to cross that river to the west as soon as it would carry them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:117:Its owner did not hurry. He had one leg that would not bend, and he came down that deck sideways, as a man goes down a stair with a bad knee. He stooped to the carter, and took the strap's tail out of the one fist that still held it. Cael watched him pass it round the rail-post in a single turn. He took the end behind his own back, and brought it forward under his other arm, and shut both hands on it against his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:143:The right hand opened. The left did not. Cael was too far up the landing to see fingers, with his eyes as they were by then; what he saw was the man sit a moment longer, and bring his right hand over, and work at the other, as a person unpacks a parcel that somebody else has tied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:225:Cael opened his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:227:There were a good many true answers, and he had every one of them ready. The man had kept a record honest for a year when it would have been easier not to. He had sat in a room with forty clocks and given a warning that cost him his place at a table. He had read a case through, and said what it was to a window; and when a seat sent for the one sentence that it still needed from him, he had declined, and put his name to the declining.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:229:None of that had been done on this side of a river. It was not Cael's to say, either. He had been answered for by other people since his Kindling, in rooms, by officers who meant well; he shut his mouth, and looked at the brown coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:15:The academic year's last month [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: season word struck; audit A1]. Two closing sittings at Ostrand. Brom's review on the year's last Fifth-day. The second Velmere envelope unopened. Three boxes roped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:47:Nobody could have told by listening, for there was nothing to hear. It was Lira who found it. She had lifted the lamp, and lowered it again at once, as though the man could have seen her from wherever he was; and then she put it behind her back altogether, so that her own body shut the light off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:69:The floor was the bluff's own rock, cut level, and it was clean. No dust lay on it as dust lies in a loft; there was only a fine pale bloom along the foot of each bay, where nobody walked, and it was the colour of a certain coat. The air smelled of dry paper, and of old glue, and of cold stone, and under those of nothing whatever. It was the smell of a book that has stood shut on a shelf since before its reader was born.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:He did not think that anybody had kept that man standing at the bottom of a room in forty years. Rooms had risen when he came into them. Books had been held out to him, open, at the gate of this very town. He stood now with a case on his shoulder, behind a girl with a lamp, while a Bronze of the least-regarded class on the roll read to the foot of a leaf; and Cael saw the right hand leave the stick, and hang, and go back. The old mouth had set. Then, slowly, as a thing is done that has been practised, the grey eyes came away from the reader's back and went to the shelves, and stayed there, on a bay of boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:127:She had the second box unroped on the floor, and the oilcloth open, and she carried the sheets to their tables one at a time, flat on her two forearms, as a person carries a dish that is full to the brim. She set each down by a measure that she had in her notebook. Twice she took one up again and moved it the width of a finger. The man in the dusty coat stood at the end of the first table through all of it, with his eyes shut and his hands behind him, and waited as she had waited for his page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:129:Then he opened them, and began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:167:Karis heard that. She did not turn her head; but her gloved hand shut on the edge of the flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:173:When he straightened, he had to feel for the table's edge. He stood holding it, with his eyes shut and the water drying on his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:179:Cael had heard her say that word once before, at a trestle under a marsh, and shut her mouth upon it afterward as upon a box. She had said then that she would not say it twice. She had kept it for this table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:199:"Today I'm no use to you. I'll be an hour getting these back." He had sat down in the chair that stood at his desk, heavily, with his hands flat on his knees and his eyes shut. "Put the lamp on the table's end, where I can find it. Then do what you like, so long as it's quiet, and it's nowhere near my paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:Brom had the figures before she had shut her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:261:Nobody had forbidden it. There was a term against a carried light, and he carried none; and a man with shut eyes had said that they might do as they liked, so long as it was quiet. He walked on the rock between the black uprights, counting, and the two flames drew together behind him into one. At forty strides the bundles on either hand had lost their edges. At sixty he could tell the shelves only as a person tells the walls of his own room at night, and he stopped there, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:283:"That's him," said the voice like a hinge, from the far end, without stirring. "He pulls a wire. You've a quarter of an hour before he shuts his yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:295:"Three people came to look at that low door in the yard, when it had shut on you," he said. "Two were yard-men, and they looked as people look at a thing which they have never seen used. The third was the clerk. He came out upon his own sill, with his book under his arm, and stood there for as long as it takes to read a page. Then he went in again to his hatch and sat down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Two days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:41:"A thing that's torn, I can shut. A thing that's tired wants what I can't give it faster than it'll take it for itself. I'll bind that, and it'll have to do its own work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:47:Her thick fingers went round the leg, above the place and below it, and her eyes shut. Cael had lain under those hands. He knew what was passing along that shin now, slow and exact. Tide, the tenth of his own, lay in him as still as a pool, and did not so much as ask; it knew its owner's work when it was near. He had been handed the reading without the mending, a ruler and no saw. Watching her now, he could have said to a breath where along that shin her attention stood, and he could not have done one thing about what it found there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:181:Oryn had been standing by the hearth's end with her bowl. She watched the door until it had shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:229:He wrote it, with an L., and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:247:"You said that a road was walking," said Vastin; and the healer shut her mouth, and Cael saw the corner of it go down, which was most of the way to a smile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:269:Vastin opened his mouth.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:5:Cael counted the room in on arrival, because he counted every room in and had never once found the habit wasted. Four file cases along the north wall with the term's whole defense inside them, eleven pounds of true record, closed. The shuttered window on the quadrangle, and past the shutter a campus with nine days of term left going about its evening in complete ignorance. Bracken's spare inkstand on the sideboard, where Bracken kept it and where nobody else was permitted to move it. And in front of his own chair, the ruling's copy: four paragraphs, a seal, and the attestation line at the foot that every binding instrument on the continent closed with, which he had read ten thousand times in Karis's citations without ever once hearing it as a sentence about a hand.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:31:So Karis argued for it, against herself, for four minutes, at full strength, the way she had argued the Greyvane hearing's weakest branch to its end before discarding it. The cache went to the proceedings; the proceedings were compelled to receive it; the record entered two hundred years of reclassification orders against their own Kindling files. And then the room asked the only question that mattered, which was who verifies it — and the answer was an evaluation seat, and an evaluation seat runs on an Arbiter, and the Arbiter runs the protocol the documents accuse. "You would be asking the machine to certify a finding against a modification inside its own certifying function," she said. "There is no seat on this continent that can receive that finding and no instrument that can test it. It is not that we would lose the argument. It is that the argument has no venue. The only thing entering the cache accomplishes is telling the Compact exactly what an assessment clerk of fifteen years kept in a locked case, at which point there is one man alive who can be found and corrected, and they will find him, because finding him is the one part of the whole apparatus that works perfectly."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:45:"I'd have brought it in the morning," Bracken said, to the room and to nobody. "It seemed to me that a man should get the best paper of his life on the day it was written and not the day after." He put it on the table at Brom's place and went out and shut the door, and the shutting was as much as Bracken had ever said about anything.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:77:The advancement was never answered. No signature, no refusal filed, no covering letter to the district seat; the form stayed on the table face-up as its own reply and was still there in the morning when the hall was opened, and somebody on the staff, not knowing what it was, squared it with the other papers and left it. The second Velmere envelope, unopened four weeks on the small table in his room, was answered the same way and in the same act, and it stayed unopened, because he did not open it, and the three boxes were the letter back.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:85:Lira: the Silver program, named for what it was — the first ladder ever built toward her, the drills the built version got, the bout sheet she had slept beside, the trajectory a Silver Rank 8 had told her was *there* — and declined in the sentence that completed the arc Zerin had opened at a Norhold board. "I said I wanted to become something they haven't seen yet." She said it evenly, a woman reading a bout's honest arithmetic. "Turns out it doesn't live here. It never was going to live anywhere with a seal on the door. The scrapyard was closer." She folded the thought the way she had folded the bout sheet at the confluence — carefully, kept. "I've said goodbye to a page before. I know how it's done."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:123:The office was as it had been for two years — the file cases, the framed charter above them, the desk with nothing on it that was not in use — except that the term's correspondence had colonized the long side table in stacks that had a system Cael could read at a glance and would not have been able to read two years ago: petitions, responses, guild inquiries, and one stack, the smallest, of letters from other chancellors, which he understood without being told were the ones that had cost her the most to answer.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:135:"I spent my tenure building an institution worth measuring. You were the best case it ever held — not the most decorated; Brom's your decorated one, and Lira's your reclaimed one, and they're both worth a chancellor's whole career. You were the *hardest*, and an institution is only ever as good as the hardest thing it can hold correctly, and we held you correctly, on the text, for two years, until an office two hundred years older than my charter decided the text wasn't the point." She looked at the framed charter above the file cases. "*Left* is the machine's verb and you are taking it out of the machine's mouth, and I approve, as a registry officer, of the maneuver."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:145:He believed her, and entered the believing rather than the sentence, because the sentence was the kind a person says at a door and the believing was the part that would still be true in ten years. She did not offer her hand and he did not expect it. She walked him to the office door and opened it herself, which she did for visiting officers of grade and had never once done for an enrollee, and Cael, going down the stair, understood that he had just been promoted out of the institution by its chancellor four days before he resigned from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:35:- [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: entry-state corrections from the edition's Book 5 that the author must carry (audit A5–A12, B5, C5, C8). (1) Seln is the assessment wing's teaching assistant at the copying table behind the counter; the counter's desk clerk is another man (A5). (2) Vastin: "the Archmarshal", "forty years", no stated age (A6). (3) Bracken: "thirteen years in the office", never "thirty" (A7). (4) Counts one year on from B5's non-incremented figures: "since his Kindling" for the system; "since Greyvane" for the watchers and Karis's materials; the landing beat four years, Compression three, the anomaly three, Shadow "two years, near enough" (A9). (5) The circle knows the Level 4 designation exists and cannot be traced; the returns' wording is unknown to them until M8; Cael has never heard of Havel's note (A10). (6) The [UNBOUND] note: read, voiced once at Halcenvane (B4 ch50), closed as "Why retire a working method?"; Karis has set the strata aside for "the cold term" (B5). (7) Brom's recess line is "The recess's problem." (A1). (8) The covenant is B4's signed minute (three rules) plus B5's one-line restatement; "directed, open-eyed" is B5's wording (A12) (the costed amendment is B5 ch59:103, four initials at ch59:107; recall its words, not the source slogan) [B5-reconciled 2026-10-06: audit §F D4]. (9) Seln's locked case: say in one clause where it sits now (C5). (10) The stamp and its renewals: two renewals on paper (the twentieth of Reaping; the mill town); no sitting sat at Norhold (B5 M9). The protected first-pole entry keeps "a stamp renewed four times" verbatim [OWNER-pending #43]; M1 stages no sitting in the interval unless the owner chooses option (c) [B5-reconciled 2026-10-06: audit §F A11].]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:5:He stood just within the post, on the hinge side, where a person coming in out of the light would be three steps into the room before he saw that anybody was there. The long board lay beyond him, by the hearth. Brom had six bowls on it and the ladle in the pot, and the stool stood at its end with nobody on it. Karis sat with her notebook shut under her hand. Oryn was on her feet beside the stool, and Lira on hers at the hearthstone, with her back to the fire and her arms folded, the right over the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:13:In all the dealings that those two had ever had, neither had said a word to the other. One had opened a door in a borrowed room and shut it again. One had read forty copied orders at a table, knowing from the doorway what sort of man had copied them, and had ridden home taking care not to wonder at his name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:Karis had not opened her notebook. Vastin's head turned to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:85:"I shall say the short thing first, since everything after it leans upon it. I hold no office." He did not raise his voice, and it had begun to lose its hoarseness. "I left the seal of mine in a tray, some months ago, and came down a stair. I gave no notice of it, and I was given no leave; whether that is a resignation or a man walking out of his place, the office will have settled by now, and I did not stay to be told which. I have no rank that will open a door for you, and no seal that will shut one. Nobody sent me, and nobody has sanctioned a word of what I am going to say; you had better know that before you believe any of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:97:"There is a thing that ought to be said, and I am the one of us who was trained to say it. In my old trade there is a grade of approach which begins exactly so. A man arrives with nothing: he has no seal, and no escort, and he limps. He has been wronged by the house that he comes from, and has left it; and he brings a gift, which is true, since only a true gift is ever opened." His hands were in his sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:129:There had been a rule in that reading room, and he remembered it then, with the man's two hands lying on the same board as his own. Five people had said it aloud, one after another, so that each should hear it in his own voice: this man was to be shown a case, and told nothing about the boy. Cael had been glad of the rule. He had been afraid of a kind question, asked late in an afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:135:"You showed me a case," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:139:"I read it, and I said what it was, and I went home. I did the one thing that I had told you I should do. After that I did what an evaluator does with any finding, which is to go back and see what it stands upon. There was a paper in that case which said that a function had been appended to the reading, and that it was not the reading."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:151:"Then I have carried one half of this a long way to people who had the other," he said. "You know that it is there. There is one thing about it which I do not think that any case could have told you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:153:"That they'll not open it," said Lira. Her hand lay on the board between them. "That's what you've come to say, isn't it? There's a thing under those plates that they found there, and somebody up a stair keeps it shut, and they'll not open it for you or for anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:159:"I supposed what you suppose," Vastin went on. "I supposed that somebody above my desk kept the thing shut, and I went to look for the door that it was kept behind, meaning to learn who held the other half of the lock. The office has one page upon the matter, and it is the office's own. The page says that the layer is not to be opened; and it gives as its reason that it cannot be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:213:"Then you will understand me the more readily when I say that my trail stops there. I could not follow it. An archive on the Line keeps a book at its door, as a gate does, and every reader is written in it with the office that he comes from. My name stood in such books for forty years as the certifying office. If I had gone to that door with the seal, the request would have been minuted, and whatever shelf I had asked for would have been held by evening. If I went without it, I should be an old man of no standing, asking for the lowest shelf of a border archive, and I should not be let past the porter." The left hand opened a little, and shut. "I have never been able to go where that line points. I have only been able to carry it to somebody who might."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:235:He had both palms on the board again. The fire had burned down a little, and Cael's eyes had come far enough home to show him those hands plainly at last. They were an old man's, and a clerk's, and a strap of plaited hide had been across both of them that morning. The right was raw from the heel to the fingers' roots, and the left had fared no better, and had never opened flat since he sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:237:"The other two are not to be said. They are to be laid upon a table, in a certain order, which I settled on the road, and I should be glad of lamplight for them and a door that shuts, if you have such a thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:239:"There's a store behind that chimney," said Cael. "This evening, when the board's cleared and the keeper's shut his casks. It was built for sacks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:"There are three reasons," he said. "I had a long road to put them in order, and I shall give you them so. The first is a thing that you did not do. Five people laid a case in front of me that would have shaken any officer in the Compact, and when I had read it, not one of them asked me for anything. I remarked on it at the time. I have been asked for something by every person who ever set a paper before me; it is what the paper is for. You asked nothing. You let me go down a stair with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:25:Lira thought about it. Her left hand lay open before her, and she shut it, one finger after another, and nodded, as she had nodded in Ostrand. He had not dressed the answer for her, and she seemed to find that she could stand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:29:It was Oryn who spoke next, and she had not meant to; Cael saw that by how her mouth opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:45:She said it without any heat. She had a rule, and it was of her own making: she put her question to a person before she put her hands on him, each time, and took the answer. This man had answered a thing that she had not yet put. So nothing had been asked, by her reckoning, and nothing refused; the matter lay exactly where it had lain before she opened her mouth. She drew the sleeve down. Cael saw her eyes go once more to the leg, and stay a breath, and come away; and he thought that the knee had not heard the last of that woman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:107:He stopped. His left hand had shut on the board, and it was that hand which he was looking at, and not at any of the six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:143:The boy put it into the bag with the others. Up at the shed the gelding was eased back round his ring, with the brown mule standing by in her own trace in case she were wanted; and the scow slid away down the curve of the cable, with twelve men at her rail, and went up the far side of it to the western landing as if the morning had never happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:191:Karis lit the lamp with a point, when the keeper had shut his casks, and seven people stood in a room that had been built for sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:193:It took some arranging. She had the table's lamp end, and her stool. Lira sat on the meal-sacks with her knees drawn up, and Seln had the sill of the one small window. Brom shut the door and put his back against it. Oryn stood beside him, in the corner by the hinges, with her sleeves down. Cael had the table's far end, where the deal was bare; and Vastin stood at its long side with the lamp between himself and Karis, since he would not take the only other stool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:203:He had carried the first in under his arm. He laid it on the deal now, at the lamp end, and turned it to face her, as a case had once been turned to face him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:229:Karis went down the row with one gloved finger, lifting a corner at a time, until she came to a sheet whose number she knew; and then she reached behind her, without looking, to the tarred case, and brought out a limp little ledger, and laid it open beside the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:25:- **Not At His Desk** — *Seln simply wasn't at his desk the next morning.* The morning clerk's window; keys in a row; the summaries pinned eleven years later; the personnel query; **the apparatus window** — *what did he have*; fifteen years of true, empty product requisitioned. **Seln's window** (~3k): the east road in the dark; the last null report drafted and not filed; the case's history; looking back, badly; firewood for people who will be cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:She did not go over what he had said. She had it by heart, every word, with the place where he had stopped to rub his eyes; and she had put the whole of it into the box where she kept things that might be so and could not yet be shown. It was a large thing for that box, and the lid would not quite shut on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:25:She knew what she thought it was. She had known it on her heels in front of the board, with a girl asking, and had shut her notebook on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:69:She did not take it to the board; she took it to the bare end of the long table, a good way from any sheet of her own, and opened it at the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:141:On the fourteenth sheet of the Stair there was a longer one, of six signs, and all six were hers. She spelled it out with her lips shut. It was the number after ten; and the hooked upright stood in it three times over, exactly where the old clerk's letter did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:209:Nobody spoke; Karis had her notebook shut against her chest in both arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:215:"I told you then that I could not tell the one case from the other," said Vastin. He was standing under the blanket, with the stick. "I cannot now. I looked for the hand that sent it, and I did not find one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:219:The old mouth had opened, and Cael saw it shut again, and saw what it had shut on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:He went over what he had, and it took no time at all, since it was so little. He had ten things in him, and not one of them could be opened on that hill; a declaration under those walls drew a clerk, and a clerk was the end of four people. He had a lamp to hold. He had a girl with a slow arm, and a man with two bad shoulders, and another with one leg; and a researcher who had this afternoon learned the tenth letter of a tongue that nobody had read in the life of the town.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:345:"I know what I have," said Karis; and she shut the tall book very gently, as a thing is shut that will be wanted in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:20:- **Seln's window** (~5k): the paper traced (the watermark's sixteenth of an inch; the fourteenth lot with a mark where a word should be); two favors spent; the four grades of pressure, named in order; **the catechism, said once, and watching it lose** — quietly, the largest event of his adult life; the case carried across campus; **"You're sure." / "No. That's how you'll know it's true."**; the annex chosen; the floor-schedule cipher (five arrivals, not six); a null report filed in the old pattern — **not** "the last of its kind" (calendar: he keeps filing until the year's close).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:34:- Seln does not open the case in this movement. Havel learns nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:35:- Brom's father: keep the forge anecdote; don't call him a farrier's son (§11 #12).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:33:- Seln's case is not opened or mentioned aloud. The cache's content is not stated.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:27:Cael counted the instrument before anyone opened it, because counting was the only work available to him at that table and because a document was a room like any other. Twenty-three leaves, written one side. Forty-one numbered paragraphs. Two schedules annexed. The word *discretion* eleven times, which he tallied twice to be sure of, and the word *evidence* four, and the word *fraud* once — in the negative, in the second paragraph, disclaiming it. Nine references to provisions of the registry code and two to the Compact's own convening minute, and, on the last leaf, above the seal, the attestation line every binding instrument on the continent closed with, in the same type as the rest, no larger: *given under the Compact's hand.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:47:The finding, when it landed, landed as procedure. "The clause's validity is conceded," the counsel said, at the end of an hour, in the voice of an advocate who had expected a fight and found the ground already ceded. "Explicitly. Paragraph six. *The petitioner does not contest the text of the provision under which the enrollment was executed, nor its lawful survival of the standardization directive.* They've handed you your whole Greyvane case as a stipulation." She set the page down. "I have been doing this for twenty-six years and I have never had an opponent concede my strongest ground in their opening instrument. It is either very generous or very frightening."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/protected-patterns.txt:46:i have nine things that aren.?t a path cael said eventually to nobody in particular no tier no rank no enrollment no protection and four people who signed out of the world that was supposed to provide them and one of the nine taken from a hand the world sent to hold me and one taken in earnest from a friend and the machine.?s own maintenance records in a case under that canvas which we can.?t file and can.?t publish and are carrying east anyway because the truth is worth carrying even when it can.?t be spent
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:73:"She never shuts sound there," he had told the meadow, from his keg. "There's a current comes in sideways and eats her from under. Keep to my green."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:101:Cael went out onto the shelf, and opened the read, and gave it the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:121:He had gone round to his tailboard to cut the load free, and the ice had opened under him as it had opened under his wheels. He hung now by both arms from the cart's hind rail, a heavy grey-bearded man in a sheepskin that had drunk its own weight in water, and he had left off shouting. His face was the colour of tallow. Before him the cart lay tipped back at the slant of a roof, with a dozen barrels and two pigs of iron lashed in its bed; and the fore-axle rested on a lip of ice that Cael could feel going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:He could see almost nothing by then. The snow a foot from his face was a white blur; he could not have counted his own fingers. He had never held the read for half so long, or leaned on it so hard, and it had taken his near sight from him piece by piece as the price. He went on backward on his elbows with his eyes shut, since they were of no use open, and called the ground to a man whom he could hear and could not see. *Left. Straight. Stand. Walk.* The boy sent each word up the bank, and up there a girl with one arm walked a mule round a drum and stopped her dead at every *stand*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:He was told afterward how it had gone in the shed. Lira could not see the river from the horse-walk, or so much as the door. She had gone round that ring backward, with the mule's nose against her chest and her right hand shut on the cheek-strap, hearing two words shouted over and over by a boy whose voice was breaking; and each time that the second came she had set her heels and stopped a beast three times her own weight with a word. Her left arm had lain in its sling throughout and held nothing. The ferryman had turned on his keg to watch her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:195:Then there was a boom under him, like a great door shutting, and the whole sheet rang.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:251:"She's a mule. You tell her, and she does it." Lira shut the hand. "It's a good deal more than I could say of some people on a count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:261:On the far side of the lamp Karis had moved her three heaps to the table's end. She had the kit's case open before her, with the cord out of it, and beside the cord a peg of peeled hazel, cut to the length of the iron stake and pointed with a knife. Under her two hands, open for the first time since a quiet man had put it into them on a road, was the dead surveyor's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:"Then it will wait for your eyes." She shut the book, and ran the cord through her fingers to the first knot. "On the hundred and sixty-eighth I shall want all five of you in the inn yard at noon, with dry hands. I have had this cord for forty-six days and I have not yet seen anybody measure with it. It is time that we learned how he did."
