editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:694:**Calendar.** No season, month or English weekday names; feet and yards. Day 58 Second-day, day 61 Fifth-day, day 64 First-day: Lira's three carters. About day 57: the confluence's stewards put the road meet back eight days for repairs to their hall (from day 63 to day 71; see owner flag 1). Day 66 Third-day: the note; "five drawers… shut" (the last two days before). Day 67: Withrow's colors sentence. **Day 68 Fifth-day: the small room**; Seln in the dark wing that night. Day 69 Sixth-day: the delegation leaves (Withrow and Bracken in the hired carriage, the coach, the program wagon; Rooke, the Stone, Lira, the Current third-year, Karis, Seln, Brom, Cael; Ephram stays). Day 70: the confluence at dusk; the card; Rooke's brief. **Day 71 First-day: the exhibition (afternoon); the production order served a quarter past two.** Day 72: finals; the corridor; the last evening at the rail. Days 73–74: home in rain. **Day 75 Fifth-day: the fourth sitting** (records produced; the one question). **Day 76 Sixth-day, second bell: the escort order at the gate**; noon refectory; Gault; the notebook; records hall; the Crown yard after dark. Bracken writes to the district seat on day 77 (the answer due about a week later). Brom's panel and the ruling share the year's last Fifth-day. The *fled* default would enter forty days after the fourth sitting's close (day 75) — the code's count, said by Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:717:- **Seln's case:** on the shelf above the copying table, untouched; the brass key in his waistcoat. Not mentioned aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:785:**Movement 4 CLOSED (2026-10-10).** Sol source-distance audit: 85 → r1 41 → r2 25 → r3 6, closed by ten Sol line fixes (eight chain fixes, two seams opened by cuts), applied by the coordinator. r2 canon checks PASS: Vastin's warning in ch27 is verbatim against B5 ch58:189 (*Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer.*) and Brom knows it via B5 ch59's long-table retelling; Seln's ch22 realisation that a return has nowhere to go past the last station stays at the M6 boundary. r2 fixes kept: Karis's notebook reply schedule; Bracken's "Leave me one more leaf…"; the "other gates" consequence after Vastin's warning. Cut in r2–r3 (later movements must not rely on them): Karis's forecast of the reply chain; the ch27 splinter, the form's blank line, the horse paragraph, Lira's written question; ch25 Withrow's public thanks and the Silver's "licences" reading; ch22's headings passage and the mother's-ledger detail. ~27,965 words. Overlap 0/7, gates 0, probe 1%/10%.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:803:**Calendar.** No season or month names; feet and yards; Halcenvane weekdays. Day 77 Seventh-day: Seln at Bracken's counter; two favours; the drop; rain from the morning. Day 78 First-day: the cards read in the annex. Day 79 Second-day: the reach priced. Day 80 Third-day: the mould-room answer; Havel given the letter. Day 81 Fourth-day: the western answer; the grades; the catechism; the case carried to Cael at dusk; the cipher (five arrivals on day 85); the null report; the bridge chained at the third ring after the fourth bell, the town ferry tied up at noon; Havel's sixth entry that night. **Day 82 Fifth-day: the upper ferry; the cut (mid-morning); the reach and the unbinding; the fifth sitting (documentary) at the hour after noon; home over the bridge at dusk (water under the second ring).** Day 83 Sixth-day: schedule posted a day early (the five moved to day 88); fixity; Storm resettles (40 min; 15 min); the doorway (twelve seconds); Rooke. Day 84 Seventh-day: the idle state found by naming at dawn; Karis's curve; the district seat's answer. **Day 87 Third-day: the eleventh of Sowing — eighteen.** Day 88 Fourth-day: the annex (tomorrow at close). Brom's panel and the ruling share the year's last Fifth-day. The *fled* default would enter forty days after day 75.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:814:- **Cael and the circle (Lira, Brom, Karis):** the woman on the Ostrand road and her craft as fought: points fixed against a surface; lines between them; points laid where a half beat says the body will go; nothing taken back; range costs a half-second stillness; a cage laid through a first point set against the body; release from inside unthreads it. The notice text. The fixity proposals; the doorway; the naming; Karis's curve *for now*. The team: six, no faces, no paper, no blades; a closed wagon with two rings. **Not reported** to the seat or registry (the four-minute decision); Withrow told; one line in her private book. The district seat's answer. Seln will open the case on day 88 in the reading room; nobody knows what is in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:815:- **Seln:** the paper traced (the second run; the fourteenth lot above the ruled line, a mark for a word); the four grades; has decided to open the case; **has not been told why Shadow was sealed and does not know a sealed thing exists**; **did not see the reach or the unbinding** (he was under the wagon in the gully; "I heard it"); knows the team broke off and the boy has ribs. **Does not know the mechanism.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:816:- **Havel:** the seal absent from every registry of seals, recorded as a fact; nothing made of it; **six sheets, the last**. Learned nothing of the truth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:825:- **Seln:** two favours left (one never to be spent); the quick bag; the case **back on the shelf above the copying table**, unopened; the brass key; the null-report bundle with this week's duplicate; the floor schedule with the five on day 88.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:839:- **Seln:** Bracken's narrow question; the favours; believes the boy; the case on the sill; "You're sure." / "No…"; the annex; "Five."; under the wagon; "Ribs?"; the cup turned; "It is a good room…"; the tenth bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:857:- **Opened:** the case's opening (day 88, the reading room, five arrivals); Bracken's next letter; the wagon left in the gully (nobody will look); Withrow's private line; Lira's three *Not fought* on her program sheet; the doorway's line on the next leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:870:- (h) The birthday is in **the reading room** (tomorrow's room), so Seln's line about the room is about where he will open the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:880:- **Ostrand bridge (flag):** five iron rings on the middle pier; shut to everybody at the third ring "since the year the middle arch went"; reopened under the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:915:**Calendar.** No season, month or English weekday names; feet and yards; Halcenvane weekdays. **Day 88 Fourth-day:** Lira's strapping off (morning); the case opened in the reading room after supper (five arrivals a quarter apart, Cael fourth, Seln on the eighth bell); the mechanism told to Seln; the seal's grounds revised. **Day 89 Fifth-day:** the counter; Brom's sling off at noon; Lira's question on the north wall at dusk. **Day 90 Sixth-day:** Rooke's "Not yet." **Day 91 Seventh-day:** the Greyvane leaves (Bracken, noon); the enrollment book lent; the clean door found that night. **Day 92 First-day:** the enrollment book returned before the second bell; the honest floor, perception only, after the tenth bell. **Day 93 Second-day:** the four findings. **Day 94 Third-day:** Vastin proposed; five hands. **Day 95 Fourth-day:** Seln reads the closures. **Day 96 Fifth-day:** the note left at the inn on the wharf. **Day 98 Seventh-day:** the borrowed office, the second bell after noon; Vastin eleven minutes early (the Ostrand station's inspection that morning). **About day 103:** Vastin at his desk (the "fifth morning after", having walked six river stations in four days). The *fled* default would enter on day 115 if Cael left. The midyear recess is ahead; not dated. Brom's panel and the ruling share the year's last Fifth-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:934:- **The case:** on Karis's reading-room shelf, second from the floor, between two of her grey Greyvane boxes; **two keys** (the room's on Karis's cord; the case's in Seln's waistcoat). Karis's four finding slips are in the lid's pocket beside the leaf. The fifth slip (one word, written on day 88 before reading) in Karis's coat, to be burned. The case went to Ostrand and back on day 98 inside Seln's post satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:959:- **Closed:** the case opened; Series Secret #2 discharged; the asymmetry debt (the mechanism and the seal told); Karis's "older strata" (paid, to the limit); the stratum line's question mark; Karis's three blank Greyvane lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:964:- (b) The case is gone from its shelf on day 88's morning (M5 put it back on day 81); the packet's "down for days" is not used.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:976:- **Seln's history (flag):** the fourth press of a district seat's top-floor records room; five weeks of night copying by one lamp on the floor; the memoranda were spares; the leaf the only original; the case bought later from a surveyors' case-maker; the green-distempered station corridor a year on; his mother kept a grain factor's books on a river quay for twenty years; he copied them for a copper a page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1050:- **The revelation's reach: six**, unchanged. Not filed, published or used. The cache is on Karis's shelf under two keys; it was not opened on the page in M7.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1065:- **Karis:** thirty slips for a closing (eleven fleshed by day 159); the grey notebook (*forty paces — of whose?*; *two of four*; *on a person*); four oilcloth bundles become **three deal boxes, roped, on the deposit shelf at the records hall's north end** (Bracken's tags one, two, three; deposit book: *Depositor: K. Dellenmoor. Contents: the depositor's own papers. To be released to the depositor, or to her order.*). The case, unwrapped, still on her reading-room shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1066:- **Brom:** on his table under the window, the summons, both dockets and the schedule of evidence under a whetstone; **the day-45 Velmere letter squared beside them, seal up, unopened**; two boxes roped under the bed; the plaque face down. Right pocket: the slip *Both.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1076:- **Deployments:** public — the band in wing three (day 151: one small burst, the give shut, the read; 23⅔; never past the Halcenvane ceiling). Private, two people, a closed floor (day 129) — Wind to seven on boards, the push at fighting load ×3, the give (three blows), one spark, one lane (ten counts), the read at depth. **Anchor: none** (idle; two namings). **Shadow: none** (one drift held). The honest floor once in the term, perception only (ten feet, faint at twelve).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1199:- Brom's **day-45 Velmere envelope** remains on his table, seal up, unopened. Beside it are the district finding and guidance slip, folded and unsigned. Brom has neither signed nor refused (`manuscript/chapter-53.md:117–123`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:1027–1028`; B6 `BOOK_MAP.md:91–96`). M9 may leave both papers there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1201:- **Seln's locked case** is in his post satchel and goes with him. Book 7 expects the three research boxes and Seln's case under one canvas, with the case riding on Seln when they walk (B6 `BOOK_MAP.md:69–73`; B7 `BOOK_MAP.md:21–23`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1202:- **Carry caution for M9:** Brom's two personal boxes are still roped under his bed (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:117–125,243`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:1066`). Book 7's binding entry names only Karis's three boxes and Seln's case. M9 must unpack, consolidate, leave, or otherwise dispose of Brom's two boxes on the page; they cannot silently become Karis's three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1216:- **The revelation's reach: six**, unchanged. Not filed, published or used; argued once more and put down. The case left the reading room on Seln, in his post satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1229:- **The rotation's book:** last line *Sixth-day. The Crown yard. Ephram. All of it.*; shut for good.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1232:- **Brom:** on his table, the district's finding folded with the guidance slip tucked in, **unsigned**, and a hand's breadth off **the day-45 Velmere envelope, sealed, unopened**. Right pocket: the slip *Both.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1234:- **Seln:** the case in his post satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1243:- **Deployments:** Blade — **none** (seen for one breath and put down). Anchor — **none** (a dozen askings at Ephram's heel, each named). Shadow — **none**. Public, on the oak, in front of the house and the town: Wind ten (four past the oak's six; one past Norhold's nine), the push at fighting load six (one past Norhold's five), two Storm lanes (ten counts each; the first on the bluff), the give opened and shut, one spark, the read at depth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1255:- **Withrow:** the collar; the code shut; "You did not lose badly"; four days; "I wanted it to be yours"; the door; last out, and a bow that was meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1273:- **Opened:** the district's notice (five days); the unsigned finding and the unopened envelope left on a table; Lira's sentence to Rooke (promised for "after the bout", not on the page); the red book's two words (unread); Karis's unwritten letter to the assistant; the Greyvane answer on its way to Denvash; Havel's request (not yet handed in on the page); Withrow's "leaf put in"; the blank over the Iron column; the coat gone "to catch a bag"; Blade's first days of asking; an unpriced cost (Ephram's shoulder).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1330:- **Objects.** Three numbered boxes (the printed registry sheet and the Ternhall straightedge in Karis's middle box); Seln's surveyor's case, which rides in SELN'S OWN post satchel from the wing to the shelf (ch55, ch57, ch59) and is loaded under the cart's canvas with the boxes at the shelf (ch60; continuity fix made in the completion pass so the protected closing line "in a case under that canvas" is true); Hesk's satchel (Cael's) with the current log (about a third of its leaves left), the full old volume with the letters under its front board, and the observation notebook; cart, gelding, purse. **The sealed Velmere envelope is NOT carried: Brom leaves it unopened on his table at Halcenvane beside the unsigned finding (ch56:147–165).** [Corrected 2026-10-11: an earlier version of this line, copied from the recheck's continuity paragraph below, wrongly put the Velmere letter in the satchel and the case under the canvas throughout.] Daeva's reply went west at the ford. The Hesk and Vell letters are written at the shelf, carry no return address and are HELD, not sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1346:**Counts, objects and documents — PASS.** All three numbered boxes reach the cart, the road and the shelf. Seln's locked case remains under the canvas; Hesk's satchel contains the current log, the full old volume and the sealed Velmere letter; [COORDINATOR CORRECTION 2026-10-11: both clauses are wrong against the prose — see the Objects ruling above, which governs.] the current log has about a third of its leaves left. Cart, gelding, purse, printed sheet and Ternhall straightedge remain accounted for. Daeva's reply goes west at the ford; the Hesk and Vell letters are written at the shelf, have no return address and remain held. Karis marks the printed sheet from the middle box; Lira records the route separately in her own book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1365:- **Cael:** eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted Tide-adjacent anomaly** (*Still open. Still real. Patience.*). **Deployment since the Line: none.** The forearm bruise is a week old and about a week from gone; no open injury. Shadow sealed on Seln's terms; it costs from waking and drifts at dusk; its term is nearly met out here and he has left it shut. Hesk's book has about a third of its leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1366:- **Lira:** Wind, Iron Rank One formal (certificate in her coat); the Silver sitting declined in one sentence. **Brom:** Iron Skin, Copper for good; the plaque in his pack. **Karis:** Ember; standing, privileges, budget and annex lapsed; tier retained and uncounted; three boxes, the second on top. **Seln:** Shadow, Bronze; left his keys and filed nothing; the case in his satchel. All uninjured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1368:- **Objects:** a two-wheeled cart, a brown gelding, three boxes and (when not on Seln) the case under one canvas. The registry's printed sheet and the Ternhall straightedge are in Karis's middle box. **Two letters, to Hesk and to Vell, written at the shelf, no return address, held in the book's back-board pocket.** The reply to Daeva went west from the ford that morning. A pooled purse, larger than expected, unspent, no figure stated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/DIRECTOR_CUT_READY.md:33:  warning; all-caps boards and cards read in sentence case; single initials are letter-names; three correct
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:47:Brom's paperwork arrived in two envelopes in the same week, and he opened them in different rooms.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:55:It came up with the evening post — the estate's mark in the wax, the hand the delegation had seen twice a year for two years and once, at Norhold, on a single line — and he did not take it to the courtyard. He took it upstairs, and it lay on the small table in his room — Lira reported this, because Lira reported things — for two days, unopened, beside the docket, the two envelopes squared together with the care he gave to useful things and neither of them touched. Lira reported it the way Lira reported things, which was as logistics, at the residence table, to nobody in particular, with the drill-book open in front of her and her eyes on the page. "Second envelope's still on his table. Squared with the docket. Two days." She turned a leaf she was not reading. "He moved the lamp so it isn't in the light."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:69:On the third evening he opened it alone, and nobody at the table ever learned its contents, because he showed nothing of them; what the table got was one fact about his face when he came down to dinner afterward, catalogued from across the room at the instrument's full attention:
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:115:The delegation coach put them down at the foot of the market street at the half-bell, and the four steps of the wool exchange had thirty-one people on them — Cael counted them from the coach window before the door opened, because counting was cheaper than deciding how to feel — of whom nine were correspondents with folding boards and the rest were Ostrand's registry-literate come early for a bench. The side entrance had a registry officer and a list. The officer read the list, and read Withrow's name off it, and the counsel's, and Karis's as *documentary support*, and Brom's and Lira's as *institutional attendance*, and Seln's as *records support, Halcenvane*, and then came to the last line and read it aloud, which he did not have to do and did because the code said parties were identified on entry.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:125:The officer entered what the instrument said. It took eleven seconds. Cael watched the pen do it and filed them where the other eleven seconds lived, and went in through a door that had been opened for the term because the ordinary one was not built for the people who had come to watch.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:131:The gallery held the correspondents at the rail and, behind them, the ordinary curious — Ostrand's registry-literate, clerks and factors and two guild officers, come to watch a chair with no name conduct the continent's most-read case — and among them, in a plain coat, at the gallery's end where the sightlines to every table and door converged, the delegation's assessment clerk, who had come down on the coach as records support and had taken, without appearing to choose, the seat a man takes when he intends to read a room and not be read by it. Cael marked him from the respondent's table and did not look again. Withrow sat at the table's head in her registry grey with the response in its two cases beside her, and did not touch them, because a chancellor at a proceeding did not fidget with her own filing. The counsel sat with the minute-book open. Karis sat with the notebook closed, which was its own announcement.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:143:*The hearing at Greyvane had Coss's face on it — you could read the whole case off him from across the room. The inspection had Vastin's; the evaluation was the man. This has a seal. A registrar of no age and no face reading a designation off a clerk's card, and a procurator with nothing on him, and a room conducting itself. I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:9:He was three, or thereabouts, with a head of hair like a dandelion, and he had been a thing to worry over before the snow: a chest that whistled, and a mother who had walked him out to the bench twice in one round to ask whether it was the kind that passed. Oryn had said that she would hear him again before the cold. Then there had been seventeen days on a mountain, and a road shut behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:23:"You'd every need. You couldn't know which sort it was, and nor could I, from the far side of a shut road. Fear's what you had instead of me." She let the boy go, and he went straight back to the mule. "I'm sorry that it was all you had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:25:It was then that the gate-house door opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:The room was hot, and smelled of tallow, and there was a young man in the corner who had been told to keep out of the way and could not find any. The woman on the bed had her eyes shut. She was perhaps twenty. She opened them when the pain let go of her, and looked at a strange square face in the lantern-light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:55:She did not hurry. She could not have hurried, with what she had left in her; and a child was not a thing that she would have hurried in any case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:57:Her hands asked. The Path eased what they asked of, a little, so that a wall which had been set like a fist went slack under her palms for the length of a breath; and in that breath she walked the small boat round by the width of a finger. Then the pain came back, and the wall shut, and she stood with her hands exactly where they were and waited for it to pass over. She did that forty times, perhaps, and lost the count, which she never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:101:"A hand is a thing that a clerk looks at for the space of a count. I was in a registry's service for fifteen years, and for the most of them I was looked through; but it was clerks who looked through me, and a clerk remembers furniture. There may be one man in that yard who has seen me walk a file from one desk to another, in a house above Ostrand or in some other. If he finds such a face among the porters, he will not ask me who I am. He will ask himself what the rest of you are, to be worth it." He drew a breath. "I should cost you the count. And there is this besides: when a door has shut on five people, somebody ought to be standing where he can see who comes to look at the door. That person cannot be on a card."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:107:That left the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:111:Cael looked at the case, and so did Brom. He saw Karis begin to turn her head, and stop herself, since she had been raised to think it rude to look at a person whom one was about to ask for something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:"You are one short of a figure, and I am standing here. I have carried that case for three days against a healer's express instruction, and I do not propose to hand it to somebody else at the foot of a hill because the carrying has acquired a name." He bent, hooked the strap with the stick's head, as he had at a stone, and brought it to his shoulder. "I have been counted once already in this town, by a man with beads. I found that it could be survived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:145:Karis gave up the card. She did it well, he thought: she did not offer it, or explain it, but only held it out and looked at the wall. The keeper with empty hands took it. He looked for the figure, as he had looked for a hundred figures since the gate opened. Then his eyes went back up the card, to its head, and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:163:He was doing it badly. Cael could see that he was. A person who has looked at his boots all his life lets his head hang, and this one held his at an angle, with care, as a thing that he had been told the trick of; but his right hand was shut on the ash, and it did not move an inch toward his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:173:Vastin went under the arch with the case on his shoulder and his eyes on the setts. It was a gate that he had ridden through on four occasions, with a keeper standing at each jamb and the board held out to him, open, so that he might see for himself how it was kept; and he went through it now as one stroke in four, on the wrong side of the pen, and Cael heard him let his breath go when the shadow of the arch was behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:"Lower archive," he said. He had a dry, exact voice. "I had heard that there was such a card. My father kept this gate before me, and he told me that he had passed one, once, when he was young." He looked up, at three boxes, and at a tarred case, and for rather longer at a courier's flat of waxed leather with a stamp across its face. "That is a good deal of matter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:Seln had known it for some time. He had read two boards at two lane-ends, and both had said the same thing in the same paint: the gate was shut at sunset and opened at first light, and whoever was outside it slept outside it. A stick could not have done three miles in what was left of the day. Nobody said so, since nobody needed to; and it was Vastin himself who looked at the sun, and at the figure on the stone, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:"When the folder had grown as thick as my hand," said Vastin, "the seat did what it does when its instruments have not served. It sent the one that it keeps for such cases. It sent me. I came up that road with a plate, and a set of questions, and three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:123:"I did say so, and it was true. It was the reason, and you are not to think otherwise for anything that I say next. Five young people had laid a case on a table that hour, and shown me what the office I served did with honest readings; I would not lend it my name for one more. I have let that stand for the whole of the matter ever since. It is not the whole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:"I wrote *declined*, on a form which has no line for why; and I should have written it if there had been no such night. The reason that I gave you at Ostrand was the reason. I do not take it back." He shut his eyes. "But when the pen was down I found that I was glad, and it was not on your account that I was glad. I was relieved that I need never learn what the plate would say. I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one. I have been thought the better of since, by six people on a moor, for the half that I let them see. That is what I have carried from the seat to this bank, and it is not in the flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:177:He had done the same once before, on a bluff, with a notebook lying shut on a table between them and one more question plainly in reach. Cael had taken it then for a thing that the man had missed, and he knew better now: it had been looked at and left where it lay, and tonight it was being left again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:191:"It is a sentence of mine. I have said it at inquiries for forty years, about frames." The left hand opened as far as it could, and shut. "I wrote it once of a person, in an assessment which a certain seat declined to receive, as lying outside its schedule; and I did not suppose that I should hear it said back to me on a roadside by the person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:213:Cael got up. He went round the post and stood on the slope below the old man, where the footing was, and put out his right arm, bent. A raw hand closed on it above the elbow. It was a good deal stronger than it looked. The stick went into the turf, and the whole long weight of the man came up through that grip in one pull, with a breath shut hard behind the teeth, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:253:"No. I know that." She shut the book over her thumb. "I never asked for it. I want that in there as well. I could have, and I'd have got it, and you'd have hated me for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:19:Vastin had not looked at the hounds since the first of them came over the bank. He was looking down the cut. Cael, who was two feet from him, saw exactly where. A mule's long ears stood against the light, sixty strides off, beside a small figure with something bright in its hand, and a square one with its arms bare. Then the grey head turned, and he looked up the hill at what was coming. His face did not change at all. It was the face with which he had read a case through to its last sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:"No." It came through shut teeth, very distinct. "It does not go anywhere. I told that girl so. It stands where I stand, and I may not lift a foot; if I shift so much as a heel, the whole of it is gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:That was the second cost, and it was arriving already. It was going out of him as heat goes out of a forge when the door is opened, steadily, with nothing kept back. Cael's own Arbiter had been dark since the day that it woke, and yet he felt it from two feet away. He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside Karis with nine points alight, and neither had been a tenth of this. Every hound in that cut could feel it from the far side of the hill. It was the loudest thing that had been done on that road in the memory of the den.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:83:But there were seven, and one bank, and it was thirty feet from the road to the lip. A thing that could not climb it quickly could still climb it. Cael saw one of them go wide of her, up to the left, where the plates were bigger and lay still. He saw another, which she had sent down to the road, come in under Brom's stone on the low side, and shut its jaws on the nearest thing that was quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:93:It was no more than a length from Cael. It did not so much as turn its eye to him; he was a quiet body, and there was a loud one two feet beyond. It went by his knee like a grey wind and took the old man by the stiff leg, a little below the joint, and shut its teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:101:Everything that he owned came to him then at once, and each of the ten had its reason. *Edge?* said the ninth, of the steel in his fist, and it would have gone through that grey neck like a wire through cheese. Pressure had been gathering since the first hound came over the bank. It lay under his breastbone as tight as a held breath, narrow and deep, and he had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It would go through the hound, and on, into a man's knee. *There?* asked Anchor, of the place where the plane met the scree, in its thin note. A point held there would have shut the bank like a gate, and it would have taken every other thing that he had while he held it. Storm showed him the air of the cut as a lane already half laid, from the head to the foot, and what a lane would do to six hounds; and after it he would be ten counts late at everything, in the one place where a beat mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:147:He had known that it would. He stood on a low bank of scree in the shadow of a hill, with one arm half out for his balance, and for one whole count there was nothing in him that answered. He could not shift a foot or shut his fist on the knife. Even his head would not turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:209:Vastin shut his eyes. He did not do anything that Cael could see. The frost that had been lying on nothing fell, in a thin straight line, across the road and the broken ice. There was a hill with a cut through it, and cold air. Up on the scree a plate shifted and ran a yard, and stopped.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:47:The custody that became paperwork by the time anyone asked. This was the grade that had ended two careers he knew of and had never once been recorded as anything but an administrative irregularity. A person is taken; a facility receives him; a file is opened at the facility with a lawful heading; within nine days there is an instrument on record that authorizes what has already happened, and the instrument is genuine, and the office that issued it exists by then, and every question thereafter is answered accurately.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:66:The case's whole value — to him, to anyone — lay in its being closed. A keeper with a sealed case was a man with a price on his silence that no one knew they were paying, which was the only kind of price that stayed paid. Fifteen years of it had bought him nineteen postings, a clean separation from a service that did not do clean separations, and a desk at a mid-tier academy where nobody had ever asked him a question he could not answer flatly.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:68:The moment the case opened, the man holding it stopped being a keeper and became a witness. And witnesses did not retire.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:98:He took the case down.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:100:It came off the shelf in his quarters the way it had come off nineteen shelves in nineteen postings — a plain locked traveling case, scuffed at the corners, the least remarkable object he owned, which was its whole design. It weighed eleven pounds. It had a maker's stamp inside the lid that belonged to a firm that had gone out of business twenty years ago, which was the second-best thing about it, and no maker's mark anywhere on the outside, which was the first. He had carried it through customs at two border stations, through a fire at an inn, and past a records office he had been burgling, and had never once had to explain it, because a mild administrative man with a case is not a question anyone forms.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:106:The covered walk had its evening traffic on it: third-years going down to the refectory in twos, a steward with a barrow, the lamp-man doing the north range, two of Rooke's cohort arguing about a hip. The fountain court had the fountain running because Withrow had ruled in her second year that a fountain that is only run for visitors is a lie about the institution. It was twelve minutes' walk. He took it at his own pace, which was the pace he took everything, and he passed twenty-three people, and he counted them, and not one of them looked at the case, because the case was furniture and so was he, and both facts had been built deliberately over fifteen years and were being spent tonight, together, in one direction.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:112:"You said the text still works." Seln stood at the stair's foot with the case in his hand, held the way clerks held cases, and his voice was the counter voice, and his face was not. "I need to show you whose text it is. Not here — all five of us, somewhere I choose, this week."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:114:Cael had been reading two and a half years of this man, and knew the case on sight the way a person knows the one sealed thing in a house he has lived in: "You're sure."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:118:Then he inclined his head half an inch, and went back up the walk with the case, and Cael stood on the stair with his hand on the rail for a while afterward, doing the thing he did instead of feeling things where anyone could see him, which was counting: twelve minutes' walk each way, twenty-four minutes, for five sentences, one of which was a no.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:140:Cael wrote the log the night before the annex with the residence quiet and the case — he had not seen where Seln kept it; he had not asked — somewhere on the campus below him, in a room or on a shelf, the most expensive object at Halcenvane and the least remarkable.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:146:*Whatever's in that case, it's about to become the most expensive thing anyone has ever handed me. Sleep. Read in the morning.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:156:He checked the registry twice. The registry of seals was two volumes and an appendix, held at the records office counter, updated by his own hand four times a year, and he knew its structure better than any living person because he had recopied the appendix in his first posting when the binding failed. He went through the current volume, and then the appendix, and then — because the honest procedure required it and because he was the only person who would ever know whether he had done it — the superseded volume, in case the office was old and had been reconstituted under a prior designation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:109:Nobody answered her. She shut the notebook on the hair, and put the two edges back into their cloths, each in its own, and would not be spoken to for the rest of that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:111:Cael wrote it at the hearth, on his knee, since the store's door was shut. He set it out as he had been setting things out since a magister first showed him how; and it was the shortest finding that he had ever entered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:It was past the middle of the night when she opened the other book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:163:She had meant to go to her blanket. She had got as far as putting the cord away, coiled, in its place in the tarred case, beside a stake that had never been in ground; and then her hand had gone to the limp little ledger instead of to the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:She knew that mark from four places. It was struck into the head of the stake in that case, and into the newer of two iron bars at a grey stone on the Stair road; it was on every bar of a fence round a place that she had not been let into, under the number thirty-four; and it was here. It stood beside the Stair twice over and beside the fourth place, and she had never found it beside the Fallow or the hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:He laid the lane on the hundred and seventy-sixth night, in the meadow below the ash, when the three fires were covered. Storm was the seventh of the ten, a Gold's worth of weather that had come to him out of a bout with a Gold, and it was the heaviest thing that he carried. It wanted the air. To give it the air he had to take the ground from the read, since at that weight he could have one or the other. It was like shutting an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:265:Cael had not touched the cord again. Karis had not offered it, and he had not asked. It lay coiled in its tarred case beside a stake that had never been driven, and one pale hair lay pressed in the fold of a notebook, and for ninety-five days the two of them had kept his ruling as carefully as if either had known what it was a ruling about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:269:She had asked Cael into the store behind the chimney, and shut the door, which she did not do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:275:"In pieces. I had not the right to say all of it until I had looked in every other place where the answer might lie, and I have looked. It is not in fifty-two sheets. It is not in that case, or in his book." She had a leaf before her with a heading on it and eleven lines. She did not read from it. "Whoever cut those courses did not think in stone. Stone is where a thing is put when it is finished. It was written first; and what is written is kept; and the oldest keeping that I know of is on the far side of that river, inside a wall, under an office of the registry."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:9:*Lira: the Silver-bracket program opened on the first day of the recess, under Rooke, with the institution's seal on the door — the first ladder anyone ever built toward her. She trains at dawn on the continental-grade drills and comes to the table at breakfast with the specific quiet of a person who has been given the thing she asked for and is discovering it is heavier than the asking. She has not said so. She has said "it's there" twice, quoting Rooke, which is how I know.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:13:*Karis: research standing renewed, with a budget line attached — the academy's first ever for an enrollee's private work — and archive privileges Prynn would have wept at, and a second-year assistant assigned to her whom she is training the way Vell trained me, by declining to explain anything twice. The founding-era shelf is on her desk in three languages of copy. She has not opened the tournament charter's provisions section since the road. She is, I think, making herself wait for winter, and winter is here.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:27:He read it back once, capped the pen, and went to bed with the shutters open, because the fountain had earned it again.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:39:"The one Karis is training. She's applied your notebook method to you." Ephram opened the file. "I told her it was a violation of something. She asked me to cite the provision."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:41:Withrow came to the lectern with one page and did not look at it, and the year opened on her register, which had acquired, since Norhold, a quality Cael had spent two months naming and had finally entered as *load-bearing calm* — the composure of a woman whose institution had been measured against the whole continent and had returned a number she could stand on.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:99:"The provisions section," she added, without looking at him, because she knew he'd been waiting two months to ask, "is on the shelf. Unopened since the road. It stays unopened until I've dated the older strata against — until I've read the older material properly, in order. Winter is for the strata. I promised myself at a rest-day table. Don't ask before midwinter."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:105:The assessment counter opened at the third bell, and Cael went down on the fifth day with the term's floor-allocation form, because enrollees requesting evaluation-linked floor time filed at the counter, and because the counter was where Seln was.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:127:Cael saw the escort from the covered walk. Two riders in registry livery flanking a third, the third carrying a document case flat against the saddle's spine — the same posture as the courier who had taken Halcenvane's roster down this road a year and a half ago, reversed: not leaving with a record, arriving with one. The gate lodge's man stood up. The escort did not dismount at the lodge. It rode through, at a walk, to the records hall, the way a delivery rode when the delivery's protocol outranked the building's.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:131:Bracken was at the counter. Withrow arrived thirty seconds later, from the direction of her office, walking at exactly her ordinary pace, which was itself a decision. The courier — a registry officer, not a rider; the livery was the escort's, and the man himself wore the plain grey of an office with a seal — laid the case on the counter, unlocked it, and produced a single sealed instrument, and read the protocol from a card, because the protocol was one nobody in the room had ever heard used.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:133:"A sealed instrument of the registry, addressed jointly to the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy and to the enrollee of record named herein, to be served upon both parties together, in person, and acknowledged by both signatures before the instrument is opened." He looked up from the card. "Both parties are present?"
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:137:Cael signed the acknowledgment beneath her signature, on a form whose type he had never seen — a joint-service form, which Bracken would tell him afterward existed in the registry code and had, in his thirty years, been used for exactly nothing he could name — and the courier witnessed both signatures, sealed the acknowledgment into his case, bowed to a depth precisely calibrated for a chancellor, and left, and the escort rode back down the bluff road at a walk, and the instrument lay on the counter between the three of them with the Compact's seal on its flap.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:5:The asking took ninety seconds in the coaching office at the sixth bell, and Cael was there for it because Ephram brought him, on the principle that a man does not send someone else to collect a debt he means to collect himself. Ephram said the words: sanctioned bout, cohort captain against the enrollee, full-earnest, rated, before the resignations filed. Rooke listened with the coaching file shut under his hand. He asked two questions — whether both parties had asked for it, and whether the enrollee's side was in condition — and got two answers, and then he did the thing that told the whole story of the man, which was to pick up his chalk and go out to the covered walk and write it on the board himself, at that hour, in the dark, while the two of them were still standing in his office.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:45:Ephram had watched him from the cohort's rail for a year. That was the whole strategic content of the second exchange, and Ephram opened it by proving it: he pressed the tempo past the band and refused the reset.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:15:"He builds in courses," Rooke said, with the delegation's charting of the veteran's last eleven rated bouts spread in front of him and the commercial file underneath it, unopened, because Rooke did not pay for pages he could produce himself. "Not like the ones you've fought. An Iron Force builder puts up a structure and holds it. This one puts up a structure and then puts a second one *under* it while the first is still standing, and the second is what's actually carrying, and by the time you've taxed the first he's three courses down and the load's somewhere you haven't been." He tapped the third sheet. "Anchoring runs on a four-count. That's the only number in the file that matters. Everything he builds is four counts from being load-bearing."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:37:The Rank 8 opened at what the file called his working rate, and the working rate was a different climate. Force construction with Silver *depth* — courses built under courses, each anchoring position sustained by a load the tier below could not hold, the structure advancing on her with the patience of a doctrine that had solved Iron-tier taxation years ago. He did not come forward. That was the first thing that was wrong with him, measured against every Force fighter she had ever met: he built where he stood, and the building took the floor, and the floor arrived at her without him ever having crossed it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:105:Mid-meet, between the Silver bracket's second and third bouts, with the confluence hall at its fullest and Halcenvane's colors on the floor for a second bout — a third-year in the Iron bracket, the program's own supporting entrant — a registry records officer came through the hall's main door with a document case and a clerk.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:107:He did not send the clerk. That was the first thing Cael charted, from the north gallery, with the whole hall laid out beneath him like a floor before a bout. The officer came in at the main door, which was at the hall's south end and was the door the paying gallery used, and he walked the length of the hall on the gallery's inside rail, past three hundred seated people, in registry grey, with a document case held at the front of his body in both hands where it could be seen — and it took him, Cael timed it, fifty-one seconds to reach the delegation's block, and by the twentieth second the bout on the floor had stopped being what the hall was watching.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:115:The demand was lawful. Bracken would confirm it by letter within the week — a routine production order, the code's ordinary instrument for building a record, served on a respondent's delegation because the delegation was where the respondent's officers were. It could have been served at the bluff. It could have been posted. It was served at a venue, at the meet's fullest hour, at the gallery rail, by an officer who walked the whole length of the hall to do it, and the timing was the message, and every registry-literate person in the confluence read the message as it was delivered.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:25:The Storm-adjacent framework, heaviest neighbor, resettled twice — the whole architecture shifting a hair around the new mass, the loaded shelf's arithmetic run again, nine weeks of the winter's settling briefly reopened. The first resettling came in the small hours of the second night and lasted forty minutes and took the Wind economy with it, so that he stood up to cross the room and the room's floor arrived a half-beat late under a foot that had been counting on it. The second came on the third morning, was smaller, and closed correctly on its own inside a quarter of an hour.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:57:"So. For now. I am entering *for now* in the record at my own insistence, and I want the reason entered with it, because a limit without its reason gets read as caution by whoever reads it next and it is not caution. The model has no ceiling in it. Not *a high ceiling*. None. Eight points on a curve, no upper bound derivable, no failure case observed, and no comparison class anywhere in the literature — and a model with no ceiling is a model, not a law. If the ninth is Gold-grade or above, this curve predicts a recovery I would describe as impossible, and the correct response to a model predicting an impossibility is not to believe the model."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:73:It was the first time anyone outside the circle had been at one of these evenings, and the invitation was itself a statement the evening did not underline: the cohort captain, arriving with a bottle and the harbor's lock-keeper song and no questions, taking the seat the configuration opened for him as though the configuration had always had it. He put the bottle down, looked at the face-down stacks on the far table for exactly as long as it took to understand them, and turned his back on them for the rest of the night, which was the most tactful thing anyone did all week.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:83:Seln sat at the table like a man who had been sitting at tables all his life. He ate what he was given, said perhaps nine things all evening, three of them dry enough to make Lira laugh out loud, and paid attention to the room's two doors in a manner that only one person present recognized and would not have recognized a year ago. Only a reader of the room — and the boy who kept the room's ledger — knew that across the campus, in quarters the office allocated, a plain scuffed traveling case was already down from its shelf, and had been down for six days, waiting on a floor schedule's revision.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:87:Travel-grade, instrument-maker's work: waxed leather over a frame built the way the man built everything, for forty years of weather; a strap stitched for a shoulder that would carry it far, with the stitching doubled at both anchor points and a spare buckle sewn flat into the underside of the strap where it would not chafe, because Hesk had never in his life given anyone a thing without its spare part. The log's exact dimensions accommodated in an interior pocket with a flap that closed against rain. A second pocket the width of a pen-case. Every seam oiled, every buckle brass, the whole object a tool whose meaning hid in plain sight and did not hide very hard.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:137:*One term, five sittings spent, one ruling pending, one road cut behind us, one case across the campus, down from its shelf.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-11.md:120:The observation notebook got its entry that night, and the entry opened a page the notebook had never had:
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:5:She had done the work in the intervening day, and the intervening day was the part nobody watched. Nine hours in the locked annex with the door wedged and a jug of water and no lunch. Three hundred and eighty documents laid out in dated courses on a floor because the table was not long enough, walked between in stockinged feet with a slate in one hand. Her own four-year working archive brought down in three crates and opened beside them: the Greyvane hearing's compiled record, the copied Ardenmere passages, the registry-law commonplace books, the standardization directive's own founding text in the form she had transcribed it at Prynn, and the founding-era compilation with the Greyvane signatures that had arrived from Prynn's archive the week before on a request the delegation's budget had funded and Withrow had signed without asking why.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:9:She worked from the oldest stratum up, because provenance ran downward and she trusted the floor of a case more than its lid. The lamp. The memoranda in dated courses. The maintenance leaf in its wax-paper sleeve, apart, handled with the specific care she gave documents that could not be replaced. Cael at the table's end with the observation notebook and the anomaly's question, because the night would have a check in it only he could run. Seln at the wall, because it was his case and a keeper attends his archive. Lira and Brom present and quiet, the way the family sat with things too large to help with.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:41:And then the case the function could not handle: an architecture with no lesser bin.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:49:"When the Arbiter meets an architecture it cannot reclassify into a lesser Path — because [SHATTERED] has no lesser bin; there is nothing beneath it to correct it into — the corrections function *cannot fire*. It has no output for the input. And what fires instead is the contingency: the suppression-advisory flag. The machinery's fallback for the one case it cannot falsify. The flag isn't surveillance. It's the corrections function failing safe — flagging the anomaly it couldn't correct, so the layer's protection behavior can handle by exception what the layer couldn't handle by rule." She set both documents down. "And the protection behavior is what Ilsev has been photographing for three years. The returns — *no authority of record*, *closed, no further inquiry* — are the sub-layer defending itself. Auto-closing inquiries no living official authorized, because no living official runs that layer. She's been interrogating the mechanism for three years and filing its answers." A breath. "She just never knew what was answering."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:151:What it could be instead she would not name at that hour. There was one man on the continent who could tell them whether any part of it could be verified from the inside, and knowing would destroy him, and she said that much and no more, and then she said the rest of it flatly, gathering the floor's courses oldest first in stockinged feet. "Which is the next decision, and I am not taking it at the third bell on nine hours' work. Tomorrow. All of us, with the door shut, and everybody saying the worst version of it out loud before anybody says the best."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:153:They put three hundred and eighty documents back into a scuffed traveling case in the order they had come out of it, which took twenty minutes and was done in near silence, and Seln locked it and put the small key back into whatever pocket of his person it lived in, and went out into the corridor first to look at it before the rest of them used it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:159:*I spent three years proving I could work within the system. Tonight I read the system's maintenance records. It isn't broken. It's maintained. The system was never designed to include me — it was designed, by name, before my name, to correct me at the door, and I'm only here at all because I'm the case the corrector couldn't parse. Every session I've won inside the instrument, I won by accident, in the one stratum the machinery can't reach, and the whole proceedings exist to walk me out of it.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:161:*So the question was never how to win inside. There is no inside to win. The question is what the five of us do with one locked case of the truth — which can't be filed, because the only instrument that could verify it is the instrument it indicts, and can't be published, because publishing it burns the one honest keeper the machine ever produced for nothing.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:169:She had been turning the case over since the third bell and had got it down to one sentence, and she gave him the sentence instead of a greeting, because it was the only part of the night that was any use to anybody.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:171:"Stop calling it a case." She came up onto the good foot. "It's not evidence. It's a map."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:17:Brom's quiet competence, two years aimed at acquiring dinner twice over, was aimed now at load-balancing a cart for a road with no waystations. He had the whole of it off and spread on the shelf's stone before anybody else had unrolled anything: the boxes, the kits, the tools, the grain, the case; and he went through it once with his hands and once with his eyes and then rebuilt the load with the heavy weight low and forward and the water where a man could reach it walking, and he moved four things from the cart to the panniers, and he did not explain any of it. When Lira asked him what the rule was he said, "Nothing you'll want tomorrow goes under anything you won't," and went back to the lashings. Cael entered that as a sentence worth keeping and noted that Brom, who had never in four years volunteered a general principle about anything, had produced one within a day of the road having no rules on it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:59:The anomaly he turned over the way he turned it over at every interval that mattered, which was: he opened the session-nine page, read his own fourteen-year-old handwriting, checked it against everything that had been added to the world since he last checked it, and closed it again.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:127:Cael catalogued them, because that was what he did at the end of anything that mattered, and because he had learned in five months that the only records that survive a machine are the ones kept by hand. Four people who had signed away, at a counter, in one morning, a ranking, a ladder, a certification and a career. One man who had signed away nothing because he had never signed anything, and who had left a set of keys in a row on a blotter instead. A cart. Three boxes and a case under one canvas. A horse. A valley with no name in it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:131:"I have nine things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no enrollment, no protection — and four people who signed out of the world that was supposed to provide them, and one of the nine taken from a hand the world sent to hold me, and one taken in earnest from a friend, and the machine's own maintenance records in a case under that canvas, which we can't file and can't publish and are carrying east anyway because the truth is worth carrying even when it can't be spent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:173:The door was on the latch. Seln opened it, and stood aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:Karis had seen it from the door. She had not gone to it at once; she had stood with her hands shut, as she must have stood in front of a locked press on a library's second floor, and then she had gone and opened it at the ribbon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:209:She did not shut it. She left it at the ribbon, as they had found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:245:Karis stopped with her toes against the stone and her notebook shut in both hands, and stepped over it without writing anything, which he had never seen her do at a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:347:"Then it's right," said Karis. She opened her notebook with her pale fingers and wrote the figure down. "By definition. I shall have to think about that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:423:Cael looked at him doing it. He had lain under a cart last night and reckoned that there was nobody within three days who could carry a thing west, and he had been wrong by one night and a mule. He was glad that he had left a certain door shut.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-23.md:169:The edge territories opened below.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-07.md:15:In the third session's week his own pattern had moved four times: down the bluff at an unscheduled hour with Brom, twice; the river path alone before dawn on a Fourth-day, which he had not run since the autumn; a Sixth-day at the ferry landing with Karis and two document cases, which was an itinerary a watcher would have paid for.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-07.md:77:It was not in the day's stack when the post-room sorted it; it was on the stack's top, because the post clerk had recognized the hand — the Denvash letters had been arriving at Halcenvane for two years and the clerk knew them by their upright address block and put them out first, as a courtesy to a boy he had never spoken to about anything but weather. Cael took it on the covered walk and opened it walking, which he did with Hesk's letters and nothing else, because Hesk's letters had never once in two years contained bad news and the habit had been built on the record.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-07.md:79:*The circulars say the proceedings are at Ostrand and the broadsides say you are a case, and I say a case is what a fiddle travels in, and the fiddle is what matters. Mind the fiddle.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-07.md:83:And then the third paragraph, which Cael read standing on the covered walk, and read again, and then carried at a pace the walk had not seen from him in a year to the residence, where the table was assembling for the evening and where he read it aloud at the window, because the letter had been opened there and its third paragraph could not be carried across the room unshared.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-07.md:157:"Nothing happened. That's the answer. Nothing happened *at* him." Her voice did not rise; it flattened, which he had learned was the same thing. "I did not go to his house. Nobody went to his house. Nine instruments went out from four offices on one morning, and by the second bell his guild had suspended his contract because a guild may not contract an unclassified practitioner, and the haulage yard had put another man on his run, and his bank had frozen the house's instrument because the instrument named his standing as security, and his daughter's academy had written to say her enrollment was under review because a dependent's enrollment carries the parent's standing, and his transit endorsement had lapsed, which meant he could not lawfully leave the district to argue about any of it." She looked at the charter above the file cases. "He found out in the order the post arrived. He came to my counter on the fourth day with all nine letters in a satchel and asked me which one to answer first. I told him the truth, which was that they were not nine problems. They were one problem with nine faces, and the one problem had no counter to be answered at, because the restriction is not an act. It is a *condition.* There is nobody to argue with. That is the design."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:29:"You're a *case* now," Ephram observed, reading the day's sack at the residence table with the cohort captain's file forgotten under his elbow. "At Norhold you were a story. Stories end. Cases get *cited*."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:31:"I've been a case since Denvash."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:33:"You've been a *file* since Denvash. Files are private." Ephram held up a broadside from a city Cael had never seen. "This is a case. Three faculties are teaching you. Somebody at the coastal academy has written a thesis." He set it down. "Try to be a disappointing precedent."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:67:The gallery was fuller. The correspondents had multiplied and the registry-literate had brought friends, and the room had the specific density of a public watching a case it had already decided in print. The seal sat as it had sat: a competent official of no age, conducting. The counsel entered the response's exhibits in sequence, each read into the minute by the clerk, each received without expression — Prynn's index, Gault's panel minutes, the inspection finding, forty pages on the integrity provisions — and Cael charted the seal through every one and got nothing, and entered the nothing that night as the session's finding: *the seat received eleven pounds of the truth exactly as it received the staffing note the clerk read before it. The instrument doesn't weigh. It receives.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:75:At the fortieth minute Cael stopped charting the seal and started charting the gallery, and found that a third of it had stopped watching. Not from hostility. From the ordinary attrition of people who had come to see a case and were being read a list. The correspondents wrote nothing for nine minutes together. He entered it that night, and it was the coldest line the session produced: *the truth is admissible, unopposed, and boring. They have built a room in which our best work is a procedural delay.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:89:The first was public. It came to Halcenvane as a courtesy copy, already filed — a statement to the proceedings, submitted at the registry seat under the filer's own name, rank, and academy, which the code permitted any practitioner of Silver or above to do in an institutional matter and which, Karis established within the hour, no practitioner of Gold had ever done in the registry's recorded history. It came up the bluff road on an ordinary evening in the ordinary post, in a registry-seat envelope that Bracken opened at the counter, read, read again, and then did the only unprofessional thing Cael ever saw him do in three years, which was to leave the counter unattended and walk the sheet to the residence himself at something faster than his own pace.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-05.md:3:The courtesy copy reached the corner office on the third floor of the Compact's western administration in the ordinary morning file, between a stations-office staffing note and a certification dispute from the coastal district, and Vastin knew what it was before he opened it, because he had been expecting it since a chamber with no windows had entered a question for formal proceedings and set his testimony aside with courtesy.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-05.md:117:Withrow received it first, in her office, from the counsel, and her response was entered in the log that night because Cael was present for it: she listened to the two sentences, and asked to hear them a second time, and then said, in the registry-official's register, "Not contemplated by the schedule. I have written that sentence. Every registry officer of my grade has written that sentence." She looked at the charter framed above the file cases. "I used to think it was the most honest thing the registry did — declining input it didn't need, in plain words, without pretending to have considered it. I'm now going to have to think about what it means that I taught it to junior officers as a kindness." To Cael: "Go and tell the table. And tell them I said the word again. *Choosing.*"
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-05.md:123:"I want to be precise about what I have and have not told you. I have not told you that the Archmarshal is on your side. He is not. He is an officer of the Compact who petitioned an office of the Compact under the Compact's own provisions and was refused by it, and every one of those words is the same institution." She drew the glove tight. "What I have told you is that the refusal was two sentences with no provision cited. In twenty-six years I have been refused perhaps two hundred times and I have been refused *without a citation* four times, and all four were offices that had already decided and did not wish to write down the ground they had decided on." She took up the case. "Write the citation down when you get one. Its absence is a finding."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-05.md:139:*He's the Compact's best evaluator. He put the instrument to me himself, in a room, for a day, and wrote down what it returned. He asked to tell the proceedings what he found and the proceedings told him, in his own grammar, that he wasn't on the schedule. Note the shape: they didn't dispute his finding. They declined to contemplate it. That's not an argument lost. That's an instrument put back in the case unread.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:5:Cael watched the courier take the switchbacks the way he had watched a courier take them a year and a half ago with a roster in his saddlebag, and catalogued the difference: not one rider; three, because a filing of that weight traveled in two cases, and the academy had hired the escort itself. The registry's ordinary post would have carried it. Withrow had declined it. "When we answer under seal," she had said, at the counter, "we answer under escort. Let them see the weight arrive."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:31:The coach took the bluff road at first light with all five of them in it, and the ride down was the term's first council conducted in the register the delegation had perfected on a season of wagon benches: nobody saying the thing, everybody circling it. Lira had the Silver drill-book and did not open it. Brom sat with his forearms on his knees, which was how he sat toward things. Karis had the response's summary in her lap and reread it the way a fighter rereads a scouting sheet, and at the ferry she said, to the river, "He'll concede it. Whoever he is. He'll concede the whole filing in the first minute and I want everyone braced for what that feels like, because it will feel like winning and it isn't." Seln, at the coach's end, had the office's cross-index of the instrument on his knee — forty pages, every clause mapped to every provision it invoked and every provision to its history, produced in three nights, the best document about the instrument that existed — and he said nothing at all, and Cael, who had watched him say nothing for two years, read the nothing as a man who had cross-indexed instruments like this one before, from the other side, and knew what a conceded case was for.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:57:The counsel's own read came in the coach on the way back, unprompted, delivered to the window in the voice of an advocate revising twenty-six years of assumptions in real time. "He's not there to argue. I've faced men sent to argue — they have a tell, a place they lean, a clause they love. He has none. He's there to *conduct* — to move the petition through the schedule at the code's pace and let the seat do what the seat was constituted to do." She turned from the window. "I have never lost a case to a man who didn't want to win it. I'm not certain I've ever faced one."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:65:*This one's been sent to win, and the difference is worse. There's no appetite to read. He complimented Karis honestly and conceded our whole case without a flicker and it cost him nothing, because none of it was ever his. He's a hand. Somebody else is the arm. And the arm has learned, somewhere in the last four years, that the way to beat a boy who reads people is to send someone with nothing on him to read.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:67:The response's drafting had its own scene, and the scene was the annex — Karis's room with the lock, the four days, the door shut and the assistant at the outer table filtering the intake's curiosity, and Cael reading every draft at the room's one window because the arrangement required it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:97:He gave it to her in small pieces, at the rail, with the valley going grey: the six steps, the doors that opened one way, the procedure for no-bin, the working definition of asset-restriction — Withrow's version, the one about every door on the continent closing at once by paperwork. Lira listened the way she listened to scouting sheets, three reads, and when he was done she looked at the river a while.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:109:Withrow's stake was the part of the term nobody had written down yet, and she wrote it herself, in her office, on the evening after the conference, with the door shut and the enrollee across the desk and the charter framed above the file cases where it had hung for two years to be pointed at.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:117:She got up while she said the next of it, and went to the file cases under the charter, and came back with the academy's charter file itself — the buff folder every chartered institution kept and no enrollee had ever seen — and opened it on the desk between them, facing him, which was not a courtesy she was obliged to extend and which he catalogued as one of the four or five things she had ever done that he could not have predicted.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-17.md:49:Cael catalogued the office while they waited, because there was nothing else to do with the forty minutes and because he had never in his life been in a room he did not itemize. It was somebody's working room and had been left mid-year: a desk with a blotter worn through at the left elbow, which meant a left-handed occupant; two chairs that did not match; a shelf of lease abstracts in a district binding he recognized from Karis's crates; a stove, cold, with last winter's ash still in it; a wall calendar four months out of date with three days ringed in ink and no note against any of them. The window's shutter had a splintered slat that let a bar of the street's lamp in across the floorboards. There was a water jug and no cup.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-17.md:83:The lamp did its small work. In the courtyard below, a gate was opened and shut, and a man crossed it whistling four bars of something and was gone. Somewhere in the building a door closed, far off, and then, some minutes later, closed again. The building's fourth-floor tenant walked the length of his own floor twice. A cart went down the street and the sound of it took a long time to arrive and a long time to leave.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-17.md:119:**And he would no longer lend the machine his hands.** "The proceedings have asked me to certify the re-evaluation protocol's applicability to the enrollee. It is the last signature the corridor wants — the best evaluator's name, on the record, saying the standard protocol is the correct instrument for this subject, so that what follows looks like an evaluation instead of a delivery. I received the request the week my petition for standing was refused." Something moved at the corner of his mouth and was not a smile. "The same office that would not hear my finding would very much like my signature. They will not have it. They will have to proceed without my name on it, and every registry-literate person on the continent will read the silence where the name should be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:25:They had the door of the old lumber room propped open with a broken chair, and they were working the table through it on its side, sweating and grumbling at it in the low courteous voices porters used on the library stair. Behind them the room itself stood half empty. It had held, for as long as Cael had known it, the house's spare benches and a map case with no maps in it and two rolled carpets nobody would admit to owning. Now the carpets were out on the landing and the benches gone, and somebody had already swept the floor in long straight strokes and left the broom against the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He read it at his own window that night, with the fountain going in the court and the copy of the instrument shut in its folder on the bed behind him. Across the top of the grid ran the columns: the sitting, the panel, the day, the finding, the seal. Down the side ran the three times his place on the bluff had been written down by somebody obliged to write it. The grant at Greyvane, from the hearing. The first renewal, at Halcenvane, in the inspection year, before the Compact's delegation. The renewal at the mill town, last year, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:He went back to the table on the sill. Its first renewal's row gave one day, the day of the trestle, which was the day the house's own enrollment book carried in Bracken's small upright hand, because that was the day the house had seen it happen. The registry's file, wherever it sat, would carry the other day, the day its own copy was sealed. The house had never kept a copy of that one. It had gone back up the road the same afternoon. The only place on the continent where both days stood side by side was a book he had filled himself and shut in a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:173:He opened the old Log on the table beside her elbow at the evaluation day, and laid his finger beside its last word without touching it, and then turned forward to the two short lines and did the same. She read both, the way she read anything with a date in it, once for the words and once for the numbers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:187:She went on ruling. Every row this time gave two days, the trestle and the seal, and the first renewal's row gave the reason for the gap in a single line. Under the grid she wrote where each day could be found: the house's enrollment book for the trestle; the registry's own file, to be produced on request, for the seals; and for the gap, *the enrollee's contemporaneous record, available to the seat*. Then she reached across the table for the instrument, opened it at schedule two, and laid it beside the old table she had just taken out.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:5:The assessment office opened at the first bell, as it opened at the first bell six days a week, and the morning clerk came in with the key already in her hand and the day's first grievance already composed, which was that the coal had not been brought up. She got four steps into the room before the composition stopped.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:21:The clerk stood in the middle of the office for a while with her coat still on. Then she did the only sensible thing available to a person of her grade, which was to work — she opened the counter at the second bell and assessed nine enrollees' term returns against a completed rank that needed no assessing, and answered a question about a floor schedule that was already posted, and was asked twice before midday where the assessment clerk was, and said "out" twice, which was not a lie and became one at some point in the afternoon without her being able to say when.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:25:The office did what offices do. It waited a day, because a clerk of fifteen years' impeccable service is owed a day and because the alternative was to file something about a man who had probably had a tooth pulled. It asked around — the residence, where his room proved to contain a made bed, no case, and nothing personal whatever, which took the residence porter some time to describe because there was nothing to describe; the gate lodge, which had no record of him going out because the gate lodge did not open its book until the first bell and he had gone before it; the coaching floor, where Rooke was asked and said, "Not my staff," and went on with his morning. And it learned, because everyone by then had learned, that the Halcenvane delegation had resigned in good standing and gone east the previous afternoon — and that the assessment clerk was not among the four names on the filed resignations, because the assessment clerk was staff and not enrollee, and had filed nothing at all.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:51:He had left in the dark of the resignation morning, before the counter opened, because a keeper does not travel in the party he is protecting and a man who has spent nineteen postings arriving early does not stop at the last one.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:53:He took the east road out of the province at a pace calculated to put him at the crossroads a full day before the coach — not hurrying, because hurrying is visible; walking the way a middle-aged clerk walks when he has somewhere ordinary to be, with a case on his shoulder that had come through nineteen postings and had never once been out of arm's reach in any of them.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:63:For the first time since a first posting nineteen years and a life ago, there was no desk to file it at, no chain to file it up, no cover it protected, no self it maintained. There was a road, and a case, and a man walking east toward the first honest employment of his adult life. He let the report dissolve where it stood. The dissolving felt like nothing he had a word for, and he did not reach for the word, because reaching for words about himself was a habit he had never had and was too old to acquire.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:65:He thought, walking, about the case on his shoulder, and thought about it professionally, which was the only way he had ever thought about anything.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:83:The machine's paperwork ran its own week, faceless throughout, and closed everything it had opened.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:143:*Four files closed complete at a counter, and one man who filed nothing, and squared his keys, and walked east in the dark, and stopped once to look back and didn't know how. The office found the empty desk this morning and started, a day late, to wonder what he kept. Let them wonder. It's four days east of anywhere they can execute a wondering, and the case is under the same canvas as Karis's boxes, and tomorrow we cross the last thing the registry drew on a map.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:147:Then he walked out to the load-line of the cart, where the two of them had ended up stacked together against the night's cold at the edge of the firelight: Seln's case and Karis's three boxes, under the same canvas, roped to the same rail. Two hundred years of the machine's own maintenance record, and four years of the best documentary architecture on the continent — the operative's archive and the researcher's — riding out of the registry's world on one axle. Neither of them able to be filed. Neither of them meant to be. He put his hand flat on the canvas to check the ropes, which did not need checking, and left it there a moment, and went back to the fire. Both of them simply carried now, east, past the reach of every office that would have wanted them both.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-13.md:25:"How long have they been here?" Karis said, coming out after him with the case still in her hand and setting it down on the step, which was the only concession to hurry she made all morning.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-13.md:61:**First exchange.** She let him come. That was the whole of it, and it took twelve seconds and taught him more than the next thirty: he moved off the crown toward her at an angle that opened the road's northern shoulder behind him, and she did not close the shoulder, and did not step, and did not declare, and the shoulder shut anyway — a new point blooming at the culvert's mouth, twelve feet behind his left heel, laid there while he was still committing to the step that made it worth laying. Her lattice was not an attack. It was *subtraction*: exits priced and then removed, one at a time, at the rate he generated them. He arrived at fifteen feet from her with the western half of the cut no longer available and understood that he had bought the distance with the only thing he had that she wanted, which was ground.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-13.md:110:Seln came out from under the southern bank at some point in the following minute without having been visible in the intervening one, brushing stone dust off a coat sleeve that had been opened four inches along the forearm. He looked at the slack course's residue on the road for rather a long time. He said one thing about it, and said it flat, the way he handed a form back across a counter.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-13.md:130:The sitting closed at the fourth bell. The seal thanked counsel. Jent gathered his four documents and put them in a case and said, passing the respondent's table, "Roads are poor this month," in the tone of a man remarking on roads, and went out — and Karis wrote that down too, afterward, in the register with no seal on it, with a note beneath it in her own hand: *insufficient. He says twenty things a day of exactly that weight. Recorded, not weighed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:He noticed that before anything else, coming in fourth, as he had come in fourth on the night the case was opened. On that night every sheet in the room had been stacked and weighted and ready, and on his birthday every sheet had been turned on its face, because the captain was coming. Tonight the cloth lay bare from end to end. The shelves behind it had gaps in them the width of grey boxes, and the only paper in the whole room was three slips in front of Karis and the book he had brought himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:9:Karis had the table's end. Lira sat on the bench with her back to the wall and one boot up, and Brom was in the good chair from the dairy, which had somehow followed them up four flights months ago and never gone down. Seln came last, on the tenth bell, and shut the door behind him without a sound. He had his post satchel over his shoulder, and when he sat he did not hang it on the chair but set it on the floor between his feet, upright, and Cael heard the weight of what was in it meet the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:11:So the case would leave the room tonight on Seln, and nobody remarked on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:21:He had Hesk's book open on the table at the leaf he had ruled on the night of the first sitting: a column, and four names at its head. There were lines under three of them now, in his cramped hand, a year of what the case had cost each of them, entered as it fell due. Under the fourth name, which was his own, the column was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:65:"I'm arguing. Let me." Karis held up a finger. "It needn't be the convening. There's the Archmarshal's office. He has read it. He stood at a window in a borrowed room and said what it was, and he has since refused the protocol his name, and a finding from him, with the case behind it, is a thing no seat could set aside as a forgery. And if not him, there's the press. There's a man in the market street who bought a second press this year on the strength of our name, and a Gold at Auremont who has already told the registry in public to classify its instruments. Forty families could be told what was done to their children. It's true, and it's proved, and in my hands it's dated." Her voice had risen a little. "I have never in my life held a true thing and not filed it. I don't know how. Everything I am says that you put it in front of somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:69:It was the best case there was, and some part of Cael, with the pen in his hand, had leaned toward it while she spoke. It was the old pole, the one he had written in his best hand on the first night: *The system has text, and text has held me.* The most honest reader he knew had just told him, in her closing voice, that there was one more text.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:"Then I'm found in a week," said Seln. "That is not a complaint, only the arithmetic. There is one leaf in that case that is not a copy. Somebody will ask who could have stood in a district's top-floor records room for five weeks, twelve years ago, and the service keeps its posting rolls very well. They would have me by the Seventh-day, and they would ask me what else I kept." He looked at the satchel between his feet. "After that it is no longer forty children and a leaf two hundred years old. It is an officer of the monitoring service who stole from a press, and the broadsides would print that instead. It is a better story, and it is also true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:121:Karis shut her eyes, and opened them, and went and unlocked the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:189:She took the carter's book out of her coat. He had seen it perhaps three times in the term: stitched, in oilcloth, with a pencil in the spine. She opened it near the back and laid it on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"No. That's the bill. Here's the other side, because you asked for full price and that means both columns." She shut the book. "Nobody ever built anything to my height before. Fenmark had the form right and the wrong room. I walked out of there on my own feet and I've been proud of it ever since, and I thought that was all I'd ever want from a room: to be let leave it standing up. Then somebody built me a ladder, and I found out I wanted to climb. I said so, and you wrote it down. *Admit.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:267:They heard two men on the stair, one heavy and one quick, and the door opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:339:Lira went to the door and opened it. Ephram was standing at the head of the stair with his back to her, looking down four flights into the dark, with the green file under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:326:The cold term, before the midyear recess [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: season words struck; audit A1]. Eighteen, eight fragments, the doorways quiet. The case has been down from its shelf for days. Five people will come to a stone room two floors below the library by three routes across an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:335:- **Two Hundred Years** — Karis's four findings, each landing on a carried thread: the mechanism and **reach, not strength**; the flag as the corrections function failing safe — *"She just never knew what was answering."* (Ilsev and Havel never told, in eleven words); **the strata** — [UNBOUND] said **once**, here only [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: this is the series' second voicing; B4 ch50 voiced the older word once, lower-case, at Halcenvane (Karis, one factual clause), and B5 carries it read-and-closed; this chapter builds on ch50 and does not discover it; audit B5]; *close the door, remove the exit, delete the name*; **the limit, verbatim**; Lira: "They *found* it… It isn't a long time compared to whatever's under it." (a Book 9 plant); the anomaly checked — *Not this either. Still open.*; the stratum note paid (re-evaluation as delivery mechanism); Seln: "A convenience cannot." It can't be filed. "It isn't broken. It's maintained." Lira on the stair: **"It's not evidence. It's a map."**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:372:Rooke stood back and looked at the board for a moment with the chalk in his hand. Then he put the chalk in his pocket and went in to the program floor and shut the door behind him, and Cael heard the bolt go home on the inside, which it never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:392:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:424:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been squared to the table's edge and turned over so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid flat with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:442:"I shut grievances," he said. "There was one waiting. A first-year with a complaint about the meal rota. I told him to come back next Third-day with two complaints and I'd hear them both at once." He looked faintly appalled at himself. "I've never shut it early. Not once in two years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:448:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:486:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:492:He sang it wrong. He had picked up the tune from the verses before and he came in at the end with great confidence and got the third line of it wrong, cheerfully, all on one note, and Brom stopped singing and opened his eyes and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:496:"The first-years," said Ephram. "Somebody on the ground floor has been singing those three lines under the residence windows since the night before the year opened. He's never once got the third line right. I thought it went like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:522:Then Ephram took the captain's file off his knees and opened it, without turning round, and took out three sheets of close-ruled paper, and held them up over his shoulder without looking, so that Cael could take them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:532:Cael gave him the sheets back. Ephram put them in the file and shut it and folded his hands on top of it again, and looked at the window, and did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:536:He opened Hesk's parcel last, when the cake was eaten and the kettle had been filled again and had once again refused to boil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:540:It was made of brown leather, the good thick kind that Hesk bought for instrument cases and would not use for anything that did not deserve it. It was plain. It had a flap that came down over the front and a strap long enough to go across a man's body and rest on the opposite hip, so that it would not swing when he walked, or ran. The buckle on the strap was brass, and it was not a bought buckle. Cael knew it at once. He had watched Hesk file buckles at the long bench since he was small enough to sleep in the shavings under it, and this one had Hesk's file marks on the inside of the tongue where nobody would see them, and a little bevel on the frame that Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:588:He took the book out of its pocket and opened it to a clean leaf and wrote the inventory, because a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:606:He opened the door. Seln stood in the corridor in his coat, with his hands at his sides and nothing in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:618:Cael shut the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:620:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
