editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:597:**Style is one of the most settled unprotected categories in the caselaw.** Sentence-length
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:965:Not all of it. The wing's other sealed things still stood there in their row, a box of old sigil cards and two bound runs of the wing's returns and a tin of sealing wafers. But at the end of the row, where the case had stood for two years, there was only a clean oblong of wood, a shade paler than the wood round it, with a soft grey edge of dust on three sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:979:Seln had said it once, in Cael's room, with the case on the sill and the rain on the glass. *Read your own line. The hour is when you come up the stair.* And then, as the boy was writing on the back of his hand: *Come the way you always come. A man who changes his road is a man who has a reason.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1009:The top landing had one lamp on a bracket, and the annex door stood shut across it. The reading room's door had Bracken's card in its brass frame at eye height. *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1041:The lecture range's bell gave the eighth hour, far off across the court, muffled by the shutters. Before the last stroke had died there was a single knock at the door, not loud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1049:Then he came in and Karis locked the door behind him and hung the key back inside her collar. Seln went to the middle of the table, on the far side, where he always sat. He did not sit. He unbuttoned his coat and took out the case and set it down on the bare cloth in the circle of the shaded lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1053:He took the key from his waistcoat pocket, a small brass key on a loop of black tape. He put it in the lock and turned it, and the lock opened with a sound like a dry twig, and he lifted the lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1119:He had been kept by that sentence. He knew it as surely as he knew the ribs. Two years of true, empty weeks had gone up out of the wing with his name on the outside fold, and every one of them had said that nothing had happened. That sentence had stood between him and the people who read returns. And here was the same sentence, word for word, clipped to the back of a girl from a river town. In her case it said that the correction had held. Nothing to report. She had gone home smaller and stayed there, and nine weeks of somebody's careful watching had found nothing in her worth writing down, because the thing worth writing down had been taken off her paper before she was old enough to know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1161:The lamp burned. Across the court, very faint through the shutters and four floors down, somebody in the residence was laughing at something, the long helpless laugh of a first-year who has been told a joke after the bell and cannot stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1167:She looked at the case, open at the end of the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1179:"That is the only letter," he said. "In forty. I copied it because it was the only one. I have read it more often than anything else in the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1195:The bundle opened on the cloth like a book with no spine. It was reports, a few sheets each, folded once lengthwise in the field service's way, the way a man folds a paper to carry it inside his coat on a horse. The hands varied, because Seln had copied each in the shape of its writer as he had copied the father's letter, and Cael could see a hurried man and a careful one, a man who pressed hard and one who barely touched the page. Every one of them was an evaluator writing from somewhere that was not a desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1273:The leaf was last, as it had been last out of the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1277:"Everything else is my hand," he said. "The orders and the records and the reports I copied, at night, over five weeks. The memoranda were spares. The press held three or four of every issue and nobody had counted them in a generation, so I took one of each. This is the only thing in the case that someone else made." He took his finger off it. "A copy of it would have been worth nothing. Everything it says is in what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1321:He did not sit down to say it, and he did not raise his voice. He stood at the middle of the table's far side with the whole of the case laid out in front of him in the lamplight, the forty and the bundle and the two stacks and the leaf, and he said it to the room the way he had once read a form to a man in a doorway at the third hour, one plain sentence after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1349:"My fourth posting, twelve years ago." Seln put one hand back on the table, flat, as if the wood steadied him. "A district seat in an old building, a records room on its top floor with presses round the walls, the kind with doors. I was looking for a subject's travel endorsements. They were filed where they should not have been, so I opened presses until I found them, and in the fourth press I found these." He moved his eyes over the rows. "The orders were in bound runs, a few years to a volume, with the records bundled behind them, and the memoranda on the bottom shelf in a box. And at the back of the box, loose, under the oldest, that." He did not look at the leaf. "Nobody had opened the press in years; the dust said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1353:"Over five weeks, at night. The posting gave me the room's key because I was a junior who stayed late, and juniors who stay late are trusted with keys." He said it without any pride at all. "One lamp on the floor, so the window stayed dark, and a few sheets a night in the lining of my coat. The case I bought afterward, in another town, from a man who made them for surveyors. I never told him what it was for, and he never asked, and he put his stamp inside the lid because he was proud of his work." He paused. "I did not understand it, and I want that said. I copied it because it was paper that sat a little above where it should have sat, and I had been trained to notice that, and I did not know what to do with what I noticed except keep it. For a year I thought it was a district's bad habit, a careless office."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1373:"Two years at that counter, and 'Mm,' and your cup turned a quarter on every table. Floor schedules on three doors in that hand, every week, so neat nobody's ever read one twice. Pleasant to every first-year who couldn't fill in a sheet, and to every Magister who wanted a room, and to me, the first week, when I didn't know where the east hall was and you walked me half across the court to show me." She took her chin off her fist. "With that on a shelf over your head the whole time, behind you, where you could feel it." She was quiet a moment. "I'd like to know what that cost, being pleasant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1395:It was past the ninth bell. The lamp in the window would be wanted out within the hour or the valley would notice a difference, and the case had to be somewhere before it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1403:He chose the place himself. He walked along Karis's shelves until he came to the row of her grey document boxes on the second shelf from the floor, the ones with her initials on their spines, which held her Greyvane papers and had not been moved in a year. Two of them stood apart by about the width of a man's hand. He measured the gap with his eyes and then with the case, and it went in between them and stood there, dark leather between grey card, a little shorter than its neighbours. From the door it looked like one more box nobody had labelled yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1405:"The room has your key," he said to Karis. "The case has mine." He touched his waistcoat pocket. "Two keys, and neither of us opens it alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1411:They put it back in the order it had come out, and it went back faster than it had come out, because Seln did it and nobody else touched it. The leaf went into its sleeve and the sleeve into the lid's pocket. The memoranda went in, oldest at the bottom. The bundle, tied again in the wing's knot. The forty, row by row, squared. The lid closed and the small key turned with its dry sound, and Seln carried the case to its place between the grey boxes and stood it there and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1443:He understood that only as he opened his mouth to start, sitting on the bench in the circle of the shaded lamp with Seln across the bare cloth from him. Lira had learned it in a yard at Ardenmere with grit in her palms, one piece at a time, because she had been there when each piece happened. Brom had learned it from Lira and from his own three mornings in an alcove, and had asked the rest across a table, and been told. Karis had come to it at Greyvane with most of it already worked out on paper in her own hand, and had asked only for the parts she could not get by reading. Each of the three had it whole now. Not one of them had ever heard it in order, start to finish, in one telling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1541:"What you have just told me, all of it, in order. Twice more, if there is ever a need, and you will know if there is, but not a third time." He said it as he would have said a rule of the wing to a first-year who had asked about floor hours. "Every telling is a copy, and a thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known. I have kept the case twelve years by never once describing it to anybody, and you will keep this by telling it as seldom as you can bear to." He paused. "I am not ungrateful. I am only telling you the price, because nobody told me mine until I had paid it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1571:Cael went on, because there was nothing else to do. He told the rule counted on five fingers against Brom's coat: earnest, a man spending everything he had in the light and meaning every word; his, begun by him, before Cael knew there was anything to begin; close, one flight; his work, the wing's officer doing the wing's work; and awake, the little finger. He told how he had not done it the way he did a burst, all at once before thinking could catch up, but slowly, on purpose, on the cold stone, knowing exactly what he did and to whom. And the quiet coming down over him like a heavy door swung shut, the stair's sounds gone, the light gone flat, and three seconds or four of the night going on above him without him in it. And then the notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1598:"The night before the final," said Cael. "In a back room on the harbour, with the shutter open a crack. We'd costed everything I was going to spend the next day: the Wind past six, the push at fighting load, the give, the spark. Everything that had been held back for two years was going to go out in one afternoon, in front of the whole continent, and be on the record for good." He looked at Lira. "And I said the quiet thing stayed sealed, and Lira said to cost the seal, out loud, and say why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1644:He opened it on the cloth to the leaf after the birthday inventory, and uncapped Hesk's steel pen, and wrote it there in front of all of them, in the smallest plain hand he had, with the shaded lamp drawn close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1664:Karis stayed, because she would turn the lamp in the window down at its usual hour and not a minute before. Brom went first, by the iron stair through the stacks, and Cael heard the ninth tread on the second flight say what Brom had said it would. Seln went second, by the bindery stair, and did not say goodnight. At the door he stopped and looked back at the shelf, at the dark leather case standing between two of Karis's grey boxes like one more box with no label, and then at Cael, and then he went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1692:He had written everything for years. Every notice word for word, every price. The book was where a thing went to be true. And Seln had sat across the table and asked where it was written, and when he had answered, Seln had said *Under the bed*. In a voice that cost nothing, he had said, *A thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known.* The case had been kept twelve years by a man who had never once described it to anybody. Cael did not think he would last twelve hours if he started writing it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1696:*Day 88. Five in Karis's room by three stairs across an hour. The case opened. What was in it does not go into a book. It is in a room with two keys, and the five of us have it, and that is where it will stay.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1758:He came out of the infirmary with the linen triangle folded small in his good hand and his left arm hanging at his side, and stood in the covered walk opening and closing the left hand very slowly, the way a man tries a hinge that has been painted shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1760:"A week of nothing heavy," he said, "and nothing over my head. She says I'm to let it remember where it lives." He opened the hand again and looked at it. "It's forgotten. You'd think it would remember a thing like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1780:"I'm not asking to be difficult," she said. "I'm asking because I sat on that bench last night and listened to you all read forty of them out, and I've been doing my drills in my head all day, and I can't make them go." She looked at the coats. "That seat down there runs on it, and so does the evaluation. Every sitting we go to, we're sitting in the room and answering it, and it's the thing in the case. They want to read you again. We know now what reads you." She pulled her coat closer. "My carters said the stamps stop four days east, loaded, or three days light. I've been carrying that in my pocket since the dairy. We could be past the last station by the Seventh-day after next if we left on the Seventh-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1888:The five of them sat in the reading room under the shaded lamp, with the case on its shelf between the grey boxes and the lamp in the window turned up as high as every night, and Karis laid it out for them on the bare cloth in the order she had found it. She had the code's second volume open on the left and the old enrollment book open on the right, and a single sheet of her own between them with five lines on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1924:"One." Karis looked at him. "It opens on one day, the day of the ruling, and stays open until the revocation takes effect, and then it shuts. And it only opens at all if we lose. If the seat rules for the house, there's no door, because there's nothing to resign from." She sat back. "Until that day there's only the corridor: every sitting attended, every form filed on time, every bout in wing three at the band, and every first-year at that counter handing in a certificate, and you standing behind her in the queue with your face doing nothing." She was quiet a moment. "Perfectly, until the ruling. If we put one foot wrong before it, the door we'd use afterward turns into the word on four files."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1950:She had the bench by the rail, with Rooke's red drill-book on her knee for something to hold and her right foot out straight on the boards. Karis had chalk and her grey notebook and her coat buttoned right for once. Brom had gone up the gallery stair and drawn every shutter down the length of the north wall, as Lira had drawn them on the first honest floor of the year. Now he stood in the hall's great doorway, with one of the two tall doors standing open a hand's breadth onto the dark of the passage and his right hand on its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1954:Cael walked out into the middle of the floor and stood there, and shut his eyes, and let it look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1964:Not lines anybody could see. He could not have said where they were with his eyes open. But with his eyes shut he could feel the hall hung together by them, foot to foot and hook to beam and leg to leg. It was like a scaffold built round a house that has not been built yet, every rest in the room joined to every other rest. It was not a picture of the room. It was a picture of every place in the room where a thing could be held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1972:"Lira," he said, with his eyes shut. "You're sitting with more weight on the left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1984:"Don't," she said. "That's cheating. You can't read my feet with your eyes shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:1992:Brom moved his weight in the doorway. Cael felt it before he heard it: a rest lifting, and another place on the boards, half a pace to the left, where a foot was about to come down, lit faintly a breath before the foot came. He opened his eyes in time to see Brom's boot come down there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2064:At twelve feet she lit the seventh and the eighth, and the asking was very faint, like somebody speaking in the next room with the door shut. At fourteen she drew the line and did not light it, and asked him, and there was nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2096:Karis wiped the chalk lines off the boards with her handkerchief, all four of them, so that Rooke's program would find the floor as it had left it. Brom went up the gallery stair and opened every shutter again down the length of the north wall. Cael put out the lanterns one after the other and stood a moment in the dark of the hall, where the new thing asked about nothing. He had told it what everything was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2116:Nobody saw her work; that was the arrangement. The assistant went home at the sixth bell every evening with a story about glue, and believed it, and told Ephram, who told the refectory, so that by the Fourth-day half the hill knew that the researcher at the top of the library was mending a founding volume with something that smelled, and kept away. The lamp burned in the window at its usual height every night and went out at its usual hour. Behind it, at the far end of the long table under the brown-paper shade, Karis sat with the case open and the two keys in the room, Seln's for an hour at a time while he sat silent at the middle of the bench, and laid the whole of it out again, and again, and again, until it would lie in the order she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2126:The case stood shut between the grey boxes on its shelf. The long table was bare again to its faded green cloth. In the middle of the cloth, under the shaded lamp, lay four slips of paper folded once and numbered on the outside in her hand, one to four, in a row. A little apart from them lay a fifth, face down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2130:"Four findings," she said, "and one limit. I'm going to give you them in the order I found them, which is the order they stand on each other in. I'm not going past the case. If anybody asks me to go past it, I'll stop, and I'll stop for good, and I mean that." She looked round at them. "Not because I'm cross, but because the minute I guess at something in front of this paper, the paper's no use to anybody, including me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2152:*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness.* He had the letter by heart; it lived inside the back board of Hesk's book, in the satchel across his chest. He had never told Karis its words. She had come to the same sentence from the far side of a case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2166:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2202:The second slip was longer, and she held it a while before she opened it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2220:"Middle." She touched the leaf's place on the table, though the leaf was in its sleeve in the case. "Read it again in your head: *The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows.* It goes out of its way to say it didn't make the reading. It's a note written by somebody who added something to a thing that was already there, and wanted it understood which part was his." She looked at Brom. "Think of your father's forge in the shed past the gardens, and the mill that must have stood on the estate's stream. If a millwright wants a wheel, he doesn't dig the river, because the river's there already. He cuts a race beside it, and puts his wheel in the race, and spends the rest of his life keeping the race clear of weed. That's maintenance, and that's what this leaf is: a millwright's note about a race. It doesn't say a word about who dug the river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2228:"Under the policy there's something older: the reading, whatever it is that wakes behind your breastbone at Kindling and reads you. The function sits on it. It's older than the function by however long, and the case doesn't say how long, and I don't know." She laid her hand flat. "But the case does show me one thing about what was there before the function came, because the first memoranda had to clear it out of the way. The first three issues, about eleven years apart, in the oldest paper. And they're not about the forty. They're about the old custom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2236:"That was the old way," she said. "Before the policy. An assessor who met a reading that went across many families could write that, pending further study, and the person went on with his life under that word, and nobody corrected anything. It was a door the old system left open. And the first three memoranda shut it, in order. Each of them cites an article of the directive by its number, the same directive I sat over at Halcenvane." She counted on her fingers, slowly, one, two, three. "The first: *close the door.* No assessor may designate by judgment of what a person shows; a station enters what the reading gives, and only that. The second: *remove the exit.* The provisional custom, pending further study, is struck; there is to be no designation that waits on study. The third: *delete the name.* The old word is discontinued, and every person who carried it is to be re-entered under a flag."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2246:"And that's where the case ends," she said. "So here is the limit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2254:"I can tell you when the policy began, near enough, and that it was a policy, and that it's been kept since by forms. I can tell you that the oldest thing it did was shut a door the old way had left open, and take the name off the people who'd used it. I can't tell you what the old word was for, before. I can't tell you what the reading is, underneath the function, or how old, or how it came to be there to have a race cut beside it. Nothing in that case reaches down that far, not a line." She put the slip on top of the first one. "We've explained the middle of the story. Not the beginning. Entered as a limit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2272:"I went through everything for the anomaly," she said to Cael, "for session nine. You asked me to, at Greyvane, and I've never forgotten that you asked." She did not look at the slip. "Anything in the forty that read across a family with nobody of that family near. Anything in the reports about a practitioner who took something from no one. Any memorandum that mentions Tide by name, or a reading at a distance, or one that came without an engagement. Anything in the leaf." She shook her head. "Nothing. Not one line in it touches session nine. The function corrects what the reading shows at Kindling. Your session nine wasn't a Kindling, and the reading didn't show it to anybody, and nobody corrected it. Whatever it is, the case didn't do it and doesn't know about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2276:He opened it at the table, under the shaded lamp, at the clean leaf after Seln's terms, and wrote it there with all of them watching, because he wanted them to see it written. He had kept that one honest for three years and he was going to go on keeping it honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2288:For the fourth she did not unfold the slip at all. She took the grey notebook out of her coat instead and opened it at the inside of its front cover, where a few lines had gathered over two years that belonged to no case: the fielding clause, the squad rule, the charter's slip, Jent's answer on its plain card, and above them all, in her small hand, the line from the first night of the year. Cael knew it before she put her finger under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2296:"Now I've got it." She put her finger on the line. "The petition asks for one thing, compelled re-evaluation under standard protocol. It doesn't care about the provision or the house or the clause; it wants him in front of a reading again." She looked at the case on its shelf. "A re-evaluation is an evaluation. The memorandum covers every evaluation at which a reading is entered. If the reading reads him this time, older, with eight things in him instead of none, the function is waiting beside it with its advice, and the advice is entered, and the order comes down, and he's a column, whatever column it chooses: one family, first rank, margin not to be entered." Her voice was very steady. "And if the reading goes out again, the way it went out at Denvash, then there's no valid classification, and it's chapter fourteen, in sequence: nullification, suspension, restriction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2330:Nobody answered him. Karis looked at him across the table for a long moment. Then she closed the notebook on its cover, and gathered up the four slips and gave them to Seln, who unlocked the case on its shelf and put them in the lid's pocket beside the leaf and locked it again. The fifth slip she kept face down, without ever turning it over, and put it in her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2348:"The case," said Lira. "If it can't be filed, if there's nobody to send it to who isn't standing on it. If we can't publish it without spending Seln, and we can't use it at the seat without the seat reading it as a forgery, and Karis won't take it a line past the paper." She looked down the main stair into the lamplit dark. "Twelve years on a shelf and five nights on her table, and it doesn't prove anything to anybody who matters. What's it for?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2378:"There's one man outside this room who's owed the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2380:They were all there. They had come up by their three ways after supper as they did every evening now, without needing a floor schedule to tell them when. Karis had put the enrollment book back on Bracken's counter on the First-day before the second bell, as promised, and Bracken had weighed it on his palm and written it in. The case stood on its shelf between the grey boxes, and the lamp burned in the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2388:"Seln paid twelve years for that case," Cael said, "and we've had it six nights. We can't file it, and we can't publish it, and we can't use it. But we can let one honest man inside the thing see what the thing is, before they hand him the pen." He looked at the cloth. "I think we owe him that, and I think it's the same debt as the stair. I don't know how to tell the difference."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2394:"He takes it up the stair the morning after," he said, "because that's what an honest man does with a paper he's been shown. He doesn't keep it in a drawer. He sends it to the office it concerns, with a covering note in his best hand, the way that clerk in the river town did." He did not look at the case. "And the stair eats it. And somebody at the top of the stair reads the covering note and asks where an Archmarshal saw forty copied orders in a surveyor's case, and the answer's a wing at Halcenvane. And Seln's gone by the end of the month, and none of us ever finds out where."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2400:"He verifies it," she said. "He's an evaluator; it's the whole of what he is. He doesn't believe a thing because it's on a table; he measures it. So he goes home and pulls a frame reading, or a station sheet, or a fresh reading on one of the forty, to see whether the case is true." She turned her pale fingers over on the cloth. "And the frame's theirs, and the station's theirs, and the reading runs through the function. Every instrument he could check it with comes back and says the case is wrong. And he believes his instruments, because he's believed them for forty years. And somewhere it gets minuted that a forgery was offered to the Archmarshal by persons connected with the respondent at Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2406:"He believes it," she said, "all of it, at once, the way you'd want him to, and it breaks him." She was looking at the case. "He's been measuring true for forty years because he thought true was the point. We show him the point was never true. And he goes home, and sits at his desk, and does nothing, because there's nothing he can do. He signs what he's given, or he doesn't, and either way he's a broken man signing things." She lifted her chin. "And we'd have done that to him. We'd have spent the one good man in the whole building for a bad night's sleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2410:"He is already theirs," he said. "Norhold was a knock, dressed as a kindness. A lent room, no paper, a warning that made you trust him. That is the third grade done well. And the case goes to the people it was taken from, by the hand of the one man you would have given it to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2416:"He reads it," he said, "and he asks about me. He's an evaluator, and I'm the one subject in the Compact he's never been able to read, and he's sitting across a table from the case and from me. He asks the natural question: why is the boy's file marked, what is he, how did the function fail on him." He made himself say the rest. "And somebody in the room answers, not because they mean to, but because it's the natural thing, and he asks well, and he's kind about it, and it's late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2420:"Then that's the rule," she said. "That's the one that matters. The rest are risks, and we can weigh them, but that one's a rule." She put both hands flat on the table. "He gets the case. Nothing about Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2426:"He gets the case," said Brom. "Nothing about Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2428:"He gets the case," said Lira. "Nothing about Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2432:"He gets the case," said Cael. "Nothing about me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2436:"He gets the case," said Seln. "Nothing about the boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2444:Nobody hurried him. They held their hands up and waited. It was his case and his twelve years and his name on a roll somewhere, and if his hand did not go up, then theirs would come down, and none of them would ever mention it again. Cael found that he did not know which way it would go. He found too that he could bear either, and that this was new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2454:The wing posted station closures every week on a board behind the counter, because an enrollee could not be sent to a station for a frame reading on a day the station was shut. Nobody ever read the closures but the desk clerk, and the desk clerk only to answer a question. On the Fourth-day Seln read them, and there it was, in a district clerk's printed form: the river stations shut in turn for the Archmarshal's yearly inspection, which he walked himself, as he had walked it every year for a decade. The Ostrand station was down for the coming Seventh-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2474:"Then we go home on a Seventh-day," said Seln, "and nothing in the world is different, and we keep the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2496:Brom had the chair with its back to the wall. Karis had the chair at the table's end, with the case on the floor at her feet, its lock still shut; Seln had carried it down inside the post satchel, between the wing's letters, and given it to her in the keeper's hall without a word. Seln came up last and locked the door behind him and gave the key to nobody. He did not sit. He went to the window and stood a little to one side of it, where the frame hid him from the lane, and looked down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2514:Seln unlocked the door and opened it and stood aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2518:Vastin stood on the landing in his plain grey coat with his hands empty at his sides, and did not come in at once. He looked at the room the way Seln had looked at the reading room on the birthday, the door and the window and the bare table and the shapes on the shelves, and then the five of them, one at a time. He did not let his eyes stay on any one of them longer than on the others, not on Cael, and not on the man who had opened the door to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2520:Then he came in, and Seln shut the door and locked it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2528:Karis lifted the case onto the table and turned it to face him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2530:Seln came to the table's other end and took the brass key from his waistcoat and opened the lock with its dry sound, and lifted the lid, and stepped back. He did not lay anything out this time. Karis did. She did it in the order it had come out of the case on the first night, the forty first, in four rows of ten, then the bundle in the wing's pink tape, then the two stacks of the memoranda, and last of all the leaf in its sleeve, a hand's width from everything else. When she had finished she sat down again in her chair and folded her hands and said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2536:He read the way Karis read, all of it, from the beginning, before he let himself think a single thing. He took the first pair from the top of the first row and read the record and then the order and then the slip on the back, and laid it face down, and took the second. His face did not change. His left hand lay on the table beside the rows, and every now and then, without his seeming to know it, it opened and closed, slowly, twice, the way a man tests a hand that has begun to dislike mornings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2564:Vastin did not move. His left hand lay on his knee, and once it opened and closed. His eyes were on the window, on the cupola, or past it, on nothing in particular, the way a man looks at the sky when he is listening for something a long way off. His face was quite still. It was the face of a man reading a figure he had been sure of for forty years and finding that he had misread it on the first day, and every day since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2574:When at last he turned from the window, his face had been tidied. Whatever had been in it was put away somewhere behind his eyes, as a careful man folds a letter he cannot yet answer and shuts it in a drawer, and he looked round the table at the five of them in turn, the first time he had let himself look at them since he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2580:"The first is verification." His eyes did not go to the case. "I want to. I have wanted to since the twenty-third pair. I could ride home and send for a frame and a station's own sheet and a fresh reading on any one of those forty children, and watch the case come true under my own hand, and sleep." The finger stayed where it was. "But there is no instrument in the Compact I could test it with that does not run on the thing it accuses. The frames are theirs. The sheets come up the same stair. The reading passes through the very function on that leaf before anybody writes it down. Whatever I measured would be a play put on for my benefit by the defendant." He was quiet a moment. "And a play of that kind has a cast. A girl's name on a request, or a junior's on an order. I will not put a child in a ledger so that an old man can feel sure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2592:"I have made something over nine thousand evaluations," said Vastin, to the case, very quietly. "I counted them once, as a young man, and stopped counting when I stopped being one. Each of them I wrote down exactly as I found it, and sent up." His left hand opened on his knee and closed. "How many went into the registry as I wrote them, I don't know. I never shall. That is what I am taking home from this room. Not the paper. That."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2616:Karis had begun putting the case back in its order and did not raise her head. Brom was standing with his good hand on the rail of the chair Vastin had left, as though it might still hold some of the warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:2628:At the fourth station, a day upriver from Ostrand, the keeper had walked him through the frame room as keepers always did, proud of his brass, and at the end of it had opened a long drawer under the counter to show him the station's own sheets for the year. A keeper kept his sheets as a miller kept his tallies. They were the station's private record of what its frame had read on each child, before any clerk of entry had touched it, and this keeper wrote a good hand and wanted it seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:3110:- **Lira**: Wind Path, Iron Rank 1 formal; Rooke's new **Silver-bracket program** opened in the
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/review-editorial.stdout.log:3119:  the locked case has been visible since Book 4 and never opened. **Ephram**: Blade, Iron Rank Six,
