editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:The east hall had its two lamps lit and its shutters down. Outside it was not yet light, and a wet wind was leaning on the north wall, so that the long room seemed smaller than it was, a lit box with six people in it and the dark pressing on the glass. The program's clerk had moved her trestle out from the wall to the edge of the painted floor and sat at it with the red book open and two pens laid ready. Rooke sat on her right with his board. On her left the Stone stood with a flag in each hand, a red and a blue, held down along his thighs like a man carrying two fish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:53:At the far rail the Force unfolded his arms and folded them again the other way. Rooke wrote something on his board. Cael let out a breath he had not known he was holding, and Lira, walking back, opened and shut her taped hands twice, quickly, pleased and not meaning to show it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:63:Twice she nearly was. The second time she stepped across to shut the girl's line, and it was a good step, and it ended on her right foot with the Current girl's shoulder a hand's breadth from her fingers. All she had to do was plant and reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:129:Lira had forgotten that until she let herself into the east hall after the tenth bell on Rooke's key, alone, with one lantern, and stood inside the great door with her hand on its edge until her eyes had learned the dark. The shutters were down, and the painted crosses went away from her across the boards, white, then grey, then nothing. Somewhere at the far end the heavy frame ticked on its springs as the cold got into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:133:Then she felt foolish, because he was not there to need it, and shut it behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:205:She knew now what it opened into. Karis had said it: both doors, the same room. If she climbed every rung Rooke had set for her and stood before the advancement sitting and was rated Silver, she would be a Silver in a registry that struck children's margins as a matter of form. She would be on the paper, and the paper was the thing in the case.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:7:The man was standing in the annex's single doorway when he came, watching the approach in both directions — and had been, Cael understood, for some minutes, and would have gone on being for some minutes more if the corridor had asked him to. He stepped aside to let Cael pass. He closed the door. He set the case on the room's one table under the room's one lamp.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:13:He opened it without preamble. A keeper's economy: no speech, no framing, the lock worked with a small key he had carried on his person and not on any ring, the lid raised, the contents there.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:17:The case held fifteen years of an intelligence officer's private duplicates, and they were organized the way the man was organized, which was completely and invisibly: not bundled by date or by posting but by a system whose key lived in his head, so that the case looked, to the uninitiated eye, like a scuffed traveling case full of ordinary registry paper, and was in fact a map with the legend removed. He lifted the layers out in order and named them as he set them on the table, in the flat register he kept for handing paper across a counter — and that register was the only thing in the room that did not shake, and its steadiness was the room's engine.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:21:Reclassification orders — the registry's ordinary instrument for correcting a filed classification, a form Cael had handled twice in his life and Karis perhaps four hundred times — each one clipped, in Seln's own hand, to the Kindling record it had corrected. He set the stack down and did not open it. Karis opened it, because Karis opened everything, and worked from the top with her repaired fingers.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:47:And the artifact at the case's floor, in a wax-paper sleeve older than everything above it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:65:"By weight, in the case, eleven pounds. By count, three hundred and eighty documents." He said the numbers without effort, which told them he had never once needed to look. "By risk — every single one of them, every day, for fifteen years, in nineteen rooms I did not choose and could not lock."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:69:"A conspiracy has a generation," he said. "Men scheme, and retire, and die, and the scheme dies or changes with them. I have worked against three. They all had the same shape: a beginning, a peak, a betrayal, an ending, and a paper trail that got *sloppier* as the founders aged out and the inheritors stopped understanding what they had inherited. This does not change. The oldest order in the case and the newest are the same order in different type. There is no sloppiness anywhere in it. There is no drift. There is no place where somebody's nephew was let through. Whatever is doing this outlived everyone who could have started it, and has not once, in two hundred years, made the kind of mistake that living men make." He looked at the table's far end where the oldest course lay. "That is the sentence I spent my eighth year refusing and my ninth accepting."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:71:**How he walked away from the job.** In the only way his trade allowed, which was by staying in it and keeping. A man in his position could not resign; a resigned intelligence officer with fifteen years of duplicates was a witness, and he had explained to Cael on a stair what witnesses were. So he had stayed in, and shrunk himself to a posting nobody watched, and filed true empty reports for a decade — true in every particular, empty in every particular that mattered, a decade of a man reporting accurately that there was nothing to report — and carried the case through nineteen shelves, and become the best clerk on a bluff-top academy's counter, and waited.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:73:"Not for a purpose," he said. "For the reason keepers wait, which is that the day the case becomes worth its cost is never known in advance, and a keeper who spends it early is a keeper who was wrong."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:77:"The paper came to the gate. The paper's next grade does not knock. And the boy has been fighting inside the instrument for a term believing the instrument is merely broken, which is the one condition I built the case to end, on the one day it could still matter." A pause of exactly its own length. "The catechism said sealed. The catechism was correct for fifteen years. It stopped being correct on a road cut three days ago. I am not sure this is right. That is how I know it is."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:95:Karis went at the provenance, because provenance was the only ground she trusted. Where had each memorandum been duplicated, and from what, and by whom; whether any of the three hundred and eighty could be shown to be a copy of a copy of a thing that had never existed; whether a hostile examiner given the whole case and a year could construct an innocent account of it. Seln answered every question without hurry or emphasis and answered the last one honestly, which was the answer she wanted and not the one she hoped for: "Yes. Given the case alone, a competent examiner constructs a story in which a disturbed clerk collected coincidences for fifteen years. It is not a good story. It does not need to be. It needs to be available."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:101:Lira went at the operational question, which was hers. "How many people have looked in that case?"
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:123:And then the annex's second movement, and the debt paid, because the case could not lie open on the table with only one archive in it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:125:Cael answered in kind. He stood up to do it, which he had not planned, and did it standing, on the far side of the lamp, with the case open between them — and he told Seln the mechanism. The whole of it. The integration, the fragments, the notices, the log. The eleven seconds of silence in a border town and a sigil that had paused and returned a word in brackets and could not do anything else with him. The eight architectures, each with its source and its date and its cost. The stakes rule, and where it had been drawn, and what it had been drawn to prevent. The line in writing at a window: directed, open-eyed, or not at all.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:139:"Good," Seln said. "Then you may say it to me twice more in your life, and never to anyone else, and you will not say it again tonight." He squared a stack that did not need squaring. "The most dangerous thing on this table is no longer the case."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:155:They put the case back together at the second bell after midnight — Karis keeping the memoranda, by agreement, until morning, and signing for them in a private register that had no seal on it, because she asked to sign and Seln let her, and both of them understood exactly what the signing was for.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:159:The log, that night, was where the whole term's weight came to rest, and Cael wrote it in the new satchel by lamplight with the case's contents committed to memory and the case itself back on its shelf across the campus:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:37:Because he should not have been. The registrar's counter was open from the first bell to the eighth, and in two years Cael had never known it open with nobody behind it. If Bracken had to be elsewhere, his senior clerk stood in his place; if there was no clerk, the counter shut and a card hung on the grille. But a first-year had come up from the records hall ten minutes ago, white-faced, to report that he had gone down about a lost key and found the counter lamp lit, the baskets half dealt on the trestle, and nobody there at all. Bracken had crossed the fountain court with this sheet in his hand at a pace nobody on the hill had ever seen from him. He had walked the length of the refectory between the benches with a hundred heads turning after him and had not looked at one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:The post room was behind the records hall's counter, a narrow whitewashed room with a long sorting bench under the window and pigeonholes from floor to ceiling along the other wall. The district courier's bag came up the hill in the dark and was opened there at first light. Usually the post porter opened it, a broad slow man who had done the sort for twenty years. This morning the post porter was sitting on a stool in the corner with his left arm strapped across his chest, looking unhappy, and the man at the sorting bench with the bag open in front of him was Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:103:It had no seal, only a fold tucked in on itself, the way people fold a letter who do not want anybody to think they took trouble over it. Cael did not turn it over in the post room. He carried the house's bundle up to the residence and left it on the hall table. He took the one letter on up the stair and opened it at his own window, where letters were opened in that house, with the light coming up grey over the river and the fountain not yet running below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:181:Seln opened the book at the week's first page and began to read it, and went on listening, and said "Mm" at the right places, while the lodge-man told him the whole of the carter. Then he told him about the carter's father, who had also scraped the gatepost, and what he had said to that one in his day, which had been a good deal more than he had said to the son. It took a quarter of an hour. Cael stood by the stove, where Seln had put him with a glance, and drank the tea the lodge-man had poured him too, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:201:Seln shut the book and pushed it back across the counter with a word of thanks. He told the lodge-man that in his opinion the gatepost was in exactly the right place and the carter ought to be made to pay for the stone. The lodge-man said that was what he'd been saying. Seln finished his tea, and set the cup down, and turned it a quarter-turn on the counter before he let go of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:They went in at the quarter to the eleventh bell, as they always did, on the key Rooke had lent Lira for the program's dawn drills and had told her he did not want to know about after dark. But the east hall was not what it had been. The shutters along the gallery had been closed since the Fourth-day by Rooke's order, and they did not need to be drawn. The chain still hung across the foot of the gallery stair with its padlock and its card, *CLOSED TO OBSERVERS*, so that Brom had to step over it to set the jug down on the bottom stair. The four lamps that the program now drilled under all day were standing at the corners of the painted crosses where the porters had left them, cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:"Well," said Lira, looking round the shut-up hall with her wrists half taped. "The whole floor's honest now. Nobody's allowed to watch any of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:33:He laid it across her line from the north wall, a road of leaning air fifteen feet long and no wider than his shoulders, tipping south. His ears shut and opened again. And on the gallery stair Karis began to count aloud, quietly, from the moment it was laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:59:"I lost my light," she said, to the boards. "Rooke shut it out on account of who was watching you. I've been angry about it for six days, all by myself, under these lamps, and I haven't told anybody, because it was the right thing to do and I knew it was." She stood up and dusted her knee. "And it turns out the lamps show me a thing the windows never did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:In wing three, four strangers had written down a figure for him that was exactly right and entirely false: the band, the house's rate, twenty-two and two-thirds. Here, on a floor with the shutters closed and a chain across the stair, there had been nobody to write anything at all, and the figure had been true. Six bursts on boards. The give opened. A lane and its bill. Every true number about him this year had been made in a locked room by four people who would never be allowed to say it aloud. And every false one had gone into a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:97:Karis wrote the four words on a card in her finding hand, one under the other, and put the card in her case for the morning. Then she sat back and rubbed her eyes, and Cael saw that her hands were not steady.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:101:"No," said the counsel. "It will be the best argument that room has heard in a long time, and it won't be enough." She put the rule in her case. "Do it anyway. Somebody reads the minute afterward. They always do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:Everything else that morning he saw only between things. The side-door officer, his cold gone, not needing to ask the last name on his list. Somebody had already copied *Caelen Hesk-ward* into his door log from the last sitting, so that all he had to write was *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. *Seventeen* under Cael's left foot again, the third time, and this time chosen; he could not choose the room, but he could choose where he stood in it. And a slip of paper folded twice that Karis had handed Brom on the ferry, which was in Brom's right inside pocket now, with orders not to be opened until the coach went home. The left pocket was for Velmere, and it had a sealed letter in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:157:Then he sat, and unscrewed his inkwell, and laid his hands on either side of his clerk's tabbed folder, which he had never once opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:181:And last, because the whole case ended in something that was not there, the drafters' list. She took up the directive that had standardized the code and turned to the schedule at its back where its drafters had listed every older provision they meant to cancel, and walked the hood down it slowly, item by item. At the bottom she stopped. They had been careful men, she said. They had listed everything they meant to end. The assay clause was not on their list. Care only runs one way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:217:In the passage behind the selling floor, while the porters brought the cases down, Lira caught his sleeve again. She had lost the thread twice from the attendance bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:231:"I hate it," said Lira, with deep satisfaction at having understood it, and went to help Brom with the second case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:259:At the ferry the coach waited its turn on the landing, and Cael looked out at the two grey coats who stood there as they always stood, changing on the bell. One of them was not looking at the coach's door or at the faces in its windows. He was looking at the luggage rail on the roof, where the house's two document cases were strapped, the way a clerk looks at a line he means to copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:275:Seln had not opened his eyes since the town. He did not open them now. When he spoke, it was to the whole coach, and not loudly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:Not all of it. The wing's other sealed things still stood there in their row, a box of old sigil cards and two bound runs of the wing's returns and a tin of sealing wafers. But at the end of the row, where the case had stood for two years, there was only a clean oblong of wood, a shade paler than the wood round it, with a soft grey edge of dust on three sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:Seln had said it once, in Cael's room, with the case on the sill and the rain on the glass. *Read your own line. The hour is when you come up the stair.* And then, as the boy was writing on the back of his hand: *Come the way you always come. A man who changes his road is a man who has a reason.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:121:The top landing had one lamp on a bracket, and the annex door stood shut across it. The reading room's door had Bracken's card in its brass frame at eye height. *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:153:The lecture range's bell gave the eighth hour, far off across the court, muffled by the shutters. Before the last stroke had died there was a single knock at the door, not loud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:Then he came in and Karis locked the door behind him and hung the key back inside her collar. Seln went to the middle of the table, on the far side, where he always sat. He did not sit. He unbuttoned his coat and took out the case and set it down on the bare cloth in the circle of the shaded lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:165:He took the key from his waistcoat pocket, a small brass key on a loop of black tape. He put it in the lock and turned it, and the lock opened with a sound like a dry twig, and he lifted the lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:227:He had been kept by that sentence. He knew it as surely as he knew the ribs. Two years of true, empty weeks had gone up out of the wing with his name on the outside fold, and every one of them had said that nothing had happened. That sentence had stood between him and the people who read returns. And here was the same sentence, word for word, clipped to the back of a girl from a river town. In her case it said that the correction had held. Nothing to report. She had gone home smaller and stayed there, and nine weeks of somebody's careful watching had found nothing in her worth writing down, because the thing worth writing down had been taken off her paper before she was old enough to know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:269:The lamp burned. Across the court, very faint through the shutters and four floors down, somebody in the residence was laughing at something, the long helpless laugh of a first-year who has been told a joke after the bell and cannot stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:She looked at the case, open at the end of the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:"That is the only letter," he said. "In forty. I copied it because it was the only one. I have read it more often than anything else in the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:Gault sat at the trestle in the middle of the north side with his case beside him on the bench. It was the case with the handle, the one he carried his instruments in, and he had set it down very straight, as he always did, as if it might have opinions about the bench. On his right sat the Ash instructor with her book held against her chest. On his left sat the Mire instructor, a broad calm woman with a pencil behind each ear. Each of the three had a card in front of them, ruled into three columns, execution, control and effect, and the wing added the three cards with no strike, which was the house's own way and not the tournaments'. The desk clerk from the counter sat at the end of the trestle with the wing's book open, to write the figure down when there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:111:Then he took his place inside the rate. That was how he thought of it now, since Norhold: a room inside the room. The house had a record of him, and the record held what it held. On sprung oak the house had seen six bursts in a bout; on these boards that meant four, and never a fifth. Karis's spark to two contacts in a bout and no more. Reydan's give kept shut, so that a blow which landed on his forearm went on up the arm into the shoulder, as it would in any honest Iron. The push no more than a flat hand. And the rest of it, the lane and the weight behind it, not anywhere at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:147:The Shield had set his plane square in front of him to turn a straight blow, and he had his whole weight in it, the way a man leans into a door to hold it shut. It was a good guard against anything that came at it from the front. It had nothing at all to say about something that came from the side. A push at a fighting load, the thing Cael had given a Gold five times in one sitting, would have taken that braced plane on its edge and turned the whole man round on his own planted foot like a gate swinging on a hinge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:153:His forearm came across Cael's ribs on the right side, low, with his weight still in it. It was not quite a blow; it was more like a door shutting on him. The give was there, Reydan's give, waiting to take the whole of it into the forearm and keep it, and he did not let it. He took it the way any honest Iron would take it. It went in under the ribs and up, and all the breath he had went out of him with a sound he did not choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:159:He walked back to his chalk with his hand pressed to his side and the room going a little grey at the edges, and he made himself breathe slowly through it. It was not the rib that cost him. He had known the rib was coming; he had written it down the night before. What cost him was the gate swinging on its hinge. He had seen it, whole and clear, the way he saw a sentence finished before its speaker finished it, and he had shut his hand on it, and now the room had the Shield's touch on its cards and not his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:199:The clerk wrote it in the wing's book. The Ash instructor closed hers and held it against her chest again, and the Mire instructor took one pencil from behind her ear and put it behind the other ear, which Cael had never understood. Gault picked up the case by its handle and went back to his office with it, and the morning's business was done. The provision had been demonstrated, the hours signed, and a figure had gone into a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:243:He realized, crossing the court, that Ephram was right about one thing and did not know how right. The edges on the chart were not his. They were the price of a door he was keeping shut, and he paid it every Fifth-day, in front of three cards and a clerk, one rib at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:45:Gault was in the lodge doorway beside Cael. Nobody had seen him come across the court; he had his case clamped under one arm and a small black notebook in his other hand, and he had evidently been standing there for the last two entries.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:49:Bracken gave it to the clerk, and the clerk wrote it. Gault opened his own notebook on his forearm and wrote in it too, standing in the doorway, a short line, and closed it on the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:59:"Anything sealed, fetch the registrar," she read aloud. "You did, and you shut your window, and you stood where you could see the whole of it." She nodded to him, the nod she gave her senior staff. "Your board did very well this morning. Put a fresh coat on it in the spring, if you would. I should like it to last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:99:Behind Cael two first-years at the bread were telling each other that there had been ten riders, with swords. Further along, a boy who had heard it from the urn said the lodge-man had been struck and his window broken. The two second-years nearest had it best and worst: the Chancellor had sent Ostrand away from her gate, and Ostrand would shut the house for it, and one of them had a cousin who knew the date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:115:They looked at him. He had not opened his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:121:"I've never stood near enough to one at work to say, and I won't guess." Seln opened his eyes and looked at the copy. "I know where they're posted, and it isn't escort. Escort is for clerks on good horses. One at that grade, standing where she stood, is enforcement." He laid one fingertip on the table beside the order, not touching it. "Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:137:Karis opened her notebook near the back and wrote NO NAME. Then she turned to the date a week ahead and entered DISTRICT REPLY, leaving the intervening leaves blank. Brom told her she was scheduling paper she had not seen. "I'm keeping room for it," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:201:That was what Cael saw first when they came back into the records hall: Seln's cup, at his elbow on the long table, exactly where it had stood when they went out. Karis and Lira were still at the table, Karis copying the evening into her notebook, Lira with her landing leg along the bench and her eyes shut. Seln sat down at the middle of the bench where he had sat before, and did not touch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-27.txt:213:Karis finished her copying and set down her pen. Lira opened her eyes. Brom sat with his elbows on his knees. And Seln went on looking at the order, longer than any of them, with his hands flat and still on either side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:11:He ruled a margin and wrote the interval shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:17:*Lira: Rooke opened the Silver program on the first morning of the recess, and she has been on its floor every dawn since. She doesn't talk about it at table. That's new. Since Ardenmere she has told me about every floor she ever stood on.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:He had never found better words for it. Daeva had her own words, and they were not his. To him it was like putting a hand flat against the morning and pressing, and feeling the whole of it give a little, along one line, the way a board gives when you step on its end. The air in front of him tipped. Fifteen feet of it, no more, in a narrow road no wider than his shoulders, and it reached perhaps a third as deep as hers had reached. His ears closed and opened, and the dust on the boards woke up. It ran, in a thin pale stream along the floor, down the length of the lane, as water runs down a gutter, and gathered against the far edge of it in a little drift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:103:Fifteen feet. Then the end of the lane, like the end of a moving walkway at a fair, and he was his own weight again and stopping. Behind him the air let go, and his ears closed and opened once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:243:"He looked at the road the way you look at a page you've already read, to see if the words have changed." She held her notebook shut against her chest with both arms. "Bracken never looks at anything without a reason. He'd think it a waste of the eyes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:16:He ruled a margin and wrote the interval shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:22:Lira: Rooke opened the Silver program on the first morning of the recess, and she has been on its floor every dawn since. She doesn't talk about it at table. That's new. Since Ardenmere she has told me about every floor she ever stood on.
