editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:240:"He looked at the road the way you look at a page you've already read, to see if the words have changed." She held her notebook shut against her chest with both arms. "Bracken never looks at anything without a reason. He'd think it a waste of the eyes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:374:She sat down, and opened the new ledger at its first page, and uncapped her pen, and that was the end of the visit. He left her tea where she could reach it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:384:Behind the counter, the desk clerk sat at his ledger by the window, adding a column under his breath and losing it whenever the Current girls' whispering rose. Gault's door stood shut, with voices behind it. And behind the counter at the copying table, rising every time the queue moved to take the next sheet, the teaching assistant did the counter's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:386:Above the copying table, on the shelf where it had stood before Norhold, sat a small locked case with no mark on it. It had gone to Norhold and come back without anybody seeing it opened. Nobody looked at it now, and Cael did not look at it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:434:He shut the book. Across the river the ferryman lit the lamp on the landing's post, and the two coats there stood in its light without moving, as patient as the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:516:Gault sat at the trestle in the middle of the north side with his case beside him on the bench. It was the case with the handle, the one he carried his instruments in, and he had set it down very straight, as he always did, as if it might have opinions about the bench. On his right sat the Ash instructor with her book held against her chest. On his left sat the Mire instructor, a broad calm woman with a pencil behind each ear. Each of the three had a card in front of them, ruled into three columns, execution, control and effect, and the wing added the three cards with no strike, which was the house's own way and not the tournaments'. The desk clerk from the counter sat at the end of the trestle with the wing's book open, to write the figure down when there was one.
