editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:670:He realized, crossing the court, that Ephram was right about one thing and did not know how right. The edges on the chart were not his. They were the price of a door he was keeping shut, and he paid it every Fifth-day, in front of three cards and a clerk, one rib at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:676:Lira unlocked the east hall at a quarter to the eleventh bell on Sixth-day night, with Rooke's key on its string, and the three of them went in and shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:678:It was dark inside and smelled of new paint and old sweat. Brom had brought two lanterns and a jug of water, and he set the lanterns on the floor at either end of the painted lines and turned them up, and the white crosses and arrows came out of the dark across the boards like the bones of something. Lira went up the gallery stair two at a time and along the gallery, and Cael heard her drawing the shutters on the high windows one after another, all the way down the north wall, until there was no light in the hall but theirs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:692:Brom came over before anything else and laid his hand on Cael's right side, low, where the Shield's forearm had shut on him the morning before. He pressed with two fingers, and Cael breathed in through his teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:708:"Stays shut." She did not look up from the tape. "That's not a rule. That's a promise, and it's yours, not mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:784:This was the one Cael had shut his hand on in wing three. He set his right palm against Brom's chest, below the collarbone, and Brom set himself to it, the way he set himself against anything, with his weight down in his heels and his knees soft and his whole body arranged to be the thing that did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:822:He asked the air to lean, along a line between them, from where he stood to the gap past Brom's left shoulder. Fifteen feet of it, a third deep, and the air tipped like a board stepped on at one end, and his ears closed and opened. Down at his feet the dust in the cracks of the boards woke up and ran, pale, along the lane toward Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:832:The forearm came across Cael's chest like a door shutting in a gale. He went down on the boards on his back, and the lane let go above him, and all the breath he owned went up into the dark of the gallery and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:838:"No." Cael lay on the painted floor and looked at the shuttered windows. "I was looking at the road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:886:He had it by heart. Every notice he had ever had he knew by heart, because each of them had found him somewhere with nothing else to look at, a yard, a stair, a shut room, and the one from Norhold on one knee on a stone floor, with a healer holding his hand. Note: pressure-differential component; corridor-seeding component. Short range. At the time it had read like a clerk's description of somebody else's coat. Tonight he thought he could put his hand on each line of it. The lane was the corridor; he seeded it, and it grew where he put it, and stayed while he left it alone. The tilt of the air, the ears, the dust running downhill, that was the pressure. Short range was fifteen feet. And the notice had said nothing at all about the read going into the air and leaving the body behind, or about ten counts of everything slow. Notices never told you what a thing cost. They only told you what it was. The cost you found out on a floor, with friends, if you were lucky, and in front of eight thousand people if you were not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:888:They sat down on the floor afterward among the white crosses, the three of them, with the jug of water going round and the lanterns burning low. Lira lay on her back with her taped hands on her stomach and her eyes shut. Brom sat with his legs out in front of him, unwinding his wraps slowly, a turn at a time, rolling each length up as he went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:900:"Back," said Lira, still with her eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:904:"I didn't need to look. The room went quieter." She opened her eyes and turned her head on the boards. "That's the first this week?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:924:"Then once a week," he said, "you come here. Here, with us, and you take all of it out of the bag and use it, every piece, and we write down what it costs." He pressed the wrap smooth with his thumb. "So you don't forget it's yours. A man who keeps a thing shut long enough starts to think the shut is the thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:984:They were coming up at a walk. Two rode a little ahead and to either side, in grey with a badge on the shoulder, and they rode the way men ride who have been told the pace and are keeping it whether it suits the horses or not. The third rode between them. He sat very upright, and across the front of his saddle, strapped tight against the horse's withers so that it could not shift, lay a case of dark leather the length of his forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:994:He did not run. A boy running across a court after a registry escort would have been a story by noon. He walked fast, at the pace of somebody late for a lecture, and he came round the corner of the lecture range in time to see the third rider dismount at the records-hall steps and unstrap the case from his saddle. The two outriders stayed mounted. They sat their horses at the foot of the steps, one on each side, facing outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1002:The rider came in behind Cael with the case under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1004:On his feet he was a different kind of man from the two outside. He had soft hands and good boots that had not been walked in, and a clerk's grey coat kept very clean, with a small seal of office in dull silver at the collar that Cael did not know. He was perhaps fifty, with thin hair combed close and the careful face of somebody who has set a great many things down on a great many counters and has never once been set down himself. He set the case on the counter and laid his hand on it and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1018:He unlocked the case with a key from his watch-chain and took out a form first, a single printed sheet with two ruled lines at its foot. Cael had never seen its like. It was headed Acknowledgment of Joint Service, and under the heading there was a paragraph of small type, and under that, two lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1026:The officer witnessed both signatures in his own hand, sanded them from a box in the case, folded the form along a crease that was already in it, and sealed it inside the case again. Then he took out the instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1028:It was thick. It lay on the counter in front of the three of them, a sheaf of heavy paper folded once along its length, and across the fold, holding it shut, sat a wafer of grey wax the size of a mark, pressed with a seal Cael had seen before only on the outside of other people's letters. The Compact's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1030:"Served," said the officer. He bowed to Withrow again, to the same depth, and to Bracken a little less, and to Cael not at all. Then he took up his case and went out. They heard him go down the steps, and a moment later they heard the three horses go away across the court at a walk, and then nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1064:The door at the top was shut. The card was in its brass holder. The new lock was locked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1066:He knocked, and the key turned on the inside, and Karis opened the door with a pen in her hand and ink on the side of her thumb and the look of somebody who has been interrupted in the middle of a sentence and means to go back to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1122:Behind them the records-hall door opened and Bracken came out onto the top step with a sheet of paper in his hand. It was green: a pale grey-green, like the underside of a willow leaf. He held it out at arm's length, away from himself, the way a man holds a thing he has found on his doorstep and is not yet sure is not alive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1132:Ephram found them there a little after, coming round the corner of the lecture range with the captain's file under his arm and the green tape trailing. He stopped at the foot of the steps and looked up at the three of them, and at the cheese, and at the shut door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1150:Bracken's boy brought the enrollee's copy up to the residence at the seventh bell, in a grey folder under the house's seal, and Cael took it to his window and laid it on the sill and opened it at the first page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1164:Then he went over what he had written, with the book shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1182:At a little past the eighth bell he took the book down from its hook by the side door, opened it on the counter at the night's page, and wrote in his small upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1196:"Because the men who write codes can't see every case coming," he said, "and they know they can't. Discretion is the room they leave for the cases they couldn't see. Most nights it's the kindest word in the book." He looked down at the instrument. "It depends who's standing in the room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1212:She opened it where it fell open, near the middle, and laid it beside Bracken's great third volume at the same page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1256:Cael ruled a line under his column. A man building a case writes evidence on every page, because one day he will be standing in front of a seat with his evidence in his hands. This man had written discretion.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1272:They read them before midnight, Bracken reading the headings and Karis the findings, the counsel listening with her eyes shut and her rule across her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1284:The other three went quickly: a mason's guild that had put an apprentice into its school by demonstration and been challenged on the form of its panel, and two guild cases so old that the paper had gone brown and soft and the parties had worn down to the names of their trades. All four sustained. Bracken closed each cover as Karis finished and laid it back in its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1364:"Twelve sections," she said. "Frames. Stations. Assessors suspected of being bought. Panels whose records argue with themselves. Instruments, or the people who keep instruments." She lifted the rule. "I've looked tonight for the case where somebody turned these sections round to point at the man standing in the frame instead of the frame. There isn't one. This is new."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1392:"Good. Their whole case will be that your floor is a description: a panel, a figure, a day, witnesses, nothing more. Mine will be that it is an evaluation, and the oldest kind, older than any frame, and I'll make it as well as anybody living could make it." She slid the notebook back. "Then the seat will choose. You've counted how many times they wrote the word it'll choose with." She tapped the column at nine. "Go on counting. Bring me a column like this every week until it's over. A count is evidence. Indignation never was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1394:The third volume lay shut at Bracken's elbow, and for a while after the counsel had finished he left it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1396:Then he drew it to him, opened it at a place he did not need to look for, laid a strip of plain paper under the first line, and put on his spectacles. Cael had heard him read a hundred pages aloud and never one like this; there was nothing in the voice at all, because Bracken would not let there be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1482:She turned to the inside of her notebook's front cover. Over two years Cael had watched a few lines gather there that belonged to no case: the fielding clause, a line about the squad rule, a few words copied from the founding articles of a charter she had promised herself not to open until the cold term. She wrote the new one under them, small, and closed the cover, and was back at her heading before the ink had dried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1546:He opened his mouth and found the answer already there, whole and certain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1596:They had the door of the old lumber room propped open with a broken chair, and they were working the table through it on its side, sweating and grumbling at it in the low courteous voices porters used on the library stair. Behind them the room itself stood half empty. It had held, for as long as Cael had known it, the house's spare benches and a map case with no maps in it and two rolled carpets nobody would admit to owning. Now the carpets were out on the landing and the benches gone, and somebody had already swept the floor in long straight strokes and left the broom against the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1702:He read it at his own window that night, with the fountain going in the court and the copy of the instrument shut in its folder on the bed behind him. Across the top of the grid ran the columns: the sitting, the panel, the day, the finding, the seal. Down the side ran the three times his place on the bluff had been written down by somebody obliged to write it. The grant at Greyvane, from the hearing. The first renewal, at Halcenvane, in the inspection year, before the Compact's delegation. The renewal at the mill town, last year, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1712:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. Renewed. That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1724:He went back to the table on the sill. Its first renewal's row gave one day, the day of the trestle, which was the day the house's own enrollment book carried in Bracken's small upright hand, because that was the day the house had seen it happen. The registry's file, wherever it sat, would carry the other day, the day its own copy was sealed. The house had never kept a copy of that one. It had gone back up the road the same afternoon. The only place on the continent where both days stood side by side was a book he had filled himself and shut in a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1738:He opened the old Log on the table beside her elbow at the evaluation day, and laid his finger beside its last word without touching it, and then turned forward to the two short lines and did the same. She read both, the way she read anything with a date in it, once for the words and once for the numbers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1752:She went on ruling. Every row this time gave two days, the trestle and the seal, and the first renewal's row gave the reason for the gap in a single line. Under the grid she wrote where each day could be found: the house's enrollment book for the trestle; the registry's own file, to be produced on request, for the seals; and for the gap, the enrollee's contemporaneous record, available to the seat. Then she reached across the table for the instrument, opened it at schedule two, and laid it beside the old table she had just taken out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1900:The queue had shortened since the first days. The intake had learned the counter's hours and stopped coming in a body. A single Ash first-year stood ahead of him with a form she had filled in twice, once in pencil and once over the pencil in ink. Behind the counter the desk clerk added his column by the window. Gault's door stood shut. And at the copying table, under the shelf with the small unmarked case on it, the teaching assistant rose to take the first-year's sheet and read it from the name to the signature at his even pace, and stamped it, and tore the slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:1998:He wrote it that night at the sill, with the copy of the instrument beside him and the answer's first leaf lying in his memory where he could read it with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2072:"I've read that section a great many times," he said. "A clerk reads everything, in case. I've kept this counter thirteen years and never once seen it called on." He took his finger off the notice. "You'll want to know who it will be. So shall I. I know every registrar who has sat that seat in my time, and which of them likes a long sitting, and which takes his luncheon at the eleventh bell whatever the advocate is saying. None of that is any use. The minute won't tell you, and it won't tell me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2102:"At Greyvane, in my first term," she said, to the window, "an advocate gave me a point at a sitting. A good point. One I'd spent a fortnight on. He stood up and said the archive was right and he wouldn't dispute it, and sat down. I went back to my room and was pleased with myself all evening." The cart lurched forward a yard and stopped again. "In the morning he took the case on a point I hadn't looked at, because I'd spent the whole of the night before being pleased."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2114:There were six of them inside. Cael had been told to come. Karis, Lira, Brom and Seln had not, and had come anyway, and Withrow had looked at them at the coach door one by one and told Bracken to enter them on the day's list as the house's attendance. And the house's counsel, who had come up the hill at first light with her steel rule and asked for a seat in the coach instead of in Withrow's carriage, which had gone on ahead. Lira had the red drill-book shut on her knee. Brom sat with his hands clasped between his knees and looked at the floor of the coach as if it were a floor he would have to stand on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2118:The wool exchange stood where the market street ran out at the top of the hill. It was a long stone hall raised four steps above the street on a plinth, with tall plain windows and a great double door, and in Bracken's grandfather's day, Bracken said, the whole wool clip of the valley had been sold across its floor by the bale. The trade had gone downriver long ago, and the registry, which liked large cheap buildings at the tops of hills, had taken this one, and it still smelled of sheep in the heat. When the coach door opened the smell came in warm and greasy and not at all unpleasant, like the inside of a new coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2150:The house's ran to a hundred and seventy-three items, in Karis's smallest hand. The counsel slid it over with one finger. Jent picked it up, weighed it a moment in his palm the way a man weighs a letter for its postage, opened it at the index leaf and read that, all of it, with his spectacles on. Then he initialled the receipt at the foot and passed it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2218:Seln was at the end of the bench with his eyes shut and the taped sheaf on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2232:Cael told him. Middle height and middle years. The brown coat, the cuffs mended in a different brown, the spectacles on their black cord, the inkwell with its screw lid. The way he had weighed the house's list in his palm before he opened it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2252:When he knocked and went in she was not at her desk. She was at the glass-fronted case under the framed charter on the far wall, with the key from her chain in its lock, and she did not turn round until she had taken out what she wanted and locked the case again behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2258:He sat in the visitor's chair with the folder on his knees. He opened it at the back, because that was where the house's counsel would have started.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2272:She opened her desk drawer, and took out a paper, and laid it on the folder on his knees. Then another, and another, without a word between them. The program's charter, dated the first morning of the year. The page of the house's accounts with Karis's line on it. The intake's roll, longer than any he had seen pinned to a board. The tournament's sheet with the house's placing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2364:They were in the east hall again, at the quarter to the eleventh bell, on Rooke's key. The shutters were drawn along the gallery and the two lanterns stood at either end of the painted lines. Brom had brought the jug. Karis had come this time, for the first hour, with her notebook under her arm. She sat on the gallery stair and said she would take the figures as they happened, counting each one from its start the way Cael had counted on the first night, and that nobody was to break anything she would have to spell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2386:He set it a stride in front of her and across her line, from the north wall toward the south, a road of air fifteen feet long and no wider than his shoulders, tipping toward the south. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the cracks of the painted boards woke and ran south down the lane, a pale thin stream. Then he took the read off the lane, deliberately, the way he had learned on this floor three weeks ago, and gave it back to himself, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2436:Karis, on the stair, read her column back aloud without being asked: First lane, slow through ten. Second, through ten. Third, through twelve; clean at thirteen. Not tested past three. Then she shut the notebook, said she had what she had come for, and went up to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2442:Lira lay on her back with her taped hands on her stomach, looking up at the dark shutters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2464:He thought about it on the stair, and in his room, and at the window with Hesk's book shut on his knee. In the morning he went down to the east hall before the first bell and found Rooke at the trestle with the red book, and told him: three lanes in a night, ten and ten and twelve, and nothing past three. Rooke listened without looking up. Then he wrote one word on the corner of his board, and turned the board so that Cael could not see it, and said, "Good. Now don't do it again until you know why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2470:Nobody had announced the trial beyond a line on Rooke's own board, chalked the evening before in his square capitals: PROGRAM TRIAL. SECOND-DAY, SECOND BELL. LIRA AGAINST FORCE THIRD-YEAR. RATED. But the house had read it, and the house knew what rated meant on that floor. It meant the first bout ever fought on the program's painted boards under the program's own seal, with a figure at the end of it that would go into the program's own book. By the half-bell the gallery was full along both sides. By the second bell the porters had opened the shutters on the high windows to let the heat out, and the light came down onto the white crosses in long bright bars, and there were people standing on the gallery stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2488:Cael had the rail at the north end, where he had stood on the second morning of the year to watch the red book. Brom stood on his left with his arms folded. Karis stood on his right with her notebook shut and her pen in her hand. Ephram was three steps down the gallery stair with the captain's file under his arm, where he could see the whole floor and be seen by the whole cohort, which Cael supposed was the point. The girl from the reading room's outer table had found a place against the far wall of the gallery, with her small notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2534:Karis, on his other side, had opened her notebook. She wrote one line and showed it to him without a word. First flag bought. Price: one flag on the record, in front of the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2646:"I've started her a page," he said. He opened the file a little way, enough to show a sheet folded in four inside it, new, with only three lines on it so far. "I keep one on everybody. You know that. Hers was a line a month. It's going to be longer now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2680:Karis reached across without breaking step, closed the notebook on his pencil, turned it over, and opened it again from the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2712:The second envelope came up with the evening post. Cael saw it on the post-room shelf when he went for his own letters, a square cream envelope with a dark red seal pressed with the bar and the ring, and he knew the seal. Brom took it from the shelf at the eighth bell. He did not open it in the post room or the court. He took it upstairs and shut his door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2718:"It's on the table," she said quietly. "On top of the docket. Edges level. Not opened."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2726:On the third evening he opened the letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2746:Bracken read it from the address to the signature. Then he took the house's budget book from under the counter and opened it at her line, and entered the sum against it in his small upright hand, and wrote the request's number beside the sum, and blotted both.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2756:She took out her notebook and turned it over, and opened it from the back, where the six slips from the long table lived in their pocket behind the last leaf with Brom's two dates beside them. On the inside of the back board she wrote the request's number and Greyvane: the founders' hands, and under it she ruled three short lines and left them empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2762:"Then I'll have three empty lines, and I'll know exactly what they cost." She shut the book. "It feels like being trusted with a key by somebody who'll notice if you lose it. I've never been so careful in my life."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2774:Cael watched the girl through the open door. She looked at the line Karis had touched. She looked at the closed volume. Her face went red from the collar up, and then it set, the jaw first and then the mouth, the way Lira's set on the painted floor when the Stone stood on the cross she should have been on. She opened the volume and began to read. She did not ask anybody anything. She turned pages, went back, turned forward again, went to the index and found the index wanting, went to the digests on the lower shelf without being told they existed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2824:Cael counted the steps of the wool exchange through the coach window before the door was opened, because counting was cheaper than deciding what to feel. Four steps, the width of the building. Thirty-one people on them. Nine of them correspondents, with folding boards under their arms and pencils behind their ears and the air of men who had been waiting since first light for somebody to step down from a coach. Two in the dark coats and badges of the river guild. The rest were Ostrand's own: clerks, factors, a woman with a market basket who had plainly stopped on her way somewhere else and forgotten where. They had come early to the top of the town to see a chair with no name on it decide about a boy with none either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2844:Cael opened his mouth to say his own name, the one people said, and the house's counsel spoke before he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2876:He came to the wool exchange at the first bell on the fortieth day, an hour before anybody else, because the rotation manual said the junior chair would be in its place an hour before the sitting, and he had been in his place an hour before every sitting he had ever been posted to. He laid out the junior chair's things on the records table in the manual's order. First the minute-book, opened to a clean leaf. The receipt book for exhibits. The door log's copy, which the officer at the side door would send in with the porter at the half-bell, to be checked against the list. Two pens. Sand. A clean blotter. He squared each one to the table's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2878:His right hand ached at the web of the thumb, as it did every morning now. He opened it and closed it twice under the table, where nobody could see, and then he trusted it with a pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2880:The senior chair came in at the half-bell, a district records officer named on Havel's sheet only by his grade, a heavy, competent man who nodded to him and sat and opened his own book and did not speak to him again all morning. That was correct. The senior chair kept the minute. The junior chair kept the times, the receipts and the door log, and checked the one against the others, and spoke when spoken to. Havel had sat in a great many junior chairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2890:The seat opened at the second bell. The clerk read the designation; Havel wrote the minute it ended. The petitioner's procurator entered the petition. Jent stood at his table with his leaf in one hand, read the petition's working paragraphs in an even voice that gave no one of them more weight than another, named his nine exhibits and sat; Havel wrote the minute he stood and the minute he sat. Nine minutes. The respondent's counsel entered the answer. She rose, small and grey with a steel rule in her hand, and summarized eleven pounds in a voice that did not hurry and did not linger; twenty-three minutes. Havel took the receipts for both lists of exhibits in the receipt book, nine items and a hundred and seventy-three, and wrote the numbers, and initialled them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2902:He had been told, once, a long time ago, in a room with the door shut, that the mark was not his clearance and he was to let it be. He had let it be. He had only written down that it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2916:The long room stood when the seat rose. Cael was on his feet behind Withrow's chair, and so he saw her bow from behind: the line of her grey collar going down, and stopping, and the back of her neck held straight. He had watched her bow on the house's steps to visiting officers for two years and could have told its depth with his eyes shut. This was the same. It was the registry's own measure, the one she had been taught at twenty and had given across a platform like that one for twenty years from the officers' side, and she gave it now to a hood with no face under it as she would have given it to a registrar she had dined with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:2942:Seln was at the far end of the bench with his eyes shut. He had not said anything since the wool exchange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3014:His left hand lay on the blotter beside the petition. Some years ago it had begun to dislike mornings; lately it had added evenings, and it did not much care for noon. He opened it and closed it, twice, slowly, and watched it do as it was told. Three trays stood at his right in the order he had kept since before he had a desk worth the name, in, held, done, and the petition belonged in none of them yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3016:He opened the lowest drawer at the far end of the long cabinet, where he kept the few things in the room that were his, and took out a letter he had kept for some years without ever deciding why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3020:The hand was careful, and had plainly practised on other paper before it trusted itself to this sheet. Her father had been a district officer on the coast who took money to enter a rank in a station book that no frame had read. There had been one other, before him: a hill-station keeper who let a cracked housing read high for six years and covered it with false returns. Both findings were his, both sound, both sent down this stair; and somewhere below, in an office whose door he had never opened, the rest had followed. He had answered the girl himself, with one word, because a longer answer would only have given her another sentence to wait on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3084:It was the outer wrapper of a seat-courier's packet, heavy grey paper stamped across in red, BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND. He looked at it for some time. Then he looked at the boy's folder lying shut on his blotter, and opened it, and found on top of his own nine leaves a single sheet of registry-seat stock with a seal pressed into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3114:He opened the lowest drawer to see that the folder was in it. It was. It had a tab, and the tab had a heading in his own hand, the only one of its kind he had written in forty years, because every finding he had ever made had come with somewhere to be sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3138:Rooke sat at his trestle with the red book. The Stone sat beside him with his hands folded on his stomach, a broad patient man from the river academy who had come up the hill for the year to teach a Wind fighter to think in courses. Down at the near end, the program clerk had two ledgers open in front of her this morning instead of one. Cael noticed that, and noticed that she wrote in the second only when the gallery door opened, and he did not think anything of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3170:Rooke shut the red book. At the end of the trestle the clerk wrote something in the second ledger, blotted it, and closed that too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3230:Withrow got up and went to the shelf behind her desk, and took down a thin book bound in faded blue, and opened it near the middle, and laid it on the blotter facing them. It was the registry's drafting book, the one its clerks were given in their second year. The page was a list of model refusals, numbered. Beside the thirtieth, in the margin, somebody had written in a young, very upright hand: the kindest one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3250:She did not read anything aloud this time. She found the two provisions, the old one and the new, and laid the volume open at the old one with her finger beside its last two words, shall receive. Then she got the digest down from the bottom shelf, the one her assistant had found for herself on the day of find it, and opened it at the table of uses at its back, and turned it round so that they could see the line for themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3402:The last one had come on the thirty-first day, and Brom had kept it two nights on his table before he opened it. This one had come in fourteen. He looked at it. He picked it up and turned it over once in his big hands, and looked at the seal, whole, the bar and the ring. Then he put it, unopened, into the left inside pocket of his coat, where the summons was, and the docket, and the confirmation, and the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3404:Nobody asked. That had been the arrangement since the recess, and it held. Lira tied her bundle with a knot she did not usually use. Karis opened her volume again at the page she had just closed. Cael watched Brom's hand come away from the pocket and settle on the board, and then, a moment later, go back to the pocket and rest flat on the cloth over it, and stay there, as if the letter might get out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3410:"If he asked, I'd sit with him while he did. I'd read it to him if he wanted, and never say one word about it after." She climbed two more steps. "He won't ask. He's carrying it the way he carries everything, so nobody else has to. And the worst of it is that's exactly what we'd all want him to do, if it were any of us." She stopped at her own landing. "So we don't ask. And he carries it. And the letter sits in his coat getting heavier." She went in at her door and shut it quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3420:The door from the landing opened, and the assistant stood there in her cloak with her face pale round the mouth and red at the cheekbones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3426:The girl shut the door. "At the end she said thank you in a very small voice and went away. Before that she kept looking at the stair." She swallowed. "Before that, I'd told her about the ninety-one. And the paragraph numbers. And that the next sitting's on the Fifth-day, and that the petitioner's conceded the provision is valid and doesn't allege fraud, and that the Compact's own inspection looked at the provision and it stood." She was going backward through it as Karis had told her to, and her voice steadied as she went. "And at the start I told her he's enrolled under a lawful provision, adjudicated at Greyvane on the public record, under seal, with the grant and two renewals on paper. Because she asked me what the enrollee was really like. A Mire girl from the north stair, on the covered walk, after supper. I hardly know her." She stopped. "That was the beginning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3490:The rest of the room was what it always was. Gault at the trestle in the middle of the north side, with the instrument case set down very straight beside him. The Ash instructor on his right with her book against her chest, the Mire instructor on his left with a pencil behind each ear. The desk clerk at the trestle's end with the wing's book open. And at the east end of the room, at the rail under the high windows, the cohort, more of them than usual, with Ephram in the middle holding his soft-folded chart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3514:She opened the way the textbook said a Current should open, which was the way she always opened: two glides in, the second one bending out wide to his left, so that the strike came off the end of the curve from a place he had not been looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3524:He did not burst. He stepped the way an honest Iron steps against a Current, a fraction late, and met the end of her curve with his forearm up. Her strike came off the bend and took him across the forearm, flat and fast, and the give was there, Reydan's give, waiting to drink the whole of the blow into the arm and keep it. He shut it. He let the blow go on up the arm the way it would have gone in anybody, into the elbow and then the shoulder, and felt it arrive there like a door slammed somewhere in a house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3596:He was on one knee on the bottom step, threading a chain through the stair's rail. It was a long chain, the kind the porters used on the cart-shed doors, and he had already run it twice round the newel post. As the four of them came up he drew the last link through, set a padlock in it, and snapped the padlock shut. Then he stood up and hung a card on the chain, square in the middle, where it would be read by anybody who came to the stair. The card was in his square capitals. PROGRAM FLOOR CLOSED TO OBSERVERS. BY ORDER.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3602:Rooke answered with one word. Cael did not hear that either, but Ephram, who had come out of wing three behind the visitors and was close enough, told him afterward what it was. It was No. The Rhagen man had then asked, very politely, by whose order, since the card said by order, and Rooke had looked at him for a while and said, "Mine," and that had been the second word. Then Rooke turned his back on them and went in at the hall's side door, and a moment later the shutters began to close along the gallery's high windows, one after another, all down the north wall, from the inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3634:Gault came out of his office into the passage behind wing three while Cael was still putting his coat on over the throbbing arm. He had the wing's book under his arm and the instrument case in his hand, and he stopped, which he did not usually do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3636:"There will be a line about observers in the minute of today's hours," he said. "I have written it. Four officers of other houses present in the gallery, by the lodge book. The provision's demonstration conducted as on every other day." He looked at Cael over the case. "That second sentence is true. I wished it to be on the page in case anyone later wonders whether it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3640:"Don't thank me for writing down what happened." Gault shifted the case to his other hand. "That is the only thing I am for." He went on down the passage, and then, without turning round, added, "Your forearm. Put cold on it. I have written that down too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3644:"He's shut it," she said. "He didn't tell me why. He doesn't tell anyone why. He told the clerk to keep the book open and write none." She leaned on the wall. "So now there's a ledger in a closed room with none in it, every day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3658:Lira stayed where she was, leaning on the wall, with her eyes half shut, listening to nothing in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3660:"He shut my light," she said after a while, "and then he came out in the cold to tell me how to do without it." She opened her eyes. "I can't stay angry at him. It's very inconvenient."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3662:He wrote a second line under her name in the column that night. The east hall shut and shuttered by Rooke's order, on account of who was watching me. She trains under lamps. She says it's fine. He looked at the last three words a long while before he blotted them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3688:Karis had told them in the coach what an exhibits sitting was, on the way down the river road that morning, with the house's two cases on Brom's knees because he would not let them ride on the roof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3698:Cael noticed it before he noticed anything else about the second sitting, because he had been watching the benches instead of the clerk for some while by then. Halfway along the second row a correspondent had laid his board on the bench beside him and folded his arms and shut his eyes. At the back a woman in a market shawl had got out a half-knitted stocking. Two clerks from the river guild were playing some game on their knees with a stub of pencil and a scrap of paper. Nobody was angry and nobody had stopped believing anything. They had come down the market street to see a case and somebody was reading them an inventory. At the front benches not one pencil had moved for nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3754:Because he should not have been. The registrar's counter was open from the first bell to the eighth, and in two years Cael had never known it open with nobody behind it. If Bracken had to be elsewhere, his senior clerk stood in his place; if there was no clerk, the counter shut and a card hung on the grille. But a first-year had come up from the records hall ten minutes ago, white-faced, to report that he had gone down about a lost key and found the counter lamp lit, the baskets half dealt on the trestle, and nobody there at all. Bracken had crossed the fountain court with this sheet in his hand at a pace nobody on the hill had ever seen from him. He had walked the length of the refectory between the benches with a hundred heads turning after him and had not looked at one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3792:The post room was behind the records hall's counter, a narrow whitewashed room with a long sorting bench under the window and pigeonholes from floor to ceiling along the other wall. The district courier's bag came up the hill in the dark and was opened there at first light. Usually the post porter opened it, a broad slow man who had done the sort for twenty years. This morning the post porter was sitting on a stool in the corner with his left arm strapped across his chest, looking unhappy, and the man at the sorting bench with the bag open in front of him was Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3818:It had no seal, only a fold tucked in on itself, the way people fold a letter who do not want anybody to think they took trouble over it. Cael did not turn it over in the post room. He carried the house's bundle up to the residence and left it on the hall table. He took the one letter on up the stair and opened it at his own window, where letters were opened in that house, with the light coming up grey over the river and the fountain not yet running below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3892:Seln opened the book at the week's first page and began to read it, and went on listening, and said "Mm" at the right places, while the lodge-man told him the whole of the carter. Then he told him about the carter's father, who had also scraped the gatepost, and what he had said to that one in his day, which had been a good deal more than he had said to the son. It took a quarter of an hour. Cael stood by the stove, where Seln had put him with a glance, and drank the tea the lodge-man had poured him too, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/clean-long-form-narration/The-Compact-s-Hand-Clean-Long-Form-Narration.txt:3908:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
