e-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:357:He read it two times. Then he opened the little stove's door with the poker and laid the sheet on the coals, flat, the way she would have expected him to, and stood with the poker in his hand until it was ash and the ash had broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:417:A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:435:He reached up and took the case off the shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:451:The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:453:He was at the window, at the sill, with Hesk's book open and the lamp lit and the notice still pinned to the frame. He looked round, and his face did what it always did when a door opened, which was nothing, and then it went still in a different way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:455:Seln unbuttoned his coat and set the case on the sill beside the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:461:The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:465:Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:469:They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:485:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:503:The boy almost smiled. Then he did not, and looked at the case again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:513:Then he made the duplicate, as he always did, word for word, and opened the cupboard under the copying table and put it into the thickest bundle on the end of the row, the one with the name of this hill on its label.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:517:He put the case back on the shelf above the copying table, where it had stood for two years and where nobody had ever once looked for it, and blew out the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:525:"The third ring," said Bracken. "They shut it to everybody at the third. Carts, horses, people on foot. Since the year the middle arch went, which was before my time." He took the glass back. "The ferryman tied up at noon. I watched him do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:561:There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:565:He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:587:*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:609:His right hand ached at the web of the thumb. It had ached every morning for a year. He opened it and closed it twice under the table, out of habit, though there was nobody in the room to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:611:It was a good rubbing. It was also a good seal. That was the first thing he noticed and the thing he went on noticing all afternoon: whoever had cut the die had cut it properly. The river ran the right way. The scale's beam was level. The beading round the rim was even and close, and in the beading, at the bottom, where every registry die carried its cutter's number, there were four small figures that the pencil had picked out faintly, as a pencil does when it passes over something raised. He took the reading glass out of his kit and bent over them until he was sure of all four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:623:So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:667:*The seal on the instrument constituting the facilities office named in the order presented at Halcenvane, and on the order itself (registrar's rubbing): absent from the district's impression book; absent from the registry's schedule of seals of office, this year's and last; its rim number absent from the cutters' issue ledger, which runs without a line between the numbers on either side of it. Checked three ways. Absent from every registry of seals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:693:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:695:Seln sat in the far corner with his eyes shut and his hands in his lap. He had sat like that in the coach on every road all year. Cael had long ago stopped thinking it meant he was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:707:Lira shut her eyes again. "He's always thinking it at them," she said, to the inside of her coat, and was asleep before anybody could answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:709:Cael sat with his back to the horses and turned the three words over. They were true. Every figure on every card in Brom's two cases had been earned on the house's floors, by him, on purpose, to the decimal. They were his in the way a rotation page was his, because he had written it the night before. He thought that if the clerk had asked whether they were all of him, he would not have known what to say, and he was glad the form did not ask it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:711:The bank road ran west along the near side of the river, high on its terrace, with the water on the left below them roaring in the dark. Twice the wheels went into standing water to the hub and the coach lurched, and the coachman talked to the horses until they leaned into it and pulled out. Once a fallen branch lay across the road and Brom got down and dragged it off into the ditch and got back in, wet to the knees, and put the cases back on his lap without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:763:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:781:He opened the door and got down into the white dust before anyone could stop him, and walked to the coach's front wheel on the south side, and bent to it as if to look at the hub. Then he went down on one knee on the road and put his palm flat on the chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:835:He did not hurry. He stepped down into the dust with the two record cases, and set them on the coach step, side by side, squared, and then walked forward along the foot of the south bank, under the lip, into the channel. He stopped in the middle of it. He turned round to face the cart and the culvert and the gully, and set his feet, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:995:It leaned. He felt the whole cut give a little along one line, as a board gives when you step on its end. His ears closed and opened. The chalk dust woke up on the crown and ran in a thin white stream along the line he had laid, east, past her, toward the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1148:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1168:"Four bursts. On chalk. That's the road's whole free budget and not one over it. I counted." She shut her eyes. "The ankle wasn't the bursts. The ankle was the ditch. I came down on the edge of the camber where it falls off, and the edge went." She opened them again. "Write it down that way. I want it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1176:Karis had gone down the gully to the culvert. She came back up it with her right hand wrapped in her own wet handkerchief and held up in front of her like something she had been asked to carry and must not drop. The fingers that stuck out of the end of the wrapping were red to the second knuckle and already shining. She sat down on the parapet beside the milestone, which said seven, and took her notebook out of her coat with her left hand, and opened it on her knee, and wrote something with the pencil in her left hand, slowly, the letters going wide and childish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1188:They had come for him. Every one of the six had been there for him, and no one else. The hobbles were for him and the hood was for him and the wagon in the gully, wherever it was, had a door that was meant to shut on him. And the four people he had come here with had paid for every yard of the road. Lira had paid in an ankle, Brom in a shoulder, Karis in a hand she would have to write with for a week. Even the coachman's horse had paid, standing there trembling with the coachman's hand on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1234:He shut the book and put it back inside his coat, against his ribs, where it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1236:The coachman had the horses back in the traces. Brom lifted Lira into the coach in his one good arm as if she weighed nothing, and set her on the seat with her foot up on the record cases, and got in after her. Karis climbed in holding her hand up. Seln shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1268:She was sitting with her strapped foot on the record cases and her back in the corner and her face still white, and she said it before anybody else could say anything. "Six people with hobbles and a wagon with rings in the floor tried to take him off a road this morning, two hours from here. The seat's sitting in there. Somebody in that room should write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1276:Lira opened her mouth and shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1350:Jent had sat at his table through all of it with his spectacles on their black cord and his travelling inkwell shut, and said nothing, because a documentary sitting gave the petitioner nothing to say. As the long room emptied he gathered his single folder and came along behind their table toward the door, and stopped by Cael's chair for a moment, and looked at the chalk on Cael's sleeve and on Lira's strapped boot and on all their knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1390:She did not interrupt them. Karis told it, in order, and Cael put in the parts Karis had not seen, and Brom said nothing, and Lira said the thing about the ditch. When they were done Withrow stood a long moment with her hands behind her back. Then she went to her desk and took out a small book bound in black cloth that was not the house's book, and opened it, and wrote one line in it, standing, and blotted it, and shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1428:Seln had moved it three days, without a word to anybody, the morning after the road. He had read the coach's arrival and the infirmary's lamps and the five of them coming up the stair with chalk on their knees, and he had gone to the wing before the first bell and copied out the coming week's schedule fair a day before it was due, with five lines moved. He had not asked whether Cael was well enough. He had not asked anything. He had simply moved the day to where a man with strapped ribs would be able to climb a stair, and posted it on three doors, where it looked like nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1480:Something large in him lifted its head. He had never found a better way to put it, though Karis had asked him for one more than once: a great slow weight that had lain quiet since the dawn on the second floor, raising itself to look at whatever had just come into the house. His ears closed and opened. The air in the room changed its weight, not much, the way the air changes before thunder. On the sill, in the corner where the porter's girl never quite reached with her cloth, the little drift of grey dust woke up and began to run, slowly, in a thin stream along the edge of the wood, toward the window frame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1510:Karis had gone, and Brom had gone to the infirmary to have his sling looked at, and for the quarter hour between their shifts Cael was alone in the room for the first time since the road, because the rota said *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.* and the healer had said that Brom's sling came first. He did not mind. He lay with his eyes shut and said *no* to the cup on the saucer and the lamp on the book, and counted the bell from the lecture range, and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1526:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1554:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's flat hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1580:He shut the door, carefully, all the way, until the latch clicked. Then he sat down on the end of the bed, because Lira had the chair, and nobody said anything else until Karis came at the fifth bell with her squared paper and stopped in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and looked at the three of them, and asked what had happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1640:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1646:"The doorway's the fire's," said Brom. "We'll write it down. Next time there's a leaf, it'll have a line for doorways." He shut his eyes again. "Go to sleep. I'm counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1656:Somebody had shut the window in the night against the wind, and its latch had dropped into the keeper with a small iron click that he had heard in his sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1684:Lira opened her eyes and found him looking at the washstand with his lips moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1747:Rooke stood back and looked at the board for a moment with the chalk in his hand. Then he put the chalk in his pocket and went in to the program floor and shut the door behind him, and Cael heard the bolt go home on the inside, which it never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1767:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1799:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been squared to the table's edge and turned over so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid flat with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1817:"I shut grievances," he said. "There was one waiting. A first-year with a complaint about the meal rota. I told him to come back next Third-day with two complaints and I'd hear them both at once." He looked faintly appalled at himself. "I've never shut it early. Not once in two years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1823:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1861:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1867:He sang it wrong. He had picked up the tune from the verses before and he came in at the end with great confidence and got the third line of it wrong, cheerfully, all on one note, and Brom stopped singing and opened his eyes and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1871:"The first-years," said Ephram. "Somebody on the ground floor has been singing those three lines under the residence windows since the night before the year opened. He's never once got the third line right. I thought it went like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1897:Then Ephram took the captain's file off his knees and opened it, without turning round, and took out three sheets of close-ruled paper, and held them up over his shoulder without looking, so that Cael could take them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1907:Cael gave him the sheets back. Ephram put them in the file and shut it and folded his hands on top of it again, and looked at the window, and did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1911:He opened Hesk's parcel last, when the cake was eaten and the kettle had been filled again and had once again refused to boil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1915:It was made of brown leather, the good thick kind that Hesk bought for instrument cases and would not use for anything that did not deserve it. It was plain. It had a flap that came down over the front and a strap long enough to go across a man's body and rest on the opposite hip, so that it would not swing when he walked, or ran. The buckle on the strap was brass, and it was not a bought buckle. Cael knew it at once. He had watched Hesk file buckles at the long bench since he was small enough to sleep in the shavings under it, and this one had Hesk's file marks on the inside of the tongue where nobody would see them, and a little bevel on the frame that Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1963:He took the book out of its pocket and opened it to a clean leaf and wrote the inventory, because a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1981:He opened the door. Seln stood in the corridor in his coat, with his hands at his sides and nothing in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1993:Cael shut the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1995:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
