editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-31.md:147:It leaned. He felt the whole cut give a little along one line, as a board gives when you step on its end. His ears closed and opened. The chalk dust woke up on the crown and ran in a thin white stream along the line he had laid, east, past her, toward the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:9:Rooke stood back and looked at the board for a moment with the chalk in his hand. Then he put the chalk in his pocket and went in to the program floor and shut the door behind him, and Cael heard the bolt go home on the inside, which it never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:29:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:61:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been squared to the table's edge and turned over so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid flat with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:79:"I shut grievances," he said. "There was one waiting. A first-year with a complaint about the meal rota. I told him to come back next Third-day with two complaints and I'd hear them both at once." He looked faintly appalled at himself. "I've never shut it early. Not once in two years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:85:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:123:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:129:He sang it wrong. He had picked up the tune from the verses before and he came in at the end with great confidence and got the third line of it wrong, cheerfully, all on one note, and Brom stopped singing and opened his eyes and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:133:"The first-years," said Ephram. "Somebody on the ground floor has been singing those three lines under the residence windows since the night before the year opened. He's never once got the third line right. I thought it went like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:159:Then Ephram took the captain's file off his knees and opened it, without turning round, and took out three sheets of close-ruled paper, and held them up over his shoulder without looking, so that Cael could take them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:169:Cael gave him the sheets back. Ephram put them in the file and shut it and folded his hands on top of it again, and looked at the window, and did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:173:He opened Hesk's parcel last, when the cake was eaten and the kettle had been filled again and had once again refused to boil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:177:It was made of brown leather, the good thick kind that Hesk bought for instrument cases and would not use for anything that did not deserve it. It was plain. It had a flap that came down over the front and a strap long enough to go across a man's body and rest on the opposite hip, so that it would not swing when he walked, or ran. The buckle on the strap was brass, and it was not a bought buckle. Cael knew it at once. He had watched Hesk file buckles at the long bench since he was small enough to sleep in the shavings under it, and this one had Hesk's file marks on the inside of the tongue where nobody would see them, and a little bevel on the frame that Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:225:He took the book out of its pocket and opened it to a clean leaf and wrote the inventory, because a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:243:He opened the door. Seln stood in the corridor in his coat, with his hands at his sides and nothing in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:255:Cael shut the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-34.md:257:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:17:Seln had moved it three days, without a word to anybody, the morning after the road. He had read the coach's arrival and the infirmary's lamps and the five of them coming up the stair with chalk on their knees, and he had gone to the wing before the first bell and copied out the coming week's schedule fair a day before it was due, with five lines moved. He had not asked whether Cael was well enough. He had not asked anything. He had simply moved the day to where a man with strapped ribs would be able to climb a stair, and posted it on three doors, where it looked like nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:69:Something large in him lifted its head. He had never found a better way to put it, though Karis had asked him for one more than once: a great slow weight that had lain quiet since the dawn on the second floor, raising itself to look at whatever had just come into the house. His ears closed and opened. The air in the room changed its weight, not much, the way the air changes before thunder. On the sill, in the corner where the porter's girl never quite reached with her cloth, the little drift of grey dust woke up and began to run, slowly, in a thin stream along the edge of the wood, toward the window frame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:99:Karis had gone, and Brom had gone to the infirmary to have his sling looked at, and for the quarter hour between their shifts Cael was alone in the room for the first time since the road, because the rota said *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.* and the healer had said that Brom's sling came first. He did not mind. He lay with his eyes shut and said *no* to the cup on the saucer and the lamp on the book, and counted the bell from the lecture range, and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:115:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:143:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's flat hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:169:He shut the door, carefully, all the way, until the latch clicked. Then he sat down on the end of the bed, because Lira had the chair, and nobody said anything else until Karis came at the fifth bell with her squared paper and stopped in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and looked at the three of them, and asked what had happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:229:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:235:"The doorway's the fire's," said Brom. "We'll write it down. Next time there's a leaf, it'll have a line for doorways." He shut his eyes again. "Go to sleep. I'm counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:245:Somebody had shut the window in the night against the wind, and its latch had dropped into the keeper with a small iron click that he had heard in his sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-33.md:273:Lira opened her eyes and found him looking at the washstand with his lips moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:38:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:58:"Four bursts. On chalk. That's the road's whole free budget and not one over it. I counted." She shut her eyes. "The ankle wasn't the bursts. The ankle was the ditch. I came down on the edge of the camber where it falls off, and the edge went." She opened them again. "Write it down that way. I want it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:66:Karis had gone down the gully to the culvert. She came back up it with her right hand wrapped in her own wet handkerchief and held up in front of her like something she had been asked to carry and must not drop. The fingers that stuck out of the end of the wrapping were red to the second knuckle and already shining. She sat down on the parapet beside the milestone, which said seven, and took her notebook out of her coat with her left hand, and opened it on her knee, and wrote something with the pencil in her left hand, slowly, the letters going wide and childish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:78:They had come for him. Every one of the six had been there for him, and no one else. The hobbles were for him and the hood was for him and the wagon in the gully, wherever it was, had a door that was meant to shut on him. And the four people he had come here with had paid for every yard of the road. Lira had paid in an ankle, Brom in a shoulder, Karis in a hand she would have to write with for a week. Even the coachman's horse had paid, standing there trembling with the coachman's hand on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:124:He shut the book and put it back inside his coat, against his ribs, where it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:126:The coachman had the horses back in the traces. Brom lifted Lira into the coach in his one good arm as if she weighed nothing, and set her on the seat with her foot up on the record cases, and got in after her. Karis climbed in holding her hand up. Seln shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:158:She was sitting with her strapped foot on the record cases and her back in the corner and her face still white, and she said it before anybody else could say anything. "Six people with hobbles and a wagon with rings in the floor tried to take him off a road this morning, two hours from here. The seat's sitting in there. Somebody in that room should write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:166:Lira opened her mouth and shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:240:Jent had sat at his table through all of it with his spectacles on their black cord and his travelling inkwell shut, and said nothing, because a documentary sitting gave the petitioner nothing to say. As the long room emptied he gathered his single folder and came along behind their table toward the door, and stopped by Cael's chair for a moment, and looked at the chalk on Cael's sleeve and on Lira's strapped boot and on all their knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/pre-repair/chapter-32.md:280:She did not interrupt them. Karis told it, in order, and Cael put in the parts Karis had not seen, and Brom said nothing, and Lira said the thing about the ditch. When they were done Withrow stood a long moment with her hands behind her back. Then she went to her desk and took out a small book bound in black cloth that was not the house's book, and opened it, and wrote one line in it, standing, and blotted it, and shut it.
