editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1086:The seven chapters form a complete local movement: the threat advances from suspect paper to an attempted roadside seizure; Cael survives by acquiring and reversing the operative's craft; the new fragment then creates an uncontrolled danger that he begins to integrate; the movement closes on fellowship, cost, and the promised opening of Seln's case. The larger story is deliberately unfinished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1090:Interest caught immediately on the question of where the paper's mark sat. The sixteenth-inch displacement turns an almost invisible physical detail into a trail, and Seln's decision to spend two of four lifelong favours gives that trail moral weight. My strongest point of commitment came in chapter 29, when he reinterprets the catechism and admits, “He believed him.” From there, the case under his coat and the warning that “the fourth does not knock” make the road feel inevitable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1096:The event I expect next is the opening of Seln's case, with its contents connecting the counterfeit paper and impossible seal to the “green corridor” he has avoided remembering for twelve years. I also expect the uncontrolled doorway incident to matter beyond this movement; naming has made the fragment idle, not harmless.
