editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1693:    11	The records hall was dark except for the one lamp on the counter. Rain had not come yet, but it was in the air, and the tall windows along the east side showed nothing but grey. Bracken stood on the little step he kept under the counter for the purpose, with the clock's glass door open and the key in his hand, and turned it eleven times. He always turned it eleven times. Seln had counted it on perhaps eighty Seventh-days and had never once heard a twelfth. Then the registrar shut the glass, stepped down, put the step away with his foot, and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1715:    33	Bracken looked at him over the spectacles for a little while. Seln let him look. Then the registrar nodded once, the small nod he gave to a form that had been filled in properly, and opened the day-book, and dipped his pen. He did not ask why. In two years he had never once asked Seln why about anything, and Seln had never once stopped being grateful for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1735:    53	He knew exactly where each of them was. A man in his trade kept that list the way a carter keeps his water barrels, full and lashed down and never once opened on the easy stretches of the road, because the one time you need water is the one time there is none to be had. A favour was not money. It could not be lent or broken into change. You spent it whole, once, and afterward the person who had owed it knew something about you that they had not known before: that you had wanted something enough to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1994:    57	That had been the last morning, in a room over a ship's chandler's on a quay, with six young men on benches who were not yet anything, and a cat asleep in the sun on the window-ledge. She had been old even then. She had opened every lesson by telling them that nobody in that room would ever be told the whole of anything and that the sooner they stopped minding the better; she had spent the weeks between on the craft, how to read a subject, how to write a return that was true and useful and no longer than it needed to be, how to live a year in a town and leave nothing behind but a room that had been let. The grades she had kept for the end, so that they would know what the other side of the table did, and where they themselves stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2022:    85	A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2040:   103	He reached up and took the case off the shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2056:   119	The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2058:   121	He was at the window, at the sill, with Hesk's book open and the lamp lit and the notice still pinned to the frame. He looked round, and his face did what it always did when a door opened, which was nothing, and then it went still in a different way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2060:   123	Seln unbuttoned his coat and set the case on the sill beside the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2066:   129	The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2070:   133	Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2074:   137	They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2090:   153	"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2108:   171	The boy almost smiled. Then he did not, and looked at the case again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2118:   181	Then he made the duplicate, as he always did, word for word, and opened the cupboard under the copying table and put it into the thickest bundle on the end of the row, the one with the name of this hill on its label.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2122:   185	He put the case back on the shelf above the copying table, where it had stood for two years and where nobody had ever once looked for it, and blew out the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2130:   193	"The third ring," said Bracken. "They shut it to everybody at the third. Carts, horses, people on foot. Since the year the middle arch went, which was before my time." He took the glass back. "The ferryman tied up at noon. I watched him do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2166:   229	There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2170:   233	He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2192:   255	*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*
