editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7214:**Knowledge boundaries.** Mechanism: Lira, Brom, Karis only. Seln's window names no motive and does not open the case; the "designation … graded well above" sentence is at his lawful altitude. Havel: three entries, "above his clearance", two rises; no market stranger, no Iron Skin watcher, no Level 4 explanation. Vastin: rank, name and collar only. Lira's window: hip and Fiske only; nothing of any slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7315:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7325:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's flat hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7344:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7370:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:19:His right hand ached at the web of the thumb, as it had every morning for a year. He opened it and closed it twice under the table, out of habit, though there was nobody in the room to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7373:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:33:So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7391:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7392:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:103:Seln sat in the far corner with his eyes shut and his hands in his lap. He had sat like that in the coach on every road all year. Cael had long ago stopped thinking it meant he was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7395:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:117:Cael sat with his back to the horses and turned the three words over. They were true. Every figure on every card in Brom's two cases had been earned on the house's floors, by him, on purpose, to the decimal. They were his in the way a rotation page was his, because he had written it the night before. He thought that if the clerk had asked whether they were all of him, he would not have known what to say, and he was glad the form did not ask it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7396:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:The bank road ran west along the near side of the river, high on its terrace, with the water on the left below them roaring in the dark. Twice the wheels went into standing water to the hub and the coach lurched, and the coachman talked to the horses until they leaned into it and pulled out. Once a fallen branch lay across the road and Brom got down and dragged it off into the ditch and got back in, wet to the knees, and put the cases back on his lap without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7409:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7414:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:He opened the door and got down into the white dust before anyone could stop him, and walked to the coach's front wheel on the south side, and bent to it as if to look at the hub. Then he went down on one knee on the road and put his palm flat on the chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7426:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:243:He did not hurry. He stepped down into the dust with the two record cases, and set them on the coach step, side by side, squared, and then walked forward along the foot of the south bank, under the lip, into the channel. He stopped in the middle of it. He turned round to face the cart and the culvert and the gully, and set his feet, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7431:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:9:Rooke stood back and looked at the board for a moment with the chalk in his hand. Then he put the chalk in his pocket and went in to the program floor and shut the door behind him, and Cael heard the bolt go home on the inside, which it never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7439:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7448:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been squared to the table's edge and turned over so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid flat with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7453:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7461:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:123:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7463:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:129:He sang it wrong. He had picked up the tune from the verses before and he came in at the end with great confidence and got the third line of it wrong, cheerfully, all on one note, and Brom stopped singing and opened his eyes and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7473:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:Only then did Cael look at the rest. The flap came down over everything. The strap was long enough to cross a man's body and ride on the opposite hip, so that the bag would sit still when he walked and not swing when he ran. And the buckle was not a bought buckle. Hesk's file marks were on the inside of the tongue, where only somebody who had slept in the shavings under that bench would think to look, and the frame had the little bevel Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth. He ran his thumb over the bevel. The leather round it was the heavy brown kind Hesk kept for instrument cases and would not waste on anything that did not deserve it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7488:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:255:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7531:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:149:It leaned. He felt the whole cut give a little along one line, as a board gives when you step on its end. His ears closed and opened. The chalk dust woke up on the crown and ran in a thin white stream along the line he had laid, east, past her, toward the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7572:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:That had been the last morning, in a room over a ship's chandler's on a quay, with six young men on benches who were not yet anything, and a cat asleep in the sun on the window-ledge. She had been old even then. She had opened every lesson by telling them that nobody in that room would ever be told the whole of anything and that the sooner they stopped minding the better; she had spent the weeks between on the craft, how to read a subject, how to write a return that was true and useful and no longer than it needed to be, how to live a year in a town and leave nothing behind but a room that had been let. The grades she had kept for the end, so that they would know what the other side of the table did, and where they themselves stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7577:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7584:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:119:The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7585:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:121:He was at the window, at the sill, with Hesk's book open and the lamp lit and the notice still pinned to the frame. He looked round, and his face did what it always did when a door opened, which was nothing, and then it went still in a different way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7587:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7588:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7590:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7594:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7599:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:193:"The third ring," said Bracken. "They shut it to everybody at the third. Carts, horses, people on foot. Since the year the middle arch went, which was before my time." He took the glass back. "The ferryman tied up at noon. I watched him do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7609:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:229:There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7614:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:255:*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7624:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:38:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7627:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:58:"Four bursts. On chalk. That's the road's whole free budget and not one over it. I counted." She shut her eyes. "The ankle wasn't the bursts. The ankle was the ditch. I came down on the edge of the camber where it falls off, and the edge went." She opened them again. "Write it down that way. I want it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7631:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:78:They had come for him. Every one of the six had been there for him, and no one else. The hobbles were for him and the hood was for him and the wagon in the gully, wherever it was, had a door that was meant to shut on him. And the four people he had come here with had paid for every yard of the road. Lira had paid in an ankle, Brom in a shoulder, Karis in a hand she would have to write with for a week. Even the coachman's horse had paid, standing there trembling with the coachman's hand on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7641:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:126:The coachman had the horses back in the traces. Brom lifted Lira into the coach in his one good arm as if she weighed nothing, and set her on the seat with her foot up on the record cases, and got in after her. Karis climbed in holding her hand up. Seln shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7660:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:240:Jent had sat at his table through all of it with his spectacles on their black cord and his travelling inkwell shut, and said nothing, because a documentary sitting gave the petitioner nothing to say. As the long room emptied he gathered his single folder and came along behind their table toward the door, and stopped by Cael's chair for a moment, and looked at the chalk on Cael's sleeve and on Lira's strapped boot and on all their knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7669:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:280:She did not interrupt them. Karis told it, in order, and Cael put in the parts Karis had not seen, and Brom said nothing, and Lira said the thing about the ditch. When they were done Withrow stood a long moment with her hands behind her back. Then she went to her desk and took out a small book bound in black cloth that was not the house's book, and opened it, and wrote one line in it, standing, and blotted it, and shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7674:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:The records hall was dark except for the one lamp on the counter. Rain had not come yet, but it was in the air, and the tall windows along the east side showed nothing but grey. Bracken stood on the little step he kept under the counter for the purpose, with the clock's glass door open and the key in his hand, and turned it eleven times. He always turned it eleven times. Seln had counted it on perhaps eighty Seventh-days and had never once heard a twelfth. Then the registrar shut the glass, stepped down, put the step away with his foot, and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7682:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Bracken looked at him over the spectacles for a little while. Seln let him look. Then the registrar nodded once, the small nod he gave to a form that had been filled in properly, and opened the day-book, and dipped his pen. He did not ask why. In two years he had never once asked Seln why about anything, and Seln had never once stopped being grateful for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7686:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:53:He knew exactly where each of them was. A man in his trade kept that list the way a carter keeps his water barrels, full and lashed down and never once opened on the easy stretches of the road, because the one time you need water is the one time there is none to be had. A favour was not money. It could not be lent or broken into change. You spent it whole, once, and afterward the person who had owed it knew something about you that they had not known before: that you had wanted something enough to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7807:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:149:It leaned. He felt the whole cut give a little along one line, as a board gives when you step on its end. His ears closed and opened. The chalk dust woke up on the crown and ran in a thin white stream along the line he had laid, east, past her, toward the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7847:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:That had been the last morning, in a room over a ship's chandler's on a quay, with six young men on benches who were not yet anything, and a cat asleep in the sun on the window-ledge. She had been old even then. She had opened every lesson by telling them that nobody in that room would ever be told the whole of anything and that the sooner they stopped minding the better; she had spent the weeks between on the craft, how to read a subject, how to write a return that was true and useful and no longer than it needed to be, how to live a year in a town and leave nothing behind but a room that had been let. The grades she had kept for the end, so that they would know what the other side of the table did, and where they themselves stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7852:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7859:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:119:The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7860:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:121:He was at the window, at the sill, with Hesk's book open and the lamp lit and the notice still pinned to the frame. He looked round, and his face did what it always did when a door opened, which was nothing, and then it went still in a different way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7862:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7863:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7865:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7869:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7874:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:193:"The third ring," said Bracken. "They shut it to everybody at the third. Carts, horses, people on foot. Since the year the middle arch went, which was before my time." He took the glass back. "The ferryman tied up at noon. I watched him do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7884:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:229:There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7889:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:255:*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7898:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:38:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7901:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:58:"Four bursts. On chalk. That's the road's whole free budget and not one over it. I counted." She shut her eyes. "The ankle wasn't the bursts. The ankle was the ditch. I came down on the edge of the camber where it falls off, and the edge went." She opened them again. "Write it down that way. I want it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7905:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:78:They had come for him. Every one of the six had been there for him, and no one else. The hobbles were for him and the hood was for him and the wagon in the gully, wherever it was, had a door that was meant to shut on him. And the four people he had come here with had paid for every yard of the road. Lira had paid in an ankle, Brom in a shoulder, Karis in a hand she would have to write with for a week. Even the coachman's horse had paid, standing there trembling with the coachman's hand on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7915:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:126:The coachman had the horses back in the traces. Brom lifted Lira into the coach in his one good arm as if she weighed nothing, and set her on the seat with her foot up on the record cases, and got in after her. Karis climbed in holding her hand up. Seln shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7934:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:240:Jent had sat at his table through all of it with his spectacles on their black cord and his travelling inkwell shut, and said nothing, because a documentary sitting gave the petitioner nothing to say. As the long room emptied he gathered his single folder and came along behind their table toward the door, and stopped by Cael's chair for a moment, and looked at the chalk on Cael's sleeve and on Lira's strapped boot and on all their knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7943:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:280:She did not interrupt them. Karis told it, in order, and Cael put in the parts Karis had not seen, and Brom said nothing, and Lira said the thing about the ditch. When they were done Withrow stood a long moment with her hands behind her back. Then she went to her desk and took out a small book bound in black cloth that was not the house's book, and opened it, and wrote one line in it, standing, and blotted it, and shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7947:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:9:Rooke stood back and looked at the board for a moment with the chalk in his hand. Then he put the chalk in his pocket and went in to the program floor and shut the door behind him, and Cael heard the bolt go home on the inside, which it never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7955:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7964:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been squared to the table's edge and turned over so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid flat with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7969:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7977:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:123:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7979:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:129:He sang it wrong. He had picked up the tune from the verses before and he came in at the end with great confidence and got the third line of it wrong, cheerfully, all on one note, and Brom stopped singing and opened his eyes and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7989:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:Only then did Cael look at the rest. The flap came down over everything. The strap was long enough to cross a man's body and ride on the opposite hip, so that the bag would sit still when he walked and not swing when he ran. And the buckle was not a bought buckle. Hesk's file marks were on the inside of the tongue, where only somebody who had slept in the shavings under that bench would think to look, and the frame had the little bevel Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth. He ran his thumb over the bevel. The leather round it was the heavy brown kind Hesk kept for instrument cases and would not waste on anything that did not deserve it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8004:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:255:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8029:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8039:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's flat hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8058:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8079:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:The records hall was dark except for the one lamp on the counter. Rain had not come yet, but it was in the air, and the tall windows along the east side showed nothing but grey. Bracken stood on the little step he kept under the counter for the purpose, with the clock's glass door open and the key in his hand, and turned it eleven times. He always turned it eleven times. Seln had counted it on perhaps eighty Seventh-days and had never once heard a twelfth. Then the registrar shut the glass, stepped down, put the step away with his foot, and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8087:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Bracken looked at him over the spectacles for a little while. Seln let him look. Then the registrar nodded once, the small nod he gave to a form that had been filled in properly, and opened the day-book, and dipped his pen. He did not ask why. In two years he had never once asked Seln why about anything, and Seln had never once stopped being grateful for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8091:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:53:He knew exactly where each of them was. A man in his trade kept that list the way a carter keeps his water barrels, full and lashed down and never once opened on the easy stretches of the road, because the one time you need water is the one time there is none to be had. A favour was not money. It could not be lent or broken into change. You spent it whole, once, and afterward the person who had owed it knew something about you that they had not known before: that you had wanted something enough to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8143:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:19:His right hand ached at the web of the thumb, as it had every morning for a year. He opened it and closed it twice under the table, out of habit, though there was nobody in the room to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8146:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:33:So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8164:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8165:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:103:Seln sat in the far corner with his eyes shut and his hands in his lap. He had sat like that in the coach on every road all year. Cael had long ago stopped thinking it meant he was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8168:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:117:Cael sat with his back to the horses and turned the three words over. They were true. Every figure on every card in Brom's two cases had been earned on the house's floors, by him, on purpose, to the decimal. They were his in the way a rotation page was his, because he had written it the night before. He thought that if the clerk had asked whether they were all of him, he would not have known what to say, and he was glad the form did not ask it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8169:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:The bank road ran west along the near side of the river, high on its terrace, with the water on the left below them roaring in the dark. Twice the wheels went into standing water to the hub and the coach lurched, and the coachman talked to the horses until they leaned into it and pulled out. Once a fallen branch lay across the road and Brom got down and dragged it off into the ditch and got back in, wet to the knees, and put the cases back on his lap without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8182:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8187:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:He opened the door and got down into the white dust before anyone could stop him, and walked to the coach's front wheel on the south side, and bent to it as if to look at the hub. Then he went down on one knee on the road and put his palm flat on the chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8199:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:243:He did not hurry. He stepped down into the dust with the two record cases, and set them on the coach step, side by side, squared, and then walked forward along the foot of the south bank, under the lip, into the channel. He stopped in the middle of it. He turned round to face the cart and the culvert and the gully, and set his feet, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8209:25:They had the door of the old lumber room propped open with a broken chair, and they were working the table through it on its side, sweating and grumbling at it in the low courteous voices porters used on the library stair. Behind them the room itself stood half empty. It had held, for as long as Cael had known it, the house's spare benches and a map case with no maps in it and two rolled carpets nobody would admit to owning. Now the carpets were out on the landing and the benches gone, and somebody had already swept the floor in long straight strokes and left the broom against the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8330:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md-9-- POV: Cael ≈35,400. **Seln cutaway ≈2,200** (night of day 112, after the council: the two slips laid side by side in his head; six years carrying a case for people he cannot see; why he wants a face; he may not open the case or decode anything). **Teague cutaway ≈2,400** (days 113–115: at Millrace, the five not back; the walk south to look; the rim's ground read in lantern light, the crossbows, "declaring"; holding the rim at dawn without being asked; no crew names).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8339:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:29:- **Day 114, the trap (≈9,000–10,500 across two chapters).** The crossing in Seln's order. **The bolt at forty meters**: "Ridge." Six hooded on the rim in road grey (two south, two east, one west, one north). **The Anchor course** closing the line behind them; Cael sees it with perception only. Bolts skip off the fused plate. **Lira runs *down***, and the unit follows. **Forty steps.** The chamber, ten strides square, its ceiling the floor; walls carved; at the middle "something cased… a cord," unread. **The doorway**: "They can shoot the bowl… They can't shoot the stair." **Oryn without hands**: lamp, eyes, two fingers; "Tape it. Leave the bolt."; "the bolt's the plug"; "I came south for exactly this. Enter that." Lira's mend reopened on the stair wall. **The stalemate, given room**: the square of grey going white and grey; a bolt sent down to stop at the thirty-first step; Brom grey and not worse; Karis with her back to the wall she would give a year to read. The design read aloud ("It thinks the floor did the same thing to all six of us."). **The order:** up alone with the one he was given. Oryn: "Ask first." / "I'm not reading anyone." / "I know. I'm saying the rule out loud so it's been said on this floor too." Lira: "Later." **The ascent, Shadow alone**, across the bowl unseen; the rim scanning for a shout that cannot exist. Anchor let off at ten meters as perception, then as a hand: **the knot found and released**, the course going slack like rigging. **Over the stone, declaring**: Wind, Storm seeding the rut, Compression at contact, Ember held out. He removes one man from the plan and does not fight him. The rim breaks as professionals break. The west one looks back for a second, hood up, nothing in it. Four crossbows left; no faces. "Back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8345:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:26:- **The ledge and the stampede (≈4,500, terrain fight):** the ledge goes under Lira; her own Wind, full, crosses the gorge; thirty heads come up; the shelf cannot hold them at a run, and they come down the face in a front. "There. Thirty seconds. Lee side." "Cart's gone." **Karis's lattice line** across the face is a declaration the size of a road. The herd splits round the fin at eleven paces; she sits down at the eighth second, emptied. Brom goes back for two boxes (the wrist goes under the cloth). Cael goes after the third. Compression and Anchor *want*, and he prices both and holds, because the split stands on Karis's line and one more loud thing turns it. He takes the shoulder raw: three ribs, left side, low; the calf opened on the shale. The cart floats and goes in with its shafts up. Lira's leg broken under a plate, the boot gone. "That was me… The loud thing." / "It was the ledge. The ledge went. Then it was you." / "Then it was me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8363:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md-25-- **Lowmarch, day 9, and the board read like the Cinder House board.** Fourteen slips, not one pricing the crew. Pike: sixties, one arm, "Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads." "Walk, carry, climb, fight." / "In that order?" / "Depends on the day." A heading opened with five names and five columns: Job, Hirer, Witness, Outcome, Price. No tier column. He reads it upside down and looks for the missing column "the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8367:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md-29-- **Thornwater:** the gate, the woman with the lantern, the bowman who doesn't point; "First crew I've hired that knew to shut up."; "Stillhound… You shut up. It went home."; "You're paper." / "Yes." / "Hm."; the slip marked; the grain loft; six days; the carter back on the fourth; Pike's copied line; the log with "Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8379:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md-21-- **Companions:** Lira, Wind, Iron R1 formal (Silver bracket declined at full price). Brom, Iron Skin, Copper formal for good, continental Copper champion. Karis, Ember, ranking forfeited, three boxes made into a field archive. Seln, Shadow, Bronze, left the Compact in form, carrying the locked case (the machine's own maintenance records). All uninjured. Lira and Brom have read the log. Karis sees demonstrations, not the log. Seln knows the mechanism since B6 Ch15.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8381:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md-42-- **Objects:** the kit (case, pacing cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke-and-bar mark and a bright point, mallet); the ledger; the 52 sheets; the map; **two brass straightedges** (Ternhall's; Hesk's, arrived on the last ferry). Both touch the three paced centres without daylight, **in Cael's hand as in anyone's** (Book 8 Ch1 owns the first hair of error). Also three boxes, Seln's case, one mule (the crew's), and Oryn's crossbow with one bolt left in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8418:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md-222-**Injuries tracked forward (no silent healing):** Cael's calf (53 → reopened 78 and 96 → closed 99); ribs (78 → taped 81 → off 99 → the Hall's bill 119 → cold-aggravated 123 → quiet at 129). Lira's right ankle (53, 78, 96; **never mended**); her leg (broken 78, mended 80); her left forearm (96 → season order → reopened 114 → closed; slung at 131). Brom's forearm (53 → reopened 78 → mended 80); his hand (96 → mended 97); his shoulder (114 → mended; reopened 119 → mended twice → bound 120). Karis's palms (53, scars); empty (78); throat and voice (96 → mended 97, voice back by ~110). Seln: unmarked. Oryn: drained at every mending, priced aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8420:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md-283-- Ch13, the covenant, whole: *Written before. Tomorrow morning, the back room, the door shut. Oryn runs the long reading. Both hands, all of her, all of me, twenty minutes. I lie still. I'm still and whole for the first time in front of her; she said so in the yard, with the tape off.* / *The conditions, as I have them. Sustained: twenty minutes at contact is longer than any bout I've stood. Stakes: I'm three days off the Ring and the nine are still settling; she's three mendings down and her route's late at the far end and she's spending a fourth on a patient who's given her nothing four times. Earnest: she'll do the thing she does as well as she does it. Karis: the model says real, not fight. I don't know if the model's right. Nobody does. Tomorrow's the first time it could be tested and I'm the test.* / *The price. She doesn't know what I am. She can't consent to what she can't know about — not won't; can't. There's nothing I could put in front of her tonight that she could say yes or no to, because the thing doesn't have a name she's heard. I've decided not to tell her before. My reasons are good, and they're the reasons of somebody who wants it, and the real one is that I don't know what she'd say. Entered.* / *If it takes what she's using on me, it takes it from the one person on this road who came to help. I don't know what it costs a source. It's never shown. Tomorrow's the first time it could, and if it does, she pays, and I chose that for her.* / *After: I tell her. In full. On her table, before I'm off it. Whatever it costs me, including the route walking north without me on it.* / *I want it. Write that too.* / *Write it before, not after. That's the whole difference between me and the system.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8428:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:295:- Ch24, the inventory: *Ten confirmed fragments.* / *Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.* / *One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.* / *Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. Both are going to need a map.* — **Audio rule:** Feryn's name never appears in a list run beside Oryn's; every inventory says "the Bronze from Ardenmere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8456:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md-118-- **Seln's locked case** on its strap: the cache, carried on every road, never off him, **never opened in this book**.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8474:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-159-He counted the sheet from where he sat before Pike opened the book. Thirteen lines. Theirs was the twelfth. He knew that and counted anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8490:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:141:The map: in Karis's book, in Karis's satchel, on Karis. Nine narrowings and their widths, and now a tenth, which was not a narrowing. It was an absence. The ledge was gone for a cart's width along three carts' length; what remained was a lip against the bulge that a walker could take with her hands on the rock, and a mule could not, and Oxhollow's carts would not, ever. Seln walked out along the lip in the afternoon and came back at dusk and said it went; that beyond the bulge the bench opened as they had seen it; that for a walker Oxhollow was two days on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8508:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:13:He put both in the satchel against the leather book, unopened, because he did not read letters in front of a meadow. Then he put the strap over the shoulder that did not have tape under it, and at first light on the ninety-second the five of them went east off the landing, past the smith and the ash, and onto the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8536:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-217-*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8544:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:233:Force Path. Cael had it from the doorway, and checked it as the man came in: the weight down through the heels, the shoulders carried low and square, the hands loose — the same setting Pike sat in, except that this one had not stopped using it. Bronze, the book inside had said once, and would still say if anyone opened it, and nobody would. There was nothing on him a registry had given. No token, no cord, no colour. A face that had priced a great many things and kept none of the prices on it. He went to Pike first, and Pike said something short and put a slip in front of him — the ford's, Cael thought, by the size of the paper — and the man did not pick it up. He put two fingers on it, as Pike had put two fingers on the ledger, and left it where it was, and looked at his table, and only when the young one had food in front of him did he look back down at the slip and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8566:books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-37-- [ ] **B6 threads honored at their correct altitude:** *The faceless faction* — reaches across the Line twice, and the book counts both: **first on paper (Ch8)** — a genuine recovery contract for a survey kit the Compact abandoned five years ago, laid on the Lowmarch board through an edge-territory broker in registry prose, which Seln flags as written-from-inside and which the crew takes anyway; its completion report travels back west (Ch16) and tells the poster exactly which crew walks into quiet ground and walks out; **then in person (Ch17–19)** — a second posting from the same broker, priced as a lure, at a site where the poster knows six practitioners will be five bodies and one anomaly; the team that waits is off-channel, nameless, and equipped for practitioners who cannot use their Paths — because the Compact *knows what quiet ground does* (plant a, above, given teeth). The knowledge progression is explicit: the first contract was recovery plus a question; the report answered it; the second contract was the trap. Still unnamed, still unlocated, still open into Book 8+. *The Compact's question about what Seln kept* — the trap is for the case as much as the boy, and Seln says so (Ch18); the case is not taken; the dread stays live. *Asset-restriction, pending jurisdiction* — the reason nobody goes west, stated once (Ch2), and the reason the trap has to come east. *Daeva's rematch / Reydan's answer* — one log line each (Ch14, Ch24), warm, unscheduled, not advanced. *Hesk* — letter beats only, via the Lowmarch courier chain (Ch4, Ch14, Ch24); his history untouched. *Vell* — one letter (Ch24). *Ephram* — one letter, from inside (Ch9), the inside friend reporting the weather. *Vastin* — one window (Ch16), three sentences, still inside, receiving a courtesy copy and writing nothing; his departure and the classified-history material are Book 8's and are not staged, hinted, or scheduled. *Havel, Ilsev, Withrow, Coss* — offstage entirely. ⚠ 2026-09-04: Ch8's posting is not a genuine recovery contract — Ch12 line 179 rules it "For a kit that was never here" — and Vell has two letter beats (inbound Ch9 line 141, outbound Ch24 line 97), not the single Ch24 letter the card allows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8571:books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-240-The acquisition chapter — the series' Chapter 13 tradition (B2 Fragment Three; B3 Directed; B4 The Operation; B6 The Ostrand Road) honored a fifth time, and for the first time the engagement is not a fight, not an operation, not a bout: it is a healer's hands on a patient, at real stakes, and the book argues the ethics before it stages the act (the B4 Ch9 covenant, applied to a new case, on the page). Thornwater, two days back along the void road, a steading with a back room and a door that shuts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8573:books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:242:The covenant, before the act: Oryn has said she will run the deep reading — the long one, the one she has never run on him, the one that costs her — because he is, for the first time in her presence, still and whole. Cael takes it to the circle first, without her, the way he took Seln's case in Book 4 and the Anchor specialist in Book 6 — the conditions are assembling, and he names them: sustained close engagement (the deep reading is twenty minutes of a Tide practitioner's full current run through his architecture at contact — not the seconds of the surface reading that has failed three times); genuine stakes (he is three days off a full-suite deployment, the frameworks still resettling, and the woman doing it is the only healer in a country, spending a mending's worth of reserve on a patient who may return nothing); earnest use *on* him (not against — and the chapter has Karis say the thing that makes the covenant honest: "Ephram fought you in earnest and the notice said *non-hostile*. This is earnest and it isn't a fight at all. The model doesn't say the engagement has to be a fight. It says it has to be *real*."). And the cost, written down in advance per the covenant, in the log, on the page: Oryn does not know the mechanism; she cannot consent to what she cannot know about; if the architecture takes what she is using on him, it will have taken from the one person in the book who came to *help*; and he will tell her afterward, in full, whatever it costs him. "Write it before, not after. That's the whole difference between me and the system."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8594:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:21:He said it flat, looking at the stone behind them, and Cael looked with the Anchor-adjacent perception — which was not a declaration, only a way of seeing, and which the rim could not have seen him use — and saw what Seln had read off the ground: fixed points, blooming, along the perimeter's inside edge north and south of the stone, laid in a course as close-set as Karis's ignitions, closing the meter of ground they had crossed. Not a wall. A door, shutting behind them, laid by somebody on the rim who had the craft and had had an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8596:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:133:Twenty meters from the stone. Ten. He let the second thing off its hold there and not before — Anchor-adjacent, a perception first, still nothing anyone could see — and the lattice's nearest point came up in it a stride inside the line, the cornerstone of the course that shut the road, and he knew its weight as he knew a floor's: a fixed point laid by a specialist, on the rim's side of the shutting, from ground where her craft worked, holding a meter of dead-Path scrub closed against people who could not have opened it if they had known it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8597:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:135:He could open it. He had opened one from inside on a road. The perception turned into a hand, and found the knot where the point was bound to the ground, and let it go, the way the woman on the Ostrand road had let go of a spent position, not broken, not overwhelmed, simply *released* by something standing inside it that had no business knowing where the knot was.
