editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2106:-"No." Karis had her hands round her own elbows. "I read every ruling in the file, in my second year at Ternhall, because I didn't believe it. He was winning every point. A coastal practitioner, a guild man, an integrity matter, eleven sittings, and the house's counsel would have been glad of any one of his rulings." She looked at the sheet in Brom's hands. "He didn't leave the case. He left the room. He wrote that he'd stopped believing in it, and he meant it, and he posted it, and a clerk read it into the minute. They thanked him. Then they used his own sentence to grant the whole petition."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2115:+"He didn't," she said. "Not once. He didn't leave the case. He left the room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2120:-She opened her notebook flat on the table and wrote under the day's date: *Default — entered at the sitting after.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2125:+Lira took them, and put them inside her coat, and Karis opened her notebook flat on the table and wrote under the day's date: *Default — entered at the sitting after.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2166:-"That's what you've got," she had said. "Five cases. All one way. None the other. It's a good finding; I'd sign it." She had put the point of the pencil in the empty space beside the gate, where the next stroke would go. "But a gate of five looks finished, and it isn't. It's only where somebody stopped counting. Every rule anybody ever saw broken looked exactly like that the evening before. The word for it is induction. Yours is strong. What holds it up is the count, and counts run out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2198:-The man had sat through the whole evening at the bench's middle with his hands folded on the slate and his eyes shut, as he sat in the coach on the river road. He had opened them twice. The first time had been at Lira's chalk, at *stamps stop*, and he had looked at the column on the shelf for exactly as long as it took her to read it out, and closed them again. The second time was at that sentence. He had looked at Cael across the table for a long while, with no expression Cael had a word for, and gone on looking after Cael had stopped, until Lira spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2241: Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2262:+Karis dated the page and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2264:-Karis looked at her for a moment. Then she wrote it, exactly like that, at the very top of the page, above Brom's three sentences, so that the page now began with the last thing anybody said. She dated it and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2273:-*We asked. Karis has it at the top of the page, so it's true. Seln wrote one figure all night and opened his eyes twice, and the second time was for me, and I don't know what I said that he'd already paid for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2278:+"A long time. Longer than he's ever looked at anybody, that I've seen." She held the lamp a little higher, as if it would show her the court better. "He opened his eyes twice all night. The other time was my chalk." She went on up the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2291:+He had opened his eyes twice in the dairy, and the first time had been for the girl's chalk. *STAMPS STOP.* He knew that road. He knew, as a matter of his trade, what she did not: that a return like the one on the table had a route, office to office, and that the route ended where the stamps did. Past the last station there was no bag for it to go in, and no clerk to open it, and no drawer. A man could stand on the far side of that line and be watched by nobody whose watching went anywhere. He had never let himself think about it in those words before. He did not let himself go further now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2295: It was a small brass key worn bright at the bow, and it had lived in that pocket for longer than he had lived in any room. He held it on his palm in the pale square of light. Above him, on the shelf over the copying table, in the shadow, was the thing it opened. He did not look up at the shelf. He looked at the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2300:-The boy believed the instrument had a fault in it, here and there, that a patient reader could find and read back into true. Tonight he had laid out his whole case for it, every piece, in order and then out of order, at full strength. It was the best argument Seln had heard from anybody in years. Every piece of it was true. And somewhere in the registry, in the same file as every one of those pieces, Seln's own returns lay too, true among true things, and the boy would have counted them as receipts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2306:-He shut the cupboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2310:+He took his hand off the shelf and shut the cupboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2312:-His fourth posting, twelve years ago. A records room in an old building, which he had been in lawfully, looking for somebody's travel endorsements; and a press he had opened for that reason, and found to hold something else. *Maintenance records*, the label said, and that was what they looked like: a long run of upkeep, routine, dated, initialled, older than the building. He had copied a quantity of them before he went, because he copied things. It was not suspicion. It was the oldest habit he had. As a boy he had copied his mother's ledgers for the pleasure of seeing the two hands come out the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2361: He carried a document case in front of his chest in both hands, as a verger carries a book in a procession, so that you saw the case before you saw the man. A clerk walked a pace behind him with nothing in his hands at all. He came at a slow even walk that did not hurry and did not linger, past the river academy's row, whose fighters were looking at their knees, and stopped in front of the house's bench under the square banner, and bowed to the Chancellor at the registry's exact depth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2366:-Cael could not see the paper. He could see Bracken's back, standing, and the registrar's head bent at his reading angle, quite still, for a long time, while the officer waited with his hands folded and his clerk waited behind him and the house's bench, one by one, stood up too, without anybody telling it to. Withrow stood. Karis stood with her notebook shut on her finger. Brom stood, and took up a space and a half doing it. On the floor the Current girl and the Mire went on with their bout for perhaps a dozen people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2487: "*Anything sealed, fetch the registrar,*" she read aloud. "You did, and you shut your window, and you stood where you could see the whole of it." She nodded to him, the nod she gave her senior staff. "Your board did very well this morning. Put a fresh coat on it in the spring, if you would. I should like it to last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2501:-The Magister stopped him on the covered walk an hour after the riders had gone, which he had not done once all year. He had his case under his arm and a small black notebook in his other hand, open, with the pencil still in the fold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2502:+He had his case clamped under one arm and a small black notebook open on the other forearm, and he was writing in it where he stood, in the middle of the walk, so that people had to go round him. Cael had never seen him write anywhere but at a table. He stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2507:-He shut the notebook on the pencil, gave his quarter-inch nod, and walked on toward the Magisters' stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2523:+*Nobody asked for the refusal in writing. They didn't want anything they could build the next paper on. They weren't here for me this morning; they were here for the door, how heavy it is and how fast it shuts and who comes to shut it. They measured it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2529: "I've never stood near enough to one at work to say, and I won't guess." Seln opened his eyes and looked at the copy. "I know where they're posted, and it isn't escort. Escort is for clerks on good horses. One at that grade, standing where she stood, is enforcement." He laid one fingertip on the table beside the order, not touching it. "Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2579:-"The faceless layer has learned to write paper," she said. "And this morning it found out where ours holds and where it doesn't. What held was a registrar who reads a seal with his thumb and a chancellor who would sooner be shut than be quick. Two people. No rule anywhere in it." She lifted her hand off the rubbing. "I'd like us all to notice how little that is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2628:     3	The residence's small room was not a room anyone had chosen for councils. It had been the winter store when the residence was a farmhouse, and it kept the shape: one door, one shuttered window, a stone floor with a drain in the corner that had drained something else three hundred years ago, and a table too big for it that could not be got out again because the door had been narrowed at some point by somebody who did not think about tables. The delegation used it for the same reason Seln had approved of it the first time he was brought there, which was that its one window faced the wall of the stores range four feet away and its walls were three feet of stone, and a voice in it went nowhere at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2722:    97	"You said the people are weather and the text is ground." She said it carefully. "I've been standing on ground I was sure of. It was a form with my name on it. Correctly filled, correctly stamped, correctly filed by a woman who wished me well. The ground was correct and it opened anyway." She looked at him. "That's not an argument against text. I know what the text has done for me; I've got a supersession because of it. It's an argument about what text is. It's not ground. It's a floor. And floors are held up by something, and I stopped being able to see what, about twelve days ago." She sat back. "I'm not saying leave. I'm saying: check the ground. That's all. Brom's three sentences. Both roads."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2740:   115	She wrote both. Then she gathered the face-down papers without turning them over, and the five of them went up through a residence that had been asleep for an hour, and the small room's lamp went out, and the one line of light at the shutter's bad joint went out with it, and the stores range and the court were dark from the assessment office's window by the time the man who had sat in the bench's middle got there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2763:   138	The office at that hour was the shape his working life had always had. A counter with nobody at it. A window with a court beyond it and, across the court, the residence's dark bulk — and in it the small room's shuttered window, which for three hours that evening had let out one thin line of lamp at the shutter's bad joint, and which was dark now, and which he had noticed in his first week and had never mentioned to anyone, because mentioning it would have meant explaining why he had looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2769:   144	And a locked case on a shelf in his quarters that had outlasted every one of the nineteen. It had traveled to Norhold and back unopened. It had ridden in a cart under the same canvas as three boxes of a researcher's compiled truth without either archive knowing the other's contents, and he had sat on the cart's bench for two days beside those boxes and had thought about the symmetry for most of the second day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2785:   160	He did not open the case. He did not go and look at it. He noted, in the audit-voice, exactly what the case staying closed cost, because the cost had been abstract for fifteen years and had stopped being abstract at a table three hours ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2791:   166	He sat with the last line for a long time, in the dark office, with the cross-index under his hands and the case on its shelf across the campus and a dark window four buildings away where a line of lamp had shown for three hours, and the line was the closest thing to a decision the audit-voice had ever recorded, and it was not one yet, and he knew the difference exactly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2808:    15	"He builds in courses," Rooke said, with the delegation's charting of the veteran's last eleven rated bouts spread in front of him and the commercial file underneath it, unopened, because Rooke did not pay for pages he could produce himself. "Not like the ones you've fought. An Iron Force builder puts up a structure and holds it. This one puts up a structure and then puts a second one *under* it while the first is still standing, and the second is what's actually carrying, and by the time you've taxed the first he's three courses down and the load's somewhere you haven't been." He tapped the third sheet. "Anchoring runs on a four-count. That's the only number in the file that matters. Everything he builds is four counts from being load-bearing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2830:    37	The Rank 8 opened at what the file called his working rate, and the working rate was a different climate. Force construction with Silver *depth* — courses built under courses, each anchoring position sustained by a load the tier below could not hold, the structure advancing on her with the patience of a doctrine that had solved Iron-tier taxation years ago. He did not come forward. That was the first thing that was wrong with him, measured against every Force fighter she had ever met: he built where he stood, and the building took the floor, and the floor arrived at her without him ever having crossed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2898:   105	Mid-meet, between the Silver bracket's second and third bouts, with the confluence hall at its fullest and Halcenvane's colors on the floor for a second bout — a third-year in the Iron bracket, the program's own supporting entrant — a registry records officer came through the hall's main door with a document case and a clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2900:   107	He did not send the clerk. That was the first thing Cael charted, from the north gallery, with the whole hall laid out beneath him like a floor before a bout. The officer came in at the main door, which was at the hall's south end and was the door the paying gallery used, and he walked the length of the hall on the gallery's inside rail, past three hundred seated people, in registry grey, with a document case held at the front of his body in both hands where it could be seen — and it took him, Cael timed it, fifty-one seconds to reach the delegation's block, and by the twentieth second the bout on the floor had stopped being what the hall was watching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2908:   115	The demand was lawful. Bracken would confirm it by letter within the week — a routine production order, the code's ordinary instrument for building a record, served on a respondent's delegation because the delegation was where the respondent's officers were. It could have been served at the bluff. It could have been posted. It was served at a venue, at the meet's fullest hour, at the gallery rail, by an officer who walked the whole length of the hall to do it, and the timing was the message, and every registry-literate person in the confluence read the message as it was delivered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3090:   120	The observation notebook got its entry that night, and the entry opened a page the notebook had never had:
