editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3633:    89	Four days to the last station, loaded. Three if light. Stamps on a sheet as far as the station, and then a gap at the paper's edge. Two people out of three who went past the gap every trip and made money doing it. *Nobody with a book,* twice, from two people who had never met. And a young man with a good wagon who turned at the gap and would not say why, which was its own kind of fact. She did not know what it meant. She only knew that if she left it off the sheet because it spoiled the sum, she would be doing exactly what she hated every registry clerk for doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3787:   243	"He didn't," she said. "Not once. He didn't leave the case. He left the room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3795:   251	Lira took them, and put them inside her coat, and Karis opened her notebook flat on the table and wrote under the day's date: *Default — entered at the sitting after.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3811:   267	Seln's eyes stayed shut. It was Karis who answered, with her finger on his line. "From the day the fourth sitting closes. Before the fortieth day, a respondent who hasn't come back is only absent. On the fortieth, he's fled, and the default goes in." She looked at the figure as if she had never seen one before. "It's a narrow thing. I had it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3896:    79	Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3910:    93	Karis dated the page and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3922:   105	"A long time. Longer than he's ever looked at anybody, that I've seen." She held the lamp a little higher, as if it would show her the court better. "He opened his eyes twice all night. The other time was my chalk." She went on up the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3950:   133	He had opened his eyes twice in the dairy, and the first time had been for the girl's chalk. *STAMPS STOP.* He knew that road. He knew, as a matter of his trade, what she did not: that a return like the one on the table had a route, office to office, and that the route ended where the stamps did. Past the last station there was no bag for it to go in, and no clerk to open it, and no drawer. A man could stand on the far side of that line and be watched by nobody whose watching went anywhere. He had never let himself think about it in those words before. He did not let himself go further now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3954:   137	It was a small brass key worn bright at the bow, and it had lived in that pocket for longer than he had lived in any room. He held it on his palm in the pale square of light. Above him, on the shelf over the copying table, in the shadow, was the thing it opened. He did not look up at the shelf. He looked at the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3964:   147	There was a cupboard under the copying table, and he opened it without looking, because his hand knew where the latch was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3980:   163	He took his hand off the shelf and shut the cupboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4013:   243	"He didn't," she said. "Not once. He didn't leave the case. He left the room."
