editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4037:   267	Seln's eyes stayed shut. It was Karis who answered, with her finger on his line. "From the day the fourth sitting closes. Before the fortieth day, a respondent who hasn't come back is only absent. On the fortieth, he's fled, and the default goes in." She looked at the figure as if she had never seen one before. "It's a narrow thing. I had it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4080:-"That's what you've got," she had said. "Five cases. All one way. None the other. It's a good finding; I'd sign it." She had put the point of the pencil in the empty space beside the gate, where the next stroke would go. "But a gate of five looks finished, and it isn't. It's only where somebody stopped counting. Every rule anybody ever saw broken looked exactly like that the evening before. The word for it is induction. Yours is strong. What holds it up is the count, and counts run out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4112:-The man had sat through the whole evening at the bench's middle with his hands folded on the slate and his eyes shut, as he sat in the coach on the river road. He had opened them twice. The first time had been at Lira's chalk, at *stamps stop*, and he had looked at the column on the shelf for exactly as long as it took her to read it out, and closed them again. The second time was at that sentence. He had looked at Cael across the table for a long while, with no expression Cael had a word for, and gone on looking after Cael had stopped, until Lira spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4155: Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4176:+Karis dated the page and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4178:-Karis looked at her for a moment. Then she wrote it, exactly like that, at the very top of the page, above Brom's three sentences, so that the page now began with the last thing anybody said. She dated it and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4187:-*We asked. Karis has it at the top of the page, so it's true. Seln wrote one figure all night and opened his eyes twice, and the second time was for me, and I don't know what I said that he'd already paid for.*
