editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8233:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md-57-"At Greyvane, in my first term," she said, to the window, "an advocate gave me a point at a sitting. A good point. One I'd spent a fortnight on. He stood up and said the archive was right and he wouldn't dispute it, and sat down. I went back to my room and was pleased with myself all evening." The cart lurched forward a yard and stopped again. "In the morning he took the case on a point I hadn't looked at, because I'd spent the whole of the night before being pleased."
