editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8577:   111	Something in him was lighter, as a man is lighter who has set down a case he has carried so long he had forgotten it was in his hand, and he did not look at it closely. He had a long habit of not looking closely at things in himself, and he kept to it now. He sat with his hands folded on the empty blotter, as he sat at his counter in the wing at the end of a day, and let the lightness be there without asking what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8579:   113	Then, because habit is habit, he took the small book from his inside pocket and opened it and wrote in it too, a few lines, in a hand nobody at the wing had ever seen him use. He put it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8581:   115	The locked case stood under the table by his chair, where it had stood every evening of the sitting, apart from the pen-case beside it. He had not opened it. He did not open it now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8597:   131	The stair went up round the inside of the tower, and the room was at the top of it. A timekeeper's boy was waiting at the door. He had the key already turned. He opened the door and stood aside and went off down the stair without a word, so that nobody would have to let anybody in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8607:   141	He was in the plain grey, with nothing at the collar. He had no case with him and no papers, not a sheet, not a pencil. Cael had seen this man in a records hall, in a held chair, across a table of questions, and every time there had been a document somewhere within reach of his hand. There was nothing within reach of it now. The hands hung at his sides. The left one was not quite straight at the fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8741:   275	"Good," he said, and opened the door. That was the last either of them ever said about the step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8798:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:It was not the end of a year by anybody's calendar but his own. But it was near enough a year since he had sat at another window, on the last night of the old term, and written down what was in the till. A thing that has been opened ought to be closed, and the ledger had been open all that time. He turned past the inventory at seventeen, and the trial, and the ring, and the minute, and the pole, and the rule, and the warning, to the next clean leaf. He ruled the margin with the edge of the closing ceremony's printed card, because he had no rule with him. Then he sat a moment before he wrote, with the pen lifted. He did that perhaps once a year and had never written it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8828:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-177-She did it at a waystation table after supper, and the whole ceremony of it was that she would not have one. She took out everything the year had made, from the cases it had ridden in: the clause and the four pages, the qualifying letters, the circular, the rulings, the trial rules, the nine charts on the backs of old seating plans, the entry. She sorted it all into two piles on the board. She tied each with tape and wrote a contents leaf for each and put the leaf on top, and the bundles went back into the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8877:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:115:*First honest warning I've ever gotten from inside the machine. He built no case, made no ask, took no note. He just told me the weather. The Compact's best evaluator thinks the next thing that comes for me won't wear a uniform or file a challenge. Write it down. Believe it. Keep competing anyway — but from tonight, we build like people who believe it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8903:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-141-He was in the plain grey, with nothing at the collar. He had no case with him and no papers, not a sheet, not a pencil. Cael had seen this man in a records hall, in a held chair, across a table of questions, and every time there had been a document somewhere within reach of his hand. There was nothing within reach of it now. The hands hung at his sides. The left one was not quite straight at the fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9162:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/EVENT-LIST.md-79-7. **Day 76, the gate.** Way in: the lodge-man doing a thing he has never done (shutting the lodge window on the road and coming out without his hat); the boy runs. Cael from the wall stair to the gatehouse gallery above the arch — a stretch of walk with a waist-high parapet behind the bell-frame. The court's geometry (exits: the arch, the lodge door, the covered walk's arch, the lane round the lecture range, the stable passage — the only one with no door). Four riders, no livery, good horses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9242:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md:41:1. **Havel, day 80–81, Ostrand** (cutaway). A clerk from the district seat at the inn by the bridge on the Third-day with a letter and a pencil rubbing: the Halcenvane registrar's inquiry, sent across "for the registers" because it concerns the proceedings and the district's records room is short. His thumb aches. Three checks over two days, each in its own room: the district's impression book (wafers pasted in by issue); the registry's printed schedule of seals of office (the engraved plates, this year's revision); the die number in the rim's beading against the strong-room copy of the cutters' issue ledger. The facilities office is on the register of offices — constituted this year by an instrument filed under seal. The seal on that instrument is the rubbing's seal. In none of the three. He drafts the usual reply: the office constituted on such a date by instrument filed under seal; inquiries to the issuing authority. The form has no line for what he found. At the inn: the five sheets, the sixth written — *a seal absent from every registry of seals* — fact only, by the rule of the sheets. He sees the first sheet's mark with no office and the sixth's seal with no register side by side and makes nothing of it. He stops; there will be no seventh, because a note of what the registers could not answer is only worth keeping while the registers answer everything else. He goes to bed with the river loud under the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9243:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md-42-2. **Cael, day 82, before light.** The coach down the bluff road in the dark; the coats at the road's foot see it turn west; the near-bank road in mud; Brom with the record cases on his knees; Karis rehearsing the attestation under her breath; Lira asleep against the window; Seln on the bench with his eyes shut. Wind getting up, the rain gone east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9397:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/source-tracking.md-267-  Daeva's statement. Karis's prediction can be disclosed from Brom's unopened slip after the seat
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9401:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/source-tracking.md-271-  a non-numbered order keyed to Cael's notes. End on the unopened folder rather than the source's
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9524:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-editorial.stdout.log-9701-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:Cael saw it from the covered walk, a little after the second bell on the Sixth-day morning, on his way across to the wing. It was a small thing. In two years he had never once seen that window shut in daylight. The lodge-man sat behind it on his high stool from the first bell to the last, with his tea and the bluff road in front of him, and talked to anybody who came up it. Now the shutter came across the glass from the inside, and a moment later the lodge door opened, and the lodge-man stood in it without his hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9525:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-editorial.stdout.log-9702-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:He sat on his heels behind the bell-frame and heard the name at the top of it go round the court. *Caelen Hesk-ward.* Nobody who knew him had ever said it to his face. It was the name the registry kept for him, the way a shop keeps a number on a parcel, and here it was coming up off the flags of his own gate in a stranger's voice, with an order underneath it. *Attendance. Transport and escort provided. Produce the enrollee.* And at the bottom, the five words he had first read on the morning of the eighth day at the foot of a challenge's first page, and had read at the foot of every registry instrument since, and had never heard spoken by anybody at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9531:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/review-editorial.stdout.log-9720-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:121:The Magister had not stopped him anywhere since the year began. He passed Cael on that walk most days, going between his rooms and the wing with his case under his arm, and nodded, a quarter-inch, and went on. Today he stopped dead in front of him with the case held to his chest in both arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:9667:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r1.stdout.log-4361-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:694:**Calendar.** No season, month or English weekday names; feet and yards. Day 58 Second-day, day 61 Fifth-day, day 64 First-day: Lira's three carters. About day 57: the confluence's stewards put the road meet back eight days for repairs to their hall (from day 63 to day 71; see owner flag 1). Day 66 Third-day: the note; "five drawers… shut" (the last two days before). Day 67: Withrow's colors sentence. **Day 68 Fifth-day: the small room**; Seln in the dark wing that night. Day 69 Sixth-day: the delegation leaves (Withrow and Bracken in the hired carriage, the coach, the program wagon; Rooke, the Stone, Lira, the Current third-year, Karis, Seln, Brom, Cael; Ephram stays). Day 70: the confluence at dusk; the card; Rooke's brief. **Day 71 First-day: the exhibition (afternoon); the production order served a quarter past two.** Day 72: finals; the corridor; the last evening at the rail. Days 73–74: home in rain. **Day 75 Fifth-day: the fourth sitting** (records produced; the one question). **Day 76 Sixth-day, second bell: the escort order at the gate**; noon refectory; Gault; the notebook; records hall; the Crown yard after dark. Bracken writes to the district seat on day 77 (the answer due about a week later). Brom's panel and the ruling share the year's last Fifth-day. The *fled* default would enter forty days after the fourth sitting's close (day 75) — the code's count, said by Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:10020:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md:1372:"So that the gate can shut on every field the same morning." Seln laid one fingertip beside each bean in turn as he spoke, not touching any of them. "Chapter fourteen lets the registry restrict an unclassified person: his standing, his papers, his endorsements, everything that's tied to him. A restriction that runs office by office leaks. Somebody hears, somebody moves a deed, somebody's cousin carries a letter across a district line before the freeze arrives. So before anything runs, a clerk goes round every fence the man has. Who sponsors him. Which houses hold his papers. What his people own, and where they own it. The tin box under the bench." His voice did not change at all on that. "Every gate is found and counted first. Then on the morning it's wanted, every gate shuts at once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:10028:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md:1388:"And then nothing at all." Seln took his finger back. "You won't find this part in any code. No office has ever needed it written down. A finished inventory isn't an instrument. Nobody serves it. Nobody publishes it. It has no title. It is five files in five drawers in five offices, every one of them lawful, every one as dull as a laundry list, and all five indexed under one name." He squared the four beans again with the side of his finger, and did not touch the fifth. "If the seat rules for the house, the five drawers are never opened together. In time each office weeds its old papers, as offices do, and not one person anywhere will have broken a single rule. If the seat rules the other way, it takes one clerk one afternoon to fetch the five files to one desk. Then every gate shuts on the same morning. And the enrollee finds out what that sixth week was for on a morning when there is nothing left to do about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:10038:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md-1598-Ilsev closed the fold of paper over the shavings, put it in her coat pocket, and bent her head toward the platform to the depth the registry taught, no more and no less. Then she opened the topmost file and began to read it with the patience of somebody who has read it three times and intends one more before she is asked a single question.
