editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:10786:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-002/recheck-r2.stdout.log:1889:The counsel's own read came in the coach on the way back, unprompted, delivered to the window in the voice of an advocate revising twenty-six years of assumptions in real time. "He's not there to argue. I've faced men sent to argue — they have a tell, a place they lean, a clause they love. He has none. He's there to *conduct* — to move the petition through the schedule at the code's pace and let the seat do what the seat was constituted to do." She turned from the window. "I have never lost a case to a man who didn't want to win it. I'm not certain I've ever faced one."
