editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:10975:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:5416:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:131:"And then nothing at all." Seln took his finger back. "You won't find this part in any code. No office has ever needed it written down. A finished inventory isn't an instrument. Nobody serves it. Nobody publishes it. It has no title. It is five files in five drawers in five offices, every one of them lawful, every one as dull as a laundry list, and all five indexed under one name." He squared the four beans again with the side of his finger, and did not touch the fifth. "If the seat rules for the house, the five drawers are never opened together. In time each office weeds its old papers, as offices do, and not one person anywhere will have broken a single rule. If the seat rules the other way, it takes one clerk one afternoon to fetch the five files to one desk. Then every gate shuts on the same morning. And the enrollee finds out what that sixth week was for on a morning when there is nothing left to do about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:10976:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log-5417-editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:165:She went to her desk and took up the brown pocket code that lived on its corner, the one she had underlined in her second year and again nine years later, and opened it near the back without looking for the page. Then she sat down, and pointed him with two fingers at the visitor's chair, and he sat. Neither of those things was usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:11047:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2416:"So that the gate can shut on every field the same morning." Seln laid one fingertip beside each bean in turn as he spoke, not touching any of them. "Chapter fourteen lets the registry restrict an unclassified person: his standing, his papers, his endorsements, everything that's tied to him. A restriction that runs office by office leaks. Somebody hears, somebody moves a deed, somebody's cousin carries a letter across a district line before the freeze arrives. So before anything runs, a clerk goes round every fence the man has. Who sponsors him. Which houses hold his papers. What his people own, and where they own it. The tin box under the bench." His voice did not change at all on that. "Every gate is found and counted first. Then on the morning it's wanted, every gate shuts at once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:11055:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2432:"And then nothing at all." Seln took his finger back. "You won't find this part in any code. No office has ever needed it written down. A finished inventory isn't an instrument. Nobody serves it. Nobody publishes it. It has no title. It is five files in five drawers in five offices, every one of them lawful, every one as dull as a laundry list, and all five indexed under one name." He squared the four beans again with the side of his finger, and did not touch the fifth. "If the seat rules for the house, the five drawers are never opened together. In time each office weeds its old papers, as offices do, and not one person anywhere will have broken a single rule. If the seat rules the other way, it takes one clerk one afternoon to fetch the five files to one desk. Then every gate shuts on the same morning. And the enrollee finds out what that sixth week was for on a morning when there is nothing left to do about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:11065:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log-2646-Ilsev closed the fold of paper over the shavings, put it in her coat pocket, and bent her head toward the platform to the depth the registry taught, no more and no less. Then she opened the topmost file and began to read it with the patience of somebody who has read it three times and intends one more before she is asked a single question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:11071:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/EVENT-LIST.md:14:4. **Way in: the denial already filed.** Day 45, mid-morning. He opens the boy's folder for something else and finds the denial lying on his nine leaves, and cannot at once remember putting it there. Work backward: the pressed seal (a grade of reply); the clerk's single unbroken tread up the flight; then the two sentences [P]. Then the letter-book test: his own refusal from his eleventh year laid beside the seat's — the same school, the same bench. Then the reading as a sentence and not a reply; the craft he taught; the missing citation; *fully constituted* meaning full before he asked; cost to its writer nothing. He reads it one last time and shuts the folder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:11080:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/EVENT-LIST.md-57-3. **Way in: Jent's retraction.** Start at the moment he stops mid-sentence and withdraws it; then backfill what he had already given away (enrollment, clause, paper — "every word"); then the grounds as Cael's notes have them, not numbered: confidence of the registry; no reading, no trust; discretion whole; describes not evaluates. **Orientation beat 1** (Cael): what's now gone from the fight and what's left that can hurt them. End on the folder he never opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:11111:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1372:"So that the gate can shut on every field the same morning." Seln laid one fingertip beside each bean in turn as he spoke, not touching any of them. "Chapter fourteen lets the registry restrict an unclassified person: his standing, his papers, his endorsements, everything that's tied to him. A restriction that runs office by office leaks. Somebody hears, somebody moves a deed, somebody's cousin carries a letter across a district line before the freeze arrives. So before anything runs, a clerk goes round every fence the man has. Who sponsors him. Which houses hold his papers. What his people own, and where they own it. The tin box under the bench." His voice did not change at all on that. "Every gate is found and counted first. Then on the morning it's wanted, every gate shuts at once."
