editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12246:   185	The lodge-man had painted it himself in white on black, years before Bracken came to the hill, and he went over the letters every spring with a fine brush. *CARTS — ROUND BY THE LANE. CALLERS — SIGN AND WAIT. POST — THE BAG ON THE HOOK. ANYTHING SEALED — FETCH THE REGISTRAR.* Cael watched Withrow find the last line with her eyes and stop on it. The lodge-man had done exactly what his board said. Anything sealed had come up the road, and he had sent his boy for the registrar, and he had shut his window so that he could stand in his own door and see the whole of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12258:   197	The rider looked at her for a moment. Then he looked at the lodge-man. Then he opened the paper, and held it at his chest, and read it out in a level voice that went off the walls of the court and came back off them, so that Cael heard every word of it twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12266:   205	He sat on his heels behind the bell-frame and heard the name at the top of it go round the court. *Caelen Hesk-ward.* Nobody who knew him had ever said it to his face. It was the name the registry kept for him, the way a shop keeps a number on a parcel, and here it was coming up off the flags of his own gate in a stranger's voice, with an order underneath it. *Attendance. Transport and escort provided. Produce the enrollee.* And at the bottom, the five words he had first read on the morning of the eighth day at the foot of a challenge's first page, and had read at the foot of every registry instrument since, and had never heard spoken by anybody at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12279:79:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12282:137:It was a small brass key worn bright at the bow, and it had lived in that pocket for longer than he had lived in any room. He held it on his palm in the pale square of light. Above him, on the shelf over the copying table, in the shadow, was the thing it opened. He did not look up at the shelf. He looked at the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12285:79:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12287:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12288:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:Cael saw it from the covered walk, a little after the second bell on the Sixth-day morning, on his way across to the wing. It was a small thing. In two years he had never once seen that window shut in daylight. The lodge-man sat behind it on his high stool from the first bell to the last, with his tea and the bluff road in front of him, and talked to anybody who came up it. Now the shutter came across the glass from the inside, and a moment later the lodge door opened, and the lodge-man stood in it without his hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:12289:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:79:Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
