editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:173:The door was on the latch. Seln opened it, and stood aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:25:They had the door of the old lumber room propped open with a broken chair, and they were working the table through it on its side, sweating and grumbling at it in the low courteous voices porters used on the library stair. Behind them the room itself stood half empty. It had held, for as long as Cael had known it, the house's spare benches and a map case with no maps in it and two rolled carpets nobody would admit to owning. Now the carpets were out on the landing and the benches gone, and somebody had already swept the floor in long straight strokes and left the broom against the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:The post room was behind the records hall's counter, a narrow whitewashed room with a long sorting bench under the window and pigeonholes from floor to ceiling along the other wall. The district courier's bag came up the hill in the dark and was opened there at first light. Usually the post porter opened it, a broad slow man who had done the sort for twenty years. This morning the post porter was sitting on a stool in the corner with his left arm strapped across his chest, looking unhappy, and the man at the sorting bench with the bag open in front of him was Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:181:Seln opened the book at the week's first page and began to read it, and went on listening, and said "Mm" at the right places, while the lodge-man told him the whole of the carter. Then he told him about the carter's father, who had also scraped the gatepost, and what he had said to that one in his day, which had been a good deal more than he had said to the son. It took a quarter of an hour. Cael stood by the stove, where Seln had put him with a glance, and drank the tea the lodge-man had poured him too, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:31:On the fourth bench Lira had her hands under her, as Karis had sat on hers in the Crown yard, and Brom sat beside her with his coat buttoned over four apples. Gault had his case very straight on his knees. The assistant was holding something flat against her chest, and Cael saw the edge of it and knew it for a clean sheet of the house's good paper, which she had brought to write on and would not put a word to. At the end of the bench, by the pillar, Seln sat as he had sat last week, with his hands in his lap, looking at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:75:"That was an honest answer, and it is the whole of this case. Every leaf the house has filed went to the facts, and the petitioner has agreed the facts. What is left is a discretion. The registry may ask this question, the seat has held, if it chooses to, and I do not come here to say that it may not; I am bound by the seat's holding and I respect it." She paused. "I come to ask what the discretion is for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:Cael sat very still behind Withrow's chair. He knew what the eleven seconds had been, and so did she. She had read them to him out of a locked case, with a glass, on a night when she wore gloves; and she had just laid them before a seat as a plain fact from a district's record, with nothing behind them, because that was all she was allowed to lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:"So this is the remedy," said Karis. "Stand a man on that scale, write down that it returned nothing, and then apply to him the chapter of the code that opens by supposing an evaluation under the standard protocol which *returns no valid classification.*" She gave the four words their full length. "I have read the fourteenth chapter a great many times this year. It is a careful piece of drafting. It was written for the case in which the registry tried, in good faith, to find out what a person was, and could not, and it gives the registry something to do in that case, so that the person is not left in no column at all. It is harsh, and I think it was meant as a kindness."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. The refusal was the Archmarshal's; the form's old column had only Gault's title to put it under. Gault sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:59:"I'm not asking to be difficult," she said. "I'm asking because I sat on that bench last night and listened to you all read forty of them out, and I've been doing my drills in my head all day, and I can't make them go." She looked at the coats. "That seat down there runs on it, and so does the evaluation. Every sitting we go to, we're sitting in the room and answering it, and it's the thing in the case. They want to read you again. We know now what reads you." She pulled her coat closer. "My carters said the stamps stop four days east, loaded, or three days light. I've been carrying that in my pocket since the dairy. We could be past the last station by the Seventh-day after next if we left on the Seventh-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:167:The five of them sat in the reading room under the shaded lamp, with the case on its shelf between the grey boxes and the lamp in the window turned up as high as every night, and Karis laid it out for them on the bare cloth in the order she had found it. She had the code's second volume open on the left and the old enrollment book open on the right, and a single sheet of her own between them with five lines on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:The records hall was dark except for the one lamp on the counter. Rain had not come yet, but it was in the air, and the tall windows along the east side showed nothing but grey. Bracken stood on the little step he kept under the counter for the purpose, with the clock's glass door open and the key in his hand, and turned it eleven times. He always turned it eleven times. Seln had counted it on perhaps eighty Seventh-days and had never once heard a twelfth. Then the registrar shut the glass, stepped down, put the step away with his foot, and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Bracken looked at him over the spectacles for a little while. Seln let him look. Then the registrar nodded once, the small nod he gave to a form that had been filled in properly, and opened the day-book, and dipped his pen. He did not ask why. In two years he had never once asked Seln why about anything, and Seln had never once stopped being grateful for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The seat wanted the maker of the copies to swear to them, and the advocate of record to sign beside his oath. Karis had said at supper that she would not put her name to a page she had not read against the thing it was copied from, and that this was not caution but manners. So the wing's original cards had come up the library stair in two shallow boxes under the porter's oilcloth, with Seln's leave and Bracken's receipt, and the second set of Seln's copies lay open beside them. He had made two of everything. One had gone down to the seat in the house's boards. The other had stayed in the annex, against exactly this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:5:Nobody saw her work; that was the arrangement. The assistant went home at the sixth bell every evening with a story about glue, and believed it, and told Ephram, who told the refectory, so that by the Fourth-day half the hill knew that the researcher at the top of the library was mending a founding volume with something that smelled, and kept away. The lamp burned in the window at its usual height every night and went out at its usual hour. Behind it, at the far end of the long table under the brown-paper shade, Karis sat with the case open and the two keys in the room, Seln's for an hour at a time while he sat silent at the middle of the bench, and laid the whole of it out again, and again, and again, until it would lie in the order she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:Cael went up on the third night only to bring her bread, because Brom said somebody had to and Brom's shoulder did not like the top stair yet. She did not look up when he came in. The forty pairs were not in four rows any longer. They lay in a single long line down the whole of the table, sorted by something he could not see, with a slip of paper on each in her small upright hand. The memoranda stood beside them in nineteen separate piles, one issue to a pile. Every seal on every one of them had a numbered slip tucked under it, and the nine new leaves from Greyvane lay open across the window end in their fresh grey wax. Seln sat at the middle of the bench with his hands flat and watched her. Karis asked Cael one question, about Denvash, with her eyes still on the seals, and he answered it standing at her elbow, and Seln said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:15:The case stood shut between the grey boxes on its shelf. The long table was bare again to its faded green cloth. In the middle of the cloth, under the shaded lamp, lay four slips of paper folded once and numbered on the outside in her hand, one to four, in a row. A little apart from them lay a fifth, face down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:19:"Four findings," she said, "and one limit. I'm going to give you them in the order I found them, which is the order they stand on each other in. I'm not going past the case. If anybody asks me to go past it, I'll stop, and I'll stop for good, and I mean that." She looked round at them. "Not because I'm cross, but because the minute I guess at something in front of this paper, the paper's no use to anybody, including me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:41:*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness.* He had the letter by heart; it lived inside the back board of Hesk's book, in the satchel across his chest. He had never told Karis its words. She had come to the same sentence from the far side of a case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:109:"Middle." She touched the leaf's place on the table, though the leaf was in its sleeve in the case. "Read it again in your head: *The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows.* It's a note written by somebody who added something to a thing that was already there, and wanted it understood which part was his." She looked at Brom. "Think of your father's forge in the shed past the gardens, and the mill that must have stood on the estate's stream. If a millwright wants a wheel, he doesn't dig the river, because the river's there already. He cuts a race beside it, and puts his wheel in the race, and spends the rest of his life keeping the race clear of weed. That's maintenance, and that's what this leaf is: a millwright's note about a race. It doesn't say a word about who dug the river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:"Under the policy there's something older: the reading, whatever it is that wakes behind your breastbone at Kindling and reads you. The function sits on it. It's older than the function by however long, and the case doesn't say how long, and I don't know." She laid her hand on the cloth. "But the case does show me one thing about what was there before the function came, because the first memoranda had to clear it out of the way. The first three issues, about eleven years apart, in the oldest paper. And they're not about the forty. They're about the old custom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:135:"And that's where the case ends," she said. "So here is the limit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:143:"I can tell you when the policy began, near enough, and that it was a policy, and that it's been kept since by forms. I can tell you that the oldest thing it did was shut a door the old way had left open, and take the name off the people who'd used it. I can't tell you what the old word was for, before. I can't tell you what the reading is, underneath the function, or how old, or how it came to be there to have a race cut beside it. Nothing in that case reaches down that far, not a line." She put the slip on top of the first one. "We've explained the middle of the story. Not the beginning. Entered as a limit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:161:"I went through everything for the anomaly," she said to Cael, "for session nine. You asked me to, at Greyvane, and I've never forgotten that you asked." She did not look at the slip. "Anything in the forty that read across a family with nobody of that family near. Anything in the reports about a practitioner who took something from no one. Any memorandum that mentions Tide by name, or a reading at a distance, or one that came without an engagement. Anything in the leaf." She shook her head. "Nothing. Not one line in it touches session nine. The function works at Kindling, and session nine wasn't a Kindling. Whatever it is, the case didn't do it and doesn't know about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:165:He opened it at the table, under the shaded lamp, at the clean leaf after Seln's terms, and wrote it there with all of them watching, because he wanted them to see it written. He had kept that one honest for three years and he was going to go on keeping it honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:177:For the fourth she did not unfold the slip at all. She took the grey notebook out of her coat instead and opened it at the inside of its front cover, where a few lines had gathered over two years that belonged to no case: the fielding clause, the squad rule, the charter's slip, Jent's answer on its plain card, and above them all, in her small hand, the line from the first night of the year. Cael knew it before she put her finger under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:185:"Now I've got it." She put her finger on the line. "The petition asks for one thing, compelled re-evaluation under standard protocol. It doesn't care about the provision or the house or the clause; it wants him in front of a reading again." She looked at the case on its shelf. "A re-evaluation is an evaluation. The memorandum covers every evaluation at which a reading is entered. If the reading reads him this time, older, with eight things in him instead of none, the function is waiting beside it with its advice, and the advice is entered, and the order comes down, and he's a column, whatever column it chooses: one family, first rank, margin not to be entered." Her voice was very steady. "And if the reading goes out again, the way it went out at Denvash, then there's no valid classification, and it's chapter fourteen, in sequence: nullification, suspension, restriction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:217:Karis looked at him across the table. Then she closed the notebook on its cover, and gathered up the four slips and gave them to Seln, who unlocked the case on its shelf and put them in the lid's pocket beside the leaf and locked it again. The fifth slip she kept face down, without ever turning it over, and put it in her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:235:"The case," said Lira. "If it can't be filed, if there's nobody to send it to who isn't standing on it. If we can't publish it without spending Seln, and we can't use it at the seat without the seat reading it as a forgery, and Karis won't take it a line past the paper." She looked down the main stair into the lamplit dark. "Twelve years on a shelf and five nights on her table, and it doesn't prove anything to anybody who matters. What's it for?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:It reached Seln on the Third-day in the ordinary post, inside a printer's circular about a new kind of ruled ledger, folded twice and pushed down between the pages where nobody opening a circular would look. It was written in pencil, in capitals, by a man who had learned his letters late and did not trust the small ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:101:The wing's clock went on with its slow tick over the counter. Nothing in the room changed its note. Seln stood with his two hands flat on the copying table and understood, quite calmly, that this was the largest thing that had happened to him since a green corridor twelve years ago, and that it had happened on a weekday morning between the post and the noon bell, and that nobody would ever write it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:He reached up and took the case off the shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:119:The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:123:Seln unbuttoned his coat and set the case on the sill beside the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:171:The boy almost smiled. Then he did not, and looked at the case again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:185:He put the case back on the shelf above the copying table, where it had stood for two years and where nobody had ever once looked for it, and blew out the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:229:There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:233:He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:255:*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. There has not been a First-day like it in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:333:The cart came down the bank and into the stream. Cael felt the water take the wheel beside him, and saw it pile up brown against the spokes. Brom was at the tailboard, hip-deep, with both arms under the bed; he was not carrying it, and was only there, braced, in case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:99:The queue had shortened since the first days. The intake had learned the counter's hours and stopped coming in a body. A single Ash first-year stood ahead of him with a form she had filled in twice, once in pencil and once over the pencil in ink. Behind the counter the desk clerk added his column by the window. Gault's door stood shut. And at the copying table, under the shelf with the small unmarked case on it, the teaching assistant rose to take the first-year's sheet and read it from the name to the signature at his even pace, and stamped it, and tore the slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:149:Cael looked round the table, because looking was what he did when there was nothing to do. Karis with her palm on the notebook's cover. Brom bent forward with his elbows on his knees and his eyes on the board. Lira with the question still lying open where she had put it, and her chin up over it, waiting for one of them to pick it up. And at the middle of the bench Seln, who had walked fences like these himself for years and knew from the inside what the morning after one looked like, sitting with his hands quite still on either side of the five beans, as if the table might tip if he moved them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:187:"The girl kept coming to my counter after there was nothing more of him to file," said Withrow. "Every week, with her mother's people's address written on the back of her hand in case I had news." She was quiet a moment. "Once, I did. A toll paid at the ferry by the last station, eastbound, in his name. I gave her that. It was the last line anybody ever wrote about him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:*Nobody has said it. Not since the wall, on the day after the case. Lira asked why we were still here and Karis priced it, and since then we have gone to every sitting and landed on the right foot and held loads and tied knots, and nobody has once said what any of it is for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:185:There was usually a trunk on it, or a fiddle in a case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:219:Nobody mentioned the boxes at the noon meal. Karis sat with a volume open beside her bowl and read it while she ate. Lira, across from her, described at length a thing Rooke had said to the Force about his knees. Brom ate for three, and Seln was at the staff table with the desk clerk, turning his cup. Each of them, Cael was quite sure, knew what stood on the deposit shelf at the north end of the records hall, and how many, and whose knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:235:He did not think that had been wrong. If he had gone east in the week of the case, the word would be on four files now, and a line on Withrow's charter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:43:Gault was in the lodge doorway beside Cael. Nobody had seen him come across the court; he had his case clamped under one arm and a small black notebook in his other hand, and he had evidently been standing there for the last two entries.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:119:"I've never stood near enough to one at work to say, and I won't guess." Seln opened his eyes and looked at the copy. "I know where they're posted, and it isn't escort. Escort is for clerks on good horses. One at that grade, standing where she stood, is enforcement." He laid one fingertip on the table beside the order, not touching it. "Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:121:Seln turned his head toward Cael without opening his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:Karis finished her copying and set down her pen. Lira opened her eyes. Brom sat with his elbows on his knees. And Seln went on looking at the order, longer than any of them, with his hands flat and still on either side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:135:It was a surveyor's case. He had bought it in his fifth posting, from a maker who built them for men who took levels across wet country. It had a brass lock and no mark outside, and inside the lid a small stamp, a pair of dividers, which he had looked at perhaps ten thousand times. Before the case there had been an oilcloth and a boot-box. Before that, for five weeks, there had been only a floor, and one lamp, and a press whose doors he shut each morning before the building woke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:141:*A thing kept is a wall.* An old woman with a stick had taught him that, over a chandler's, with a cat on her knee. *A thing spent is a door, and doors open both ways.* He had said those lines on nineteen first mornings. He had kept the case shut for twelve years and it had been a very good wall: it had stood between him and every person he was ever sent to watch, and he had sat behind it and written true things, and been safe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:205:She knew now what it opened into. Karis had said it: both doors, the same room. If she climbed every rung Rooke had set for her and stood before the advancement sitting and was rated Silver, she would be a Silver in a registry that struck children's margins as a matter of form. She would be on the paper, and the paper was the thing in the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:69:She did not go back over the year. She laid a single sheet on the edge of the table, with her steel rule across it, and told the seat what the house had found when it went looking for a like case, and how far back it had gone, and in whose digests.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:129:He was not afraid of it. A month ago he would have been. He had stood at a rail in wing three and watched Ephram write on the back of a sheet, and had gone cold, because for two years the worst thing anybody on the hill could do to him was to ask. Now there was a date he could count to, and after it there would be either no band or a door, and in neither case anything left to guard from a friend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:211:He stood by the great door a moment after the others had gone through it, with the clock ticking in his hand and losing its minutes. Seln still held the door, and the lanterns were out behind them. He had laughed tonight, at a haystack in a chair, until his ribs remembered the road. He could not think when he had last laughed like that on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:9:She was at her desk with the lamp turned low and her hands on the blotter, a hand's width apart, and she was not writing. The two piles of letters were gone. The charter hung in its frame behind her, and under it the locked case stood open, and the folder from the case lay on the desk in front of her, shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:75:"That is what a year of this comes to on paper. An enrollee was challenged. The house answered and was cleared. The enrollee left. Every inspector who opens that case for the next thirty years will start at the grant and turn forward, and that is what he will read, and he will draw the natural conclusion from it. He will think the boy saw which way it was going, and ran, and that the house was well rid of him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:227:On the fourth side was the faculty stand. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair at its middle, upright, with her hands in her lap, and Bracken stood behind her. Gault was at the stand's end with his instrument case set very straight beside him, though there was nothing in a bout for it to measure. At the long table Rooke stood with his glass and his board, and his clerk sat at his elbow with the house's book open, and the house's healer had the bench below with her bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:The bundle opened on the cloth like a book with no spine. It was reports, a few sheets each, folded once lengthwise in the field service's way, the way a man folds a paper to carry it inside his coat on a horse. The hands varied, because Seln had copied each in the shape of its writer as he had copied the father's letter, and Cael could see a hurried man and a careful one, a man who pressed hard and one who barely touched the page. Every one of them was an evaluator writing from somewhere that was not a desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:83:The leaf was last, as it had been last out of the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:87:"Everything else is my hand," he said. "The orders and the records and the reports I copied, at night, over five weeks. The memoranda were spares. The press held three or four of every issue and nobody had counted them in a generation, so I took one of each. This is the only thing in the case that someone else made." He took his finger off it. "A copy of it would have been worth nothing. Everything it says is in what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:He did not sit down to say it, and he did not raise his voice. He stood at the middle of the table's far side with the whole of the case laid out in front of him in the lamplight, the forty and the bundle and the two stacks and the leaf, and he said it to the room the way he had once read a form to a man in a doorway at the third hour, one plain sentence after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:155:"My fourth posting, twelve years ago." Seln put one hand back on the table, as if the wood steadied him. "A district seat in an old building, a records room on its top floor with presses round the walls, the kind with doors. I was looking for a subject's travel endorsements. They were filed where they should not have been, so I opened presses until I found them, and in the fourth press I found these." He moved his eyes over the rows. "The orders were in bound runs, a few years to a volume, with the records bundled behind them, and the memoranda on the bottom shelf in a box. And at the back of the box, loose, under the oldest, that." He did not look at the leaf. "Nobody had opened the press in years; the dust said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:159:"Over five weeks, at night. The posting gave me the room's key because I was a junior who stayed late, and juniors who stay late are trusted with keys." He said it without any pride at all. "One lamp on the floor, so the window stayed dark, and a few sheets a night in the lining of my coat. The case I bought afterward, in another town, from a man who made them for surveyors. I never told him what it was for, and he never asked, and he put his stamp inside the lid because he was proud of his work." He paused. "I did not understand it, and I want that said. I copied it because it was paper that sat a little above where it should have sat, and I had been trained to notice that, and I did not know what to do with what I noticed except keep it. For a year I thought it was a district's bad habit, a careless office."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:201:It was past the ninth bell. The lamp in the window would be wanted out within the hour or the valley would notice a difference, and the case had to be somewhere before it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:209:He chose the place himself. He walked along Karis's shelves until he came to the row of her grey document boxes on the second shelf from the floor, the ones with her initials on their spines, which held her Greyvane papers and had not been moved in a year. Two of them stood apart by about the width of a man's hand. He measured the gap with his eyes and then with the case, and it went in between them and stood there, dark leather between grey card, a little shorter than its neighbours. From the door it looked like one more box nobody had labelled yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:211:"The room has your key," he said to Karis. "The case has mine." He touched his waistcoat pocket. "Two keys, and neither of us opens it alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:217:They put it back in the order it had come out, and it went back faster than it had come out, because Seln did it and nobody else touched it. The leaf went into its sleeve and the sleeve into the lid's pocket. The memoranda went in, oldest at the bottom. The bundle, tied again in the wing's knot. The forty, row by row, squared. The lid closed and the small key turned with its dry sound, and Seln carried the case to its place between the grey boxes and stood it there and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:83:The reason line he left empty. It was not that he had no reason, for he had about two hundred years of it, in a case, in a post satchel; but it did not go into a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:There was a bed, and a press, and a table, and a sill, and he had lived among them for two years and the better part of a third. Seln had given the rule in an orchard: forty pounds for a day, and thirty for thirty days. Cael's came to less than thirty. He folded two shirts and the spare coat, and rolled the strapping the healer had left him, and put Hesk's clock into a stocking; it was a quarter of an hour behind the bell, and losing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:249:He carried it down the corridor to the stove at the stair's head, which the porter kept in through the last week, for the damp. He opened the door and fed it the book a gathering at a time, and watched each one catch and go grey and fall in. It was the first record he had ever destroyed, and he thought that Karis would have understood it, and that Seln would not have needed to be told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:126:The coachman had the horses back in the traces. Brom lifted Lira into the coach in his one good arm as if she weighed nothing, and set her on the seat with her foot up on the record cases, and got in after her. Karis climbed in holding her hand up. Seln shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:158:She was sitting with her strapped foot on the record cases and her back in the corner and her face still white, and she said it before anybody else could say anything. "Six people with hobbles and a wagon with rings in the floor tried to take him off a road this morning, two hours from here. The seat's sitting in there. Somebody in that room should write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:168:"The coachman will tell the stables tonight," said Seln, from the open trap, without turning round. "The stables will tell the town by the end of the week. That's a record. It's the only one that'll get written that anybody reads."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:101:Cael watched him do it. He had watched Seln look at rooms for two years, at counters and sittings and in the long room at Ostrand from the one seat that saw every door, and he knew the order Seln looked at things in, because it was always the same. The door first: the brass lock, the frame, the card in its brass holder at eye height outside. Then the windows, both of them, the one over the window seat and the narrow one at the end, and what they looked out on, which was the dark and the top of the library's roof and, a long way below, the river. Then the landing behind him, and the annex door across it, and the stair going down. Then the table, with its field of turned backs. Then the people in it, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:179:Only then did Cael look at the rest. The flap came down over everything. The strap was long enough to cross a man's body and ride on the opposite hip, so that the bag would sit still when he walked and not swing when he ran. And the buckle was not a bought buckle. Hesk's file marks were on the inside of the tongue, where only somebody who had slept in the shavings under that bench would think to look, and the frame had the little bevel Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth. He ran his thumb over the bevel. The leather round it was the heavy brown kind Hesk kept for instrument cases and would not waste on anything that did not deserve it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:239:He opened the door. Seln stood in the corridor in his coat, with his hands at his sides and nothing in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:253:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:141:Above the copying table, on the shelf where it had stood before Norhold, sat a small locked case with no mark on it. It had gone to Norhold and come back without anybody seeing it opened. Nobody looked at it now, and Cael did not look at it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:"I've read that section a great many times," he said. "A clerk reads everything, in case. I've kept this counter thirteen years and never once seen it called on." He took his finger off the notice. "You'll want to know who it will be. So shall I. I know every registrar who has sat that seat in my time, and which of them likes a long sitting, and which takes his luncheon at the eleventh bell whatever the advocate is saying. None of that is any use. The minute won't tell you, and it won't tell me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:57:"At Greyvane, in my first term," she said, to the window, "an advocate gave me a point at a sitting. A good point. One I'd spent a fortnight on. He stood up and said the archive was right and he wouldn't dispute it, and sat down. I went back to my room and was pleased with myself all evening." The cart lurched forward a yard and stopped again. "In the morning he took the case on a point I hadn't looked at, because I'd spent the whole of the night before being pleased."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:181:Lira laughed out loud, and put her hand over her mouth, and laughed again behind it. Brom's shoulders moved once. Even the counsel's mouth went up at one corner before she stopped it. Seln did not open his eyes. Cael thought that the man might just have made the first joke of his life in front of witnesses, and that he would deny it at the counter in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:167:He did not know that it would be different from what the plate had shown. Most were not. There had been forty pairs in a locked case, from a few districts over a few years, and Karis would not guess past them and Seln had stopped estimating. This girl might walk out of that door with the truth on a slip of paper. He would never know. That was the whole of the horror of it, standing in the sun by a trough. Neither would she. If the margin had run past her column, nobody would enter it, and somebody would be told to observe her for nine weeks, and would see a girl with a green ribbon going about her life, and would write *nothing to report*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:17:"Twelve sections," she said. "Frames. Stations. Assessors suspected of being bought. Panels whose records argue with themselves. Instruments, or the people who keep instruments." She lifted the rule. "I've looked tonight for the case where somebody turned these sections round to point at the man standing in the frame instead of the frame. There isn't one. This is new."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:45:"Good. Their whole case will be that your floor is a description: a panel, a figure, a day, witnesses, nothing more. Mine will be that it is an evaluation, and the oldest kind, older than any frame, and I'll make it as well as anybody living could make it." She slid the notebook back. "Then the seat will choose. You've counted how many times they wrote the word it'll choose with." She tapped the column at *nine*. "Go on counting. Bring me a column like this every week until it's over. A count is evidence. Indignation never was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:She turned to the inside of her notebook's front cover. Over two years Cael had watched a few lines gather there that belonged to no case: the fielding clause, a line about the squad rule, a few words copied from the founding articles of a charter she had promised herself not to open until the cold term. She wrote the new one under them, small, and closed the cover, and was back at her heading before the ink had dried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:147:The fire-watch climbed down off his bucket at the turn of the hour, and the square of light on the counter slid sideways and went out. Seln sat on in the dark with the key still on his palm. Before he put it away he said one sentence to himself, in the voice he kept for the audit, which was the only voice in himself he believed, so that it would be on a record somewhere even if the only record was in his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:33:So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:115:Cael sat with his back to the horses and turned the three words over. They were true. Every figure on every card in Brom's two cases had been earned on the house's floors, by him, on purpose, to the decimal. They were his in the way a rotation page was his, because he had written it the night before. He thought that if the clerk had asked whether they were all of him, he would not have known what to say, and he was glad the form did not ask it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:117:The bank road ran west along the near side of the river, high on its terrace, with the water on the left below them roaring in the dark. Twice the wheels went into standing water to the hub and the coach lurched, and the coachman talked to the horses until they leaned into it and pulled out. Once a fallen branch lay across the road and Brom got down and dragged it off into the ditch and got back in, wet to the knees, and put the cases back on his lap without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:241:He did not hurry. He stepped down into the dust with the two record cases, and set them on the coach step, side by side, squared, and then walked forward along the foot of the south bank, under the lip, into the channel. He stopped in the middle of it. He turned round to face the cart and the culvert and the gully, and set his feet, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:19:"Because the men who write codes can't see every case coming," he said, "and they know they can't. *Discretion* is the room they leave for the cases they couldn't see. Most nights it's the kindest word in the book." He looked down at the instrument. "It depends who's standing in the room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:81:Cael ruled a line under his column. A man building a case writes *evidence* on every page, because one day he will be standing in front of a seat with his evidence in his hands. This man had written *discretion*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:109:The other three went quickly: a mason's guild that had put an apprentice into its school by demonstration and been challenged on the form of its panel, and two guild cases so old that the paper had gone brown and soft and the parties had worn down to the names of their trades. All four sustained. Bracken closed each cover as Karis finished and laid it back in its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had said, in a harness room, that he could not read a registry sheet anymore; that since the case he looked at one and saw only what it was a form of. He was not reading these. He was doing the one honest thing he could still do with such paper, which was to say what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:"I have not yet seen it. I shall have seen it before you do." Seln took the sheet back. He rose, without hurry, and opened the stove door with the hem of his cuff, and put the sheet in, where it caught at one corner. Behind it the stove was deep in fine grey ash, a great deal more than one mild morning should have made. "A drawing can be found," he said, and shut the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:5:"There's one man outside this room who's owed the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:7:They were all there. They had come up by their three ways after supper as they did every evening now, without needing a floor schedule to tell them when. Karis had put the enrollment book back on Bracken's counter on the First-day before the second bell, as promised, and Bracken had weighed it on his palm and written it in. The case stood on its shelf between the grey boxes, and the lamp burned in the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:13:"Seln paid twelve years for that case," Cael said, "and we've had it six nights. We can't file it, and we can't publish it, and we can't use it. But we can let one honest man inside the thing see what the thing is, before they hand him the pen." He looked at the cloth. "I think we owe him that, and I think it's the same debt as the stair. I don't know how to tell the difference."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:19:"He takes it up the stair the morning after," he said, "because that's what an honest man does with a paper he's been shown. He doesn't keep it in a drawer. He sends it to the office it concerns, with a covering note in his best hand, the way that clerk in the river town did." He did not look at the case. "And the stair eats it. And somebody at the top of the stair reads the covering note and asks where an Archmarshal saw forty copied orders in a surveyor's case, and the answer's a wing at Halcenvane. And Seln's gone by the end of the month, and none of us ever finds out where."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:25:"He verifies it," she said. "He's an evaluator; it's the whole of what he is. He doesn't believe a thing because it's on a table; he measures it. So he goes home and pulls a frame reading, or a station sheet, or a fresh reading on one of the forty, to see whether the case is true." She turned her pale fingers over on the cloth. "And the frame's theirs, and the station's theirs, and the reading runs through the function. Every instrument he could check it with comes back and says the case is wrong. And he believes his instruments, because he's believed them for forty years. And somewhere it gets minuted that a forgery was offered to the Archmarshal by persons connected with the respondent at Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:31:"He believes it," she said, "all of it, at once, the way you'd want him to, and it breaks him." She was looking at the case. "He's been measuring true for forty years because he thought true was the point. We show him the point was never true. And he goes home, and sits at his desk, and does nothing, because there's nothing he can do. He signs what he's given, or he doesn't, and either way he's a broken man signing things." She lifted her chin. "And we'd have done that to him. We'd have spent the one good man in the whole building for a bad night's sleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:35:"He is already theirs," he said. "Norhold was a knock, dressed as a kindness. A lent room, no paper, a warning that made you trust him. That is the third grade done well. And the case goes to the people it was taken from, by the hand of the one man you would have given it to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:41:"He reads it," he said, "and he asks about me. He's an evaluator, and I'm the one subject in the Compact he's never been able to read, and he's sitting across a table from the case and from me. He asks the natural question: why is the boy's file marked, what is he, how did the function fail on him." He made himself say the rest. "And somebody in the room answers, not because they mean to, but because it's the natural thing, and he asks well, and he's kind about it, and it's late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:45:"Then that's the rule," she said. "That's the one that matters. The rest are risks, and we can weigh them, but that one's a rule." She put both hands on the table. "He gets the case. Nothing about Cael. Say it, everybody, out loud. I want to hear it in every voice in this room, so that whoever's tired at the end of an afternoon in a strange room hears their own voice saying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:47:"He gets the case," said Brom. "Nothing about Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:49:"He gets the case," said Lira. "Nothing about Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:They reached the water in the afternoon. It was the third the carter had promised, the one a person could walk across most of the year, and it ran wide and shallow over gravel, clear, no deeper than a boot, so that the gelding went through it without being spoken to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:181:"Somebody mends it." Brom was looking up. Above the counter a patch of the roof showed from inside, where the boards had been taken off and put back, and the new slates were a different colour from the old, and hung on twists of wire, not nails. "That's a carter's job. That's a man who had wire in his wagon and no slater's hammer. He's done it more than once, too. There's three lots."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:181:Seln opened the book at the week's first page and began to read it, and went on listening, and said "Mm" at the right places, while the lodge-man told him the whole of the carter. Then he told him about the carter's father, who had also scraped the gatepost, and what he had said to that one in his day, which had been a good deal more than he had said to the son. It took a quarter of an hour. Cael stood by the stove, where Seln had put him with a glance, and drank the tea the lodge-man had poured him too, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:He had very little to leave behind him: a room at the carters' inn, paid a week ahead, a bag, a coat, and an oilskin wrapper he had bought for whatever Vell would give him. On the Wednesday he went round the hill and paid what he owed. It was not much. He had always paid as he went, a copper a round to the newcomers who hit him and a mark an hour to the dock partner and a week ahead at the inn. But there were a few small things outstanding, and Brom was not a man to leave a hill with a copper owing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:53:He knew exactly where each of them was. A man in his trade kept that list the way a carter keeps his water barrels, full and lashed down and never once opened on the easy stretches of the road, because the one time you need water is the one time there is none to be had. A favour was not money. It could not be lent or broken into change. You spent it whole, once, and afterward the person who had owed it knew something about you that they had not known before: that you had wanted something enough to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:109:"Middle." She touched the leaf's place on the table, though the leaf was in its sleeve in the case. "Read it again in your head: *The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows.* It's a note written by somebody who added something to a thing that was already there, and wanted it understood which part was his." She looked at Brom. "Think of your father's forge in the shed past the gardens, and the mill that must have stood on the estate's stream. If a millwright wants a wheel, he doesn't dig the river, because the river's there already. He cuts a race beside it, and puts his wheel in the race, and spends the rest of his life keeping the race clear of weed. That's maintenance, and that's what this leaf is: a millwright's note about a race. It doesn't say a word about who dug the river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:141:On a floor he had only ever had his own hurts to price, and he had grown so used to pricing them that he did it now without thinking, as a carter greases an axle. He could not do it for Lira's feet, or for Brom's hands. He watched Karis's small straight back beside the wheel, and he had no figure for it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:205:He took his cart over without another word, and it was the first time Cael had watched him do it as a thing that could go wrong. He led the mule a step at a time. Brom went at the tail-board with both hands on it, and halfway across the near channel the off wheel slid a hand's breadth toward the white stones, quite gently, as a plate slides on a wet table. Brom leaned, and the carter clicked his tongue, and it came back into its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:219:The rock was worse than the carter had said. The green on it was a short close weed, and the water had combed it all one way, and a boot set down on it had no more hold than on a fish. Brom went along the lip on the near side with the bill reversed in his hand, feeling ahead of him with the ash haft as a blind man feels with a stick. Where the white stones stopped he reached out past the edge and let the haft go down. It went in to his fist, and his arm after it to the shoulder, and he drew it up again with nothing on the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:157:Cael marked nothing in that at first; a busy smith might well keep two. It was Brom who slowed, and then stood, and then set his box down on its end and folded his arms on the top of it, as a man does who means to be some time. He had spent a good many nights of his boyhood on a heap of sacks in a shed where his father kept a forge for the pleasure of hitting something, and he looked at a fire as Karis looked at a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:159:The left-hand forge was the kind that Cael knew. It had a hood, and a bed of charcoal, and a great leather bellows with a lad swinging on the pole. A smith in a scorched apron was fitting a shoe to a carter's cob, in a stink of burned hoof, and three more carters waited behind with their beasts on short ropes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:223:The fifth was a carter, a big slow man with a red beard, and he was not of that hold. He sat at the bench's end, holding his right wrist in his left hand as a person holds a cup that is too full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:"He said that he didn't mind it," said Brom. "The carter. I'd like to know what he does mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:145:Brom reached out with the bill and got its hook through the carter's belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:31:They went on between the hedges, and for half a mile nobody used the leave that he had been given. Cael found that he had a great deal to say and no notion of how a person began. He had not been silent all those days; they had talked at every fire, behind every wall, and through a whole cold term in a back room. It was only on a road that the mouth was shut, with the sigils in their pockets and his ten lying closed, and he had left off noticing it as a carter leaves off noticing his own wheels. He noticed it now, as it lifted. It was like setting down the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:21:They went over with the Oxhollow cart, a copper a wheel and a copper a beast out of the carter's own bag. Halfway across Brom lifted his head. In the round shed on the eastern bank something brown was walking its circle, steadily, with one white foot coming round and round into the light, and Brom watched that foot for the rest of the crossing with the face of a man who has seen a child of his doing well at a trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:83:It went down the meadow in that level carrying voice, past five fires. A woman looked at Lira's boot. A man by the nearest tent said something to his neighbour, low, and the neighbour looked at the strange cart, and then at the empty place beside the smith's shed where a hired one had stood. The Oxhollow carter had halted his mules at the edge of the grass to listen. At *carts go round* he had nodded slowly, like a person who hears his own errand read out to strangers; and at *cart lost* he looked at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:"What's a surveyor want out there?" said Brom. "On ground a carter won't put a wheel on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:"I've had no salt up since the first frost. I've had no wheel up of any kind. They're stood on your landing, I don't doubt, thirty of them, saying that they'll come when it's fit." The crook pointed down over the wall, to where the spur fell away. "That's my road. It goes through the rock there by a cut that my grandfather's people made, a hundred strides and a cart's width, and it's the only way a wheel can come to this gate. Since the frost it's had three carts off me. And a carter, and two mules."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:97:"The dog lived," said Brom. "It lived to bite the carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been taken at his word. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:61:Nobody stood next to him. There was room for two at that end of the counter, and the clerks had left it, as the carters on a bench had made room for a keeper; but this was the other kind of room, and Cael knew it well. It was the space that people leave about a person whom they have long since stopped trying to talk to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:287:The carter came over the saddle the next morning with his breeches wet to the thigh. The road was open, he said, if a person did not mind the ford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:211:"My father had a forge," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:215:"At the back of the estate, past the kitchen gardens, in a shed with a dirt floor. Not the estate's forge. His. Mother's people had a smith for the horses and the gates and the real work. My father had a shed with a dirt floor and a little forge he'd built himself, because he liked iron, and he liked a thing you could make with your hands that came out the same every time." Brom moved his feet. "He'd let me in when I was small, if I sat on the bench and didn't touch anything. I sat on that bench a lot of afternoons."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:13:Brom built the fire in the ring, from thorn that he bought of the carters for a copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:She came down the long hall as if she had been sent for about a gutter. Cael had watched her cross courts and corridors for two years and knew the walk as well as he knew Brom's step on a stair, even and unhurried, the walk of a woman who had decided long ago that nothing would ever be allowed to set its pace. With half the hill already talking outside, he understood that she had chosen it on the stair and was holding it now the way a carter holds a nervous horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:81:"Same thing, to the carter." She hefted the volume onto her own shoulder, which was not built for it, and stood with it there, frowning at the window. "When I was fourteen and walking from guild door to guild door, I'd have given a great deal for a copper. Just one. For bread." She went to the stair. "Somebody in four towns is paying a copper a week to read whether you're a person. I'd like to meet them. I'd like to ask what they'd do with the answer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:255:He looked round the fire at five faces. Every one of them had watched a thing go north out of a lane that they had been certain could not happen, and had walked beside him for a day without a word about it. They had not gone quiet, or careful. The big man had let another person make his bread sooner than leave the others without, and the small one had made a joke of it on paper, and Lira had told a carter's boot what she thought of it. It was the same crew that had sat at a fire above a wash, with a healer added, who was looking at her own heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-38.md:55:The side floor by the north wall was thin at that hour, as it always was: fifty on the benches, carters and newcomers and a knot of Orvet's people at the west post. The barge lad was nineteen or twenty, with arms so long that his practice blade looked short in his hand, and he talked to himself between cuts in a low steady mutter. Cael had watched him twice from the bench in the season without writing anything, because there had not seemed anything to write. He chained threes. He chained them on everybody, every exchange, high and across and back; somebody had once told him the chain was the whole of fighting, and he had never heard otherwise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:93:"Find them how?" said Brom. "There's not a carter in this country who'd walk us up to one. They've been going round them since before there were carters."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:329:"Two feet, clean through," said Karis. "He took our cart over it on his back." She touched Brom's sleeve. "No carter crosses that by himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:77:It had been a slow day. The carter would not take his mule faster than the worst leg in the party, and that was sometimes Cael's and sometimes the cart's own, for a hind wheel had begun to talk. In the middle of the afternoon the tail had slid in a wet rut, toward Brom, and he had caught the rail before it could go over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:The carter turned his head and considered Lira for some time. He did not seem offended. He seemed to be working out how much of an answer a person was owed who knew no better than to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:87:Nobody spoke for the whole of that day, and Cael found that it was no longer anything he did on purpose. Brom carried the bill across his shoulder with the blade out. Lira's knife rode on her hip. At noon they sat in the ruts and ate cold cake, and the carter, who had watched them out of the side of his eye since the saddle, stopped watching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:105:Brom looked at it. Then he looked at the carter, who was looking at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:105:At a ford a little further on, another timber wagon had gone in too deep and stuck, and the columns stopped behind it without anybody's ordering them to. For the better part of an hour the green wagons' fighters and the coast house's riders stood up to their knees in cold water with their shoulders against the load, and Brom and both reserves with them. The carter apologized to every one of them in a dialect nobody could follow. When the wagon came free he drew straight over onto the verge and stood there, hat in hand, while every column went by him, and Cael watched him stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:The first was the pair from Brom's stone. They had left a large silent man kneeling in a river and come up out of the channel onto the gravel, and they passed within ten feet of Cael, and of Lira sitting in the water with a knife; and neither of them turned its head. They went by as carters go by a milestone, and trotted away down the spine of the bar, shoulder to shoulder, toward the tail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:227:The carter stood inside the door with his hat in his hands. He was a short thick man with twenty-two years on one road, and he had driven a light cart up a frozen valley since first light; his eyebrows were white with it. He had held a scrap of bark out across the board, and Cael had had a book in one hand and a pencil in the other. So it was Brom who had taken it and turned it to the fire, and who was giving it to the table now in his slow voice, a stop at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:171:"And the sum. It is written in words, and then again, in a bracket. A person is taught to do that who has been warned that a figure may be altered and a word may not. I was so taught, at a ruled desk, when I was not a great deal older than he is." The mild eyes came up. "The place is given as a description. A day, and a rise, and a road that bends. Every carter in that meadow has a name for it, and our friend at the plank used the name at once. Whoever wrote this either did not know it or was not permitted to put it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:69:She said it shortly, as a thing that needed no more saying. He had heard her say it of a carter's hand and a child's chest, and it was what she said of every person whom she had put her hands in and meant to see again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:101:"My father kept a forge," he said. "In a shed, past the gardens. He was no smith. He liked the heat, and to have a thing that he could hit. I used to sleep out there when the house was loud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:241:"It's got a name," said Brom. "Quiet ground. The carter's wife said it, and the one-armed man, and that old keeper at the ford would have, I dare say, if we'd asked him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-58.md:230:"I'm going back to bed," she said. "You're going to bed too. And in the morning you'll go down to the carters' inn before Brom's up and tell him, because you tell him the same day, and it's after midnight, so it's already tomorrow, and if you don't tell him before breakfast he'll know from your face and be hurt that you didn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-58.md:240:He went down to the carters' inn at first light, through a cold so raw the cobbles sweated with it, and Brom was already up, sitting on the bench outside the stable door with his breath going up in the cold. Cael gave him the binder open at the new page. Brom read it from the front, every word, the notice and the four heads and the line underneath, and then went back to the notice and read that again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:"That's what I wanted you to see. I couldn't have told you." He walked down to her. "Out here a burst is a shout. I don't know who it is that listens, and I don't think Brom's deer were telling us, but every carter on that road believes somebody does. And yours has just shouted without asking you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:189:"No. You don't ask a woman whether her cloth runs. It's like asking a carter whether his horse kicks." Brom regarded the shirt sorrowfully. "She'd have been offended."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:It went out exactly as it had come to them on bark, beside a well, and not a word of it had been changed. Cael listened to *four injured* go down the meadow in that level voice, past the woman with her dough, and a man mending a trace, and a big fellow in a new coat, who looked at Brom's sling and then at his own boots. Under the ash an old carter with an unopened letter in his belt had lifted his chin at the first word, and he kept it lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:There was one line more, which read *the shingle pair*; and at the next boards two men in tarred aprons watched it go on at the foot of the sheet. One of them looked across at Brom, and lifted his cup an inch, without rancour, as a carter does who has been passed on a hill by a lighter load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:331:A carter came up the south fork with two mules while the last bucket stood on the curb. He stopped them at the edge of the halting-ground and stood watching Brom, plainly waiting to learn whether a large young man would drop where he stood. When a full minute had gone by he came and dipped his own cup, and then he scratched a wheel on the slip with the point of his knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:221:She shot from the rail first. Cael could see only the nose of the bow over the fence, and one goose feather; he heard her breath go out, and then nothing at all, for so long that Brom shifted his feet and one of the carters began to say something and was hushed. Then there was the slap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:163:It was only a plank on two kegs. A small brown mule stood beside it with her pack still on and her head down, and on the pack, lashed along its top, lay a long shape in an oiled cover. Astride one end of the plank sat a carter whom Cael had not seen before, a heavy man with a grey stubble, who held his right hand out in front of him, palm upward, as a person holds a hand out for coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:"Then it's wrong," he said. "I'd not stop you; I'd not know how, and it isn't mine to. But somebody ought to say it who's glad of what you carry, and I am. I was glad of it on a ridge and at a ford, and three nights back, for her arm." He jerked his chin at the sling. "It's wrong to do that to a person who climbed a mountain for five strangers on a carter's word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:43:Most of it they already knew, from Ansel and the carters, set down at more length by a man who had been paid by the page. The clerk had seen the start of the burst perhaps twice in two seasons, and thought the boy did not gather so much as stay gathered, the way a spring is wound before anybody touches it. The four rematches he had watched each read like a different fighter, with a different distance and rhythm and a different first exchange. His last page, for the house, said only that every good plan against the boy lasted an exchange or two, and that sooner or later every opponent ran out of plans before he ran out of answers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:193:There were both. They did it that afternoon in the cart-yard, the two of them, with the wheel off and laid across a pair of trestles. Brom cut the split timber out of the rim and shaped a new one to the curve with a drawknife, taking a shaving at a time and trying it and taking another, and the carter held what he was told to hold. It took them until the shadows were long. When the wheel was on again Brom spun it, and stood with his head bent, listening; it ran round four full turns and never ticked once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:227:The carter had said it as he might have said that the hay was in. *That's the season open.* He had meant the roads, and the weather on them, and a board in a meadow with slips enough on it for every crew that could walk; and he could not have known what the word had been to the person he said it to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:*The carter says the roads are dry and the season's open: work on every line of his map from now until the snow. I have been in somebody's season since my Kindling. The registry had one, and the circuit, and the house on the bluff, and the seat at Ostrand. I never chose the day any of them began, and each one ended with a ruling about me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:17:Below them Brom made a noise that might have been a laugh or might have been his knee, which had a bag of ice bound round it and plenty to say about stairs. He was carrying the Copper Crown dangling from two fingers, the way a man carries a hat he has taken off indoors, because Lira had both hands busy and somebody had to. Karis brought up the rear, loaded like a carter: two loaves; a stone jar the kitchen had pressed on her, since the kitchen had watched the final too; Bracken's copies; and a flat grey folder with Cael's name on the tab. She had collected that from the wing's counter on the way past, because the counter was closing and she disliked leaving paper where it might be mislaid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:"The second he had from a carter, who had it from his father. Men used to come up a side water off the Thornwater road with carts, for roofing stone. It lies ready split at the foot of a great slide, and need only be loaded. One year they did not come back down, and the next year nobody went up to learn why." He lifted his eyes to the wheel-ruts under their feet, with the turf grown over them. "I had not supposed, until this morning, that I should be standing in the second tale."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:139:It was not only the hold. A reading taken with that thing could be checked by nobody, and Cael had lived since his Kindling under figures that could not be checked by the person they concerned. What Karis did with a knotted cord, any carter could do after her, at the same place, and get the same number or call her a liar. That was the map the man was buying. The other would only have been a boy on a hillside saying that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:79:These people had come for a particular bout, and they came early to be sure of a place, and they talked to each other while they waited in the low, pleased voices of people who expect to see something. They were quarry people mostly, carters and masons and their wives, in heavy coats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:He did not turn to meet him. He did not need to. The seam had been mended since the slate country, and the door on his left side was the strongest in the house, and he stood there and let the young man arrive at it like a carter arriving at a toll-gate he had forgotten was there. The funnel took him in. Brom's left forearm came across and closed, and his right came through, and the flags went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:75:He had not seen her use it since Ardenmere. It was a thing she had found in the market yard behind the grain exchange, where the circuit fighters who could not afford a hall trained in the evenings between the stacked barrows. The lanes between the barrow lines were so narrow that you could not slip anything wide. You had to go under. When somebody came down the lane at you with his arm out, you ducked under the arm and turned in against him, so close that your shoulder went into his armpit, and you kept turning, the way a carter turns a laden barrow on a lane too narrow for it. His own weight, still going forward, went over his own front foot, and there was nothing he could do about it. She had used it on half the market yard in her first season. She had stopped using it when the fighters she faced got good enough to see it coming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:The sister looked up. The heavyset man lowered his carter's bill an inch, as he had for Brom, and looked at Lira over the top of it, and raised it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:51:The carter was a long young man with a narrow sleepy face and the largest feet that Cael had ever seen on anybody. He was the keeper's sister's son. He had two grey mules, and a cart a foot wider in the bed than the one that lay in the gorge. In the six days that followed he said perhaps forty words, and most of them were to the mules. The red oxen drew his wheels over for nothing, since the wheels were the hold's own. He walked through behind them with his breeches rolled to the thigh and his boots hung round his neck by their laces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:135:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:147:So they ate, and the shadow drew in. Nobody spoke of it, but Cael noticed that nobody strayed far either. The carter chewed, and looked from the rod to the big man and back, with the mild puzzlement of a person watching the customs of a foreign hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:173:The carter lifted one shoulder. "My father's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:213:He carried it the whole way over. He came back for the left-hand one, and then for the right; and when Seln stepped forward for the third, since three crossings of that moss was a long way under a box, Brom shook his head and took that too. The carter watched him go by each time with a hundredweight in his arms and a look of deep content on his plain face, and plainly could not make out what he was seeing. Carrying boxes was only what a big man was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:255:Brom had the carter's pot between his knees, since their own kettle was at the bottom of a gorge, and he was scouring it with a handful of grass, slowly, with his lips moving. Cael knew that look as well. He was getting two speeches ready, one for a ferryman and one for a smith, and he was putting the worse of them first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:"Me. Because I'd tell you, and he knew I'd tell you, and he knew I'd tell it straight." Brom picked up his spoon again. "That's his apology. He sent it by carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:7:They were alone under the ash. The nearest of the other fires was forty yards off, with two carters asleep at it, and the mule stood between with the boxes under her blanket. Brom had built their own fire up. Lira sat on the middle box with her foot set down whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:29:"The first described a rise and a bend, which any carter could have told a clerk across a table. This one is written by somebody who has stood on that ridge, or who had beside him the notes of a person that did. *The ground opens.* He knows what is at the foot of the hill, and he has chosen it." He laid the second sheet upon the first. "I have sat at the desks where such things are drawn up, though never for a matter of this weight. Before an office moves to take a thing out of somebody's keeping, it makes a list. It writes down what is to be taken, and who holds it, and what each of the holders is able to do if he should object. Then it looks for a place where he cannot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:65:Halfway up they stepped into a passing bay to let a wagon come down, four casks roped in its bed and the brake shoe screaming on the wheel. The carter lifted two fingers off the reins at them and went past. Brom lifted two fingers back. When Cael raised his eyebrows at him, Brom shrugged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:67:"Twenty-two years," said Brom, to the flames, in a fair imitation of a carter, and Lira laughed into her sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:105:They talked about nothing after that, which was the best part. Brom told them about the carter and the thing he had done for him, which turned out to be lifting the back of a loaded cart out of a ditch on the north road on his own while the carter held the horse. Lira told them about the sister's hen, which had laid an egg on the coal-box lid that morning in full view of the kitchen, to make a point. Cael told them about Dace's tick and the betting man's prices, eleven to four, and Brom laughed his short laugh through his nose, and Lira wanted to know why Cael had never told her, and he said he had only just finished counting. Through the floor they could hear the sister telling somebody about the smoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:105:He sat. Yorlan read the notice into the record: the matter and the parties, the challenge filed by the Compact's field service, the academy's counsel for the academy, the respondent for himself. He read the challenge itself in the same voice, every clause of it as level as a list of goods on a carter's bill. The sentences that had taken the whole of Cael's winter went past in the room no louder than the scrape of a bench. At the recording desk the young man's pen moved without stopping. Then Yorlan closed the thin book on his finger and looked, for the first time, at the room in front of him, not at any one person in it but at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:117:The bout was in the middle of the afternoon, on the side floor by the north wall, and the room was thin, as the Ironyard was thin at that hour even in the season. There were fifty or sixty on the benches, carters between loads, the newcomers from Keth's ring, a few of Orvet's people at the west post in a knot round Orvet himself. Orvet was already shouting, with a red neckcloth on and a grey beard that wagged when he shouted, and Cael had long since stopped hearing him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-55.md:17:At the top of the market steps Brom stopped. He had his lodging at the carters' inn, which was the other way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:"Then your father is like most fathers," said the instructor mildly. "Copper Rank Five is a good, honest rank. It works a forge. It builds a house." He moved the pointer along. "A quarter of practitioners reach Iron. Those are your journeymen, professional fighters and master tradespeople. One in ten reaches Bronze, and you'll meet a few Bronze instructors here. Silver, perhaps four in a hundred. Gold, fewer than one. Platinum—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:He sat. The front room of the inn was the kind of room a carter with money would think of as fine: a carpet worn through to the threads in a path from the door to the hearth, a clock on the mantel that ran a quarter of an hour fast and that everybody in the house allowed for, and a bow window full of the market row going about its morning, barrows and baskets and breath going up in the cold. Quenna had her back to the room and her face to the window, so that she could see the whole row without being seen from it, and Cael noticed that, and filed it, and then gave her the page. Lira had written Brom's questions out in her own fast slanting hand, every one, in the order he had asked them, and at the bottom, underlined, *DOES THE FORM ASK FOR HIS FAMILY*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:He did it quietly, a little at a time, in the course of ordinary work. When a file came across his desk for any reason he turned to the classification block before he did anything else. He ran his eye down the right-hand column, line by line, and looked for a line with nothing beside it. He did it with every file he touched for a month: files from the river side and the Ranked streets, carters and dyers and widows' pensions, and a Bronze practitioner's licence that had been on his desk for a week. He did it with three of the files of practitioners under ordinary watch, whose classification blocks were dense with markers, every one with its initials and its date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:115:He did not know what he was reading, and he did not like not knowing, and he found that he liked it very much at the same time. That evening, at the carters' inn by the north gate, eating his supper at the end of the long table where the drivers left him alone, he tried to put it into words for himself, as he had once put the boat shed into arithmetic. He could not. The nearest he came was that he had spent two years building a library of every shape a person could make on the floor above him, and the boy was not a shape in it. The boy was the floor creaking where nobody stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:37:"Nothing. He didn't wait for anything." Dace rubbed his thumb along the pencil. "I'll tell you the other thing, because you'll hear it in the market by noon and I'd sooner you heard it straight. I asked the carters this morning. The wool-wagon man has carried him before, to two other towns, and stood at the back with him in one of them. The talk on the road is that he was in a training yard at ten, his father's, and at an academy from eleven. And that he never fights a man the same way twice." He paused. "Not twice, the carter said. If you've seen him do a thing once, he's done with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:173:"Dace asked the carters. Carters see the road." Brom wiped his bowl with a heel of bread. "Washouts see the inside of halls, and then they come down the hill with a bag and never talk about it. I know. I came down a hill with a bag myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-37.md:163:They sat on the bench by the ring afterward, when the newcomers had gone up to the alcoves to pay Brom's post a copper each and be sent sideways. The sweepers had finished. The high windows had gone from grey to white, and the first carters were coming in at the side door to see what the afternoon card would be, stamping their feet against the cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-23.md:81:"They know already," he said. "The man was told Torvin's. Whatever he wanted from the clerk, he's had it, and he can't have it twice." He counted the rest on his fingers, because that was how he made a decision hold still long enough to look at. "If I stop using the hut now, the clerk notices before anybody, and the next person who asks him hears that the boy at Torvin's has started sending his letters some other way. That's a tell. It's the loudest kind. It says I know I've been found." Third finger. "And a carter going west can lose a letter in a ditch, and then Hesk gets nothing for a fortnight and sits at the kitchen table thinking the worst." He put his hand down. "So I keep posting at the hut. On purpose. Same day, same bag. And nothing goes on the outside but the address, which they've got already."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:155:The cookshop was full of dockers and carters at that hour, loud and warm, with the windows running wet and the long board crowded at one end, and the cookshop man gave them the far end without being asked because he had seen Ansel's face. They sat there, the three of them, with the stew in front of them and the steam going up. Ansel ate two spoonfuls and then sat with the spoon in his hand and did not eat any more. Brom, who never left food, ate nothing either, and after a while Cael understood that this was manners and put his own spoon down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:225:Many he did not know at all. There were carters in their long coats, smelling of horses. There were two men by the side door in plain dark coats too good for the district, who stood very straight and did not sit, and who had the look of people who would later tell somebody they had not been there. There was a woman with a Stone fighter's shoulders whom he had never seen, who must have come from another circuit altogether, because she was looking at everything in the building, the floor and the posts and the lamps, as Brom had once looked at the keeper's book.

