    57	It did not swing to, as a gate does in a wind. It was put to, hard, and a bar went down across it; and the sound came up the slot like a blow on a cask.
    58	
    59	Every one of them turned. Cael got his head round over the box's corner, and looked down past a girl and a small pale woman and a big man, to the bottom of twenty-two steps.
    60	
    61	There were two people inside the gate.
    62	
    63	They stood with their backs to it. They wore long coats of no colour, and hoods, and each had a cloth tied across the lower half of his face, so that there was nothing under the hood at all. Each had a stave. They were not porters' poles. They were as long as a man and as thick as a wrist, and shod.
    64	
    65	Nobody spoke. One of the two shifted his grip, and they began to come up the steps, side by side, without any haste.
    66	
    67	Cael's breath had stopped. He had ten things in him, and every one had come awake at that bar.
    68	
    69	He felt the stick turn.
    70	
    71	It was the smallest sound in the slot: a ferrule grating round on stone, a step and a half above his head. He was looking downhill, as they all were. He did not at once understand why the old man was not.
    72	
    73	Vastin had stood on eleven fields. He had given that figure beside a hold's hearth, and again on a bank, and Cael had taken it for a count of honours. It was a count of something else. A man does not hold a line for forty years at gates and hearings without learning what is done at the other end of a street to make him look away from his own; and when a thing was shown him at the bottom of a stair, with a great noise, it was to the top that his head went.
    74	
    75	"Above," he said. It was said quite low.
    76	
    77	Cael looked up.
    78	
    79	There were four at the head of the cut, against the strip of sky.
    80	
    81	One knelt on the highest landing, with a thing laid along his forearm that Cael knew before he could see it plainly. It was short, and it had a bow across its end. A second stood over him with a stave. A third was already coming down the flight below them, in the middle of the steps, as a man walks who knows that the ground is his. And at the very top, where the slot opened beside the parapet, the fourth stood by himself. He had one hand on a bar of iron that was let into the wall there for people coming up under loads. In the other he held a lantern, low, against his coat, with its shutter closed.
    82	
    83	They were hooded, all four, and there was a cloth across each face.
    84	
    85	It was a stair a stride and a half wide. It had no door in it, and no window, and nothing to stand behind. There were ten yards of it between a crossbow and an old man's chest.
    86	
    87	Cael saw the bow come up.
    88	
    89	The stick came down.
    90	
    91	It struck the edge of the ninth step, once, with the whole of a tall man's weight upon it. The sound was not the sound of ash on stone. He had heard it on a moor. A line went out from the ferrule to either wall, along the lip of the tread, as a crack runs across ice. Then the air stood up from it.
    92	
    93	There was no frost here to show where it was. For the space of a breath he saw it all the same: a bloom went over the dusk, from wall to wall and from the step to the sky, like a breath on a pane of glass. It was gone, and there was only a place in front of the old man's coat that the eye slid off.
    94	
    95	It roared. He could not have said how a thing roars that makes no noise. He stood two steps beneath it, with an Arbiter in his breast that had been dark since the day it woke, and it came over him all the same, as the heat comes out of a forge when the door is thrown back. It was going out of the man steadily, as it had gone out of him on a road, with nothing kept in hand.
    96	
    97	The bolt was already in the air.
    98	
    99	It stopped a yard in front of a brown coat. It stopped with a crack like a slate struck by a hammer, at the height of a man's breast, on nothing. It dropped to the landing and rolled against the wall.
   100	
   101	Vastin made a sound, shut behind his teeth. Cael saw the bound leg take it. Whatever struck that plane came down through the man who was its foot, and it went into the knee.
   102	
   103	His boots had not moved. They stood on the lip of the step, on the line, and they would stand there until he let the thing go, or until it left him. He could not lift a heel. He could not turn round. He had shut a stair with his own body, facing uphill, and whatever was done at his back was to be done without him.
   104	
   105	Cael had seen that wall go up once before, across a cart's width of road, and had seen what it was worth there. Eight hounds had gone round it. A bank had been enough.
   106	
   107	He looked at the two walls of the slot, blind and sheer, going up into the dusk.
   108	
   109	A wall on a road may be gone round. In that place there is no round.
   110	
   111	---
   112	
   113	It was Brom who met the two below.
   114	
   115	He had turned at the bar, with the rest, and he had not turned back. Cael did not see the beginning of it. He had it afterward from three people, and from the stair. He saw the end, over his shoulder, between one breath and the next.
   116	
   117	The big man let his box go. He did not set it down; he had no arm that would have set it. He dropped his right shoulder, and the thing slid off the webbing and fell across the fourth step with its long side to the gate, and lay there. It shut one half of the slot. He stood in the other.
   118	
   119	He had nothing in his hands. He had not been able to lift the left above the elbow for four days, and the right was in another woman's knot.
   120	
   121	"That's far enough," he said to the two with staves, exactly as he would have said it to a carter.
   122	
   123	They came on. Neither had said a word. The nearer put the shod end of his stave at Brom's chest, like a man shoving a boat from a bank.
   124	
   125	Brom stepped down.
   126	
   127	It was not what a person does who is being pushed. He went a step toward them, onto the third tread, and trod on the back of it with his whole weight; and the stone that had rocked under him for six evenings tipped up at its front edge like the lid of a box.
   128	
   129	He was off it, and had it before it could fall back. He could not raise his arms. He could bend, and he bent, and got both hands under a slab of the dark stone as long as his own forearm and a hand thick; and he took it up as far as his thighs, on his legs and his back, as he had taken a dray.
   130	
   131	He had said on a moor that a rock only wants lifting and letting go.
   132	
   133	He let it go. He did not throw it. He opened his hands, with a heave of the hips behind them, and it went down the last two steps edge foremost, turning over, at the height of a shin.
   134	
   135	The first of the two was a quick man. He got one leg clear, and the slab took the other across the boot and the foot of his stave together, and he went back into the gate with a noise of breath and timber and sat down against it.
   136	
   137	Brom had not straightened. Something had gone in his left shoulder as the stone left him, deeper than what the dray had done, and he was bent over it with his teeth showing.
   138	
   139	The second came past his fellow in one stride. His stave was up in both hands, above his hood, and it was coming down on a bowed head.
   140	
   141	Lira went off the landing.
   142	
   143	She had been on the second flight, two steps under Cael's heels, with a box on her back and a small pale woman between her and the fight. She had got out of her beckets. Cael never learned how; the box was found afterward standing square on the seventh step, as if she had set it there for a seat. She went down past Karis, who had pressed herself to the wall, and took the two strides that the landing gave her; and she did not go on down the flight. She stepped off its head into the air.
   144	
   145	It was not far, as she reckoned falls: the height of a table, and the length of a man outward. She came down over Brom's box, with her knees drawn up and the long knife in her right hand, and her left arm was folded against her ribs as tight as a wing.
   146	
   147	She did not strike at the man. She struck at what he held. The knife went into the ash between his two fists, point first, with the whole drop behind it, and stood there to a third of its length.
   148	
   149	Her boots hit the step in the same instant. Her knees went, and took it. She hung from the hilt with all that she weighed.
   150	
   151	A stave cannot be swung with a girl upon it. It came down between them, slowly, like a gate-bar, and its shod end struck the tread and stayed. The man had a hand free. He let go with it, and reached for her hood.
   152	
   153	Brom's boot met him at the hip.
   154	
   155	It was a carter's shove, with a leg like a newel post, from a man who had got his breath; and the hooded one went backward down three steps and into the other, and the pair of them into the gate.
   156	
   157	Lira had gone over to the left with the stave. The wall was there. She took it on the point of her shoulder, and Cael heard that. She slid down it, and sat, with a man's stave across her knees and her own knife standing up out of the middle of it.
   158	
   159	"It didn't touch," she said, to nobody, through her teeth. She was holding the left arm against her with the right. "It didn't touch a thing."
   160	
   161	There was a small sound higher up.
   162	
   163	Karis had flattened herself to let the girl go by. She had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them up and down that stair for seven days, and she had not a hand to spare for the wall. Her boot was on the sixth step. It was the wet one, worn into a dish.
   164	
   165	It went from under her. She sat down hard on the stair, and the flat did not leave her arm. It was her left hand that went out, flat, to save the rest of her, and found the lip of the step above.
   166	
   167	That stone had not been worn round. It had been cut to an edge by whoever laid it, before there were walls on that hill, and seven days of porters going by on the other side had done nothing to blunt it.
   168	
   169	She did not cry out. She looked at her palm, and then she shut it, and put it inside her coat.
   170	
   171	"The flat is dry," she said, in a voice that was not quite steady. "I should like that known."
   172	
   173	The two at the gate had got up.
   174	
   175	One had no stave now. He looked up the stair, past Brom, and it was not at any person that he looked. He looked at a box lying across a step. He went to it, low, with one arm up against a boot that did not come, and got his fist through its becket, and hauled.
   176	
   177	Brom sat down on it.
   178	
   179	He could do nothing else, and so he did that. He lowered himself onto the lid, with his two arms hanging and his heels dug in against the tread. The man below pulled at a rope with a great deal of a Copper from Velmere seated on the other end of it.
   180	
   181	---
   182	
   183	Cael had turned back uphill before the slab was out of Brom's hands.
   184	
   185	It was the hardest thing that he had done on that stair. Everybody that he had was behind him: a girl going through the air, and a big man with no arms, and a small one with a courier's flat. He heard the stone go down, and the gate take a man's back. He heard a knife go into wood.
   186	
   187	He had the whole ten, and they had come up in him like hounds to a gate.
   188	
   189	Wind was first, as it had always been, the dry hiss in his feet. *Away?* it said; and there was nowhere on that stair that was away. Under it the millstone went round, and told him the weight of a slab that he could not see, and which way it was falling. Ember ticked in his right hand, and showed him a cord, ten yards off, drawn back along a stock; a single point would have gone through that cord as through a thread. Pressure had drawn itself as tight as a held breath. A slot between two walls was the very thing that it was made for; it would have gone up that stair like a cork up the neck of a bottle. *There?* asked the thin held note, of the place by the parapet where the slot began; and a point set there would have shut the head of the cut like a door.
   190	
   191	It was dusk. Shadow had lifted while he was on the first flight, and it stood at his knees now and asked harder than all the rest together. He was in a place with no window, in failing light, with four people looking down at him. He could have been gone from every one of those eyes between two breaths.
   192	
   193	He told each of them no. He did it one at a time, as a man goes along a row of dogs.
   194	
   195	It was not the clerk that held him, though a clerk was at the end of every one of them, and behind the clerk an hour, and four names. It was a lantern.
   196	
   197	He had been looking at it since he raised his eyes. A man stood at the top of that stair, with one hand on a rail, and a lantern held shut against his coat. He was not fighting. He had no stave. He was doing nothing whatever, and he was doing it with great attention, with his hood turned straight down the slot.
   198	
   199	Nobody brings a light to a beating and keeps it dark. That man had been put there to show it when a certain thing happened. It had not happened yet. He was waiting for it, and so were the other three; the one with the bow had not laid a second bolt on his string.
   200	
   201	Cael had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for. He knew the look of it through a hood.
   202	
   203	They had been told. Somebody had stood these six in a place with no way out of it, and had given them a bow to frighten with and staves to hurt with; and all that they wanted in return was to see what a boy with no tier did when he was hurt enough.
   204	
   205	Then they should see him do nothing, from as near as he could bring it. A wall wanted a reason behind it; he would be the reason, and stand.
   206	
   207	An old man had said a thing at a board, on a moor, with his bowl set down upon a sill, about a wall and what it wants behind it. Cael had been that once, by going ten strides off and landing like a post.
   208	
   209	He went one step up.
   210	
   211	It put him on the eighth tread, at a brown coat's left hip, in the open half of the slot, where there was nothing between the bow and himself but a place that the eye slid off. He stood square. He lifted his face, so that the last of the light was on it, and looked up the stair at four hoods.
   212	
   213	The bow came round to him at once.
   214	
   215	He had known that it would. He watched the man lay the bolt, and draw, and he did not shift his feet. He had put his skin behind this wall once already, on the word of having seen it hold. He had seen it hold a good deal since.
   216	
   217	The bolt broke on the air a hand's breadth from his eyes.
   218	
   219	He did not blink. It was not courage. He had only nowhere else to look. He heard the breath go out of the man beside him, and he saw the laths move under the linen.
   220	
   221	"You need not have come up," said Vastin, without turning his head. His voice was thin, and quite steady. "It will hold as well for one as for two."
   222	
   223	"It's not for the wall that I came."
   224	
   225	"No," said the old man, after a moment. "I see what it is for. I had not thought to have it done for me twice."
   226	
   227	The third hood had reached the landing.
   228	
   229	He came across it without hurrying, and stopped where the air had bloomed. He put up a gloved hand. He laid the palm of it flat on nothing, a yard from Cael's face, as a man lays a hand on a window to learn whether there is glass in it.
   230	
   231	He pushed. He set his shoulder to it, once, with his weight behind, and Cael heard the knee take that as well.
   232	
   233	Then he stood back by half a pace, with his palm still upon the plane, and looked through it.
   234	
   235	Cael looked back. There was a cloth, and the shadow of a hood, and inside the shadow no face that he could have sworn to.
   236	
   237	After that nothing moved on the upper stair at all.
   238	
   239	It was the quietest minute that he had ever stood through. There was noise below him, of stone and timber and people's breath, and he could do nothing about any of it. In front of him four people had gone as still as he had. They were waiting for a boy to be whatever they had been told that he was; and the boy stood on a step, with a box on his back, and gave them a porter.
   240	
   241	He had never been so near that wall for so long. On the moor he had stood by it for a count of twenty, and gone.
   242	
   243	He felt what it was, from two feet beneath it, with nothing to do but feel. Every one of his ten went somewhere. The hiss ran, and the millstone turned, and the point ticked, and the bellows drew; even the thing that read went along with whatever it read. This did not go. It had been told to stay, and it stayed, with an old man's whole strength poured down into it like water into sand. It was a heel set upon a line, and meant.
   244	
   245	Everything that he owned was a way of going somewhere, or of getting out of the way. He had not known that a person could be given a thing which only stayed.
   246	
   247	Something in him set.
   248	
   249	It was not as the others had come. Tide had filled him like a cup, under a healer's hand. This did not fill. It was as a post is stood in its hole: not driven, but put there, and the earth trodden down about it a heel at a time, until a person could have hung a gate on it.
   250	
   251	He had not reached for it. He had been telling ten things no, and it had not been there to tell.
   252	
   253	*This far?* it said.
   254	
   255	It asked of the step in front of his boots, in a voice like a bar going down into two sockets. He knew what it was before he knew that it was new.
   256	
   257	*No*, he told it; and that was the eleventh time, in that stair.
   258	
   259	---
   260	
   261	The hand came away from the plane.
   262	
   263	The hooded man had taken it off as a person takes his hand from a door that he has decided not to open. He turned his head, up the stair, and lifted two fingers.
   264	
   265	At the top, the one by the rail moved for the first time.
   266	
   267	He raised the lantern. He brought it up from his coat to the height of the parapet, where the whole town lay below to see it, and his thumb went to the shutter. A wall would serve, it seemed, if the other thing was not to be had. A wall was a Path opened in a district. He did not doubt that somebody down there in the dusk had been told to watch for a light, and had a pen.
   268	
   269	A line came out of the sky.
   270	
   271	Cael saw it only because he was looking at the lantern. It was no thicker than a bootlace. It came down from the roof's edge, above the slot's mouth, with something small and heavy at its end that swung once, round an arm and a bar of iron together, three times, quicker than he could follow, and bit.
   272	
   273	The lantern jerked sideways. It struck the rail, and fell on the top step with its shutter still closed, and rolled, and went out inside itself with a small flat cough.
   274	
   275	The man wrenched at his arm. It was bound to the iron from the wrist to the elbow, and it did not come.
   276	
   277	Cael looked along the roof. There was a parapet, and a strip of violet sky, and for the space of half a breath a shape upon it no bigger than a chimney-pot; and then there was only the parapet. Seln had said once that if they saw him, he had done it badly. He had been seen by four people in hoods, and Cael did not think that it was badly done.
   278	
   279	The one on the landing looked up at the roof for a good deal longer than Cael had.
   280	
   281	Then he whistled. It was two low notes, such as a carter gives a horse.
   282	
   283	It ended as quietly as it had begun. The man with the bow rose from his knee, and unstrung the thing, and put it under his coat. The one beside him went up to the rail, and drew a knife, and cut a thin cord in two places; and the fourth took up his dead lantern. None of them ran. They went into the lane, one after another, like people who have come to the wrong door, and the hood on the landing went last. He walked up the two flights backward, with his face to the plane, and at the top he stood for a moment and looked down it.
   284	
   285	Then the head of the cut was empty. Nobody had spoken. Nobody had run.
   286	
   287	Below, a bar was lifted. Cael heard the gate swing, and two pairs of boots on the paving of a yard, going away at a walk.
   288	
   289	He counted. He had been counting since the stick came down, as he counted anything, and he was at eighty-one. It had been a minute and some.
   290	
   291	"You can let it go," he said.
   292	
   293	"You are certain."
   294	
   295	"I counted. Four went up, and two went out by the gate. That's all of them."
   296	
   297	The old man shut his eyes. His hands opened on the stick.
   298	
   299	There was no frost to fall. The place in front of him that the eye had slid off was only a stair again, with two bolts lying on it; and the roar went out of the slot as a fire goes out when the door is shut on it.
   300	
   301	He stood one moment longer on the lip of the step. Then the knee went.
   302	
   303	It did not bend. It let go, sideways, all at once, as a prop is knocked from under a beam. The whole long weight of him came over backward, with the case and the stick, onto the step where Cael stood.
   304	
   305	*Both heels*, said Compression. It had come up into them already, of its own accord, and his frame creaked like hemp.
   306	
   307	He told it no. He took him as a porter would have. He got both arms round a brown coat, under the shoulders, and the box on his own back dragged him the way that they were both going. His left heel lost the tread, and found the next. They went down two steps so, the pair of them, and a tarred case came after and struck him on the ear.
   308	
   309	Something stopped them. It was as hard as a gatepost, and it had four yards of webbing round it.
