editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:43:They went into it in the order that the stair had taught them. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, with the stick going up a tread ahead of him and the bound leg swung after; and Cael came two steps below, where a man would fall. Lira followed, with her arms through her beckets. Karis was behind her, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and Brom came last, and his box all but brushed both walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:109:A wall on a road may be gone round. In that place there is no round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:129:He was off it, and had it before it could fall back. He could not raise his arms. He could bend, and he bent, and got both hands under a slab of the dark stone as long as his own forearm and a hand thick; and he took it up as far as his thighs, on his legs and his back, as he had taken a dray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:299:There was no frost to fall. The place in front of him that the eye had slid off was only a stair again, with two bolts lying on it; and the roar went out of the slot as a fire goes out when the door is shut on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:A careful man writes a sum twice, once in figures and once in words, so that the one may be checked against the other, and a clerk at any counter did it still. If this cutter had done it, then those little groups were the names of his numbers; and if they were, each sign in them was not a thing that bore or hung. It was a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:"I do not say that they are yours. I say that a man may be followed without being told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:305:"She told you not to fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:307:"She told me not to fall *on* it. I've not. I've done that eleven times since the first day, down the dark end, when nobody had the light." She was breathing a little quickly, and her chin was up. "I can't use it to catch me. So I'm learning to come down without it. If I'm ever to go off something in a hurry, I'd sooner not find out then where it goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:39:A sheet had been laid before him once, at a spring, with its head turned away; and the person who laid it had gone to a basin and stood with his back turned, so that it need never be asked what was made of it. Cael had understood only afterward what that had cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:He had stood over a spring six days since, with a stolen sheet in his sleeve, and had gone through the three things that his training let him do with a fact. He had not been able to look at the case that morning. He had said to himself that a man cannot hold two closed things up to the light at once; and it had been true, and it had also been convenient.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:He had passed it three days ago, under a keg. He had not needed to turn his head. He knew how it would be: a slot of light, and a night clerk behind it, half asleep, with a book. The courier lays his packet on the ledge. The clerk enters it by its seal and its weight, and tears off a slip. Neither is permitted to ask the other a question. It is the oldest rule in the service, and the only one that he had ever wholly respected. A registry enters what is laid on its ledge. It may bury the thing afterward, or burn the copy, or hold it for six years in a drawer where nobody will receive it; but for the length of one line, in one book, on one night, the thing has been set down as having come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:105:There was a fourth thing which he had not thought of at the spring, since it had not then existed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:173:"He told you at a spring. You look at what's there. You leave your hands where they are." Cael felt for the next tread with his heel. "That's all I've ever had of it. It's nine steps to the next landing. I'm below you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:"I'm going up for an hour. Set it on the table's end. Touch nothing." He went by them toward the stair with his eyes already half shut. "There's a jar of water on the shelf. You may have what's in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:287:The cut was harder going up. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, and Cael went two steps below him, where a man would fall. On the bottom flight a tread rocked under Brom's weight, and he stopped, and trod on it again to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:1:# Chapter 32 — No Round
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:3:The fourteenth letter came out of the Hall on the two hundred and ninetieth day, an hour before the bell, and Karis did not say so until she had found it twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:Cael had the lamp. He had put it where the reed was going, three times that afternoon, before the rough voice had finished the word; and the third time the man had not troubled to begin it. He only moved, and the light was there. Neither of them remarked on it. A lamp is not thanked for burning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:The old man had stood by the nearest bay since the morning, with both hands on his stick. He had not gone toward the ring once. Cael had watched for it, at the trimming and at the noon hour and when the light was wanted high; and each time the grey eyes had gone to the thing, and rested, and come away. He thought that it was a good deal harder than the four had been. It is one matter to stop a foot that has started. It is another to stand in a room with one office in it for six hours, and look at the office, and leave it where it is.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:"Fourteen," said Karis. She said it to her notebook. "It closes the number after five. I had the head of that word two days since, and I could not have the tail."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:"Of twenty-three," said the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:13:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:15:"Three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:17:"I can count as far as three," said Karis. "I have the figures for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:19:He made a sound in his chest that was nearly a laugh; it was the first that Cael had heard out of him, and it seemed to surprise him as much as anybody. He wrote the card for the morning with his nose an inch from the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:21:The clerk's bell went, small and flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:They roped the boxes. Brom took his on the right shoulder, as he had for four days, and Lira got her arms through her beckets, the right and then the left. Cael felt the third box settle between his shoulder-blades, with a purse in it under five thumbs. The old man hooked the tarred case with the head of his stick, and brought it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:25:At the iron the man in the dusty coat drew the bar and stood back. His red eyes were on their boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:"Second bell," he said, as he said every evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:29:The clerk walked behind them through the vault with his lantern, and turned his key in the low door, and Cael did not look round at him. He had not needed to for five days. He was a quarter of *the same*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:31:The yard was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:33:He had never seen it so. They had used the whole of the clerk's quarter-hour that evening, since a small pale woman would not leave a tally-book lying open; and the wagons were gone from the dock, and the pan had been hooked up on the weigh-beam, and the carts' gate at the bottom was shut and barred. The light had begun to go. It lay grey on the long worn trough of the paving, and there was not a soul on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:35:"It's quiet," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:37:"It's late," said Lira. "They'll count us out at that arch with a face on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:39:Cael looked up, at the roof of the last building, where a parapet ran along above the slot in the wall. There was nothing on it. There had been nothing on it for two days, morning and evening, and he had learned to read that as a small grey man doing his work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:41:The gate at the cut's foot stood open, as it always stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:43:They went into it in the order that the stair had taught them. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, with the stick going up a tread ahead of him and the bound leg swung after; and Cael came two steps below, where a man would fall. Lira followed, with her arms through her beckets. Karis was behind her, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and Brom came last, and his box all but brushed both walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:45:The slot took the light from them at once. The walls went up three times the height of a man on either hand, blind and dark and damp, and the sky was a strip of dull violet a long way over their heads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:47:On the third tread of the bottom flight a stone rocked under Brom. He had told it for six evenings what it wanted. He only looked at it tonight, and came on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:49:"Five," said Lira, at the first landing. She counted them every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:51:The second flight was the short one, of four; and it had the wet tread in it, the one worn into a dish, where a stick had slipped on the first morning. Cael saw the old man find it with the ferrule, and go round. He saw the sound leg bend and take the whole of him up onto the ninth step, where the next landing began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:53:Behind them all, at the foot, the gate shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:57:It did not swing to, as a gate does in a wind. It was put to, hard, and a bar went down across it; and the sound came up the slot like a blow on a cask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:59:Every one of them turned. Cael got his head round over the box's corner, and looked down past a girl and a small pale woman and a big man, to the bottom of twenty-two steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:61:There were two people inside the gate.

