/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n -e 'toes|foot|shoulder|arm|knee|twenty-five|nineteen|five days|healed|well' editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-{27,28,29,30,31,32,33}.md | head -160" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:He sat on the seventh step with his back against the wall and the bound leg thrust out across the slot. He had not fainted. His eyes were open, but they were looking at a strip of sky, and the hands that lay in his lap had fallen open like a pair of gloves. The stick had gone down two flights by itself. Lira fetched it, and laid it across his knees, and he did not take hold of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:7:Cael had seen that man at the floor once, on a moor, where a healer had said so with two fingers on his wrist and had likened it to a well. He had bent over his stick then, and stood. He could not have stood now if the stair had been on fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:11:"Shoulder," said Lira. "I hit the wall with it. It wasn't the arm, and I want that written; I never put the arm to anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:"The left," said Brom. "It's worse than the cart left it. And I've had the right round the pair of you, so that's said something to me as well." He lowered himself onto his box. "I'll be hearing the rest of it from two healers at once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:15:Karis did not answer. She was still on the sixth step, where she had sat down, with the flat across her knees and her left hand inside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:43:He put it into his sleeve. Then he went down on his heels, which Cael had seldom seen him do for any person, and got his shoulder under a brown coat's left arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:49:At the top Seln slid out from under the arm, and Brom came into his place with the wrong shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:53:It was full dusk in the lane. The four alleys had no white in them. Each had, at its far end, the paleness that did not flicker, and it lay out along the cobbles in a bar. Cael went through all four with an old man's arm across his neck and a box dragging at his back, and he did not turn his head. It was not hard, tonight. He had too much on his shoulders to look at anything but the next sett; and a girl went by on his left each time, under two loads, with a tarred case knocking at her hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:55:"You're past the bell," said the old keeper at the round arch. He had his board on his knee, and a lantern beside the stool, and the point of his pen went along them. "One, and four. Your lame one is a good deal lamer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:65:"It's two streets from the blue gate. We've gone by it a dozen times. *Second bell: blue.* She's Iron, the third rank, and she's got it on paper." She had Karis's wrist in her right hand, and was holding the whole arm up above the pallet, as a healer had once told her to hold her own. "They'd have that shut in a quarter of an hour, with a Path. It'd not even leave a mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:67:"They would," said Seln. He was on the ladder's head, where he had been when they came in under the arch. "It would be the best-mended hand in the district. And before any healer in that house laid a finger on it, a clerk would write a line on a sheet at a bed's foot: her name, and where the name is registered, and the tier; and the sheet goes to the counter at the first bell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:99:It was the last thing that anybody said to make another smile. He held the needle in the lantern's flame, turning it, until the steel had gone dull, as he had seen a smith do for a horse; and then he sat down on the boards, since he could not lift either arm to a table's height, and laid her hand palm upward upon his own knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:109:He was slow. He had hands like shovels, and no arm behind either of them, and he worked from the wrists. He drew each stitch down flat and looked at it before he made the next. Karis did not watch. She had turned her face to the slates, and her lips were moving; and it was a little while before Cael understood what she was saying over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:131:Brom had brought the pot up, late, with seven on the board, and had set the seventh at the end by itself. The old man had been got up against the gable's foot, with a rolled coat behind him; and when he put out his right hand for the thing, the fingers would not shut upon its rim. The left, which had held a ferry's strap and a mule's head, lay open on the straw and did not stir.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:135:"You're about to be fed like him," said Lira, with a jerk of her chin at Cael. "He couldn't lift a spoon for a day, after a stair. I did that as well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:139:Brom had the linen off the knee. One of the two laths had cracked along its length. He took a slat from the lid of his own box, and split it with Lira's knife, and bound the joint again as he had seen a healer do it, turn upon turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:149:It had been waiting for him since the stair. He had felt it there through all that came after, through a lane and two gates and seven stitches, standing in the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, as each of the ten had stood, as patient as a clerk. He had not looked. He had had an old man's arm across his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:167:Its field was a new one as well. He had nine that had come out of a fight, and their fields said so. He had one that a healer had given him, lying down, and that said *clinical*. Here was a thing that had come out of a fight in which the person who owned it had struck nobody. A man had put his heels on a line and been hit, and the lines had found a name for that, and it was two words long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:191:"The first is the knee. I was forbidden a wall on it, by the one person on this side of the Line who had the right to forbid me, and I stood one. I did not forget her; I had her in my mind as the stick came down, word for word." He shut his eyes. "I do not ask that it be excused. I ask only that she be told that I heard her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:197:"The second is what they came for. I have stood at the foot of eleven fields, and I know when a crowd wants a door and when it wants a man, and those six wanted neither. They had a light, and they kept it dark, and they looked past me. I stood uncovered, a yard from one of them, with a face that forty years of clerks have been obliged to know; and he did not once look at it." Something moved at the corner of the old mouth. "I find that I was a little affronted. They were looking at you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:201:"I do not know what they hoped to see, and I do not ask. I told you on a bank that I had measured a person and could not read him, and it is as true tonight. I sat four feet from you for three days, with a plate, and I have walked a shoulder off you since a ferry. I know that six people shut a stair to watch you do something, and that you went up a step into a crossbow's eye and did nothing whatever." He drew what breath he had. "I know no more of what you are than the brass did. That is the third head, and it is the one that I wished to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:211:He had said it so on a bank, with a hand on a boy's arm; he had no hand to put there tonight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:Lira was not asleep. She had turned on her right side, with the slow arm uppermost, and her eyes were open in the lantern's last light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:285:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:The old man had stood by the nearest bay since the morning, with both hands on his stick. He had not gone toward the ring once. Cael had watched for it, at the trimming and at the noon hour and when the light was wanted high; and each time the grey eyes had gone to the thing, and rested, and come away. He thought that it was a good deal harder than the four had been. It is one matter to stop a foot that has started. It is another to stand in a room with one office in it for six hours, and look at the office, and leave it where it is.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:They roped the boxes. Brom took his on the right shoulder, as he had for four days, and Lira got her arms through her beckets, the right and then the left. Cael felt the third box settle between his shoulder-blades, with a purse in it under five thumbs. The old man hooked the tarred case with the head of his stick, and brought it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:29:The clerk walked behind them through the vault with his lantern, and turned his key in the low door, and Cael did not look round at him. He had not needed to for five days. He was a quarter of *the same*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:41:The gate at the cut's foot stood open, as it always stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:43:They went into it in the order that the stair had taught them. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, with the stick going up a tread ahead of him and the bound leg swung after; and Cael came two steps below, where a man would fall. Lira followed, with her arms through her beckets. Karis was behind her, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and Brom came last, and his box all but brushed both walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:53:Behind them all, at the foot, the gate shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:One knelt on the highest landing, with a thing laid along his forearm that Cael knew before he could see it plainly. It was short, and it had a bow across its end. A second stood over him with a stave. A third was already coming down the flight below them, in the middle of the steps, as a man walks who knows that the ground is his. And at the very top, where the slot opened beside the parapet, the fourth stood by himself. He had one hand on a bar of iron that was let into the wall there for people coming up under loads. In the other he held a lantern, low, against his coat, with its shutter closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:101:Vastin made a sound, shut behind his teeth. Cael saw the bound leg take it. Whatever struck that plane came down through the man who was its foot, and it went into the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:115:He had turned at the bar, with the rest, and he had not turned back. Cael did not see the beginning of it. He had it afterward from three people, and from the stair. He saw the end, over his shoulder, between one breath and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:117:The big man let his box go. He did not set it down; he had no arm that would have set it. He dropped his right shoulder, and the thing slid off the webbing and fell across the fourth step with its long side to the gate, and lay there. It shut one half of the slot. He stood in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:129:He was off it, and had it before it could fall back. He could not raise his arms. He could bend, and he bent, and got both hands under a slab of the dark stone as long as his own forearm and a hand thick; and he took it up as far as his thighs, on his legs and his back, as he had taken a dray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:135:The first of the two was a quick man. He got one leg clear, and the slab took the other across the boot and the foot of his stave together, and he went back into the gate with a noise of breath and timber and sat down against it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:137:Brom had not straightened. Something had gone in his left shoulder as the stone left him, deeper than what the dray had done, and he was bent over it with his teeth showing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:145:It was not far, as she reckoned falls: the height of a table, and the length of a man outward. She came down over Brom's box, with her knees drawn up and the long knife in her right hand, and her left arm was folded against her ribs as tight as a wing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:149:Her boots hit the step in the same instant. Her knees went, and took it. She hung from the hilt with all that she weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:157:Lira had gone over to the left with the stave. The wall was there. She took it on the point of her shoulder, and Cael heard that. She slid down it, and sat, with a man's stave across her knees and her own knife standing up out of the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:159:"It didn't touch," she said, to nobody, through her teeth. She was holding the left arm against her with the right. "It didn't touch a thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:163:Karis had flattened herself to let the girl go by. She had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them up and down that stair for seven days, and she had not a hand to spare for the wall. Her boot was on the sixth step. It was the wet one, worn into a dish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:165:It went from under her. She sat down hard on the stair, and the flat did not leave her arm. It was her left hand that went out, flat, to save the rest of her, and found the lip of the step above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:175:One had no stave now. He looked up the stair, past Brom, and it was not at any person that he looked. He looked at a box lying across a step. He went to it, low, with one arm up against a boot that did not come, and got his fist through its becket, and hauled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:He could do nothing else, and so he did that. He lowered himself onto the lid, with his two arms hanging and his heels dug in against the tread. The man below pulled at a rope with a great deal of a Copper from Velmere seated on the other end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:185:It was the hardest thing that he had done on that stair. Everybody that he had was behind him: a girl going through the air, and a big man with no arms, and a small one with a courier's flat. He heard the stone go down, and the gate take a man's back. He heard a knife go into wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:191:It was dusk. Shadow had lifted while he was on the first flight, and it stood at his knees now and asked harder than all the rest together. He was in a place with no window, in failing light, with four people looking down at him. He could have been gone from every one of those eyes between two breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:221:"You need not have come up," said Vastin, without turning his head. His voice was thin, and quite steady. "It will hold as well for one as for two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:231:He pushed. He set his shoulder to it, once, with his weight behind, and Cael heard the knee take that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:271:Cael saw it only because he was looking at the lantern. It was no thicker than a bootlace. It came down from the roof's edge, above the slot's mouth, with something small and heavy at its end that swung once, round an arm and a bar of iron together, three times, quicker than he could follow, and bit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:275:The man wrenched at his arm. It was bound to the iron from the wrist to the elbow, and it did not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:283:It ended as quietly as it had begun. The man with the bow rose from his knee, and unstrung the thing, and put it under his coat. The one beside him went up to the rail, and drew a knife, and cut a thin cord in two places; and the fourth took up his dead lantern. None of them ran. They went into the lane, one after another, like people who have come to the wrong door, and the hood on the landing went last. He walked up the two flights backward, with his face to the plane, and at the top he stood for a moment and looked down it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:He stood one moment longer on the lip of the step. Then the knee went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:He told it no. He took him as a porter would have. He got both arms round a brown coat, under the shoulders, and the box on his own back dragged him the way that they were both going. His left heel lost the tread, and found the next. They went down two steps so, the pair of them, and a tarred case came after and struck him on the ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:311:"I've got you," said Brom, from below, between his teeth. He had his right arm across Cael's back. It was the one that a healer had said was to carry nothing. "Stand still, the pair of you. I've only the one, and it's the wrong one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:3:Karis sat up that night against the boxes, with the lantern turned low between her boots and her notebook open on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He had believed her, and that was what she could not get used to. She had walked into a cook-house with fifty-two sheets and a lifetime of being smiled at by better-placed people, and a stranger had cleared three tables and given her his cushion and told her that he knew the hand. She had not understood until now how light a pencil is in the fingers of a person whom nobody attends to. She might draw a line wrong on one evening and rub it out on the next, and no harm came of it to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:Under the three lines she wrote the ways in which it could fail. There were four. She found a fifth, and wrote that as well; and somewhere behind the fifth, with the lantern guttering and Brom breathing like a felled tree, she understood that she had not been so nearly content since a store behind a chimney.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:61:She went up it like a cat going up a curtain. Karis stood at the foot with her neck bent back, and saw the great boot find each rung, and the left hand come to the rail a beat behind the right. It did come; it closed, and held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:67:It was as broad as a paving stone. It came down the ladder against a thin chest, a rung at a time, and it was put into Karis's arms with a cloud of that pale dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:75:She did not look toward the plank at all; she kept her shoulder to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:She had not meant to say it so. The boy came, without a word, and went down on one knee a hand to the left of the plank; and she saw the corner of his mouth, and was too frightened to be ashamed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:85:She laid the leaf on the rock at the board's foot. She put the point of her pencil in the air above the first group, the six signs that followed two rings and two pecks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:135:It was slower than anything that she had ever done, and she did it through the whole of that afternoon, with the lamp moving beside her and the man's reed going over her shoulder from time to time, to tell her where a run began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:157:It was on the Hall's eighth sheet, near the foot. It took her until the reed had gone twice more round the long table to find it: eight signs, with the two strangers standing second and fourth, and everything else in it hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:195:Seln was at the ladder's foot that evening before they had the boxes off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:201:Brom set his box down very slowly, with his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:209:Nobody spoke; Karis had her notebook shut against her chest in both arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:He went over what he had, and it took no time at all, since it was so little. He had ten things in him, and not one of them could be opened on that hill; a declaration under those walls drew a clerk, and a clerk was the end of four people. He had a lamp to hold. He had a girl with a slow arm, and a man with two bad shoulders, and another with one leg; and a researcher who had this afternoon learned the tenth letter of a tongue that nobody had read in the life of the town.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:247:"I have five days of his week," she said. "I should like to be alive for them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:269:He had his right boot off before he went up the hill; he sat on the mounting block, and unwound a strip of linen from two toes, and bent them one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:273:"You've two shoulders owing," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:275:"That's shoulders. I'm speaking of a foot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:281:She handed them down this time, since they were the size of hearthstones, and there was nobody at the ladder's foot who could take them. Brom could not lift either arm above the elbow, and Cael had the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:287:"I can take them against my chest, with the one arm," said Vastin, "and I can stand under them. I cannot undertake to carry them to a table. I should require the other hand for that, and it is spoken for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:293:He stood at the foot, with the stick's head in his right hand and his left arm crooked, and she put the books into it one upon another, as wood is stacked. He held them as he would have held a seal on a cushion, with his chin lifted and his eyes on nothing; and when the third was laid on, and the dust of it went into his face, he did not sneeze. Cael thought that he had seldom seen a person so pleased to be a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:299:He had the lamp, and could do nothing but watch. She dropped the height of a man, and her knees went, and she took the whole of it on her two feet and her haunches, with the left arm folded tight against her ribs; and she came up out of the crouch without having put a finger to the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:303:"I didn't. Did you see that arm touch anything?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:309:"And if you're wrong, it's the arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:311:"If I'm wrong, it's the arm whether I've practised or not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:317:"No," said Lira. "It didn't." She took the top book off an old man's arm. "You can put those down now. Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:329:He had moved it before the word. He knew that he had, since the word had been cut off in the man's mouth; and he had not decided to. He went back over it: the eyes had been at the foot of the third row, and they would go up from there, and across, as they always went. He had put the light where they were about to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:333:He turned it over on the cold rock, with his forearm along his thigh. He had thought that he was learning one man's habits, as a groom learns a horse. That was not quite it. All the looking in that gallery ran one way. It began at the low left corner of whatever was looked at, and it climbed to the right; and it did so on the plank, and on every sheet of fifty-two, and it had done so at a cook-house counter. The board stood propped against the table's edge, with its head a little back; and he found that he knew, without the grey head's help, which end of each row the reed would come to first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:13:It was the near wheel that went. Cael saw the pin jump from its hole and ring on the stones; and after that the wheel walked off its arm, quite slowly, as a drunken man leaves a doorway, and rolled away down the yard by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:19:"It's on my foot. It's on my foot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:21:Two yard-men were running already, with a pole. The man in the cloak was shouting for a lever, and pointing. They went where he pointed, to the high side, where the other wheel stood a foot clear of the ground, and got the pole's end under the axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:39:"Lira. His belt, at the back. Both hands, if you've got them. Not till I say, and when I say, you take him straight toward yourself and you don't stop." He did not look round. "Cael. The wood wagon. Two splits, thick, as long as your arm; and one of those men to drive them. Under the axle first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:He got down on one knee beside the kerb, and put his left shoulder under the corner of the bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:It was the good one. The right was in four yards of webbing, under his shirt, with another woman's knot upon it, and he held that arm across his thigh as a man keeps a tool out of the way. He set his left foot flat, and his hand on the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:It had. The lifting had tipped the heap again, and a dozen plates had run down into the corner all together; and what Brom had raised by two fingers was now coming down upon him with a good deal more than he had taken up. Cael saw his knee begin to open. He could not set the thing down. The kerb was still under the rail, where a foot had been, and Lira's hands were on the stones beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:79:Brom came out from under it on his hands, and stood up slowly. He tried to lift the left arm, and it went as high as the elbow would carry it, and stopped; and he looked at it with the deep patient dislike that he had once given to a hound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:Cael turned, and the mule was standing. It had been throwing itself about in the shafts a moment since, and every plunge had rocked the bed. It stood now with its head down and its sides going, and an old man had hold of its cheek-strap. He had the stick in his right hand. The left, which would not open flat, had shut on the leather; shutting was the one thing that it could still do well. He was looking at nobody; he had held a ferry's stern so once, in a flood, with a turn round a post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:89:The lad had his toes in both hands, and was counting them aloud. There were five, and they bent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:117:Brom walked back to the bench, and bent to his box, and put his left hand to the becket, and the arm would not take it. He stood a moment; then he turned his other side to it, and swung the box up, in one heave, onto the right shoulder, over the webbing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:Cael had his mouth open, and found that there was nothing in it that would serve. He was the one who ruled on slips. He could not rule on a shoulder; it had never been his, and the man under it was looking at him with perfect mildness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:She had said that she would, at the door, with the lamp in her hand; and she had looked at Brom's right shoulder as she said it, and then at Cael. So he came down last but one, with nothing to do but watch a box ride where a healer had said that nothing was to ride. It sat well. Brom carried it as he would have carried it on the other side, and if the knot spoke to him, he did not pass on what it said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:He saw it from the gallery's mouth, with the carried lamp still thirty strides ahead of him: a long dark thing stood on its end against the table's edge, beside the Hall's fourth sheet, with a fold of sacking on the floor at its foot. It was a plank. It was of oak, as black as the shelves, as long as a man's arm and two hands broad; and as the light came up he saw that one face of it had been cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:165:Karis had stopped a yard from it, with both arms full, and she did not go nearer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:173:Cael took the ring from Lira, and went down on his knee, a hand to the left of a plank, and let his breath go long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:179:The bowls held counters: white pebbles in one, and black in another, and in the third a heap of small red squares that had been broken from a tile. The reed was as long as his forearm. He did not touch paper with his skin, and Cael had seen by now that he did not mean to; the reed did it for him, from a hand's breadth off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:183:He pointed, and Karis laid. A white pebble went down on the first sign of the row. Three black followed it, on the next three. Then red, and red; and white again; and black. She set each one below its mark, not upon it, so that her own pencil should not be hidden. It went the length of the row, twelve signs, and when it was done there were twelve counters in a line across the foot of her drawing: two white, seven black, three red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:201:"How d'you know?" said Brom. He had come to stand at the table's far corner, with his right arm folded and his left hanging. "They're scratches. One's as deep as the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside a wall on a moor that had roared like a forge with its door open. This made no sound at all. The man only looked, from the foot of a thing upward, without blinking, while the water ran down beside his nose; and whatever it was that he did, it went into the oak as quietly as ink goes into paper. It was the stillest thing that Cael had ever known a person do with what a plate had given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:"To do a thing; to what; and how many times." The stick's foot turned on the rock. "I have drawn some thousands of papers in that form, and had as many drawn for me. I have followed fifty-two of them down a stair for three days, on other people's backs; and it did not once enter my mind that I was looking at my own trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:275:The fourth row was the shortest, of seven signs, and it stood near the foot of the Stair's twenty-first sheet. When he had found it he straightened, and put both hands in the small of his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:283:He had come to that town with one question. He had written at a fire that he was about to hand it to a stranger, and had been afraid of it for two days, in a loft, with a small pale woman's roll across his knee. He need not have been. The stranger had told a gallery, without being asked, that he could not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:291:He did not say so, since there was nobody to say it to. The man took no more note of him than of the iron arm that his own lamp hung from, and Cael had asked to be exactly that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:299:When the bell went they roped the boxes, and Brom took his on the right shoulder again, and nobody said anything about it. On the long stair Cael counted the steps behind that box, twenty-seven, and the twenty-two after them in the open air; and the big man did not once shift it to the other side, since there was no other side to shift it to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:305:*Brom: the left shoulder wrenched, under a dray in the registry's yard, with no Path opened. He is carrying on the right, in the old healer's knot, against Oryn's word at the fork. I did not tell him to put it down. I couldn't have made him. Owed at the mill, to two healers, by me as much as by him. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:It was done as a thing is passed over a counter. The man in the dusty coat had shot the bar home behind them, a slide of black iron as long as his arm, with the flat of his palm; and he turned from it, and found a free hand at the height of his own, and hung the lamp's ring upon its fingers. He did not look to see whose hand it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:Cael stood on the first tread and listened to him go. The steps were quick and flat and perfectly even, with never a hand put out to the wall, and they grew small as a dropped stone grows small in a well. A man who has gone up and down one stair for the better part of his life does not need to see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:They went down in the order that the stair allowed. Lira went first, with the lamp held low at her knee, as she had seen it held; and the light went before her boots in a small yellow round, three treads at a time, and showed nothing beyond them. Karis followed with her bare hand on the wall. Brom came after her, bent under his box, since the roof of that stair had been cut for shorter people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:31:The cold deepened with every flight. He had felt cold in a good many places since the Line: on a mountain, and on a river that had turned into a road, and in a shed with the hurdles tied up against the wind. All of those had been moving. This one stood. It lay on the stair like water in a cistern, and when he went down into it he felt it close over his knees, and his belt, and his collar, a tread at a time. The flame in the lamp did not lean. It stood up as straight as a painted one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:37:"Twenty-seven," said Vastin, on the floor. He was breathing through his nose, and he stood for a moment with both hands on the ash and the bound leg thrust out. "He can count as well. I had begun to think it a rare accomplishment in this town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:They were in a passage that ran across the stair's foot, a dozen strides from end to end. The dark stone lined it. Where the lining stopped, on the far side, there were three openings, side by side, each as broad as a cart and half as high again as Brom; and each was a square of perfect black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:The shelves began a stride inside the mouth and ran on down both walls, in bays, each bay as wide as a man's two arms and divided by an upright of oak that the years had turned black. The lamp showed four strides of them at a time. Cael saw bundles tied in tape, laid flat, and rolls stood on end in racks like spears; he saw boxes of thin wood with no mark on them, and things in sacking, and a whole bay of boards, each as long as a forearm, stacked like slates. Nothing had a ticket. He looked for one in bay after bay, as he would have looked on any shelf in any office that he had ever stood in, and did not find so much as a number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:69:The floor was the bluff's own rock, cut level, and it was clean. No dust lay on it as dust lies in a loft; there was only a fine pale bloom along the foot of each bay, where nobody walked, and it was the colour of a certain coat. The air smelled of dry paper, and of old glue, and of cold stone, and under those of nothing whatever. It was the smell of a book that has stood shut on a shelf since before its reader was born.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:77:"I certified the keeping of this town's books on four occasions. There is a line in the return, near the foot, which reads *lower archive: undisturbed; sound*. I put my hand to it each time." He drew a breath. "I had supposed it to mean a cellar, with a shelf. I did not ask to be shown it. I see now that the line was exact, and that I had no notion of what it was exact about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:83:At ninety strides the shelves on the right hand gave place to tables. They stood end to end down the middle of the gallery, long and dark and as heavy as church doors, and Cael knew what they were from a hall above Ostrand: somebody had once sorted paper here by the cartload. They were heaped. Bundles lay on them a foot deep, squared, with slips of card thrust between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:91:Beyond the bare tables the gallery ended, in a wall of the pale rock with the pick's strokes still on it. A high desk stood against that wall, sloped, of the same black oak as the shelves; and over the desk, on an iron arm let into the stone, a lamp burned inside a chimney of glass. It was a far better light than the one that Lira carried. It lay on the desk's slope like a cloth, and on a leaf that lay there, and on the bent grey head of the man who was reading it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:99:Brom looked at Cael. Cael lifted one shoulder, under the strap. The box on it held a third part of fifty-two sheets, and a purse sealed under five thumbs, and it had been on his back since a frost in an inn yard; he found that he could carry it for one page more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:It was a long page. Lira counted the lines by the finger, since she could not help it, and he saw her reach nineteen. Brom let his own box down to the floor by inches, with his knees, so that it should make no noise, and stood beside it with his arms hanging. Karis did not put anything down. She stood with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, at the very rim of that good light, and her face had gone as still as water in a butt. She had waited the whole of her life for this room. It appeared that she could wait for a stranger to finish his paragraph.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:He did not think that anybody had kept that man standing at the bottom of a room in forty years. Rooms had risen when he came into them. Books had been held out to him, open, at the gate of this very town. He stood now with a case on his shoulder, behind a girl with a lamp, while a Bronze of the least-regarded class on the roll read to the foot of a leaf; and Cael saw the right hand leave the stick, and hang, and go back. The old mouth had set. Then, slowly, as a thing is done that has been practised, the grey eyes came away from the reader's back and went to the shelves, and stayed there, on a bay of boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:The finger reached the page's foot. The man turned the leaf face down, and laid a slip across it, and turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:127:She had the second box unroped on the floor, and the oilcloth open, and she carried the sheets to their tables one at a time, flat on her two forearms, as a person carries a dish that is full to the brim. She set each down by a measure that she had in her notebook. Twice she took one up again and moved it the width of a finger. The man in the dusty coat stood at the end of the first table through all of it, with his eyes shut and his hands behind him, and waited as she had waited for his page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:131:He did not read. Cael had seen that at a cook-house counter, and he saw it now for two hours together, from four strides off, with nothing to do but watch. The man bent over the first of the Ring's eleven, at its southern edge, where the foot of the sheet lay nearest him. His eyes went to the lowest row of her drawing and rested there. Then they climbed. They went up the sheet as a mason's eyes go up a wall that he has been asked to buy, stopping wherever a thing seemed to bear weight, and going back down from it to see what it stood on; and when they came to the top they did not go on to the next line, since there was none. They went to the next sheet's foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:137:Lira was on one knee at his left side, with the lamp's foot resting on the floor and the ring in her right hand. She had not stirred. Cael would have sworn to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:149:"I've half a shoulder on my paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:159:She held it in her right hand alone after that, and she did not change. Cael watched the hand begin to shake a little, at the wrist, somewhere among the Stair's twenty-nine. She put her elbow on her knee, and it stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:171:He stopped over that one as a dog stops at a gate. He looked at it from its foot. Then he went round the table's corner, and looked at it from the side, with his head laid over; and he came back, and bent until his face was a hand's breadth from her pencil, and stayed so for longer than he had given to any six of the others. Lira brought the light round to his left side, a hand off, without being told. He did not seem to know that she had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:183:He asked nothing more. He let go of the table, and walked to his desk with one hand before him, by habit, as a man crosses his own bedroom in the dark. There was a shelf above the desk, under the lamp's iron arm, and on it a cup, and a jar, and a thick bundle tied with tape; and at its end a long flat thing wrapped in sacking. He took that down. He stood with his back to all of them, and turned the sacking open, and looked at whatever lay in it. He looked from its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:199:"Today I'm no use to you. I'll be an hour getting these back." He had sat down in the chair that stood at his desk, heavily, with his hands flat on his knees and his eyes shut. "Put the lamp on the table's end, where I can find it. Then do what you like, so long as it's quiet, and it's nowhere near my paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:205:She stood at its corner, where a square pale hand had rested, and looked down at the fourth of the Hall's twelve. Cael came and stood beside her. He had left the box against a bay. The carried lamp burned at the table's end, a yard from them, where Lira had set it down as carefully as an egg; and the other, on its iron arm, lit a grey head that did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:207:He looked at the sheet, since she was looking at it. He saw what he had seen under a marsh, by a lantern, with water to his knees: rows of small cut signs, drawn square, in her hard clean pencil. He could not have said why a man should stop at that one. He was reading it the wrong way up, from the far side of the table, as he read everything; and it came to him that he stood at the sheet's foot, where the man himself had begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:219:She was shaking again. It was not as she had shaken in a hayloft, with a card on her knee. He saw her take hold of it, a breath at a time, and put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:"How long's it going to take him?" said Lira. She had sat down on the floor with her back against a table's leg and her right hand open on her knee. She was working the fingers. "I only want to know how many days I'm a lamp."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:265:He had been in a great many buildings since he was fourteen, and in every one of them there had been a drawer. There was a counter at Denvash, with a pen on it. There was a house on a bluff, where a file had grown as thick as a man's hand; there was a district seat, and a borrowed office, and a counter in every town between them. He had learned to feel for the drawer on any threshold, as a dog feels for the one board in a floor that gives. Even on the kind side of the Line there had been a book, kept by a man with one arm; that one had been honest, and he had been glad to stand in it, and it had put him eleventh all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:273:It was a strange thing to find in himself. He would have expected to feel light; and he did, a little, as he had felt at a hedge when a rule was lifted off a road. But he did not know how to stand. He had braced against that drawer for so long that his shoulders had forgotten any other way to hang, and he stood between two walls of other people's records with his hands open and nothing to set them against.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:291:It was dusk in the lane under the wall. The four alleys had lost their white. There was a paleness at the far end of each now which did not come from any sky, and it lay along the cobbles in a bar that did not flicker. Cael walked through all four with an old man's breath labouring at his shoulder; and a box went by on his left hand each time, between him and it, at the height of a girl's head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:293:Seln was at the ladder's foot when they came in under the arch. He looked the five of them over as a man counts his change.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:295:"Three people came to look at that low door in the yard, when it had shut on you," he said. "Two were yard-men, and they looked as people look at a thing which they have never seen used. The third was the clerk. He came out upon his own sill, with his book under his arm, and stood there for as long as it takes to read a page. Then he went in again to his hatch and sat down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Lira had begged a pot of something from the stable, which was meant for horses, and smelled of it. She rubbed it into the left shoulder by the lantern, with the heel of her right hand, while he looked at the slates and said that it was a waste of good grease. He did not let her near the right. That one was another woman's knot, he said, and nobody was to lay a finger on it until the woman had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:57:He went up it, and out along the leads, and sat down with his back against a chimney that was still warm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:69:The boy had not looked at it once. Seln had watched him not look, from a roof, down the whole length of a lane with four alleys in it, and had thought it very well done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:73:It weighed something under three pounds. He had known that weight for twelve years, to the ounce, as a man knows the weight of his own arm; which is to say that he had not felt it since the first month. It had gone through three postings on him, and across a Line. It had stood for some months between a researcher's grey boxes, on a shelf, with its key in his waistcoat, and had gone back onto the strap on the night that five people left a hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:123:There was a man in the smith's doorway, across the open place, where the trough was. He stood well back, out of the light of the lamp at the corner. He had been there while the cart went by, and he was there after it, and he had not shifted his feet. He was not looking at the gate. He was looking at the carriage-arch of an inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:127:It was one man, in one doorway, seen once. A district is full of people who have reasons to stand in doorways at night, and nine in ten of the reasons are a girl or a debt. Seln did not make a finding of it. He put it where he put a date read once by lamplight, over another person's shoulder: to be looked at again by day, before anything was done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:There was a cushion on the second chair that morning. It had been flattened by a great many years into the shape of one person, and the chair that stood before the desk's slope had none. Brom looked at it, and then at the grey back bent over a page, and opened his mouth; and Lira trod on his right foot, which was very nearly well, and he shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:"Same tool. Same day, near enough." He did not turn round. "They're not packing. They bear nothing, and nothing's hung on them; but they're not red. They stand outside the row, like a figure at the foot of a bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:203:Cael took the ring from Lira at the Stair's table, as he had on the two days before. He saw a raw hand come out, a little ahead of his own. It had not come far. It stopped a foot from the ring and hung there, and went back to the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:209:The man with the reed went through the Stair's twenty-nine that morning, and it was slow. Karis laid, and wrote, and laid. Somewhere in the second hour Cael's forearm began to tell him how long it had been lying on his thigh. He shifted his fingers on the ring, by as little as he could; and the shadow of a white pebble moved across her pencil by the width of a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:Cael did not tell him. He sat against a table's leg, with a heel of bread, and watched a man who had held an office in every room that he had entered for forty years stand in one that had exactly a single office in it. It was a lamp. It was held by a boy of no tier, on one knee, at a stranger's word, and it was the only thing in the whole of that gallery that anybody was doing for anybody else. The old man could not kneel. He had one hand. He was drawn to it all the same, as a nail is drawn across a table to a lodestone, and each time he found himself half way there without knowing that he had set out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:241:It was a tall man's task. Cael knew it as he rose. He was the shortest man in the gallery but one, and he would have to hold the ring at the full stretch of his arm; Brom had one arm in a knot and the other hanging; and there was a person by the nearest bay who stood half a head above any of them, and held himself like a post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:249:Cael stood, and put his arm up, and held the flame above the plank's head for as long as it was wanted. It was a good while. His shoulder was shaking before the end, and he let it shake, and kept the wrist still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:273:[[TIER-B: Cleon's close (S11): "You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." … "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." … "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." … "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since." — the plan quotes this speech in four pieces with elisions; staged as Cleon's, to Karis, at his desk at the end of the 287th, with the bundle untouched on its shelf; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:287:It had been forty-nine steps for that knee, with the stair inside the iron. At the head the old man stopped, by the parapet, and put both hands on the ash, and stood with his chest going. The others had gone on into the lane. The town lay below, with its lights coming out along the lowest wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:289:"Four times," said Vastin, when he could. "I counted them, as the young woman did. I had no business with that lamp. I have one hand to give it, and a leg that will not kneel, and nobody in that place had asked me for either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:309:"I was fourteen. I'm nineteen. I'm not there yet either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:319:Cael looked out over the roofs, at the bridge, and not behind him. He had knelt two feet from a pair of red eyes since the morning, and been a hook for a lamp, and done it well. He had wanted the whole time for the grey head to turn. A person who was truly nobody would not have minded whether it did.

