    90	- **Seln's night (the 286th).** The district is empty at night; a watch's handcart goes its round with a lantern; the blue gate is shut, with a brazier in the arch. He sat on the leads of **the chandler's warehouse on the ring road, the tallest roof below the blue gate** (four floors, a hoist-beam, an outside stair with no door). The case weighs "something under three pounds"; its key is in his waistcoat. **Every registry on the Line has three doors**: the front for names; the yard for loads; and a returns hatch at the side, with a lamp and a night clerk, where a courier lays a sealed thing at any hour and neither may ask the other a question: "A registry enters what is laid on its ledge." Treswick's is down a passage off the square's eastern corner. He considered the hatch and put it by; he considered leaving the case on the Deepstacks' unticketed shelves and saw that for filing; he ruled nothing. He marked that a clerk had seen his face and that nothing had fallen.
    91	- **The watchers.** A man in the smith's doorway opposite the inn's arch on the night of the 286th; a porter on the kerb opposite the cut's head on the 288th, from the second bell to the third, with nothing on his frame; the same boots both times, a horseman's with the spur-rests cut away. On the night of the 290th the same boots, hooded, on the step of the painted door under its lamp, waiting until the five had come in under the arch.
    92	- **Vastin.** At the yard on the 286th he held the dray's mule by the cheek-strap with the left hand and gave no order. On the 287th he moved toward the lamp four times (Lira counted); on the 288th twice; on the 290th not at all. On the 288th he began to post a watch and stopped ("That's one"). On the 289th he held three tall books at Lira's word, qualifying it ("I cannot undertake to carry them to a table"). His three heads on the night of the 290th: the knee (he had Oryn's words in his mind as the stick came down, and asks that she be told that he heard her); what the six came for (they looked past him at Cael); and that he knows no more of what Cael is than the plate did.
    93	- **The alley.** The gate at the cut's foot can be barred from inside the cut. **An iron bar is let into the wall at the cut's mouth, beside the parapet, for people coming up under loads.** Six, hooded, each with a cloth over the lower face: two with shod staves from the yard; above, one kneeling with a crossbow on the highest landing, one with a stave beside him, one who came down to the plane, and one at the rail with a shuttered lantern. The plane stood from the lip of the ninth step, wall to wall and to the sky, seen for a breath as a bloom on the dusk. Two bolts broke on it (the second a hand from Cael's face); a palm and one shoulder were laid on it. Brom dropped his box across the fourth step, tipped up the loose third tread (a slab of dark stone a forearm long and a hand thick), lifted it to his thighs and let it go down the steps at the first stave. Lira came off the landing over the box and drove her knife a third of its length into the second stave's shaft; she took the wall on her right shoulder. Brom's boot put the second man down the steps; one of them then hauled on the box's becket, and Brom sat on the box. Karis's boot went from under her on the dished sixth step; her bare left hand opened on the lip of the step above. The hood on the landing lifted two fingers; the lantern was raised to the parapet; **Seln's cord (a clerk's page-weight: a pear of lead on a thin cord) took the arm to the rail** before the shutter opened. Two low notes whistled. They left at a walk: the bow unstrung, the cord cut in two places, the dead lantern taken up, the gate unbarred. Cael's count was eighty-one. **A stave was left** (a watchman's, shortened, its ferrule filed) and **two bolts** (plain, no fletcher's thread).
    94	- **Seln's finding.** They opened no Path, though the one on the landing stood like a man who has one: a Path opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it, and they took the same care as the six to have nothing of theirs entered. The bow was to make somebody open; the light was to tell somebody below. They went for a box and not for Brom: they were told to bring away what was carried. "What they would have been glad to find was on a roof above their heads." The four went two east and two west along the lane, hoods back before the first corner; the two below left by the carts' gate, which had been left on the latch for them.
    95	- **Karis's hand.** A straight cut across the heel of the left palm, from the root of the thumb to the wrist's other side; "not deep… only long". Bound in the cut with Brom's neckcloth; sewn in the loft with **seven stitches** of waxed thread on a curved roping needle held in the lantern's flame; the thread bitten; bound with a strip of his shirt. She said her fourteen letters over, one to a stitch. She writes with the right. Why no healer: in the district's house the entry on the sheet at a bed's foot (name, registry, tier) precedes the treatment, and after the fourth bell a card waits until those with names have been seen.
    96	- **Small things.** Brom keeps a twist of cloth under his box's lashing with a roping needle and waxed thread. A pot of horse liniment was begged from the stable for his left shoulder. Vastin mended the becket of Cael's box with the stable's awl. The old keeper at the third gate did not write the lateness against the card on the 290th. The third tread of the cut's bottom flight is now out of its bed.
    97	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the carter's lad; two yard-men; the broker's factor; the under-registrar (spoken of); the watch; the six in hoods.
    98	
    99	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   100	
   101	**Calendar.** The night of the **two hundred and ninetieth day past the Line**, in the long loft at Treswick. Days on the page: 284 (the Deepstacks), 285 (the hatch; the lamp), 286 (the dray; the board; Seln's roof at night), 287 (the morning log; the figures; four times; Cleon's week and close; the parapet), 288 (the test; ten letters; "We are looked at"), 289 (the loft paid; the ladders; thirteen), 290 (fourteen; the alley; the loft). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   102	
   103	**Where everyone is.** Six in the loft: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Vastin (on a pallet under the blanketed gable window, unable to stand), and Seln on the ladder's head. Cleon is under the registry with fifty-two sheets on three tables, the board against the third, and tomorrow's card written and given. The clerk has written *the same* on six days. Oryn and the brown mule are north on her round (not on the page in this movement). Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
   104	
   105	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Eleven fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He has stood unfiled in the middle gallery. He has been the lamp since the 285th and has knelt two feet from Rune in use for five days; nothing has come of that. He has begun to put the light where the looking will go. He wanted the Path tried on himself, wrote that he had not said so, and has not said so. He has the eleventh (Iron-Wall-adjacent), has read its lines, has told it no three times, and has not used it. **He has not told Vastin**, and holds that he cannot without telling the mechanism, which is the six's. He has not set foot on the square or its steps and has not looked at the crown; he has walked the lane with its four alleys fourteen times. He has ruled that the cut is not climbed at dusk again and has ruled nothing else for the morning. Body: sound; a tarred case struck his ear.
   106	
   107	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Arm: day twenty-five out of the wool on the 290th.** It held a lamp-ring, a ladder's rail and a belt; she has practised dropping off a ladder with it tucked (eleven times before the 289th) and came off a landing so in the alley. **Right shoulder bruised on a wall; the arm took nothing.** Her knife is sound. Her registry paper has not been touched. She keeps the square's side of Cael in the lane. She has read the 285th's log and the 290th's.
   108	
   109	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **No grey at any point.** Toes done on the 289th. **Left shoulder wrenched under the dray on the 286th and made worse by the slab on the 290th; he cannot raise either arm above the elbow.** He has carried his box on the bound right shoulder since the 286th and took two falling people on that arm on the 290th: owed to two healers at the mill, entered by Cael as his own debt as much as Brom's. He was offered the broker's post and has, in his own mind, turned it down (the factor has been told only "I'll think on it"). **The purse: a hundred and thirty-four.** He sewed Karis's hand. He means to buy the rope going out.
   110	
   111	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork. She has said "Older than [UNBOUND]" once, at Cleon's table. She has been believed. **Fourteen letters of twenty-three; she can read the cutter's figures and number-words and nothing else, and will not guess.** She knows that seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried and eleven are nothing; that a bearer heads every run; that the board and the floors carry the same rows. She has not asked for the bundle. **Vastin's page is in her coat, unshown; the title on it has not been spoken.** She holds the flat, the hide, the ledger, her notebook and the day's card. **Left palm: seven stitches, bound in a strip of Brom's shirt.** Three days of Cleon's week remain.
   112	
   113	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. A clerk of the Treswick registry knows his face and has heard him speak. He watches from the roof over the cut. He has looked at the registry, its three doors and the station's light from a roof, with the case on him, and has ruled nothing. **The case is on him, shut; its contents are not named in this movement.** He has the better half of his cord and the stave. He has seen the same boots three times.
   114	
   115	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **He stood a wall on the eleventh day of thirty.** The knee let go sideways when he released it; one lath cracked; Brom has bound it again with a slat; it is twice its size; he cannot stand or hold a bowl on the night of the 290th. He knows what Oryn said it would cost. He gave his name, rank and office at the yard hatch on the 285th and was not written. He has apologised to Seln and been told to be nobody. He has asked for the wall to be entered and has been told that it is. **He does not know** the mechanism, what Cael carries, that anything was taken from him, the sum, or what is under the bar; he does not know what the six in hoods hoped to see and has said that he does not ask. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. Whether he heard Seln speak of the roof is not known.
   116	
   117	**Cleon.** Rune Path, Bronze; shown from outside only. He has said that he knows the hand; has shown the board; has said what bears, what is carried and what is nothing; has given the limit in both its parts; has given a week from the 287th; has spoken his close; has Karis's name and has used it once. **He has taken no notice of Cael.** He does not know that anybody was set upon in the cut.
   118	
   119	**The clerk.** Unnamed. One question asked ("The same?"). He has heard a name, a rank and an office said at his hatch and has written none of them.
   120	
   121	**The faction.** Six, hooded, unnamed, faceless. It reached inside the third wall, opened no Path, took nobody and nothing, and saw a wall, a rock, a knife in a stave and a boy standing on a step. **It saw nothing of what Cael carries.** It saw Vastin's face uncovered and an Iron Wall. It knows the loft, and has shown that it knows.
   122	
   123	**Objects and money.** **134 marks** (136 less two for the loft's second week, paid on the 289th; the loft is theirs to the 295th). The reserve sealed in the third box. Three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide are in the loft. The fifty-two first copies are on Cleon's tables. In the loft besides: a shortened watchman's stave with a knife-cut in it; the card for the 291st. Two bolts were left lying in the cut. Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman.
   124	
   125	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; not Vastin, Cleon or the clerk. The eleventh: Cael and Lira (from the log). The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone. Cael's wish to be read: Cael and his book (Lira reads the book).
   126	
   127	**Open threads.** Five markers. A debt to a man who cannot be told. A knee that may have bought a stick. Two shoulders and a hand for two healers at a mill. A bundle that nobody has untied, and a page that has not been shown. Nine letters to find, and one word covered with a glove. A boy who knows which way a lamp will be wanted. A question that he has not asked, of a man who has not looked up. A case that has been looked at. A clerk who has heard a name. Boots under a lamp. A stair that will not be climbed at dusk, and no ruling yet on what is to be climbed instead.
   128	
   129	## Owner flags
   130	
   131	1. **The notice's typography and frame.** The protected text of fragment eleven names its source ("Vastin."), counts ("Eleventh.") and gives "Deployment: none", none of which the edition's earlier system blocks do (Book 7's reads `[unnamed]`, and Cael remarks there that it always does). I set it verbatim in a fenced block, as the edition sets system text, and framed it as the lines "put into the plain words in which he would have to write" them, so that nothing says the system itself has begun to name people. If it should be italic log text, or the frame cut, the wording is untouched either way.
   132	2. **How the eleventh came.** It is not reached for (unlike Tide in Book 7). It arrives, as "nine of the ten" did, from standing within a stride of a Path used in earnest, in the minute when he is refusing the other ten. Its question (*This far?*) is mine. Cael notes that he stood a count of twenty by the wall at the cut and a minute and some here, and draws no cause.
   133	3. **"I put the wall in front of it anyway."** The plan does not pin "it". I staged it as what Cael is, which Vastin could not read at four feet and cannot now (his "third head"). If the source's antecedent is otherwise (the healer's order, say), the three sentences before the line want re-composing; the knee and Oryn are his "first head", a few lines earlier.
   134	4. **Seln's case and Book 6.** The map and the packet call the case "unopened" and its contents "unknown". This edition's Book 6 opened it once, on a table above Ostrand, for five people, and Book 7 says only that it has been "carried shut". I wrote *shut* and never *unopened*; no content is named; Seln keeps his Book 6 rule of never describing it, even to himself; Cael's narration says that he knows what is in it and that it does not go into a book.
   135	5. **Who was in the yard on the 285th.** For Seln to answer the clerk he had to be within speech, though he kept off the 284th's count because a clerk might know his face. I put him on the hatch's own bench as a porter waiting for a return load, unseen by the five until he stood. What not lying cost him is that face.
   136	6. **The clerk's difficulty.** The plan gives Vastin's reflex and Seln's answer but not what was at stake at the hatch. Mine: an honest clerk who would not write six boxes for three and was about to send up for the under-registrar. He asks one question in this movement, of the boxes.
   137	7. **Cleon's week (from the 287th).** Minted so that the packet's "three days left of Cleon's week" on the 290th and §13's "seventh day" (the 293rd) are both true. Karis's own "Give me a week" was said to the crew on the 284th.
   138	8. **The purse: 134.** The loft's first week ran out on the 288th. The map's cash track (136, then 130 on the 292nd after a four-mark meal) needs two marks spent between; I paid a second week on the 289th.
   139	9. **The decipherment's method**, the board's figures (a ring for five, a peck for one), the counts **seven / twenty-three / eleven**, and **fourteen letters by the 290th**. The number-words are tested by the pattern of repeated letters, which is given in the story's own tongue as it is everywhere else (twelve, nine, seven; then ten, the number after ten, and four more). No word but a number is read; the bundle is not opened; Vastin's page is not shown and its title is not spoken. If Movement 6 needs her further on or further back, the figures in chapters 31 and 32 are the places.
   140	10. **The faction's aim and limit.** Mine: they wanted a declaration seen from the town (the shuttered lantern at the parapet) and a box carried off, and they opened no Path because they cannot be entered in that town's books any more than the six can. The plan's "the cache was on the roof the whole time" is Seln's one sentence about a parapet. Whether they knew Vastin is left open (they looked past him).
   141	11. **Vastin's wall was a declaration inside the third wall**, in a slot with no door or window. Nobody but the six in hoods saw it. I have not said whether anything on the crown felt it.
   142	12. **Iron Wall "has no tier" in the lines.** Cael reads "Tier equivalent unknown" against Vastin's remark at the shoulder hold that the Path's rank "would mean nothing on this road". No tier is stated for Vastin.
   143	13. **The rail at the cut's mouth** (BOOK_MAP §8 names "the rail"; Movement 4's cut has none). I added a single iron bar let into the wall at the top, beside the parapet.
   144	14. **Order on the stair in the alley:** Vastin, Cael, Lira, Karis, Brom. Movement 4 went down it Lira, Brom, Cael-below-Vastin, with Karis first into the yard; going up the slowest leads.
   145	15. **Lira's remark** that she fed Cael for a day "after a stair" rests on Book 7's bill for Shadow at the Stair (dead to the elbows). It is new as an event.
   146	16. **The under-registrar, the returns hatch and "three doors to every registry"** are new furniture of the Compact's houses.
   147	17. **A sequence lock, kept by a hair:** on the 289th Lira tells Vastin to hold three books and he does, having first qualified it. §13 says he takes no order without walking it back before Millrace; he walks this one back before he takes it.
   148	18. **Cael's ruling for the 291st is left open** (only that the cut is not climbed at dusk again), so that Movement 6 may send them by the carts' street in daylight, as the map has it, on its own terms.
   149	19. **Length:** 36,066 against 35,000 (about 330 of it marker text).

