editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:They did not go back as they had come. Seln had read the hide by Karis's lamp on the crest, and had found them a shorter way than a marsh and a stair: along the ridge to the heap of chock-stones, and then north by the hide carters' road, which ran through shut holds toward a river that all of them knew. He walked it ahead of them, as he walked everything. Four times in a day he came back along the file and said his word, and after the second day Karis left off writing down the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:11:"How long?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:23:Lira walked in front of him, and he watched her do it for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:43:"I rode a mule off a mountain once, for that," said Lira. "I'll walk home on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:61:He had wanted a face, and had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods. This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:123:They had come down the south road since noon, past a well with a new rope on its windlass, under a sky the colour of a slate. Cael felt something touch his face as the meadow opened, and took it for ash from somebody's fire. Then there was another, on Lira's sleeve, which did not melt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:135:Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:151:"You were to see a woman about a knot first," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:161:He had the ledger open already at their heading. He turned his shoulder to the room and gave out a line that nobody had yet heard, in the voice that he used under his eave. It was a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting. It was taken. Six had gone in. There were instruments found there, and brought up, and kept by the crew that found them; and six had come out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:185:He turned his shoulder again. Cael saw the hide-woman put down her cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:187:"*Crew of six. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:203:"You've left it empty," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:She turned to Lira, and held out her two hands, and Lira gave her the sling without a word. The wool came down. The healer's fingers went round the forearm, overlapping, and she took the short reading, to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:19:"All eight. They've taken no harm from a stair or a wall, which is more than you deserved of them." She drew the wool up and tied her own knot, high, with the hand at the collar-bone. "It holds nothing. I said first snow, near enough, and I'll say when; you'll not say it for me because a flake's come down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:51:"Find me at Thornwater," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:85:Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:129:"I can't make it," said Lira. She had her right hand over her eyes. "It's not one thing, I think. Or it is, and there's sky in it. I'd be guessing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:At the old ruts Lira stopped, and looked down the slope, and then at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:157:"Whoever drove a cart round this one kept a furlong off." Lira had not moved from where she stood. "The iron tells us where to stop. Without it you'd be well down the hill before you thought to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:167:He had seen four such stakes. There had been one at a grey stone on the Fallow, and two at the Stair with a road between, and one at the track's end beside a marsh. Each had stood alone at an edge where a cord had been laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:179:Lira had gone along the fence to the left, counting under her breath, and Brom to the right. They came back together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:181:"Forty," said Lira, "and it doesn't stop. I could see a dozen more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:205:Lira was standing a pace above the iron, where she had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:213:"I have not a single sheet of this place," said Karis. "I should like that entered beside whatever is ruled. I want it as I wanted the Stair. I am not going to say that I would go alone, since I have learned what that sentence costs other people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:61:"Everyone I've ever had my hands in, this is the part where I know." She said it as a finding about herself. "Iron Skin, you feel the banks first — dense, walled, everything held. Ember, the banks are a fire's, hot at the edge. Wind you barely feel the banks at all, the water's too fast. But there's always a bank. It's how you know which river." A pause, long, her current going along Ember and stopping at the ignition point and lying there as if it had found a stone in a bed. "I can't find one here. I keep putting my hand where the bank ought to be and there's more water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:81:It was at about the hundred and thirtieth that she came to where the ninth lay, and that one did a thing that none of the others had done. It drew in. He felt it go from her as a snail's horn goes from a finger, small, and smaller, into some place behind the rest where even he could hardly have put a hand on it. He had not asked it to. He had not asked anything of it since a lane. It was a thing made for not being found, and somebody was looking, and it had hidden by itself, as its first owner had hidden on a back stair before he was old enough to choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:89:It did not. It lay quiet under her hands. She went along the top of it as a boat goes over deep water, and it did not so much as lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:171:The place behind his breastbone took on weight. It filled slowly at first and then all the way to the brim, like a cup under a careful hand; and he lay under a healer's palm and kept his eyes open through all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:218:She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:1:# Chapter 6 — Thornwater
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:17:He did not mean their mouths, or not only. Cael had seen him look at Brom's forearms as the tailboard came through the gate, and at Lira's feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:19:"What was that?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:37:The woman had been looking at the cart. She lifted the edge of the small canvas and saw three roped boxes on top of her grain, with a paper tag on each. She let the canvas down. Then she raised the lantern a little, for the first time, and went over the five of them with it, their boots and their coats and the cut of Lira's hair, and stopped at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:It was the third time he had been asked, in three different ways, and the first time the answer seemed to be put away somewhere for later. She took the slip from the carter's hat, where he had left it on the shaft. She read Pike's hand, slowly, and the five strokes under it, and then she fished a stub of charred stick out of her apron and made her mark on the bark, pressing hard. It was a thorn twig, three spines to a side, with a wavy line under it for water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:51:"You're hired. That's different. He'll not go down again till the sun's straight over, and I don't feed people for sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:71:There were forty-one sacks, and each weighed about as much as Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:Brom took them up one at a time, on his right shoulder, with his left hand for the ladder. It was the tree on the gravel bar over again, with a twelve-foot drop under it and a floor of flagstones, and Cael, who was drawing water at the well, watched every trip from the side of his eye. On the seventeenth, a rung went. It cracked through under the leading boot with a sound like the wheel had made, and the whole of Brom's weight and a sack of barley came down eighteen inches onto the rung beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:75:He did not drop this load, for Lira was standing under him with the next one. Cael had his own breath stopped, and Anchor had already found the ladder's rail and asked whether it should be held. It had held a doorway once, for twelve seconds, without waiting for his leave, and he answered it quickly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:89:Lira ran the fence at first light, on the second morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:The bowman had been watching her come. He was a lean, weathered man of no particular age, who seemed to live on that platform, and Cael had not yet heard him use his voice. He looked down at Lira standing under him with her hands on her knees. Then he lifted two fingers from the stave of his bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:95:Lira saw it. She went red to the ears and ran the whole fence again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:101:"She was fast enough before she stopped." The woman threw her water at the geese. "Is that a thing they teach, where you're from?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:103:"No. They teach the other. Every school west of the Line gives you a rank for declaring quicker than the next person, and none of them has a word for this." He watched Lira go round. "We call it the hold. We're having to find it out for ourselves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:141:There were three ways out of Thornwater. He had known that from the loft door on the first morning, and he had taken them one at a time, at this hour only.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:177:Lira read it with her lips moving, and then looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:207:"I shall keep it dry," said Karis. Her voice had gone very small. "I shall keep it flat, and dry, and I shall not let anybody put a cup on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:217:He had not written much at Thornwater, for the first night he had not trusted his hand, and since then he had drawn water, and carried what Brom could not, and helped Karis to pace out a number along a fence. He was eighteen, and for three days he had been a hired man on a grain hold, and it had suited him better than he would have guessed. But there was a scrap of bark in the pocket of Hesk's book now, where two letters had lain, and it seemed to him that the book ought to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:219:*Forty-sixth day past the Line. Thornwater: a grain hold at the head of a valley, two days east of the board. Fourth line, in Pike's hand, copied and brought up by the carter. Nine marks, in his box. Earned, eighteen. In hand tonight, a few coppers and a map.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:Down in the yard Brom was spinning a wheel again, for the pleasure of hearing nothing, with a row of geese drawn up behind him like a jury. Lira sat on the well's coping, binding the hilt of her knife. On the platform by the gate the bowman stood as he always stood, with his bow at the length of his arm, looking down the valley at a white stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:243:Cael looked where he was looking. There was nothing beside the stone, and the light was going. He buckled the book into the satchel and climbed down the ladder, to draw the evening's water before it was dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:23:"And the maps?" said Lira. "He counted eight sheets with holes in them. That's not in your four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:33:"Thirty-one," said Lira. She had been turning it over. "If there's a thirty-one, there's a thirty. And like as not a thirty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:77:Lira was sitting on the keg, with her sling, and her flat foot stretched out to the lantern, and she had been watching Karis's face and not the leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:85:The others went up; and Brom took the lantern's fellow from its nail and lit Lira up the ladder ahead of him, a rung at a time, with his big hand held ready under her heel and never touching it. He had been told what that arm might not do, and had decided for himself what the rest of her was likely to want. Seln went last and made no sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:117:It was the coldest morning that they had yet had at that gate. The trough had frozen through to the stone in the night, so that Brom had broken it with the back of his hedging bill, and every breath in the yard went straight up. Lira was on the coping, where she had sat at that hour for ten mornings, with her foot set flat and her right hand shut on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:127:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:135:Lira set her jaw and nodded, as she would have taken a figure off a slate; but Cael saw her right hand come open on her knee, a finger at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:141:"On what?" said Lira. She lifted the great boot an inch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:179:"She's yours," said Lira. She had come up behind them. "You've had her thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:181:"I've had her thirty rounds, and she's a mule. She'd as soon carry boxes as panniers. Boxes don't want mending at the end of the day." The healer spoke more quickly than she was used to, and looked at none of them. "There's a beast at every gate on my round. They feed mine, and one of them shoes her. The next has a jenny that's eaten her head off since the barley was in and done nothing for it. I'll have that one off them, and they'll thank me. You've nobody who'd lend you so much as a goat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:191:"She goes on the low side of nothing. You go on the low side of her. She'll not cross moving water with anybody on her near hand, and it's no use arguing; she was like that when I got her. Her near hind's been shod since the Oxhollow road, and it'll want looking at by the thaw. She answers to a click." She showed him, with her tongue. "She's never had a name, and she's not to be given one. It's a beast. You name a thing and then you're sorry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:203:Lira stood looking at a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:213:The stake was moved to sixty paces at noon, and half of Thornwater found that it had business near the western gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:257:"I can't span yours either," said Lira's voice, from above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:265:He had had the order of it for fifteen days. He had settled it on the evening that five people stopped at a rise and he did not: there was a thing that he would have to say, when he had it, and Lira was to hear it first, and after her the rest. He had not kept to that. He had told a healer the oldest part of it on a table, since that debt would not wait. But the part that was Lira's he had carried past a stone, and a rut, and ten nights in a loft; and she had let him carry it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:279:"Of mine," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:301:Lira turned her head at last, though it was too dark to read her face, and he could see only the pale shape of it and the sling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:17:She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:51:"Then he found what I found, at the Stair. I should like it entered beside mine that he did, and by what."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:67:She did not trust that, and she set about breaking it. A straight thing will lie through any two marks on a skin, and so two of those dots proved nothing whatever; the whole question was the third. She moved the brass. She laid it through the Fallow's dot and the hall's, and looked at the Stair's: it sat under the edge. She laid it through the Stair's and the hall's, and the Fallow's sat under the edge. She took her pencil and made each dot again a pencil's breadth to one side, which was as far out as she believed that she could be on a map of days, and laid the brass a fourth time, and a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:105:"It's got numbers on," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:123:"And if there is?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:171:"They'll not hire till the thaw," said Brom. "Nor feed a stranger. I asked him once what a crew does out here when the board shuts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:"Twice," said Lira, who had not looked up from the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:Lira said nothing. Her right hand came along the frozen grass, and lay against the side of his boot, and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:21:"Below," said Lira. She glanced up the fan, and then along the edge of the shelf at the top of it, and her mouth went thin. "That's a bench. It's got a mountain on one side of it. Where else is there to be?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:"The second he had from a carter, who had it from his father. Men used to come up a side water off the Thornwater road with carts, for roofing stone. It lies ready split at the foot of a great slide, and need only be loaded. One year they did not come back down, and the next year nobody went up to learn why." He lifted his eyes to the wheel-ruts under their feet, with the turf grown over them. "I had not supposed, until this morning, that I should be standing in the second tale."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:"I asked him that in those words. He said, the kind a man would not be on a road." Seln spread his hands a little. "He means all three, and he does not know which. It is the rule that Thornwater keeps, for the reason that Thornwater gave us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:41:Cael looked at the slope. Nothing on it moved. A small wind came down the gorge and stirred the heather, and across the water the far wall gave back the sound of the stream, late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:45:"We're about to drag a cart up a hill of plates," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:71:"She what?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:85:Brom took the middle box by himself. He knelt, and had Lira set it on his back, and passed his rope over it and across his chest. Then he stood up under it and walked onto the fan with his hands clasped before him, as a porter walks under a chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:93:He took the third box after it, in the same manner, while the light went yellow on the far wall. Lira carried the stores, two steps and a slide, and would not be told that a bound ankle had no business on loose plates. By the last of her journeys the cart stood at the foot of the fan with nothing in it but its own seat board, and looked like a thing that had been robbed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:One end went down to a bridle on the cart's axle-tree, where a pull from above would lift her nose. At the other end he put himself. He stood in a loop that went round his chest and under both arms, facing downhill, with his hands behind his back; and when Karis, up at the log, raised her arm, he walked down the mountain. Seln and Cael went down with him, hauling hand over hand, and for every yard that three people descended the cart rose one, nose first, half buried, with the plates parting round her and closing behind as water closes behind a punt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:107:They held. Lira scrambled across below the cart and wedged slabs under both wheels, and the rope was carried up to the next island, and the log after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:115:"Lira," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:117:She was there already. They had knotted the box-lashings into a second line and made one end fast to the cart's near side, and Lira had carried the other up the slope and to the left, away from the chute, to a boulder as big as a barrel. She sat down behind it now with both boots against the rock, and passed the line round her back, and took it in her two fists in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:125:It was a small slip, a barrowful of plates running out from under the rim, and the cart dropped a foot and snatched. Cael saw the line take Lira. It lifted her off her seat and dragged her a yard up the boulder's face, with her heels scraping, and he saw what happened in her calves, because he had been watching for it for sixty days. Every sinew in her stood ready to be somewhere else. It was the burst, asking, with the whole of her Path behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:149:It was a road, or the stone that a road might have been made on. It ran west from the top of the fan along the wall of the gorge, as level from end to end as a shelf in a shop, thirty feet broad where they stood; and no hand, by the look of it, had ever cut it. The mountain rose on the left in a long grey slope of plates, steep as a roof, to a skyline that he had to lean back to find. On the right there was an edge, and beyond the edge blue air, and the far wall three hundred yards off with a white thread of water hanging on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:157:Lira was to run first. She would go out a hundred of her own strides along the shelf, and stand a stone on its end where she stopped, and come back and say what she had found; and nothing with a wheel was to go on any stretch until she had been over it at speed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:161:"Because a thing that'll bear me running will bear her walking," said Lira. She was looking down the shelf, and not at anyone. "And if a piece of this is going to go, I'd sooner it went under me than under three boxes and the four of you tied to them. I can get off it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:165:Lira went, with her Path where she had kept it since the ford. She ran lightly, well in from the edge, and her right foot came down a shade behind her left, and he looked at the gorge until she was a small figure standing a stone on end. She walked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:171:"Mine are bigger," said Lira, with satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:"One. Twenty-six feet. A hand and two fingers in the yard, outward. Slab; hollow at fifty on the outer third. No water. Cart: yes, keeping in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:179:Lira had come to look over his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:185:"Seventy lines." Lira began to laugh, low, mindful of the rule. "Seventy marks. He's being paid by the line." She pointed at the book in his hand. "You've written in one of those every night since a lane in Ardenmere, and nobody ever gave you a copper. Now there's a man with a ford who'll give you a mark every time you rule one off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:199:"I am of no use at a shaft that she is not of more," he had said, with a small bow toward Lira. "I cannot run, or carry, or count by a clock. There is one part of a day's work on a road that I can do better than any of you, which is to go on in front and learn where a person would be glad to lie down. I should like to do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:205:The word was at the end of the sixth hundred. Lira came back from her run with a flat pebble in her palm, which she had found lying on the very stone she would have stood on end; and on the pebble, scratched with the point of a knife, there were two short strokes and an arrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:211:Seln himself was forty yards farther on, down on one knee at a place where water came out of the rock, and he was not filling the skin. He was looking at the wet ground beside the lower of two small pools.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:219:They ate cold under it, and nobody proposed a fire. Brom let the tail-board down by its chains as gently as he could, and one link slipped and struck the frame, a small clear note, like a spoon on a cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:231:*Seventy-third day. The bench, at the end of the eighth hundred. The cart came up the fan in six stages, by Brom's reckoning and nobody's Path. Eight lines in Karis's table, and each of them says yes. Lira says that I am paid by the line.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:237:There was ice on the kettle in the morning, a skin as thin as a leaf, the first that any of them had seen past the Line. Lira ran the ninth hundred with her breath smoking, and they drew the cart on after her with Brom leaning into his rope on the up-slope side. Wherever a gully came down the face it had laid a tongue of loose plates right across the shelf, and on those she floated. There he walked against the strap with his whole body slanted, like a man in a gale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:241:Lira went out for the twelfth, and she did not come back at a run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:19:"Well?" said Lira. She had come up on his left. "Aren't you going to open them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:27:Lira stared at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:49:"Aloud," said Lira. "If it's his. I want to hear how he sounds on paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:79:It was Lira, and she had said it between her teeth, with great satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:"Bracken. A map reference to the Line and nothing past it. Somebody up a stair wanted to know which way we'd gone, and he gave them a place that everybody already knew we'd been, and wrote it out properly, I expect, with a number." She was grinning. "I never liked him. I'd like to shake his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:91:"All of us, I should think," said Lira. "You broke more benches than I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:They stood round it, and it was a handsome thing. The registry had printed it from a plate, in black, on a sheet as long as Brom's arm, and the whole of a continent was there: every district with its bound ruled round it, and every chartered road a firm double line, and the towns in three sizes of letter by their standing. Cael found Ardenmere, and a river he had crossed on a bridge, and a bluff above Ostrand with a small square on it. Somebody had written *ridge* beside a pass in the east, in pencil, and a little farther on, *Shelf; a pine; water at 36*, in the same small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:123:"Seven," said Lira. "Which is ours?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:125:"I make it the third from the top, by where it crosses the first water. They have drawn the crossing and left out the ferry. They have left out Lowmarch." Karis put her little finger on the last dash of the third road. It ended in nothing, three fingers east of the river. "That is about the saddle. By this sheet we walked off the end of everything that is known the first time we climbed out of that meadow, and we have been standing on blank paper ever since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:137:She showed them. The first they knew: it was the double line that left its straight, two days beyond Thornwater, and went round a patch of bare skin and came back, and she had ringed the patch in pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:"That is the only place where one of his roads walks round a blank. I said so on the fifty-eighth day, and it is still so. But he draws a hill with short hatching, and water with three wavy strokes. Look here, south of the ring with the bar, a day down and a little aside. He has hatched hills all across this corner, close as thatch, and in the middle of the hatching there is a round place the size of my thumbnail where he lifted the stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:143:"And here, a long way south-east, among his water. Strokes all round, and a round of bare skin in the midst, with no road within a day of it." She had ringed both. "Three blank circles. One I can source, since a one-armed man has named it. The other two are a man lifting his hand from a skin. I have written *guess* beside each, and I shall not call them anything else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:145:"Could you rule a line through them?" said Lira. "You've got that brass thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:155:They came up to Thornwater's gate an hour after noon on the ninety-third day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:159:On the platform the bowman lifted two fingers from his stave. Lira put up the great boot, an inch, and set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:189:He told her at the long house door, with the steam of a barley broth going up between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:The steading's woman had given the healer her back room without being asked; and when Karis inquired whether that was the custom, she had looked at her as at a person asking whether the well had water in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:13:"That's cheerful," Lira had said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:21:He had watched him go in. It had been off the near beach, a little below the white stones, where the bottom fell away a stride from the edge. Seln had left his coat and boots under the cut bank and walked into the pool in his shirt with a knife that Cael saw then for the first time, a narrow dull thing no longer than a hand, which gave back no light. The water had taken him to the waist, and the chest. He had gone on until it closed over his shoulders, and only then, with nothing above the river but his face, had he done what he had not done since a fire at a crossroads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:23:Nothing changed that Cael could have pointed to. A man's head was there on the green water, and then it was a thing that the eye did not care to rest on, like one stone among a great many. Behind eight others the ninth of his own fragments turned over, as a dog lifts its head at a voice it knows. It was Shadow, which had come to him out of that same Path, and he put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:27:The rest of them were where the charcoal had put them. Brom stood on his stone in the near channel, five yards out, with his left heel against the lip and the bill in his right hand, facing the beach they had come down. Karis was at the bar's blunt head, beside a boulder the size of a loaf, and she had her small knife out; the blade of it shook a little, and her face did not. Cael and Lira had the far landing, where the two green grooves came up out of the water onto the gravel, with one of them on either side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:33:He had thought that there would be frogs. There were none; the only sound at that ford was the water going over the lip into the two pools, a long steady hiss like rain on a roof, and it covered everything. Nothing here would stop to warn him. He watched the far beach, and the grass above it, where three paths came down that he could not see, and the sun went lower behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:35:It took the colour first. The river had been green all afternoon, and brown in the shallows, and gold where the light struck along it; and when the western ridge took the sun, the whole valley went grey in one breath, as it always did. He looked at the water sliding over the rock at his feet, and he knew what the man on the settle had meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:41:He counted the large stones along the far waterline, having nothing else to do with his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:45:He went over them a second time from the left, slowly, with his mouth dry. Yesterday at noon there had been seventeen. He found the seventh from the upstream end, which he did not remember, long and low, with the water lapping at it; and another near the middle, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:He lowered his left hand beside his thigh and put out three fingers, where Lira would see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:59:They did it all together and without any sound. They had been lying along the waterline with their bellies on the wet shingle and their chins on their paws, and they came up as mist comes off a pond. They were just as he had seen one on a rise of ground ten days ago: low, long in the back, with the head carried level.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:61:"Behind," said Lira, not moving her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:63:He turned his head. On the near beach, on the stones that five people had walked down an hour before with their boots crunching, three more were standing. Brom faced them across fifteen yards of water with the bill held low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:73:Brom had not forgotten what he had been asked for. Cael saw him shift the bill, and draw his own knife left-handed, and strike its pommel once against the hook. It rang out over the water like a hammer on an anvil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:89:It was twenty yards of fast water on green rock, and they went over it as cats go over ice, flat-footed, with each paw set down and tried. Their heads never wavered from the yellow seed. Karis was counting under her breath, as she had counted to ninety on a gravel bar with a stone in her lap. He did not hear the number at which she stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:97:The grooves came up onto the gravel between him and Lira. There were two yards from his boots to hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:105:"Six," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:111:They came up out of the water apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:113:One went to the right, toward Lira, and the other came for him. He set himself to meet it, with the knife low, and it was not there. Its teeth clicked on the skirt of his coat, and it was back out of reach, a clear yard, with its head level, before his arm had finished moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:127:On his right the other was at Lira in the same way. She had never in her life let a feint go by without an answer, and she went after it with the long knife, and it gave back before her down the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:129:"Lira," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:135:Brom did not hurry. He stood on his stone with three grey shapes in front of him across the water, and he rolled his shoulders once, as he did before he lifted anything. Then he declared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:159:Cael saw his face take the teeth. He did not cry out, and he did not try to pull the arm away. He turned his left shoulder in over the animal's neck and went down on one knee with all his weight, off the edge of the slab into the fast water, and bore its head and its forelegs under.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:161:The current took the hound's hindquarters. It swept them round and out over the lip, into the falling water, and the river pulled one way and Brom's heel against the rim of his stone held the other, and the animal was drawn out straight between them like a flag in a wind. Its jaws dragged down his arm as the river hauled on it. He let them drag. He kept his shoulder where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:167:The two that had followed came up to the stone through the water, and found no declaration there to meet them, and stood. One of them set its teeth in the tail of Brom's coat and tugged at it, twice. It was asking. He gave it no answer whatever, having a river to hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:171:"Five," said Brom, to the water, through his teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:173:Cael turned to look for Lira then, and found her not three yards from him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:177:Lira had stopped thinking of it as a dog at about the time it took her sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:193:She could not have said what stopped her. It might have been the sound of the water, which had changed, for the hiss of the lip was behind her now and there was a deeper noise on either hand. Or it was only the back of her neck. She had stood in a great many yards with somebody waiting behind her for the bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:197:The beach was a long way off. The boy was a small dark shape up at the head of the bar with a grey thing dancing round his knees. She had come twenty yards down the gravel, or more, a pace at a time, and all of them in one direction; and there was black water close on her left and close on her right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:209:She had run round by the bed of a wash for less, with the water coming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:251:It went all at once, like a cart lifted off a foot. The big fool had put his Path down. In front of her the dog checked. It had come to the edge of the gravel at the bar's head, where the channel began, and the declaration it was running to had gone out like a lamp; it stood there on three legs with one forefoot raised over the water, and its head began to swing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:261:The edge was round stones lying on round stones, with the water through them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:273:The pain was coming. She could feel it on its way, like somebody climbing a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:275:"Five," said a voice across the water, through its teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:279:"Lira."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:She stood on the kerb, where it was two strides broad, and set her pannier down between her feet. She was a long way inside the line, with no Path to count by, and she knew it. She folded her arms, and looked across thirty yards of water at a gap in a grey wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:The water came to the middle of his shin. He did not stand in it; he went straight on, at the pace of a man crossing a yard, and the four of them went down after him one by one. The plates were slick under Cael's boots, and each of them gave a little as his weight came onto it, and rocked back as he left it, so that it was like walking over the lids of a row of great chests. He counted his own strides. He did not let any one of them be longer than its fellow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:23:The gap in the wall had a sill of the grey, a hand above the water. Seln went over it without breaking step, and the rest of them after him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:25:It was a room. He could see that much while he walked, and he took it in without stopping. It was twelve strides each way. The walls were a yard through, and they stood three times Brom's height at the far corner and were broken down to the height of a man's chest in two places on the left, with every break worn as round as a river stone. There was no roof and there never could have been a beam; the mist lay across the top like a lid. And the inner face of every wall was cut. The sunk lines ran in their courses from the water's skin to the broken tops, course over course, straight and hooked and crossing, nine deep where the wall was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:29:The floor was a ledge. It went round the foot of the four walls, three or four strides broad, under a foot of water; and in the middle of the room there was no floor at all. The plates had gone in there, a long time ago. He saw a hole as wide as a cart is long, with green depth in it. The water over it stood up in a low smooth dome and slid away on every side, and that was the spring. In the very middle of the hole one great slab lay aslant, like a table that has lost two legs, with its upper corner a hand under the surface.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:33:He went at the first of the breaks without slackening, and put both palms on the round of it, and was sitting on the wall's top before Cael had seen how. Lira held the bow up to him by its stock. He laid it behind him, and took her right hand in both of his, and she walked up the cut face of the wall on her whole sole as though it had been a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:35:Karis came to the foot of the break with the roll under her arm, and reached up. She was a head shorter than Lira. Her fingers went over the round of the stone, and found nothing to close on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:57:It opened as a trap-door opens. The plate under Brom's boots went up at its inner edge, toward the hole, with a roar of water; and Cael saw Brom do the only thing that was in him to do. He did not jump. He straightened his back. He drove up from his heels with the whole of his strength, and Karis went off his shoulders like a sack off a hoist, and landed on her chest across the top of the wall with her legs kicking. Seln had her by the coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:He had been thrown toward the gap, and he had gone on as he was pointed, wading, with the water flying from his knees. The wall beside the gap, on its right hand, was the lowest in the room; it was broken down to the height of his waist. He threw himself across it, chest first, and dragged his legs up after him, and lay there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:79:"Karis," said Lira. "Here; I've the bow. Cael's along from us, at the corner. You're across the hole. Brom?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:He had a pebble of Lira's. He dropped it from the eastern wall onto the ledge below, where it lay under a foot of water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:99:"Then we jump down running," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:101:"A person who lets himself down off a wall stands for as long as it takes him to find his feet. It took a man at three, and he had been walking the breath before." Seln's mild eyes went across the water. "And that man has one arm to come down with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:It was cover, and it was a trap, and the same yard of stone was both. Cael understood it: nothing could come at them where they sat, and they could not leave it. He watched the plates lift below him, in a line that went round the hole and came back; and when it passed beneath his boots he could see a length of back under the clear water, as broad as the ledge itself, going by for a great deal longer than he liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:113:Lira looked at the soles of her boots, and then at the back of her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:119:"No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:It was the only thing that was said about it. Cael heard the pencil going, steadily, on thin paper against stone. He heard Karis say *sheet*, in a voice that had the blood in it, and Lira let go of the belt, and tore one from the roll with her teeth, and passed it down, and took hold again. A finished sheet came up over the edge and was pinned under a pebble. The two of them moved a yard along the wall on their knees, and Karis went over once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:139:Out on the kerb, thirty yards beyond the gap, a square brown shape had taken one step toward the water and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:149:The hand opened. He swung outward on his left, away from the sill, and his heel struck the stone and turned him. He went down on the wrong side of the wall altogether, outside the room, onto the plates between the hall and the kerb. Cael heard him strike the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:"Brom," said Lira. There was no answer. "*Brom.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:153:Through the gap Cael could see a boot, and a length of grey coat, lying in a foot of water. The boot moved. It did not move far. Brom had his face out of it, and that was all that anybody on a wall could tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:161:There was nothing fine about how she went. She had stood on a kerb for an hour with her arms folded, thirty yards from a shoulder that she had mended, on the wrong side of a line, with nothing in her hands. She had watched that shoulder go, through a gap in a wall; and she was the nearest living person to a patient who had fallen. She stepped down into the water as she would have stepped off a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:171:The line of lifting plates began again, and it was not going round. It went straight along the ledge below Karis's boots, toward the gap. The plate nearest the sill rose, and dropped. For a breath there was nothing; and then, beyond the wall, out in the dish, a swell stood up on the water and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:177:Lira had the bow up, and nothing to loose it at but water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:179:He had ten fragments. He went through nine of them in the time of one breath, as he had on a frozen road, and it was different, because here there was nobody to see, and he need hold none of them back. Wind was in his feet already. It would put him over the wall in a count. Then it would lock him where he landed, dumb, for a count more, the stillest thing on that floor, at a time and a place that he had not chosen. Storm would lay the water flat, and what was that to a thing with no eyes? Ember's fire was for things that could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:193:It was the oldest thing that he owned and the quietest, and it had never cost him so much. It went out from under his soles into a foot of moving water and a slab of grey, and what it brought back was dull, and late, like a voice through a wall; he had to lean the whole of his mind upon it to have anything at all. He shut his eyes. There was the plate, and under the plate a hollow, and in the hollow nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:The head lay at the ledge's lip beside his knee, half out of the water, quite still. He could have put his hand on it. It was the brown of the marsh, and smooth, and seamed down the middle where the two halves met; and it had no more face than the toe of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:223:The water took him to the waist, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it. He waded two strides, with the ground gone soft and sliding under him, and fell forward across the great slab that lay aslant in the middle. It was a hand under the surface. He got his right palm down upon it, and told it to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:227:"Out," said Lira's voice, cracking. "It's coming up. Cael, get *out*—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:229:He was already moving, since he had not yet begun to pay. He threw himself back across the two strides of deep water, and got his chest onto the ledge, and his knees; and behind him the whole of the hole stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:251:Lira did not look at the gap either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:267:"Span," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:283:"Three," said Lira. She was calling them for him, as a lean man had once called hers. "Low. It throws low."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:291:"They're on the sill," said Lira. "Both of them. She's got him sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:295:There was no point. The jaws shut on water, with a sound that he felt in his teeth, and the slab went over at last and slid down into the green.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:297:For as long as it takes to count five the thing lay as it was, heaped round a place where there was nothing; and in the clear water about the four grey feathers a darkness had begun to spread, like ink let into a basin. Then it loosened. The coils went slack along the ledge and drew off it, one after another, down into the hole; the pale went under, and the brown; and the head went last, slowly, without turning toward anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:301:"It's alive," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:305:They came off the wall on the side of the gap, one at a time, and did not stand. Seln had Cael's arm across his shoulders. Lira carried the roll, and Karis had her finished sheets held against her chest in both arms, with her face scraped raw along one cheek where the stone had had it. They went along the ledge, and over the sill, where the healer got up from her knees with Brom's weight on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:On the kerb, which was the hall's outer coping, Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:325:Nobody proposed going back for anything. They left it in its water, with four of a healer's bolts in it, and went out along the grey path in file, at one pace, as they had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:He did with it what he did with everything, which was to measure the thing and then live inside the measure. He drew water in elevens and carried in elevens, and he had begun to reckon the whole hold as so many of them from the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:23:Lira sat down at the top. She came down on the seat of her breeches, a rung at a time, with the bound foot held out in front of her like a lance, and at every rung she told the barn its number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:*Fifty-fourth day. The ladder. Brom, one hand. Karis, elbows. Lira, seated, counting aloud. Mine, by the arms; the calf gives eleven strides on the flat and no rungs at all. Seln, as usual. The nine: idle, and asking. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:39:So he stood. There was work for a large man in that yard at every hour of the day: sacks to shift and water to carry, and a gatepost that wanted lifting back into its socket. He was forbidden all of it. He stood by the barn door with his hands hanging and watched two of the hold's men take a post out of the ground between them, with a lever, in the time it would have taken him to spit on his palms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:63:Nobody at Thornwater was deciding what his leg meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:65:A woman came twice a day with warm water and unwound the linen. She looked at his calf, and said "Dry," or "Wet yet," and bound it up again, and went away to see to her geese. That was the whole of the attention it got, for he was a hired man with a bitten leg, and the leg belonged to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:67:He drew water, and sat on the well's coping when the count ran out, and kept the observation notebook, since there was a good deal to put in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:69:The hold had changed toward them, though no one made a speech of it, Thornwater making none about anything; he read it instead in small things. There were five bowls on the long board at supper, set down among the others, where on their first stay the crew had eaten at the end. A child of four came across the yard on the second morning, with great care, carrying an egg in both hands, and gave it to Lira, and ran. And the old man in the chimney corner, who had not woken for anything in Cael's hearing, opened one eye when Brom came in under the lintel and moved his feet to make room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:73:He had been told that the hold did not feed people for sitting, and he was the only one of the five who could stand on both feet and use both hands. So he carried for all of them, though he was not built for it. He went up the ladder with a sack of barley across his narrow shoulders and the expression of a man doing a long sum in his head, forty times in a morning; and at the end of each day he reported to nobody at all how many he had taken up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:77:"He had a rung go on the seventeenth," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:81:She had been set on an upturned trough by the barn wall, with her foot up on a sack. The woman had put her there on the first morning as she might have put down a bucket, and Lira had endured it for an hour. Then she had asked for a whetstone, and after the whetstone, for every blade in the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:83:By the third day there was not a knife in Thornwater that would not take the hair off an arm, and the woman had begun to hide her scissors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael had not known that he ever did. He walked across the yard in his slow dry way with a length of ash in his hand, peeled, as tall as his shoulder, with a fork left at the top of it for a palm to sit in, and he stood it against the trough where Lira's hand would find it. He kept his eyes off her foot; he lifted two fingers from his side, an inch, and let them fall, and went back up his ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:89:"I never asked for that," said Lira, to the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:119:"It's a library," said Lira, from her trough. She had been carried in to see it. "You've built a library in a barn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:They divided her between them. Cael turned leaves and fetched what she named, for he could stand eleven strides at a time and the boxes were within three. Brom, who was allowed to hold nothing, read out the tags on the bundles in his slow voice, and was corrected. Lira was given the pencil, which was a mistake, and everybody knew it by the second line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:151:Lira wrote it out, pressing hard enough to mark the leaf beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:153:In the middle of the morning Cael read her the finding. He stood at the end of the trestle with Hesk's book open at the one page, and read it from *Finding* to *five dead animals*, slowly enough for a pencil to follow; and Lira took it down into the field archive in her large angry hand. It was the first time a leaf of that book had been read aloud for copying. He turned no other page, and Karis sat with her face toward the barn door until he had shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:173:"It's a beginning. A start is what a horse does." But she had said it without heat, and after a moment she turned her head. "Lira. A clean leaf. Head it, *This side.* Capital T. Underline it once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:201:*Stillhound pack, Thornwater ford: cleared. Five confirmed. Crew of five, four injured, none lost. Price paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:205:"He's put it in," said Lira. She was on her stick by the trough. "*Four injured.* He's written that. For the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:233:*Fifty-seventh day. Thornwater. Fifth line, in Pike's hand, by the carter. Forty marks, in his box.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:245:Down in the yard Lira was going along the fence on her stick, by herself, slowly, in the last of the light. She stopped under the platform, and the bowman looked down, and she looked up, and neither of them lifted a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:255:"That's worse," said Lira. She took three more steps. "No, it isn't. I don't know what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:263:So on the fifty-eighth morning she sat on the tail-board of a cart in Thornwater's yard with the calfskin unrolled beside her. She could not hold its corners down, and so Brom had brought four stones from the well's foot, and set one on each, and stepped back with his arm in its sling, looking as pleased as if he had been allowed to carry something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:267:The grain was moving: three carts had come in by the east gate at first light, with mules bigger and darker than the Thornwater beast. The people with them were strangers to her, and they gave five bandaged persons in a yard no longer a glance than they gave the geese.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:273:Lira had never written anything of the kind.
5:He sat on the seventh step with his back against the wall and the bound leg thrust out across the slot. He had not fainted. His eyes were open, but they were looking at a strip of sky, and the hands that lay in his lap had fallen open like a pair of gloves. The stick had gone down two flights by itself. Lira fetched it, and laid it across his knees, and he did not take hold of it.
11:"Shoulder," said Lira. "I hit the wall with it. It wasn't the arm, and I want that written; I never put the arm to anything."
13:"The left," said Brom. "It's worse than the cart left it. And I've had the right round the pair of you, so that's said something to me as well." He lowered himself onto his box. "I'll be hearing the rest of it from two healers at once."
27:So that was the count, and Cael went over it as he would have gone over a cut on a moor. There had been six of them, with staves and a bow, in a place with no way out of it; and nobody was dead, and nobody had been carried off. One box had been dragged down two steps, and sat upon. Not one of the six had shown so much as a chin.
53:It was full dusk in the lane. The four alleys had no white in them. Each had, at its far end, the paleness that did not flicker, and it lay out along the cobbles in a bar. Cael went through all four with an old man's arm across his neck and a box dragging at his back, and he did not turn his head. It was not hard, tonight. He had too much on his shoulders to look at anything but the next sett; and a girl went by on his left each time, under two loads, with a tarred case knocking at her hip.
57:"He fell on your steps," said Karis. She had her left hand inside her coat. It was true, and she said it to the wall.
59:"I told you to mind him on them; they are older than he is." He made his stroke. "Go on. I shall not write the lateness against the card, this once, since I should not care to explain that card to anybody."
63:There was a house inside the second wall with a board at its door, and Lira spoke of it before the lantern was lit.
67:"They would," said Seln. He was on the ladder's head, where he had been when they came in under the arch. "It would be the best-mended hand in the district. And before any healer in that house laid a finger on it, a clerk would write a line on a sheet at a bed's foot: her name, and where the name is registered, and the tier; and the sheet goes to the counter at the first bell."
77:"I am not going to be written down for a hand," said Karis.
79:She said it quite calmly. She was as white as the wall, and she sat very upright, with her hurt hand held up for her and the other flat on a courier's flat.
81:"I have three days, and I have fourteen letters. If a paper is served on me tomorrow, at a registry's own yard door, then four people have carried fifty-two sheets up that hill for a week to no purpose, and a man below will never be told why I did not come down." She drew a breath. "It is my left. I write with the other."
87:He had got up off his box. He felt under its lashing with his right hand, and brought out a twist of cloth; and in the cloth there was a needle, as long as a finger and a little curved, and a hank of thread that had been drawn through wax. He had roped three boxes with those, at a ferry's shed and in an inn yard.
121:"I looked which hand it was, before I started," he said. "If it had been the one that draws, I'd not have touched it. I'd have carried you up to that board myself, and given them my own name to write beside yours."
137:She held the bowl to the old man's mouth, and he drank from its edge. He did it with his eyes on the far wall, and he thanked her after every mouthful, until she told him that she would stop if he did it again.
139:Brom had the linen off the knee. One of the two laths had cracked along its length. He took a slat from the lid of his own box, and split it with Lira's knife, and bound the joint again as he had seen a healer do it, turn upon turn.
145:Nobody answered him. Cael had heard those words given, on a frozen road, by a square woman with her sleeves pushed up: that a man who stood a wall on that leg inside the month would keep the stick for the rest of his days. It was the eleventh day of thirty.
147:He went and sat down on his own pallet, at last, with his back against the third box.
153:The lines were where they had always been, and nobody under those slates could see them but himself. He took them as he had taken the last, on a healer's plank: slowly, from the top, putting each into the plain words in which he would have to write it.
171:He had it, then. It sat under his breastbone with the rest, as real as any of them, and as quiet as a gatepost in a field at night.
183:The lantern had been turned low. Brom was down, on his back, and Karis lay by the boxes with her bound hand on her chest. Lira had gone to her own pallet; and Seln sat on the ladder's head, where he could hear the yard, and might have heard anything else besides.
191:"The first is the knee. I was forbidden a wall on it, by the one person on this side of the Line who had the right to forbid me, and I stood one. I did not forget her; I had her in my mind as the stick came down, word for word." He shut his eyes. "I do not ask that it be excused. I ask only that she be told that I heard her."
205:"I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered somewhere, since I'm not the one who keeps the ledger any more."
215:It was entered. He had not lied to the man by the width of a hair, since it was written already in the only place where such a thing was ever written first; and there was more in the entry than the man had asked for, or could have guessed.
223:This man knew nothing of it. He had not asked on a bank what read a still place, and he had not asked tonight what six people came to see. To say *I have your wall in me* was to say how it had got there, and how it got there was the one thing that Cael did not own alone. Five others held it. They had kept it out of every book for longer than he could say, and they had kept it above all from the office that lay on that straw with its hands open.
249:"I have a finding," he said. "I have looked at it by a lantern, and I shall look again by day; but I do not think that it will alter."
257:"Because a Path that is opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it; and they took the same care that we did to have nothing of theirs entered. They wished to have something of ours." His eyes went to Cael, and away. "The bow was to make one of you open, and the light was to tell somebody below that it had been done. When the light could not be shown, they had no reason to stay, and they went. That is their limit: they cannot be written down in this town any more than we can."
261:"And the box?" said Brom, from the dark. "One of them had his fist through my becket. He never looked at me."
277:Cael thought about it. He had six people in a loft, of whom one could not stand, and another had seven stitches in her hand; and there was not a second house under that wall which would take them without a name. There were three days of a week, and fifty-two sheets on three tables, and a small pale woman who would go down to them tomorrow if she had to be carried.
285:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
287:*Finding, S.'s: they cannot be written here either. They wanted one of us to open, and a light shown for it, and a box; they got a wall and a rock, and they left when the light failed.*
295:*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. Since my Kindling I've learned to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*
299:*Not told. I can't tell the taking without telling the rest, and the rest is six people's. He asked for the wall to be entered, and I said that it was.*
