    93	He had passed it three days ago, under a keg. He had not needed to turn his head. He knew how it would be: a slot of light, and a night clerk behind it, half asleep, with a book. The courier lays his packet on the ledge. The clerk enters it by its seal and its weight, and tears off a slip. Neither is permitted to ask the other a question. It is the oldest rule in the service, and the only one that he had ever wholly respected. A registry enters what is laid on its ledge. It may bury the thing afterward, or burn the copy, or hold it for six years in a drawer where nobody will receive it; but for the length of one line, in one book, on one night, the thing has been set down as having come.
    94	
    95	He could be at that hatch in a quarter of an hour, if he chose to show a keeper what he was.
    96	
    97	He considered it with care, since he distrusted himself on the subject.
    98	
    99	A line would be written. *One case, locked. By hand.* It would go up in the morning to a counter. Somebody would open it there, with a key or without one. He could not see past that. He had been able to see past it once; he had known the names of the men who read that sort of return, and what each of them wanted, and which would carry it up a stair and which would carry it down. An old officer had stood in a hayloft four days ago and said that he no longer knew who read this town's books at night. If that man did not know, a copying clerk could hardly pretend to.
   100	
   101	There were four names, besides, which must not be written inside a wall. He had counted an hour for them, on a ladder. What was in the case had those names nowhere in it, and it led to them all the same, as a road leads to a house.
   102	
   103	So it was not that door. He had known it before he sat down. He had come up here to learn whether he still wished that it were, and he found that he could not tell.
   104	
   105	There was a fourth thing which he had not thought of at the spring, since it had not then existed.

