editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:It was smaller than Cael remembered from the sill. Dark leather, scuffed at the corners, a brass lock and nothing else. No mark on the outside. There was a stamp inside the lid from the man who made it, Seln had said once, that nobody had ever seen but him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:188:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:185:"Two of the second-years came to see me last night." Ephram undid the tape. "They have a list. Every meal of the term, two names against it, and a table chosen from which the pair of them can study how you hold a spoon. They wanted it countersigned by the captain, as a cohort activity."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:173:Brom nodded. "Open it. I've held the pads inside the rates as long as I've known you. I'd like to see what I've been holding them for." The corner of his mouth went in. "So would you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:329:Brom put down his spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:"There's a place tonight. The reading room, after supper. Karis has said yes and Brom's bringing the kettle and I've asked the kitchen for a honey cake, and they said yes because I didn't tell them who it was for." She picked up her spoon at last. "And I've asked Ephram."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:157:Brom put his spoon down and looked at him, long and slow, and Seln ate through it without appearing to feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:51:"He was in the hall from the first bell," Seln said. "Not with the officer. On his own, on the south benches, three rows back, with a sausage roll. He watched the Copper draw and the Iron first round like a man who'd paid for a seat. He watched the exhibition." He turned his spoon over on the table. "At the second touch for the confluence he got up and went out by the street door. Nineteen minutes later he came back in behind the officer, carrying nothing, and they took fifty-one seconds from the street door to our bench. I counted. So did half the hall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:61:"They waited for the hall." Seln laid the spoon down square. "The clerk's errand was to tell the officer when the room was at its warmest. He judged it well. I'd have gone at the same touch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:243:Cael could not lift his voice, and what he had came out of him in pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:77:She showed it to Brom at supper, because it was his date, and he had a right to it before anybody else made anything of it. He read the slip with his spoon in his other hand and gave it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:73:Cael had seen the man before without knowing whose he was. He was small and spare, a head shorter than the steading's woman, with a bald brown crown and flour to both elbows; and in all his days at that hold Cael had never heard him say a word, since he had never been near enough to the hearth. Thornwater, it appeared, kept its talk in one place. The woman at the gate counted people with her lips and spent a word on every six of them, and the man on the platform had said perhaps forty in a hundred days. Here was the hold's whole share of speech, walking beside Brom with a wooden spoon in his belt and getting through it at a great rate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:131:The hold had eaten. Its fourteen had drawn off to the far end of that low room, to their mending and their dice and an old man asleep in the chimney corner, and they did not stare; but Cael felt himself counted from under a good many eyelids. The board had been scrubbed. Six bowls stood on it, and six horn spoons, and a round of bread that was black on both faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:171:"There is a third verse, at Ternhall, in the old tongue." Karis had put down her spoon. "Nobody knows what it means. I can't sing, so I shall say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:183:It was Lira who put her spoon down, when the bowls were half empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:151:He set the stock down on the rail of the walk. There was a place on that rail worn pale and hollow, where his own left hand had rested for thirty years, and the stock lay in it as an egg lies in a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:65:There was the water, of which they had five flasks among six, since the healer had come over a stone at a run with nothing in her hands; and Brom, who could not lift his right arm, had them all set down in front of him in the dark and shook each of them at his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:315:"A week before you hold a spoon. Longer for that." She nodded at the notebook's corner, which showed at the breast of Karis's coat, and Karis looked as though she had been sentenced by a court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:187:There was *silencing*, which she held longest. It was the right sort of word; it was made out of a verb, and a verb was what she had seen. But it claimed too much. To silence a thing was to stop its mouth, and she had copied a great many pages on what an Arbiter was, in a room where the pages were counted before she left it, and not one of them said that it spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:187:"I count days. It's the only thing I do count." She got up off the plank by pushing on her knees. "I eat now, and I'm on my back two hours, and then there's a yard of people. You can tell me where you're going while I've a bowl in my hand. Five don't walk up this valley in the cold to keep an appointment about three ribs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:193:Oryn ate, and heard him out, and scraped the bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:He stood at the long house door with an empty bowl on the sill beside him. She had a round of seven gates, and she was late at every one of them already, and nobody had asked this of her; and she had just spent five days of it on a road that every carter she had ever mended told her to keep off. He had wanted to say the sentence for nine days. He was glad now, with his whole heart, that he had left it for her, since from him it would have been a request, and from her it was a decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:229:That was no longer new to him, and he did not count her lips; he watched the yard instead, where nobody stood about. He had seen a healing house's corridor once, from a bed, and it had been full of people waiting to be told their turn by tier. Here the five whom she had passed over got up from the bench without complaint and went back to what they had been doing, and the woman of the hold set a lamp on the back room's sill, unlit, and a bowl beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:115:She did it a mouthful at a time, with her chin down and a look of sharp inquiry, as she would have tried a new pen. Across the front of her throat, where there had been a mark the colour of a plum at first light, there was something yellow and old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:131:Shorter than the throat; less than half, by the floor. The fingers, when she opened her hands, had four dark lines across the pads where there had been four open mouths, and Brom closed the hand and opened it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:79:He stopped there, with his mouth a little open, as a man stops on a stair to feel for the next tread. Cael knew what the next one was, for he had heard it on a river bank at dusk, from a man with a band of leather on his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:267:"He counts what he hasn't done," she said. "That's all that is. Two hundred times he didn't. You'd have gone round it the first time, and the second, and you'd have written both down. And the third time you'd have stood there with your book shut, working out what it would cost to look." She scuffed the great boot along the shadow's edge. "I've watched you price things since a lane in Ardenmere. You've said *not yet* to me, and *not here*, and *no* a hundred times. You don't say *never*. I don't think you know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:17:Karis had the sleeve. She had taken charge of it at noon, when Brom was told what he might not do, and she slid the bolt in beside the rest and showed them the mouth of the leather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:65:It did not hurt. That was the stupid part. She had been bitten to the bone at dusk and by midnight she had a forearm with eight small pink marks on it, as neat as stitching, and when she moved the fingers they moved. It was a perfectly good arm. It was not hers yet. It belonged to a square woman who was asleep six feet away with her mouth open, and it would go on belonging to her until there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:105:So he had known on that floor, with her arm in its mouth, and he had held its head and let Brom shoot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:247:She opened her mouth then, and Cael saw her throat work. It was the first time that she had tried since a lane. What came out was a small rough noise with the shape of a word in it, like a hinge that wants oil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:251:Karis shut her mouth, and wrote *Tomorrow*, and underlined it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:25:"It can. The arm can't." Her mouth was hard. "So keep that bow up, and somebody look the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:109:She said it as Brom had once said the hour of a week, to have it exact. Then her eyes went down the length of herself to the great boot, which lay on its side in the grass with the foot still in it, and came up again; and she shut her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:119:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:121:The hiss of the seed-heads against his coat stopped. That was how it came: the world went on, and he went out of it. His boots came down on trodden grass and gave him nothing back; he could not hear his own breath. He went wide of the lane on the south side, through stems that stood to his hip and bent for him and did not rustle, past the box on Brom's back and Brom's left shoulder. Brom's eye came round and slid over the place where he was, as an eye slides over one fence-post among forty, and then came back to it, since Brom knew. He passed Lira on her left hand, a yard out. Her head turned. She was looking at grass that leaned with nobody in it, and he saw her mouth open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:141:Karis lay with her fists under her chin and her mouth open. No sound was coming out of it. Her eyes were on him, and they were quite clear. They went to his hands, and to the place ten strides back where he had been standing, and returned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:188:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:238:Oryn looked at him for as long as it takes to draw one breath, and he could not tell what was in her face. It was not fear. It had never been that. Then the corner of her mouth went down, a very little, as it went when a patient on her table began to sit up before she had done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:69:The grey of the yard came in, and the cold with it. Brom was sitting on the bench under the window, alone, with his hands on his knees. He had come down the ladder before it was light, Cael understood, and had put himself where a person could be sent from; he was on his feet before she had opened her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:225:That was the difference, and there was no other. Every mending of her life had been given, by a woman who knew the price and said yes. This had been taken off her while her hands were full, by a boy who had thought the price might be high and had kept his mouth shut. Her purse weighed the same. It was the asking that he had had from her; and she, who asked the dying before she laid a finger on them, knew to a hair what an asking was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:245:She could have wept, and she did nothing of the kind. She held a field-hand's leg for the better part of an hour, and paid for it at the rate that such a thing costs, and when she took her hands away there was a pink seam on his shin where a wet red mouth had been since the barley was cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:275:He had done as he was told. He had put his shirt on and crossed the yard, and the steading's woman had met him at the long house door with a bowl before he could ask for one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:279:He ate it standing, and tasted none of it. Nobody came to him. Brom looked across once from the bench under the window, and got up, and climbed the ladder to the loft. On the platform by the western gate a lean man was watching the valley, as he did at every hour of every day. Cael had been left alone with a bowl by four people who knew when a person was not fit to be spoken to; and when the bowl was empty he went to break the ice for the geese, since it wanted doing, and since it could be done by somebody who was not thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:151:Then she opened her mouth, and he saw what it cost her. The word came out cracked across the middle, and it was the third that she had spoken since a lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:173:"I'm not to make one come. That's the first rule, and it's kept; I never put this in her mouth. She said it herself, at a ford, before I had so much as thought of it. *When you're not broken, I run the long one.* She put a day on her route for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:183:Under the lip there was a hollow. It ran back beneath the floor farther than Cael could see, low and dark, with a mouth as long as a cart and as high as his thigh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:185:He went over what lay before that mouth a piece at a time, as he went over everything. The prints lay one upon another, hundreds deep, so that it was only at the margins that he could tell a single foot. To one side there was a heap of bones, deer for the most part, cracked and grey. There was scat, old and new. Tufts of coarse hair had caught on the underside of the stone lip, at the height of a low grey back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:187:And just within the mouth, where the daylight still reached, there was a place in the bare earth the length of a hound, scooped and worn as smooth as the seat of an old chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:195:"This morning," he said, very low. "Before we had laid the first cord. More than six. I should say eight, or nine; they have gone over each other's marks and I cannot do better." He pointed, without lifting his hand from his knee, at the newest prints. They led away from the mouth, down the slope, to the east. "Out. All of them, and none back since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:"I looked at it at noon and left it lying. I'd come to find out about my hands, and I didn't want anything in them." Her mouth went hard. "That was a healer's reason. Six bolts, in a sleeve on the stock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:193:At noon it had bought her a step. It bought her a yard of floor now, sliding, with the animal's forefeet sliding after her and her arm in its mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:321:Then she looked at Cael. In that light her face was all planes, and there was a thing in it that he had not seen there at noon, when a count stopped in her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:It went slowly at first. Lira walked with her arms a little out from her sides and a look of deep concentration, like a person carrying a bowl filled to the brim; twice in the first cord she said "Out," sharply, and stepped to her right. Karis felt her way along beside her. At the cord's end she stopped, and shuffled a hand's breadth inward, and a hand's breadth out, and stood still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:37:They came to the fork an hour out. The valley divided there round a spur of hill, and one road went away up the left-hand water toward a smoke that he could just see. The right-hand branch climbed to a low place in the skyline and went over. It was a road like any other, two pale ruts with a ridge of grass between. There was nothing at its mouth to say what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:123:"One more night of that," said Seln, feeding it. "I should not light one tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:201:It stopped. It did not trail away; it stopped as a word stops when a door is shut on the speaker. She stood a stride inside the line with Brom's forearm between her palms, and her mouth was a little open, and there was no count in it.

