editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:221:He sat and picked up the pencil and put it in his right pocket, and the chalk in his left, and the slip in his coat. Lira watched him do it, checking that he had learned the order. Brom came from the hatch with his barley and sat down across from them, and a moment after him Karis came in from the archive passage, dust on her cuffs, and took the fourth bowl and did not eat from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:11:Karis was not at breakfast. Her chair at the end of the table stood pushed in, and her cup was gone from the rack, which meant she had been down before the bell and taken it up with her. Lira sat across from his place with her elbows either side of a bowl of porridge she was not eating. Brom ate for two, steadily, as if somebody had told him he would need it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:"She says she slept." Lira pushed the bowl an inch away. "She also says the floor in her room has a slope to the west, and she's going to have it measured. So no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:She had gone to Ephram. Ephram, as captain, still held the meal rota the two second-years had drafted on the first night, the one with a column of names and a table chosen for watching how Cael held a spoon. Lira asked him for it. She took it to the common room, crossed out the heading, and wrote a new one above it in her large capitals. *RECORDS HALL STEPS. ONE PAIR AT A TIME. NOBODY SITS. NOBODY ASKS. ANYBODY ELSE IS SENT TO THE FRAMES.* Then she gave it back to Ephram to sign, and he signed it, and pinned it up on the covered walk beside the house's one-line answer from the day before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:It went slowly at first. Lira walked with her arms a little out from her sides and a look of deep concentration, like a person carrying a bowl filled to the brim; twice in the first cord she said "Out," sharply, and stepped to her right. Karis felt her way along beside her. At the cord's end she stopped, and shuffled a hand's breadth inward, and a hand's breadth out, and stood still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:She wanted to know about everything. She asked about the coast house's coach and the green wagons with the onions, and the flag at the crossroads, and the walled yard. When Lira told her about the corner game, she put down her spoon and listened all the way through, and asked two questions at the end that showed she had understood every step of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:109:"She's very quick," said Lira, with her mouth full. "I'd forgotten that about her. Watch your bowl, Brom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:181:Lira had the stories at supper, all six of them, and she counted them out at the scarred table between the four bowls on her fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:Lira had eaten for both of them. She sat across the table with her staff against her chair and worked through two bowls and a heel of bread in a steady businesslike way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:125:He showed the page to Lira at supper, folded back so that only Havel's lines showed, and she read it with her spoon in her other hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:69:The hold had changed toward them, though no one made a speech of it, Thornwater making none about anything; he read it instead in small things. There were five bowls on the long board at supper, set down among the others, where on their first stay the crew had eaten at the end. A child of four came across the yard on the second morning, with great care, carrying an egg in both hands, and gave it to Lira, and ran. And the old man in the chimney corner, who had not woken for anything in Cael's hearing, opened one eye when Brom came in under the lintel and moved his feet to make room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:303:Torvin's yard door was propped open with a kitchen chair. Torvin was at the head of the table with his bowl, and when they came in by the yard door, walking, he looked up. He looked at Cael's arms, and at Lira, and then, for a longer time, at Cael's legs, which were carrying him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:163:It was Brom who began the song. He had a great deal of voice and no notion where to put it, and he got through the first line by himself. Lira came in on the second with the words that they used at Fenmark, which were not Brom's words, and the two of them stopped and looked at each other across a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:183:It was Lira who put her spoon down, when the bowls were half empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:207:He found her at the scarred table at supper with the flat box beside her bowl and a copy of the notice in front of her. Lira had taken it off the main board with the tacks still in it, and would put it back before the eighth bell. Lira and Brom sat on either side of her, and nobody was eating very much.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:Lira was quiet a moment longer, and then she reached across, picked up Karis's spoon from beside the untouched bowl, and put it into her hand, and closed Karis's fingers round it with her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:It was the ninth morning after the stair, and the deferral had run out the evening before, and the three clean days Lira had demanded had come and gone without a flicker. He was in the refectory queue at the second bell with a bowl in his hand, waiting behind two Stone girls who were arguing about a boy, when a Mire second-year came down the line from the kitchen hatch with a tray loaded to the edges with somebody else's breakfast as well as his own. The second-year was looking for a space at the end of the long table. He found one. He set the tray down on it, briskly, and the corner of the tray came down on Cael's forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:215:"Everybody would like to see it," said Ephram, and went on toward the door with his bowl, and said over his shoulder, without breaking step, "It's leather. Double-stitched. It's a very good handle," which was, Lira said afterward, more words than he had spent on any object since she had known him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:13:Lira was at the long table in the refectory with her strapped foot up on the bench and a bowl in front of her that she was not eating. She looked at his face as he lowered himself onto the bench across from her, and nodded, and pushed the bowl an inch further away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:11:He heard himself say it, and looked up. Lira, beside him, had stopped with her spoon in the air, and Karis, at the fourth bowl, had her cup lifted and was not drinking from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:223:Cael took it to the hearth. There were not a dozen people in that long room so early. Brom was laying out the bowls at their end of it, as he had at every halt, and Lira sat on the hearthstone with her right hand full of kindling. Karis had come out of the room behind the chimney with the hide under her arm, and Seln stood at the far end of the fire, by the small window that looked down on the landing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:253:She had not written it. She said it once, to the board, in the small exact voice; and nobody at that fire answered her. Cael counted four seconds by the tick of the wood. Lira put a stick on. Brom was looking at the sixth bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:257:Lira, one-armed, feeding the fire: "Then we follow it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:53:"Fifteen years of being sent to places. You don't do that without walking between them." Brom turned his spoon over. "I've done one road in my life that wasn't in a coach, and I did it badly, and I had Lira's blisters to carry as well as my own. I'd like to do the next one better. Whenever it is, and wherever." He kept his eyes on the spoon. "Can it be taught?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:203:Lira put her spoon down. "Asked who what?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:113:Lira put her spoon down. "That's nothing. A man's been calling the provision a mistake since the first week, in a faculty session, with the clerk writing it down, and he says *noted* to a board on his way past?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:"Me. Him." Lira pointed her spoon at Cael. "And Brom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:25:"I asked him." Lira was mending a strap and kept her eyes on it. "He's eaten yesterday's bread on that wall every day for a month, and he's paid a week ahead at the carters' inn, and he's never once in your hearing said anything about anybody feeding him." She bit off the thread. "And you sit with him every morning and come home with your arms blue and you've stopped telling me what he says unless I ask. So I'm going to sit across a table from him and find out for myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:53:So he laid the little table from the landing, in Lira's room, with the two plates and the two bowls the house would lend and a third plate of his own that did not match. He put the three cups where they would go and moved the lamp twice, and found he was more nervous about it than he had been about the Shield, and could not have said why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:89:It was a good supper; Cael would think so afterward, and he thought so at the time, which was rarer. The barley was soft and the mutton was not, quite, and the dark onions tasted, as Lira had promised, of something, though it was not clear what. Brom ate two bowls of it without a word and then a third with several, all of them in praise. He said it was the first hot meal he had eaten sitting at a table since he left the river city, and he said it as a fact, without asking anybody to be sorry. Lira went pink and said that it was mostly onions. They balanced the bowls on their knees because the little table would not take the bowls and the pan and the jar at once, and the jar would not leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:91:After the second bowl Brom sat back on the trunk Lira had given him to sit on, with his cup in his hands, and looked round the room again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:219:Nobody mentioned the boxes at the noon meal. Karis sat with a volume open beside her bowl and read it while she ate. Lira, across from her, described at length a thing Rooke had said to the Force about his knees. Brom ate for three, and Seln was at the staff table with the desk clerk, turning his cup. Each of them, Cael was quite sure, knew what stood on the deposit shelf at the north end of the records hall, and how many, and whose knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:Lira did not like to eat at Torvin's. She said it was because Torvin's wife looked at her as though she might steal a spoon, which was not true, and Cael suspected the real reason was that a table full of boarders was a table full of people who would ask her things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-22.md:21:Lira did not interrupt. She ate while he talked, slowly, and when he got to the old man she stopped eating and set her spoon down in the bowl, carefully.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:165:Cael understood what he was watching about four steps in, because Lira had once drawn it for him on the back of a feed bill at Ardenmere, late at night, when she could not sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:325:Lira had a bowl of stew in front of her and was eating it steadily, without looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:341:"That's Fenmark," said Lira. "That's it exactly. You could put that over their door." She finished her stew and pushed the bowl away. "The boy was all right, though. He laughed when I told him. He didn't have to laugh. He had a whole hall of his own people watching him lose to the girl they threw out, and he laughed." She considered it. "I'd have liked him at fourteen, I think. I hope somebody up there is kind to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:121:The reserves were pressed into service as Zerin's furniture. The Shield reserve held one end of the counting-string and the Stone reserve the other, and they stood at the chalk marks like a pair of gateposts while Lira ran between them. Brom kept the count on the bench with a spoon against his mug, because he said it was the only instrument in the house he trusted, and Gault, passing through, stopped to watch and went away again looking pained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:135:Lira picked it up and went through it slowly, her thumb moving down the margin, which was how she read anything she had not yet decided to believe. At the underlined line her thumb stopped. Then she set the sheet down very carefully beside her bowl and kept her hand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:It was dull work, and it was the hardest of the day. Nobody did anything a broadside could print. Ephram let a platform go a beat before Rhagen arrived to take it, and took another a beat before Rhagen thought to defend it, and the marshals' quarter-minutes ticked into Halcenvane's column while nobody in the bowl could have said why. Lira kept the west crossing and spent nothing she could keep. Karis moved her frame twice, and each time she planted again she paid the heat for it in her palms without looking down. Brom stood two strides wide of whatever mattered, and things that mattered kept walking into him. And Cael held the whole floor in his head at the rates the record knew, the same as he had held the trial against Auremont, and did not let himself feel what it cost until it was over. There was one bout left for him after this. He would spend nothing early.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:171:The heavyset man's wife began leaving a covered bowl on the range for him at night, without being asked and without saying so. He found it there after the evening hour with Lira, still warm, with a plate over it. On the second night the sister had written on the plate in chalk, *EAT, L SAYS*, and drawn a hen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:147:He could not do it. In the shut hall with Lira he had put her down like a parcel and listened past her, and there had been nothing past her but boards and quiet. Here there was a bowl of eight thousand people breathing. There was the referee's silver bar flashing at the edge of his eye, and a woman at the west barrier who mattered more than anything he had stood in front of. He reached for the air and every one of them came with it. The whole bowl was loud in his head, and she was the loudest thing in it, and he could no more put her down than he could have put down the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:53:Lira looked at her, and then across the table at Cael, who had a spoon halfway to his mouth. He watched her work it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:He had not meant to say it aloud. He had been sitting on the low wall outside the stable door since the third bell with his knees drawn up and his notebook shut, because Karis had told him the coach was coming and he had found he did not want to see it through glass. Lira had come out with her bread and sat down on his right without asking why. Brom had come after her with his barley in a bowl and sat on his left, and the three of them had watched the coach come in at the gate like people at the rail watching a bout they had not been asked to judge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:27:In the morning he carried the Log down to breakfast, open at the entry, and set it beside Lira's bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:"It holds me up." Lira lowered herself onto the bench beside Cael with the leading leg out straight, and set her bowl in front of her, and did not look at anybody. "It isn't happy about it. It holds me up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:189:At the far end, where an old hitching post stood up out of the turf, Brom was turning nothing at all, as slowly as he knew how. Rooke had given him the post two days before, and the instruction with it, and the instruction was simple and cruel. Brom was to turn nothing until he was certain it was real. He was to stand at the post, and Karis or Lira or Cael would come at him with feeds, honest ones and empty ones mixed, and he was to let every empty one go by without so much as a twitch, and turn only the honest ones. Everything he had built, it ran backward. Tonight it was Lira feeding, from in front of the post, her hands flat, and every third feed she sold was empty, and Brom turned on two of every three of them anyway, and groaned at himself, and went back to the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:199:Lira gave Brom one more empty feed, watched him fail to let it go, and then came and leaned beside Cael on the coping with her eyes on the water. Down at the ferry landing the two grey coats were standing under their lamp, and she watched them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:71:He told the others at supper about the recess sheet, in one sentence, as a fact about the wing's rota. Lira put her spoon down for a moment and picked it up again. Brom looked at his bowl. Karis wrote nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:177:Lira considered that the way she considered a feed she had decided to let go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:The replies began coming up the hill in week seven. The first, from a house on the coast, said in a page and a half that it held no such compilation and wished her well in her studies. The second, from an academy in the north, said the clause was obsolete and that it knew of no academy that had used it in living memory, and Karis wrote *obsolete is not repealed* in the margin in pencil, very small. The third and the fourth said nothing useful at some length. The fifth came from a provost's secretary, and Karis read it aloud at supper because it was beautifully written and said nothing at all, and Lira laughed until she had to put her spoon down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:239:Lira read the sheet on her way past with a bowl of something in each hand. She read it the way she read the standings, from the bottom up, and stopped at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:29:Her table was the landing table, in her room at the boarding house, the one with a short leg that she had wedged with a folded bill from the paper stall. They sat round it with the pot in the middle and the lamp beside it and the door to the landing propped open, because three people and a lamp in Lira's room was too warm for anybody with their coat on. The heavyset man's wife came up the stair while they were spooning out the stew and put a fourth bowl on the landing without a word, as if a fourth bowl were simply what happened now. She went down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:Cael could not hold a spoon in either hand. Lira held it for him, as she had the night before, without any comment at all this time, between her own mouthfuls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:95:"Not the name," said Brom. "Anybody can have a name. Whether they want the house. Whether they want a letter from your father, or your father's friend, or the station that did your reading, saying who you are and what you're for." He had his big hands flat on the table, one on each side of the empty bowl, as Lira had held hers. "Because if they do, I'll not go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:219:She did not say anything more, and Lira did not ask, and Gerda finished her bowl and carried it to the hatch and left without looking back. Lira watched her go with an expression Cael had seen on her only when she was looking at someone across a ring whom she had begun to respect.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:47:Karis had the marbled book open beside her bowl. When Lira sat down, Karis reached under her chair and brought up the two practice batons, still tied together in the middle with Lira's own hip bandage, in the ugly knot Lira had pulled tight nine days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:99:At breakfast he read it to the other three over the porridge, in a low voice, because the procedure said within the day and it was within the day. Lira listened with her spoon still. Brom pushed half his own bread across the table and went on eating, and Cael ate it, and found he was very hungry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:Lira considered that, and him, and nodded once, the nod she gave a feed she had decided to let go by. "Data first," she said. "I know. I've met you." Then she went back to the coats, and did not ask again, which from Lira was a kind of gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:147:At first he could not get to the scarred table at all. The whole of the stable seemed to want to stand near him without speaking to him, and the second-years kept coming past the end of the table and stopping and opening their mouths and going away again. The tout sent a heel of bread down the table with his compliments, and somebody from the movement floor whom Cael had never spoken to clapped him on the back so hard that his bowl jumped. In the end Lira stood up from the bench, turned round, and looked at the room. It did not quite go quiet, but it remembered all at once that it had business at the other end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:143:They did not talk about the match. They talked about the honey thief, who had been caught at last by the cook with a spoon in his hand and turned out to be a third-year from the Edge floor, which Lira said explained a great deal about the Edge floor. They talked about Wray's lecture, and the tout's book, and the rude baker, who had put the price of a loaf up by a copper and been reported by Lira to nobody in particular. It was the most ordinary supper they had had in a month, and Cael sat in the middle of it with his barley and understood after a while that it was ordinary on purpose, that all four of them were making it so, and that nobody had needed to agree to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:11:Lira put her spoon down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:85:Gerda came to the scarred table at the midday meal on Saturday, because Lira brought her. She sat at the end of the bench with her bowl in front of her and ate with the careful economy of somebody who had once been very hungry for a long time and had decided never to waste anything again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:227:Nobody at the table moved. In the grate the coals ticked as they cooled. Brom's spoon had come to rest against the rim of his bowl at a tilt that would have tipped it in, if Brom had been minding anything but Karis. In the corner, Lira's finger still marked a page she had stopped reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:235:"It's dead," said Lira, sitting down at the scarred table with her bowl and his bread. "The story. *The observer made fire.* You killed it this morning. I watched it die on the benches." She tore the bread. "Nobody can tell it any more, because now it's on the record, and the record's the dullest place a story can go. Three little black marks on a post. A size. A count. The second-years were bored by the time Hobb fetched the sand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:365:He put out the lamp. In the morning there would be four bowls at the end of the scarred table, and Lira would already have taken his bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:131:Karis came to the scarred table late at supper on the sixth day, before he went back down to the archive for the night, with dust to the elbows of her coat and a smear of something grey along one cheekbone that she had plainly not noticed. She sat down at the fourth bowl and ate her barley in silence, and Lira slid half a heel of bread down the table to her without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:"Herons are very good at standing still," said Lira, sitting down beside him with her own bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-15.md:147:Lira put her spoon down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-15.md:161:Lira picked up her spoon again and looked at her soup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-55.md:33:"I didn't say anything," said Lira. "I haven't said anything to her in two days." She picked it up. "Come on. I'll hold the spoon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Lira looked at the bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-04.md:203:"Then I've got two," said Lira. "And a question." She put a plate in front of him and stood over him with the spoon, which made the question feel like a third condition. "Where do I hit you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-41.md:101:Lira pushed her bowl across to him without a word, and Brom took it without a word, and Cael sat back in his chair with the lamp in his eyes and the cordial sweet on his tongue and listened to the two of them begin to argue about whether pear cordial counted as a drink. It was an argument nobody could win, and they were both enjoying it. He did not say anything. He did not think about the third exchange or the knock or Keth's face on the stone. While the stew lasted it was only an evening, in a room with two people in it who had seen him do a thing and had stayed to eat afterward, and he let it be that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-20.md:241:Lira had not come to Torvin's table; she had a sparring hour across the district with somebody she would not name. So he had eaten his soup at Torvin's end, beside the ledger, while Yeni's needle went in and out at the other, and the woman in the boots told the brothers a long story about a goat that seemed to have no middle and that they laughed at in all the wrong places. He had only noticed, halfway down the bowl, that the food sat lighter in him than any meal had since Weaver's Row, and spent the rest of it trying to see why.

