   145	Nobody answered him. Cael had heard those words given, on a frozen road, by a square woman with her sleeves pushed up: that a man who stood a wall on that leg inside the month would keep the stick for the rest of his days. It was the eleventh day of thirty.
   146	
   147	He went and sat down on his own pallet, at last, with his back against the third box.
   148	
   149	It had been waiting for him since the stair. He had felt it there through all that came after, through a lane and two gates and seven stitches, standing in the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, as each of the ten had stood, as patient as a clerk. He had not looked. He had had an old man's arm across his neck.
   150	
   151	He looked at it now, in a loft that smelled of hay and horse and a pot of barley.
   152	
   153	The lines were where they had always been, and nobody under those slates could see them but himself. He took them as he had taken the last, on a healer's plank: slowly, from the top, putting each into the plain words in which he would have to write it.
   154	
   155	```
   156	Iron-Wall-adjacent. Vastin. Partial. Iron family. Held. A plane, not a body: the ground told not to give. Distinct from the third — Iron-adjacent, the bed; this is not the bed hardened, this is a line drawn on it and kept. Engagement field: defensive; rooted. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Eleventh.
   157	```
   158	
   159	Eleven.
   160	
   161	He went over what was new in it, since he could not have helped doing so.
   162	
   163	It was of a family that he had already. The third had come to him from a big man, a long while ago, and it read the ground under his feet; and he had half expected this to be the same thing made harder. It was not. The millstone told him what a floor weighed. This told a floor where it was to stop.
   164	
   165	It had a word in it that he had not met, and the word was *held*. The ten had each been named for what they did, since they read, and burst, and burned, and bound; this one was named for what it did not do.
   166	
   167	Its field was a new one as well. He had nine that had come out of a fight, and their fields said so. He had one that a healer had given him, lying down, and that said *clinical*. Here was a thing that had come out of a fight in which the person who owned it had struck nobody. A man had put his heels on a line and been hit, and the lines had found a name for that, and it was two words long.
   168	
   169	And it had no tier. Every one of the others had been weighed, Iron or Bronze or heavier; but an old man had told a factor on a moor that this Path had a rank beside it on a roll which would mean nothing on that road, and it seemed that it meant nothing here.
   170	
   171	He had it, then. It sat under his breastbone with the rest, as real as any of them, and as quiet as a gatepost in a field at night.
   172	
   173	*This far?* it said, of the ladder's head; and of the boards between his boots; and of the door of the stable underneath.
   174	
   175	He told it no, and thought that he would be telling it so for a long while.

