    71	## New canon minted
    72	
    73	All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.
    74	
    75	- **The Deepstacks' ground.** Inside the iron door a bar of black iron as long as a man's arm, shot from within. A stair of the dark dry-laid stone, **twenty-seven steps in three flights of nine**, with two landings, low-roofed, unworn. At its foot a cross-way a dozen strides long, lined with the dark stone; off it **three galleries side by side**, each as broad as a cart, cut in the bluff's pale bedded rock with a pick. **The middle gallery** is shelved to the roof in bays divided by uprights of black oak, about a hundred and ten strides long; nothing on its shelves has a ticket (some bundles have tags on their under sides). From ninety strides the old town's sorting tables stand end to end down the middle; at the end, against the cut rock, a high sloped desk of black oak with a lamp in a glass chimney on an iron arm, and a shelf above it (a cup, a jar of water, a thick bundle tied both ways in brown tape, and the board in sacking). **The eastern gallery** holds the old town's ledgers; its tall books stand as high as the roof and are reached by ladders. **The western gallery** is the quarry's own: the board lay on its bottom shelf under quarrymen's wedges. The cold does not move; a flame stands straight; no sound comes down from the registry.
    76	- **The clerk's bell.** A small bell on a wire at the stair's foot, pulled by the clerk from the vault a quarter of an hour before he shuts his yard.
    77	- **Why the loads go up and down daily.** Cleon keeps on his tables only what he is reading: the fifty-two first copies lie there from the 284th, in three heaps as they lay on the floors (the Stair's twenty-nine in three rows, north toward the stair). The boxes go up at the bell and come down in the morning. Each day's card is written by Cleon the evening before.
    78	- **Karis on the stone (her stated conclusion):** the rock was cut first; the dark stone was laid against the cut; the town's mortared work is third. She puts no year to any.
    79	- **The clerk at the hatch (285th).** His voice: thin, dry, exact. His difficulty was his own form: matter entered on the 284th had gone up at his bell and come back, and his book would have said six boxes and two cases. He said that he must send up to **the under-registrar, who keeps the schedules**. After Seln spoke he wrote *The same*, the day, his mark and *four*, and has written *the same* each day since. He asked one thing ("The same?"). He looked at Seln "properly, from the cap to the boots".
    80	- **What Seln's truth cost.** He had been on the yard's bench since the first bell as a porter with a hired frame. By standing and speaking he showed his face to a registry's clerk and joined it to the four and the card. He watches from **the roof of the last building above the cut's head, which has a parapet**, from the 286th. His reason, said in the loft: his own rule of the 282nd, which he had not meant to except himself from.
    81	- **The lamp.** Low; a hand to the left of what is looked at; held on one knee. Lira on the stair; Brom ("You're a wall. I asked for a lamp."); from the third hour of the 285th, Cael. Cleon says *lamp*, *left*, *up a finger*; he has carried it himself for thirty years, "one hand for the light, and half an eye for where I'd put it". By the 289th Cael moves it before the word: all the looking runs from the low left corner of a thing upward to the right.
    82	- **The chairs.** One before the desk's slope, with a cushion in the shape of one man; one against the wall, heaped with bundles on the 284th, cleared and wiped on the 285th, and carrying his cushion on the 287th (his own chair has none). Karis is found sitting on it on the 288th.
    83	- **Cleon.** He missed his noon bowl on the 284th and has gone up for it on the other days. He does not touch paper with his skin: he points with a reed as long as a forearm. Three bowls of counters (white pebbles, black pebbles, red squares of broken tile). He does not blink while he looks; his eyes run; he rests them shut for an hour. To Cael at two feet the Path "made no sound at all". Brom said Karis's name in his hearing on the 286th ("I said no names." / "I never gave it to you. I gave it to her."). "It was the first thing I did when I came here": he has looked at the grey bridge twice. He made a sound "that was nearly a laugh" on the 290th.
    84	- **The board.** A plank of black oak as long as a man's arm and two hands broad, cut on one face with four rows of the floors' kinds of sign; found by Cleon ten years ago on the bottom shelf of the west gallery, with its face to the wall. At each row's end a count in figures (**a ring is five, a peck is one**) and after the figures a short group of signs. Rows of twelve, nine, twelve and seven signs. Its first row is the top row of the Hall's fourth sheet; its second is on the Stair's ninth; its third on the Ring's sixth; its fourth on the Stair's twenty-first.
    85	- **Bearer, carried, nothing.** "White" bears (take it out and the row comes down); "black" is carried (it hangs from the white); "red" is nothing (packing, so that a row fills its stone). **Of the forty-one kinds: seven bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing** (Karis's count on the 288th, when all fifty-two sheets had been laid). A bearer stands at the head of every run. Vastin: "To do a thing; to what; and how many times."
    86	- **Cleon's week.** A week of his, from the 287th; then every sheet goes up and the clerk writes that it is gone (he will not have matter lying a month under *the same*). Its seventh day is the 293rd; three days are left after the 290th.
    87	- **The bundle.** On the shelf beside the jar, tied across both ways in tape gone brown; it came off the same shelf as the board, behind it. "Paper. Not stone." He put two fingers on it and did not take it down. Nobody has untied it. Karis has not asked for it.
    88	- **The decipherment.** (1) The pecked figures equal the number of signs in each row: the cutter counted his own work. (2) The sign-group after each figure is the count in words. Karis tested it without looking at the board: from the oldest of the old town's tally-books (the eastern gallery, third bay on the left, the eleventh shelf) she wrote only the pattern of like and unlike letters in that hand's spelling of twelve, nine and seven, and then laid the pattern against the cutting. It held in every place. The tongue is the old town's "or that tongue's grandmother". **Eight letters** from the board. (3) The carried signs on the floors spell further numbers: *ten* on the Ring's fifth sheet and the number after ten on the Stair's fourteenth confirm the eight; two more letters from the Ring's ninth, confirmed on the Hall's eighth (**ten**, the 288th); three more from the Stair, confirmed on the Hall (**thirteen**, the 289th); one more from the Hall (**fourteen**, the 290th). Three signs are described: a hooked upright, a thing like a gate, a thing like a fork. **She has read no word that is not a number.** One group of six signs stands four times in the Stair's twenty-nine, always after a bearer; she has four of its letters and a word that she wishes to find there, and she has covered it and not written it.
    89	- **Brom's yard.** A broker's dray (two wheels as tall as Lira, one mule, a square of slate painted on its side) loaded with roofing slate shed its near wheel on the yard's slope; the slates slid and pinned the carter's lad by the boot against the dock's kerb. Brom stopped the yard-men's lever at the high side, took the low corner on his left shoulder with no grey, held while Lira drew the lad out by his belt, and was let down onto two split logs driven under the axle by Cael and a yard-man. The lad's five toes bend. **The broker's factor** (a good grey cloak, a broad hat, a shaved chin, clean hands, a tally; no name, no face): the house is the third on the brokers' street with the slate above its door; a guard's post at its store inside the second wall; a standing pass, sealed, by the month; a bed in the house above the wall; the house's coat; **four marks, and found**. His master "keeps eleven on his doors" who will not shift a keg until they have lit up, "and then it's a line in somebody's book". Brom: "I'd have taken it at sixteen and written home the same night."
    90	- **Seln's night (the 286th).** The district is empty at night; a watch's handcart goes its round with a lantern; the blue gate is shut, with a brazier in the arch. He sat on the leads of **the chandler's warehouse on the ring road, the tallest roof below the blue gate** (four floors, a hoist-beam, an outside stair with no door). The case weighs "something under three pounds"; its key is in his waistcoat. **Every registry on the Line has three doors**: the front for names; the yard for loads; and a returns hatch at the side, with a lamp and a night clerk, where a courier lays a sealed thing at any hour and neither may ask the other a question: "A registry enters what is laid on its ledge." Treswick's is down a passage off the square's eastern corner. He considered the hatch and put it by; he considered leaving the case on the Deepstacks' unticketed shelves and saw that for filing; he ruled nothing. He marked that a clerk had seen his face and that nothing had fallen.
    91	- **The watchers.** A man in the smith's doorway opposite the inn's arch on the night of the 286th; a porter on the kerb opposite the cut's head on the 288th, from the second bell to the third, with nothing on his frame; the same boots both times, a horseman's with the spur-rests cut away. On the night of the 290th the same boots, hooded, on the step of the painted door under its lamp, waiting until the five had come in under the arch.
    92	- **Vastin.** At the yard on the 286th he held the dray's mule by the cheek-strap with the left hand and gave no order. On the 287th he moved toward the lamp four times (Lira counted); on the 288th twice; on the 290th not at all. On the 288th he began to post a watch and stopped ("That's one"). On the 289th he held three tall books at Lira's word, qualifying it ("I cannot undertake to carry them to a table"). His three heads on the night of the 290th: the knee (he had Oryn's words in his mind as the stick came down, and asks that she be told that he heard her); what the six came for (they looked past him at Cael); and that he knows no more of what Cael is than the plate did.
    93	- **The alley.** The gate at the cut's foot can be barred from inside the cut. **An iron bar is let into the wall at the cut's mouth, beside the parapet, for people coming up under loads.** Six, hooded, each with a cloth over the lower face: two with shod staves from the yard; above, one kneeling with a crossbow on the highest landing, one with a stave beside him, one who came down to the plane, and one at the rail with a shuttered lantern. The plane stood from the lip of the ninth step, wall to wall and to the sky, seen for a breath as a bloom on the dusk. Two bolts broke on it (the second a hand from Cael's face); a palm and one shoulder were laid on it. Brom dropped his box across the fourth step, tipped up the loose third tread (a slab of dark stone a forearm long and a hand thick), lifted it to his thighs and let it go down the steps at the first stave. Lira came off the landing over the box and drove her knife a third of its length into the second stave's shaft; she took the wall on her right shoulder. Brom's boot put the second man down the steps; one of them then hauled on the box's becket, and Brom sat on the box. Karis's boot went from under her on the dished sixth step; her bare left hand opened on the lip of the step above. The hood on the landing lifted two fingers; the lantern was raised to the parapet; **Seln's cord (a clerk's page-weight: a pear of lead on a thin cord) took the arm to the rail** before the shutter opened. Two low notes whistled. They left at a walk: the bow unstrung, the cord cut in two places, the dead lantern taken up, the gate unbarred. Cael's count was eighty-one. **A stave was left** (a watchman's, shortened, its ferrule filed) and **two bolts** (plain, no fletcher's thread).
    94	- **Seln's finding.** They opened no Path, though the one on the landing stood like a man who has one: a Path opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it, and they took the same care as the six to have nothing of theirs entered. The bow was to make somebody open; the light was to tell somebody below. They went for a box and not for Brom: they were told to bring away what was carried. "What they would have been glad to find was on a roof above their heads." The four went two east and two west along the lane, hoods back before the first corner; the two below left by the carts' gate, which had been left on the latch for them.
    95	- **Karis's hand.** A straight cut across the heel of the left palm, from the root of the thumb to the wrist's other side; "not deep… only long". Bound in the cut with Brom's neckcloth; sewn in the loft with **seven stitches** of waxed thread on a curved roping needle held in the lantern's flame; the thread bitten; bound with a strip of his shirt. She said her fourteen letters over, one to a stitch. She writes with the right. Why no healer: in the district's house the entry on the sheet at a bed's foot (name, registry, tier) precedes the treatment, and after the fourth bell a card waits until those with names have been seen.
    96	- **Small things.** Brom keeps a twist of cloth under his box's lashing with a roping needle and waxed thread. A pot of horse liniment was begged from the stable for his left shoulder. Vastin mended the becket of Cael's box with the stable's awl. The old keeper at the third gate did not write the lateness against the card on the 290th. The third tread of the cut's bottom flight is now out of its bed.
    97	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the carter's lad; two yard-men; the broker's factor; the under-registrar (spoken of); the watch; the six in hoods.
