    16	## Decisions made before drafting
    17	
    18	1. **Deployment stays at thirty-two through the whole movement.** Nothing is used outward. The ten ask (in the yard, on the stair, in the alley) and are told no. The eleventh is acquired and not used: "Deployment: none."
    19	2. **Why loads go up and down daily.** The form's line for hands is "to carry"; a hand with nothing on his back is a person, and a person is asked. Cleon keeps on his tables only what he is reading (the fifty-two first copies stay laid out below from the 284th). The second and third copies, Karis's working paper, the reserve, bread and water go down each morning and come up at the clerk's bell. So each day: card, two gates, lane, cut, yard, hatch, iron door; and back before the fourth bell.
    20	3. **The Deepstacks' ground (mine).** Inside the iron door a stair of the dark stone, twenty-seven steps in three flights of nine with two landings. At its foot a cross-way; three galleries open off it side by side (east: the old town's ledgers; middle: the longest, shelved to the roof, a hundred and ten strides, with the old town's sorting tables down the middle of its far end and Cleon's desk and fixed lamp at the very end; west: the quarry's own, unshelved, tool-marked). The stair and cross-way are lined with the dark dry-laid stone; the galleries are cut in the bluff's pale bedded rock. A small bell on a wire at the stair's foot, pulled by the clerk from the undercroft when the yard is about to shut ("the clerk's bell").
    21	4. **"A man is already reading."** Cleon lets them in, puts the lamp into the nearest free hand, and goes down ahead of them in the dark, which he does not need a light for. By the time the loads reach the middle gallery he is at his desk under his own fixed lamp, and he reads to the end of his page before he gets up. He has cleared three tables; thirty years of his own bundles stand on the floor beside them.
    22	5. **Who holds the lamp ("the lamp trade").** Lira on the stair; Brom at the tables (his shadow, his breathing); Lira again (the slow arm; it moves); from the 285th, Cael, who can be a post. The rule: low, a hand to the left of the line. Cleon speaks to "Lamp", not to its holder; he takes no notice of who it is.
    23	6. **The unit "Being believed — … It's the end of being able to be wrong."** is elided and unpinned: MARKER. Staged so that Karis says it to Cael at the Hall's table while Cleon rests his eyes.
    24	7. **"Give me a week."** Karis, to the five, on the 284th (the 285th's protected log gives it to her).
    25	8. **The yard door (285th).** The clerk's own difficulty, not a question about people: the matter was entered yesterday and went up again at dusk; his form has no line for matter that comes back, and he says that he will have to send up for a ruling. Vastin, who knows the ruling, steps to the hatch by reflex and gives name, rank and office (verbatim, cut off). Seln has been on the yard's bench since the first bell as a porter waiting for a return load; he gets up and answers: MARKER (elided). The clerk settles his own book (*the same*; *four*). **Seln did not lie**: he said nothing false about the form or the man, and left "what he was" true in a clerk's ear; it cost him the bench, and a face that no registry had been able to describe for fifteen years. He watches from roofs after this. Then, in the yard: "I'm sorry," (Vastin) / "Don't be sorry," Seln said. "Be nobody." The clerk asks one thing in this movement ("The same?"), of the boxes.
    26	9. **The board (mine).** A plank of black oak an arm long and two hands broad, cut on one face with four rows of marks in the floors' kinds; at each row's end a count pecked as figures (a ring for five, a peck for one) and, after the figures, a short group of signs. Cleon found it a decade ago on the lowest shelf of the west gallery. Its first row is the top row of the Hall's fourth sheet, mark for mark.
    27	10. **Bearer, carried, ornament.** Cleon has Karis lay three kinds of counter on her own sheet (he never touches it): the marks that hold a run up; the marks that hang from those; the marks that hold nothing. Of the forty-one kinds: seven bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing (my figures; flagged). "They're orders." and "Same make." are quoted whole but their speakers are not pinned: MARKERS, staged for Cleon. The two units of the limit are Cleon's and are placed verbatim.
    28	11. **Rune from outside.** Nothing lights. He looks from the foot of a thing upward and does not blink; his eyes run; afterward he keeps them shut for an hour (it is why he eats with them shut). To Cael at two feet it is the quietest Path he has stood beside. Its inside is not shown and the word *lean* is kept out of Cleon's mouth. Nothing comes to Cael from it.
    29	12. **Cael's wish.** Hearing "it doesn't work on a man", he wants it tried on him anyway; he notes the wish and says nothing.
    30	13. **Brom's yard (286th, second bell).** A broker's dray of roofing slate sheds a wheel on the yard's slope and tips; the slates slide to the low corner and pin the carter's lad by the boot against the dock's kerb. Brom gets his left shoulder under the low corner with no grey on him, holds while Lira drags the lad clear and Cael and a yard-man drive split logs under the axle. Vastin holds the mule's head. The left shoulder is wrenched. The broker's factor (no name; no face described) offers a guard's post, a standing pass and a bed inside the second wall. "I'll think on it." Brom takes up his box on the bound right shoulder; "Cart was heavier." Cael enters it as a debt to two healers. On the inner stair: the protected exchange (Brom / Cael / Brom).
    31	14. **Seln's night walk (night of the 286th).** Cael hears the room change shape and does not ask. Seln goes up onto the tallest roof below the second wall. He has the case on its strap; I state its weight and that it is shut; **its contents are not named or gone over** (he keeps his rule of never describing it, even to himself). He looks at the registry's block and the station's steady light, and at where the returns door is that his old trade would have used; he goes through what laying it there would be; he decides nothing. He sees one other watcher in a doorway and files it as seen once. Morning: "I looked at it." The morning log has the two protected lines.
    32	15. **Cleon's week (mine; flagged).** On the 287th he says: a week of his, from today; then all of it goes up. That makes the 293rd its seventh day and leaves three days after the 290th (the packet's closing pressure, and §13's seventh day).
    33	16. **Cleon's close (287th)** is quoted with elisions: MARKER, with the scene written round it. Before it, mine: he points at the bundle on his shelf and does not take it down.
    34	17. **Vastin and the lamp.** On the fourth day below (the 287th) he moves toward the lamp four times and stops each time; on the 288th, twice. At the parapet on the 287th: "How long did it take you." / "I was fourteen. I'm nineteen. I'm not there yet either." (verbatim).
    35	18. **The decipherment (Karis; mine).** Step one, the board's figures: the pecked counts equal the number of marks in each row. Step two: the sign-group after each figure is the count in words; she does not assume it; she tests it against the number-words of the old town's first ledgers (east gallery), by the pattern of repeated signs across twelve (twice, alike), nine and seven. It holds; eight letters. Step three: the floors, with ornament and bearers taken out, give further number-words, and each gives one or two letters: the Ring, the Stair, the Hall. Fourteen of twenty-three by the 290th. She reads no word that is not a number. She does not ask for the bundle and does not show Vastin's page: she will not read with half an alphabet what she hopes to find. **The leaf is not readable; the title on the page is not spoken.**
    36	19. **The chairs.** Day one: Karis stands all day. Day two: the second chair at his desk has been cleared of bundles. Day three: his cushion is on it and his own chair has none. Nobody says so to him.
    37	20. **Lira's beat (289th).** She is the one who goes up the stack ladders for the old town's ledgers; she has been practising coming down off a ladder without her left arm, landing on both feet with the arm tucked. It pays in the alley.
    38	21. **The purse.** The loft's week runs out; Brom pays two marks at the half-door on the 289th: **134** (the map's track needs 136 → 130 with a four-mark meal on the 292nd; flagged).
    39	22. **Being looked at.** On the 288th Seln has seen the same boots twice; he tells the six. They keep the route (the only one that crosses neither the square nor a book) and Seln takes the roof over the cut from the 289th.
    40	23. **The alley (290th, dusk).** Order going up from the yard: Vastin first (the slowest sets the pace), Cael two steps below him, Karis, Lira, Brom last. The gate at the cut's foot is shut and barred behind them; two hooded figures with staves come up from it. Everybody turns downhill. Vastin turns uphill: four at the head of the cut, one kneeling with a crossbow, one at the iron rail at the cut's mouth with a shuttered lantern. He strikes the stick on the top step of the second flight (from the yard) and roots the cut wall to wall and to the sky: **there is no round**. Nobody else declares. Below: Brom drops his box across the steps, prises up the loose tread of the bottom flight and lets it go down the steps at the first stave; his left shoulder goes. Lira drops from the landing over the box and drives her knife into the second stave's shaft; she lands on her feet and takes the wall on her shoulder, not her arm. Karis goes down on the dished wet tread and opens her left hand on a step's edge; she keeps the flat dry. Above: two bolts on the plane; one hood comes down and lays a palm on it. Cael steps up to the old man's left hip, into the open half of the slot, and stands still to be looked at: the reason. The ten ask; he tells each no. In the quiet middle minute, with a hood's hand on the plane a yard from his face, **the eleventh comes**. A minute and some. Seln's weighted cord (a clerk's page-weight) takes the signaller's arm to the rail before the shutter opens. A whistle; all six leave, walking; none taken, no faces, nobody dead. A stave is left with Lira's knife in it.
    41	24. **Costs.** Vastin holds until Cael has counted them gone, then lets go and falls backward onto him; the knee has gone again; they lose two steps and are stopped by Brom's bound arm. He is spent past what the road spent. Karis's left hand: sewn by Brom in the loft with waxed thread, bound with a strip of his shirt, the thread bitten; no healer inside the gate would lay hands on it without a name on a sheet. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: bruised shoulder; the arm did not take it.
    42	25. **The notice.** Set in a fenced block (the edition's typography for system text), verbatim, once, framed as the lines put into the plain words in which he will have to write them (so that the name, the count and "Deployment: none" are his). Flagged.
    43	26. **"I put the wall in front of it anyway…"** Whole; Vastin's; placed. Its "it" is staged as what Cael is, which Vastin could not read at four feet and cannot now. Flagged. Cael: "It's entered," — and he has entered more than the man knows. **Not told**: he cannot tell the taking without telling the mechanism, and that is the six's.
    44	27. **The faction's limit (finding; mine).** They came hooded, with staves and one bow, and opened no Path; they wanted a declaration seen and a box carried off, and left the moment a light could not be shown. They cannot be written down in this town either. What they wanted to carry off was on a roof the whole time.
    45	28. **They know where the six sleep.** Seln, coming down by his own way, finds a hood standing in the lamp of the inn's painted door, not hiding, who waits until the five have come in under the arch and then walks away.
    46	29. **The log of the 290th**, verbatim.
