   197	He had been looking at it since he raised his eyes. A man stood at the top of that stair, with one hand on a rail, and a lantern held shut against his coat. He was not fighting. He had no stave. He was doing nothing whatever, and he was doing it with great attention, with his hood turned straight down the slot.
   198	
   199	Nobody brings a light to a beating and keeps it dark. That man had been put there to show it when a certain thing happened. It had not happened yet. He was waiting for it, and so were the other three; the one with the bow had not laid a second bolt on his string.
   200	
   201	Cael had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for. He knew the look of it through a hood.
   202	
   203	They had been told. Somebody had stood these six in a place with no way out of it, and had given them a bow to frighten with and staves to hurt with; and all that they wanted in return was to see what a boy with no tier did when he was hurt enough.
   204	
   205	Then they should see him do nothing, from as near as he could bring it. A wall wanted a reason behind it; he would be the reason, and stand.
   206	
   207	An old man had said a thing at a board, on a moor, with his bowl set down upon a sill, about a wall and what it wants behind it. Cael had been that once, by going ten strides off and landing like a post.
   208	
   209	He went one step up.
   210	
   211	It put him on the eighth tread, at a brown coat's left hip, in the open half of the slot, where there was nothing between the bow and himself but a place that the eye slid off. He stood square. He lifted his face, so that the last of the light was on it, and looked up the stair at four hoods.
   212	
   213	The bow came round to him at once.
   214	
   215	He had known that it would. He watched the man lay the bolt, and draw, and he did not shift his feet. He had put his skin behind this wall once already, on the word of having seen it hold. He had seen it hold a good deal since.
   216	
   217	The bolt broke on the air a hand's breadth from his eyes.
