     1	# Chapter 56 — I'm Going
     2	
     3	The lamp was lit in the north window of the Ironyard at dusk, for the first time in a fortnight.
     4	
     5	Cael saw it from the bottom of the market steps on his way back from the tannery lane. It was a small yellow square high up in the dark wall of the old foundry, and he stood in the cold and looked at it for some time before he went on down. All fortnight there had been no lamp there, because she had put it out on the third day so that every hour she had would be his. He had not known, until he saw it lit again, how much he had missed it. It had been there through the whole of the autumn. It had been the last thing he saw from his window at night and, often, the first thing in the morning. On the nights when the Log would not come right, or a bout would not leave his head, he had lain and watched that square of yellow across the roofs and known that somebody else on the hill was awake and working at a thing that would not come right either. It had not made the thing easier. It had made it less lonely, which was a different help, and in some ways a better one.
     6	
     7	He did not go up. It was her hour. He went round instead to the alcove by the side passage, which was dark and cold and smelled of chalk and straw, and sat on the bench against the back wall with his arm in the scarf. Through the arch, across the empty main floor and up the north stair, he could hear her.
     8	
     9	He could not see her. He did not need to. He had heard her at that lamp a hundred nights from his bed, through two streets and a wall, the sound of a staff end on stone and feet on boards, and he knew the shapes of the sounds as well as the shapes of the words in Hesk's hand. Tonight they were slow. There was a step, and a long pause, and a step. A turn. The staff end touching down once, lightly, like somebody tapping a table to see whether it is sound. Then the same again, from the other side.
    10	
    11	She was not training. She was asking her feet questions, one at a time, and waiting for them to answer.
