    14	
    15	Brom came in at the side passage when the hour was nearly over. He had a covered pot in one hand, held out in front of him as if it might go off, and a loaf under the other arm.
    16	
    17	"The cookshop," he said. "I told him who it was for. He gave me twice as much and wouldn't take the second copper." He sat down on the other end of the bench, carefully, with the pot on his knees. He listened for a moment to the sound from the north stair. "She's found it."
    18	
    19	"She never lost it."
    20	
    21	"No," Brom agreed. "But she was afraid she had." He was quiet a moment. "That's not the same, and it's not nothing either."
    22	
    23	The sound on the stair stopped. They heard the lamp go out, and her feet come down the north stair and across the main floor in the dark, quick and sure. Then she was in the arch with her staff on her shoulder and her face pink from the cold and the work, and she looked at the two of them sitting on the bench like a pair of owls, and at the pot on Brom's knees.
    24	
