editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:There was no need of it. Nobody in that hold would have come up uninvited; and the one person whom he did not want at its foot had gone to her bed at dusk, across the yard, behind a small window with no light in it, like somebody who has work in the morning. He drew it up all the same. He had asked four people among the barley sacks to hear a thing, and he wished to have done something with his hands before he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:13:"She called it that at Oxhollow. Both hands, and all of her, for minutes, with the one under her hands lying still and whole. You were all in that yard, and I saw four people hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:17:He was on the boards with his knees up, and his hands lay open on them, one of the two pale across the pads of the fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:"And none of you said what it was, and nor did I. I've had fifteen days in which to say it, and a ring; and I'm saying it now with one night to go, which is late." He looked at the flame of Karis's lantern. She had held up one finger when she lit it, to show him how much oil an hour at a stone had left her, and he meant to have said all of it before that was gone. "It's the thing that I take them in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:29:"So I've laid tomorrow beside her page, a piece at a time. It wants to be sustained. She says twenty minutes, with both her hands on me, and I've never stood a bout that was a quarter as long. It wants stakes, on both sides. Mine are that I'm three days off that floor, with every one of the nine still shaken by it. Hers are that she's spent three mendings on us and she's late at the top of her round, and she means to lay out a fourth on a boy who has given her nothing four times. And it wants to be in earnest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:69:"I've told her nothing. She has put her hands on me four times and found no banks, and she watched me go up a hill with fire in my fist, and in all that time she has never asked me one question that wasn't about a hurt. She thinks that she is going to lay me on a table and read me. If something is taken from her while she does it, she'll not have been told that it might be. She can't say yes to it, and she can't say no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:"I know that it's never shown, and I've looked. Lira was no slower in the week after Ardenmere, and you were no softer; Karis set sixteen points for a panel inside a month of hers. Not one of the people that they came out of has ever said that they felt it go." He turned his palm up. "But every one of them was whole when it happened, and fighting. She will be spending. It costs her a mending to do this at all; so if it comes, it comes out of a purse that she has open, and I've nothing whatever to tell me what that does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:157:"I'd tell her after," said Cael. "That is what I've built in place of the before. On her table, before I have got up off it, whatever has happened and whatever hasn't; all of it, from the brass plate on, with the nine named for what they are. And then it's hers to deal with as she likes. If she says that I'm never to come within a mile of her round again, then that's the price of it, and I walk south."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:161:It was Seln. He had come away from the loft door, and he stood at the edge of the light with his hands behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:185:He called for no hands, since it was not a matter that hands could carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:*Written before. Tomorrow morning, the back room, the door shut. Oryn runs the long reading. Both hands, all of her, all of me, twenty minutes. I lie still. I'm still and whole for the first time in front of her; she said so in the yard, with the tape off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:207:*The conditions, as I have them. Sustained: twenty minutes at contact is longer than any bout I've stood. Stakes: I'm three days off the Ring and the nine are still settling; she's three mendings down and her route's late at the far end and she's spending a fourth on a patient who's given her nothing four times. Earnest: she'll do the thing she does as well as she does it. Karis: the model says real, not fight. I don't know if the model's right. Nobody does. Tomorrow's the first time it could be tested and I'm the test.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:213:*After: I tell her. In full. On her table, before I'm off it. Whatever it costs me, including the route walking north without me on it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:That was all that the last line meant. A registry had read him for years, and written down what it found, and had never once told him beforehand what it was reaching for or what the reaching might cost him. He was about to reach for somebody else in the same way. The one thing that he had which that office had not was a leaf with tonight's date at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:225:It was a thin difference, and it did not make the thing allowed. He was exact with himself about that, since he was trying to be exact about all the rest. To cross the yard now and knock would be right. To say in the morning, before she had so much as washed her hands, what those hands might lose, would be right. Four people had shown him both doors. A leaf written first opened neither of them; it meant only that when he walked past them he would be doing it with his eyes open, and could never afterward tell anybody, himself least of all, that he had not known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:235:There was ice on the trough. The geese were asleep in a heap by the long house wall, and on the platform by the western gate a lean man turned his head, and turned it back. Cael had eaten nothing. He had his shirt on and no coat, and his feet were bare inside his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:241:The healer was washing. She had a basin on the bench and her sleeves pushed above the elbow, and she did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:247:The plank was cold under his shoulders and very clean, and there was a folded sack for his head. He lay and looked at rafters with barley dust on them, and listened to her drying her hands, a finger at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:255:She came to the table's side, smelling of pitch and cold water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:267:She said it without any disappointment, as she would have said that a door was shut. It was the fifth time. She took her fingers away and shook both hands out from the wrist, twice, hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:7:For the first dozen there were only her hands. They were warm, and broad, and they did not move. He could feel the short nails of the left one against the back of his skull, and the heel of the right resting on bone. He could feel her pulse in it, which was slow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:19:He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:He lay very still. It was not even, and that was the first thing about it that he could have sworn to. Down his right side it bore a shade more lightly than down his left, and came a hair late. Each time, the heel of her hand on his breastbone eased and settled with it, by so little that he would never have caught it without three weeks of a tape to teach him what a hair was. He knew that unevenness. He had walked behind it for a day and a half down a mountain before he saw what it was: a raw heel, taped, and favoured for so long that it had been built into everything she did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:He could feel her keeping time. He had not expected that, of all things: that he would lie under an instrument and be able to tell that it was a person. A brass plate in a district hall had waited over him for eleven seconds, and he had not felt it wait; an assessor's panel had read him across a floor, and he had got nothing from it but a number on a slate. This one limped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:33:He had been afraid, coming over the yard, that it would be like being searched. He had been searched by a good many careful people; he knew the hands of a clerk going through a satchel, and the eyes of a panel going over a stance for the thing that was wrong with it. This did not rummage. It went down him and came back as a carter goes down his own road, looking at what was there, and it took up nothing to see what lay beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:47:He had supposed that it would be like the talk at a bench, short and to the purpose. It was nothing of the kind. It was the low running murmur of a person who is feeling her way down a passage in the dark and saying what her hands meet, less to be heard than to keep herself from being lost; and he lay under it and counted, and tried to do as he had been told, which was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:61:"Everyone I've ever had my hands in, this is the part where I know." She said it as a finding about herself. "Iron Skin, you feel the banks first — dense, walled, everything held. Ember, the banks are a fire's, hot at the edge. Wind you barely feel the banks at all, the water's too fast. But there's always a bank. It's how you know which river." A pause, long, her current going along Ember and stopping at the ignition point and lying there as if it had found a stone in a bed. "I can't find one here. I keep putting my hand where the bank ought to be and there's more water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:67:Compression lay through the whole of his frame, and it did nothing at all; it had been built to wait for a blow, and no blow came. The read wished to open. It wished it very much. There were two hands on him and a square woman standing over a table, and it could have told him the weight of her to a pound and which way she leaned. He left it shut. He did not hold it shut. He found, after a while, that there was a difference, and that it lay in whether his jaw was tight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:89:It did not. It lay quiet under her hands. She went along the top of it as a boat goes over deep water, and it did not so much as lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:97:He had been waiting for it without knowing that he waited. He had lain under hands before, and under eyes; he knew to the breath when it came. The thing reaching for him would find that he was not what it had been built to hold, and then it would change. A brass plate went dark. A panel's faces shut. A clerk with a form that had no box for him wrote a word in capitals, because he could find no tier to write; and after that there was a file, and a watcher at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:101:She had found it eight minutes ago, and he had heard her find it: *there it is, I've got the moving.* She had been walking up and down the length of something for twelve minutes that was like nothing she had ever put her hands in, and her voice was the voice in which she had told an ox-driver that four small bones were broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:113:She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was no weaker, but it had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge: under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:133:Her hands did not lift. He felt her draw a breath all the way down, and let it go, and there was something in the going that he had not heard in her at a bench or on a hill or beside a bar of cold iron. She was not tired; he knew what her tiredness sounded like. A person who had only ever owned one thing had put the whole of it down on a table and found that it would not buy what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:"Eleven years," said Oryn. "I've never had my hands in something and not known what it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:That was the first thing that he was sure of. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for a hundred days, and he was better at that one act than at anything else he did. He had only to say no, at the first stride, as he had said it on a gravel bar. Her current would go by the empty place and find it shut, as it had found the ninth; she would come to the end of her twenty minutes and lift her hands and tell him that there was nothing, as before. He would get down off her table with what he had lain down on it with. Nothing would have been taken from anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:155:He went through what came after that, as quickly as he had gone through it on a white floor with his hands on a skull.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:157:It would be right. He did not hide that from himself, lying there. Four people had said so in a loft, and nothing that he had written afterward by a bead of oil had altered it by a hair. There was even an honest way to do it: he could shut the place, and when she lifted her hands he could tell her all the same what had stood open in him while she worked, and what he had come within a breath of taking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:188:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:194:It was the first that had ever come to him lying down. It had come out of nobody's blow, in a room with a basin in it, from two hands that were trying to learn what he was made of. That was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:196:It was the first whose last line had a healer's word in it. He had seen *non-hostile* once before, at the end of a bout with a friend, and had not known what to do with it. He had never seen *clinical*. Whatever kept those lines had looked at what she did and had found the right name for it. That was two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:198:There was a third. He knew what it was. It was already written, on a leaf, in a loft across the yard, in his own hand, with last night's date at the head of it; and he did not set it in the row with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:202:Her hands had gone still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:218:She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:220:He watched her do what she did at a bench. She did not guess. She went over what her hands had found, strictly, and came to the one thing that a healer must ask first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:226:He could have let her think so. He saw it open in front of him, as easy as a gate: it would have been a healer's hands doing something that they had never done before, to a boy that they could not read, and she would have carried it north with her and turned it over at every hold for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:232:He had a hundred days of road behind him in which he had been asked what he was by nobody. He had thought, on a shelf above a valley, that this was freedom, and had written it down. He lay now under the hands of the one person past the Line who had tried to find out without a seal, and who had just paid for the trying with something that he did not yet know the size of; and he had a leaf in a loft that said what he owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:242:Her hands stayed where they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:"It came. There are ten. I told her all of it on her table before I was off it: the plate, and what I am, and where each of the nine came from, by its Path. She had Brom fetch the leaf, and she read it." He looked at Seln. "I gave the ninth its Path and no more. I told her that I'd used it once on that hill, and that I had looked to see where her eyes were before I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:"I'd priced it for her on the leaf. Whatever it cost me, down to her going north without me. She won't have that. She says a patient doesn't hand her his own fine ready counted, and that I'm not the one who knows the price, and shan't be until she does." He drew a breath. "And she knows what she'd have said last night, if I had knocked. She's keeping it. I'm not to be told, today or ever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:29:"She's set two things on it. I'm to use it on myself before I use it on anybody. And whatever I find, she is the one I tell, on a day that she names." He looked round the four of them. "So if there's anything to find, it's hers before it's yours. I'm saying so now, in order that nobody should think later that I kept a thing back without leave. It's part of what's owed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:41:It was Karis. The words came out of her like a gate swinging on a dry hinge, each with a creak in the middle of it, and all four of them turned to look at her. She had spoken three words on the day before. She touched her throat, and frowned at it, and went on regardless, since it was plain that she had been saving this up since first light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:105:"The first night I thought I'd go mad. A forge doesn't go quiet when you bank it. The iron ticks while it cools, every piece at its own rate, and the coals settle, and the hide of the bellows creaks as it dries. There's water dripping off the tongs into the tub. I lay on a heap of sacks and I could have told you which sound was which with my eyes shut. I had the lot by heart inside an hour, and I never slept a wink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:123:It was the last of the year's. The carters had come up through the stretch at noon the day before and slept in the cart-yard, three heavy men in sheepskins, with frost on their mules' eyelashes; and they wished to be unloaded and gone before the roads grew any harder. Holds farther up the valley would fetch that barley through the whole of the cold term, a cart at a time. Until then it lay in Thornwater's loft, under Thornwater's thatch, which was all that a grain hold was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:127:So Brom took two sacks at a time, one on each shoulder, and went up the rungs like a man going upstairs to bed. Seln took one, and made no sound with it. Karis sat on an upturned keg at the ladder's foot with the hold's slate on her knee, and cut a stroke for every sack that passed her. Lira had been given nothing, since there was nothing that a person could do with a sack and one arm. She sat on the well's coping with her foot flat on the stones and counted the strokes on Karis's slate, aloud, to annoy her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:143:He knew that lag, for he had run up a ditch in it with eight hounds behind him. Whenever the heaviest thing in him had been used, everything else answered him a beat late for ten counts or twelve, and he had entered that in Hesk's book as a bill and learned to wait it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:145:He counted to twelve. He took a stride, and the ground arrived late under it. He counted twelve more, and it was no better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:147:He had laid no lane, and so this was not a bill. The thing had moved house, and it meant to be late until it had done unpacking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:155:"The ground's late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:157:"Late how?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:195:"You've always been early. You load it on the three." She showed him, sitting, with a small turn of her one free shoulder. "I saw it the first week in that lane. I never said, for it worked. You'd have it ready, and then you'd go, and you never once went late. I thought it was how you did it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:203:"Two. Three. Late, both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:205:He tried asking sooner, by a hair, and then by another hair. The hiss came up, and the lean came with it, and the ground was not there to meet either; and twice more he went sideways across the yard with his arms out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:223:"Your foot's lying to you. Wait for the ground. It's late, so let it be late, and ask when it gets there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:235:"Eleven," she said. "One on your face. Two late and two early. Six right." She let her breath go. "I walked that count for a month on a new leg, with a man at the rail who'd have struck me for a burst. He never once told me to wait for the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:237:"He'd never had a floor that was late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:245:Cael wrote one thing in Hesk's book before he went back to the sacks, since he wished it to be in his hand and dated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:247:*Hundred and first day. The yard, at noon, inside the fence. Wind, eleven leans of a foot each, to learn it with the ground late. That is practice, and I am not putting it in the number; the number is what I spend on a road, or in a fight, or on a person. I enter it here so that nobody has to take my word for what's left out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:255:She had been at it since the middle of the day, with the door of the back room standing open, and he had counted four go in and come out: a carter with a wrenched back, and a woman of the hold, and two that he did not know, who had walked in with the grain. She stopped her stride and a half from the coping, wiping her hands on her breeches. Lira had gone up to the loft by then, on her right hand and both knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:261:"I didn't say it to make you glad. I said that you'd hear, and you're hearing. Write that it doesn't show in the hands. Write that it was I who said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:269:He told her. He kept it to what it was like, since he had nothing else: his own current going along at the edge of whatever he thought, in fits; and the nine coming up out of it singly, each with its own sound, so that he could have told her with his eyes shut which was which. He gave her two or three of the sounds, the grass and the millstone and the wick. He said that it had kept him awake, and that it did not pull at him as every one of the others had, and that the heaviest had moved over at noon to make room and had left the ground half a beat late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:291:"Don't say it to please me. Say it because you were the one who didn't, once, and you've seen what it's like from where I stand." She turned back toward her bench. "I've four more before dark, and I'm six days short at this gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:29:At a ford, seven of their kind had learned inside a quarter of an hour to leave a silent body alone. These had learned nothing whatever since sunset, with one of their own lying dead on their own floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:31:They were careless. He turned the word over, and it fitted every count that he had. They had no cause to be anything else. Whatever walked over that line left its Path at a stone, and came on as a deer comes, or as a man with a stick; and in all the years that a pack had lain under that floor, no prey in this place had ever shouted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:35:*Give it one.* He had said that aloud on a gravel bar, and gone down thirty yards to do it. Whichever of them was nearest to a declaration moved first, and the rest fell in behind whatever moved; that was in his own handwriting, by Karis's dictation. He was the only thing between that floor and that stone that had one to give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:37:He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:41:He lengthened his stride and came up past Brom's elbow, and along the edge of the lane to the small figure behind Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:69:He fell back to the rear. As he passed Brom he said, "At the stone, turn round and keep that thing pointing in," and Brom grunted. Lira had her head down and her teeth shut. The healer's eyes went over him from a foot away, tired and level, as they went over everybody, and found nothing that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:89:She went over on her back with the animal on top of her, and he heard the breath go out of her. It struck for her throat, and she got both her hands there first, shut, one fist upon the other under her own chin, as a man of Thornwater had once got a hand between; and the jaws closed across the two fists together and drove them back into the place that they were guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:97:Seln was five strides beyond her, turning, with the knife coming up; and between Seln and Karis one of the three that Cael had never told apart had stepped out into the lane and stood there, broadside, with its head level. Lira and the healer were five strides on the near side; Oryn had stopped dead, with her whole weight under Lira, and both of them were looking at the thing on the ground. Brom was a stride behind the two women, with the bow at his shoulder already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:111:It was sealed. He knew the seal's reason as he knew his own name, and he went through it in the half of a breath, since it was the one sum that he had never done carelessly. A defector's craft in his hands would tell whoever saw it where he had learned it. So: who could see?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:125:It was ten strides. On the tenth he let it go, with his hands already on the animal's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:127:The sound of his own feet came back a beat late, like an echo off a wall. His hands had gone as cold as if he had held them in a spring. And the dark-backed one, which had heard nothing come and seen nothing come, shifted its grip to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:141:Karis lay with her fists under her chin and her mouth open. No sound was coming out of it. Her eyes were on him, and they were quite clear. They went to his hands, and to the place ten strides back where he had been standing, and returned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:155:He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:193:They came after him, and they did not come as they had come all evening. There was nothing idle in them now. They came through the grass as seven had come across a river to a seed of fire on a rock, flat out, with the leaders drawing together and the rest falling in behind; and he had the sound of them on both hands, a dry rushing, like a wind that had found its direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:195:There was one thing more that he had refused at every doorway and coping since a river, and he let that off its hold with the rest. Anchor fixed nothing, for he had no point on that hill that he could afford to nail to the ground. It only looked, west, as a dog let off a chain looks first at the gate. It would be within ten feet of a thing before it could tell him what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:211:It was a corridor of air the length of a cart and team, high at his end and low at the other, and everything in it went downhill at once. The pale grass stood along both lips of the rut with its seed heavy on it. The lane stripped it. Seed and chaff and dry stem went down that channel in a flood, as thick as meal out of a sack, straight into the faces of whatever was coming up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:215:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:219:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:225:He laid the third. It was as many as he had ever laid; he did not know what a fourth would do to him, and it was not the night to learn. The rut behind him was a river of chaff. Twelve counts, for the third. His own breath reached him late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:247:It answered a beat late. He felt that as a man feels a stair that is not where he thought. Then the air broke, and he was out of the rut and over its southern lip, twenty feet down the open slope, with the knife still lit in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:253:He had priced this count too, and the price was a bad one. The dark-backed one had not gone into the chaff. It had run the rut's rim, and whether a boy's hands had taught it something in a lane he could not have said. It was the only one of the eight on the right side of the channel when he left it. It was behind him now, up the slope, and he could not turn his head to learn how near.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:259:It took it late. For the first beat there was nothing there at all, and the animal's whole weight went into his legs as a cart's tail goes into a man in a lane. His knees went. He pitched forward down the hill with its chest on his shoulders, and kept the knife up, and put out the other arm. His left forearm met the ground under the two of them. Then the thing that a fighter had once put through him, and left with him, arrived and did what it was for. There was only one road open to it. The whole of that blow went down one bone into the hill, and he felt it ring from the elbow to the wrist like a struck rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:261:The hound had run into a thing that gave like a gate and then did not give. It went on over his head, as a sack comes off a tipped cart, and rolled away down the slope in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:297:She was on her knees a stride from the stone, with Seln's hands under her arms to keep her upright. She had no voice to answer him with. Her two hands were out in front of her, shaking, and he saw the right one make the smallest movement that it had ever made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:309:Karis held it for a count of six. He counted it for her. At six the wall went down all at once, and she sat back against Seln's knees with her hands open in her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:313:Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. Four of the six were hurt, and he would have the count of that by lamplight. He knew already that every hurt but his own had been taken by somebody who had no means of answering. Nine things lay under his hand again, and they had not yet stopped shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:15:She put her hands round the arm, one above the place and one below, as she had put them round it at the head of a lane and had them come away empty. Her eyes shut. Her lips moved, and this time there was a count in them; he saw it go to four, and to five, and on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:19:She said it to the arm, on a breath, and that was all the notice that she gave it. She had waited thirty rounds to learn what a line did to her hands, and had learned it at noon, and tonight she had no time to spare for having them back. She opened her eyes and looked up at five people, and spoke quickly, as she had spoken on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:31:There was one thing more to do before she went under. It was Karis's to do, and Karis could not; she sat in the grass with her two hands at her throat and her eyes on him. She held, as a one-armed man at a plank held, that a thing not said was not a finding; and so he said it for her, aloud, as Pike read a line before he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:33:"Ninety-sixth day. Dark. The west side of the stone. There were nine. Two are dead, one on the floor with a bolt in it that we'll not see again, and one there." He did not point. "Seven went out when the wall lit, north and south, along the outside of the line. I didn't see where they went after. Did anyone?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:55:She shut her eyes. Her hands settled, with the fingers overlapping, and her face went as quiet as a pond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:63:Lira did not speak. She had been told once, on a mountain, to lie still and let it, and she lay still, and her right hand was the only part of her that told him anything. At first it had hold of the grass, a whole fistful, with the knuckles standing. A little later it left the grass and found the strap of Hesk's satchel, where that hung from his shoulder beside her, and took two turns of it round the wrist. By the time the oil stood halfway down the fourth finger the strap had gone slack, and the hand lay open on her own breast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:His left arm had gone dead to the elbow before the first quarter was out. He hung the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand. Something in his right arm had begun to ask about the lantern's brass rim. *Edge?* It had put that question to half the world for a season, patiently, and tonight for the first time it had been told yes; it asked now like a child that has been given one sweet. He told it no, and it asked again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:77:When her hands opened, she did not take them away at once. She turned the arm over in them, toward the light, and looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:89:Brom said something to the dark that was not a word. Cael found that the lantern was shaking, and that it was not his dead arm that shook it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:103:Nobody answered her, and she did not seem to want an answer. She held out her hand to the south, and a narrow dull knife came into it out of the dark, haft first. That one cut. She took a strip off the blanket a hand wide and as long as her two arms, and passed it under Lira's elbow and round her neck, and tied it short, so that the mended arm lay across the breast with its hand under the opposite collarbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:105:Lira looked down at it. She had been the quickest pair of hands on any floor that she had ever stood on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:119:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:123:There were six of Thornwater's blankets on that mule, and there were six people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:Seln brought them down, since he was the only one with two hands and nothing wrong with either, and he laid two on Lira and one on the healer where she had fallen. He stayed a breath longer over the healer than a blanket needed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Karis had her notebook open on her knee, and she was writing with the pencil held in her fist. Her knuckles were skinned across both hands, where teeth had gone over them; she had looked at them once and left them. When she had three lines she turned the leaf to the lantern and to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:135:*Throat: pressed, not cut. Air: enough. Voice: none whatever. Hands: skin.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:147:Then she shut the book. She did not lie down. She was looking at his left leg, which lay stretched toward the lamp with the cloth black from the knee to the boot, and he watched her think it through as she thought through everything. The healer was asleep and would be asleep till morning; Seln had the south and Brom the north, and Lira had one arm. That left a person with two skinned hands and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:177:Nobody had said a word to Cael about a hill. They had eaten bread beside him, and passed him the water, and Seln had put the last blanket round his shoulders without a remark, as he would have put it round anybody. Four of them knew what he was and had not known until tonight that it reached in there. One of them knew nothing, and had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:191:*Nine hounds, inside. Two dead, both Brom's, with a healer's bow that he had never shot. Seven out, north and south along the line's outside. Not seen after. No instruments were there. No price.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:195:*Mine: left calf open on the old seam. Left forearm struck; dead to the elbow. Ribs taped, three days to run; they held.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:201:*Shadow: once, ten strides, in the lane. Four saw it who have always known. The fifth had a man and a box between. I did that sum before, not after. It holds. Cold hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:217:*Compression: in that lock, late. That's the arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:245:He heard her breathe. She was doing what she had done in a loft at Thornwater, before a ford, when he gave her a plan that she disliked: going through it, a piece at a time, to find the place where she could make it better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:257:"Later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:261:"Later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:265:He sat on beside her with a dead arm across his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:273:*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:9:The camp was quiet behind him. He listened to it as he listened to any room that he had his back to, and sorted what he heard: a pencil, and then no pencil; a big man washing his hands without a sound; and the healer's breathing, which was slow and went all the way down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:19:It was an accurate word. He had submitted a great many accurate reports, and few of them had sat so ill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:35:It had gone on for ten strides. Then there had been a boy with both hands on a hound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:39:He would have said *go*. He had not needed to. The boy had stood there for as long as a jaw takes to shut, doing the same sum, and had got the same answer; and he had lifted it, and carried it exactly as far as it was wanted, and put it down with his hands already full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:The big man came down out of the north when the east had begun to grey, walking as a person walks who has stood since midnight, with a bow in his two hands and a rag round one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:101:He could not tell, from memory, where on those sheets a round had fallen. He had the look of one: a water crossed, and dashes running on east of it, and then the white place. To say more than that he needed a ford drawn by somebody who had put a wheel through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:113:By the time the sun stood straight overhead, Karis had a horn cup in both hands and was finding out whether she could drink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:117:The healer had named the hour herself. She had woken with the sun and eaten the sixth of a loaf that lay by her head, and slept again, on purpose, for two hours more; then she had taped Lira's foot without a word, and sat down against a pannier with her eyes on the top of the sky. She would do her two when it was straight up, she said. They had all told her that it was the hour at which those animals had never been seen about, and if she was to shout twice more she would do it then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:119:The throat had taken her a quarter of an hour. She had sat back from it on her heels, with both hands hanging, and given her judgment to the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:129:The hand was the pads of four fingers laid open by a string in the dark, bled into the linen and dried there, and she soaked the linen off with the pan's water and a corner of the lantern's oil, slowly, while Brom looked at the scrub. When it was off she turned the hand over in the light and said, "String," as a verdict, and put her palms round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:131:Shorter than the throat; less than half, by the floor. The fingers, when she opened her hands, had four dark lines across the pads where there had been four open mouths, and Brom closed the hand and opened it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:149:He lifted the edge. She leaned, with her hands on her own knees, and looked under the wool from a foot away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:155:She had come up on her good elbow. The healer turned her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:159:"Not since the hill. You've had a night's sleep, and you've seen more of him than you had. Put your hands on him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:175:"No." Oryn let go of him, and wiped her hands on her breeches. "And I don't say a finding four times to a patient and change it on the fifth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:"The arm. That was last night, and it was one, and it was the size of two. The throat. The hand. Three mendings in two days." She turned her hands over on her knees and looked at them, and let them lie. "That's a route. Near enough, that's what I carry between a hold and the next one, for the people who feed me and shoe the mule. Three serious hurts and a road's worth of small ones. And I've spent it on a road two days from the nearest roof, on a crew that's not on my circuit, sitting against a piece of iron somebody drove where they'd measured to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:201:"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:203:He had been counting, since a far lip, what the day had cost, and he had supposed the count complete. He had not had this. A woman had added a stop to the only thing she owned, for two reasons of her own, and seven gates that had never been asked were paying for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:219:"I have traced three things from his map, and no more. Here is the first water; those are his three strokes. Here is his square with a dot, which is the hold we slept at. And here is his day's measure, a tick to a day, so that what I draw shall be on his scale and not on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:223:"The rest I have put in from memory, with the paper off the skin, so that I could not be led. On the sheets that I counted, a road went out from the Line in dashes. It crossed a water. It ran on east. And at a certain distance past the crossing, the dashes went round a white place, ruled with a compass, that had nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:251:It lay on the afternoon like the iron itself. Cael looked at the stamp, a thumb's breadth below the beaten head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:315:She thought about that. Then she shut her eyes again, and whatever she made of it she kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:3:They moved the dead hound in the afternoon, and Brom was not allowed to help.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:"It's a dog," said Brom. He had both hands tucked under his arms, like a man kept in from a harvest. "It weighs what a calf weighs. I could have it under one arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:29:He could not cut it. He sat on a pannier with his hands on his knees and told Seln where to put the knife, and Seln cut, without expression, as he would have taken dictation. They had three loaves of Thornwater's, and a cheese of the healer's, and five onions. It was two days to the fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:39:She said nothing about it. She ate hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:41:It was a strange afternoon. They spoke of a good many things. Lira asked how far a mule could smell a dead dog, and the healer told her. Brom wished to know whether a bow was meant to pull to the left or whether that was him. Karis wrote that she would open the box under a roof and not before, since there was wind, and held it up; and they agreed that there was wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:73:She turned her head. The boy was sitting up, a little way off, with his dead arm in his lap and his face to the east. He was not on watch, and he had not lain down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:She knew what that was. She had read it in his book before she ever saw it, in a boy's cramped writing, with *sealed* beside it and a mild man's name left out. She had seen it since, a handful of times, and never once in front of anybody who did not already know. It had gone by her elbow like a draught, and then he was on the dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:95:Seven hours later it had gone off in his feet like a whip, twenty feet north, inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:107:She went through it. It was what she did with a plan that she hated. There had been nine on the white and two at Brom's legs, and Karis and the healer standing at the lip with nothing. If he had gone off like that there, every one of them would have come to the place where he stood, and that was the place where she was lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:That left *since when*. She had the right to it. She could have had it out of him last night with one more word; he had been sitting there waiting for it with his breath held, and he would have told her everything, badly, in the wrong order, with his arm dead and hers tied, because she had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:115:*Later.* She had put it on her own side of the fire, and it was hers to keep there. It sat in her as the burst used to sit in her calves, wanting out at every stride. She said no to it. She had some practice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:121:The healer got up in the grey. She did not go to the mule. She walked the ten yards to the stone by herself, past the place where a dead thing had lain, and stood on the near side of it with her boots together, as she had stood on the first evening with her hands held out over nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:She led the animal up until its forefeet were against the stone, and laid her whole hand on the warm neck under the mane, and stood with her head bent, reading whatever it is that a healer reads in a beast. Then, keeping her hand where it was, she stepped over, so that she stood inside with her arm stretched back across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:137:She was a long time at that one. The mule turned its head and nosed her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:141:She came out. She rubbed her hands together slowly, as she did when they had been in cold water, and she looked across the grass and saw him sitting with a book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:167:She had not known how much of walking was done with the hands. Her balance was all on one side. When the wind pushed her she put out the right and nothing answered it from the left; when she stepped over a rut she felt the tied arm start, under the grey wool, and stop against the knot. It did that forty times in the first hour. It was like walking beside a dog that keeps going for the same gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:191:She stopped it. She did not know afterward how. It was like stopping dead off a full run at the lip of a gravel bar: there was all that going in her, and then there were two feet. She shut the hand and jammed the elbow back against her ribs, and she turned her right shoulder in, hard, the wrong way round, and took Karis on that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:207:It was not praise. Lira knew what praise sounded like; she had been given it on painted floors by people with slates. It was a count, said by a person who kept no book, and she found that she would sooner have had it than a sheet of figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:225:"More water than that. It wants to be like a wet clay. Now fold it; no, toward you. You're not folding a letter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:243:*Finding. A method correctly dictated for ninety-eight days and never once followed by its author. Tested: one cake. I am entering it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:257:He had not known that it could be. He had supposed, at a far lip with his pencil lifted, that whatever was true by dark would cost him this; and it had cost a good deal, on other people's bodies, and it had not cost this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:259:It came to him that he was being trusted. That was all the silence was. Lira had said *later*, and three people had taken their time from her; and the fifth asked nobody anything that was not a hurt. They were waiting, without making a show of it, for him to have a sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:261:He did not have one. He ate his bread, and it was very good, and he said so; and Brom told him that there was no call to be unkind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:277:She would lay both hands on him in the morning, and all that she was, for as long as it took. She would do it in earnest, since she did nothing in any other way. He had felt that ninth thing come to him at the end of a bout with a friend who had asked only to be shown straight; he knew, as well as he knew anything about himself, what her morning was likely to be made of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:They came down to the fork an hour after noon, and up the valley with the smoke of a hold standing over the fence in front of them. The east gate was open. He had gone out by it five days before with five other people and a calfskin, to pick up something that somebody had left lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:295:It was all that she said, and she said it to the ladle. He thought it the longest speech of approval that he had ever had from her; she had counted them out of that gate, and she had not been certain of the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:311:He got it over his head with one arm and the other, which had come back to him on the road as far as the wrist. He stood by the well's coping in the last of the sun with five bands of linen round him that he had hated on the eighty-sixth day, and measured on the eighty-seventh, and since a certain evening had not once had the leisure to think about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:317:She did not put her hands round him to count. She had tried that. She set her thumb on the lowest of the three, where the first small sound had been, and pressed, as she had pressed on Lira's shin under a rock; and she watched his face while she did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:327:"I said the rule was twenty-one days. I'd have laid money on sooner, and I don't lay money." She was rolling the dead tape onto its peeled stick. "Leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:341:"Tomorrow. First light. The back room, with the door shut, and I'll not have eaten, so nor will you. It's both hands. It's twenty minutes, near enough; I'll not know till I'm in." She tucked the stick into her coat. "It costs me a mending, and there's a bench under that window I owe four days to. They'll keep till after noon. I've looked at every one of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:351:She was not being short with him. It was only how a healer's table was run: nobody was promised what the hands would find; they were told afterward, whatever it was, to their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:369:They got up, the five of them, and went back to whatever they had been doing; nobody on that bench had ever argued with her about a day. Cael stood by the well with his shirt in his hand and the cold on his ribs, and watched five people give up a morning to him that not one of them knew they were giving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:377:She went up it sideways, in the end, with her right hand and both knees, and her left elbow tight against her ribs; and at the top she sat down on the loft's edge with her legs hanging, breathing hard, and looked at the sling as a person looks at a dog that has stayed when it was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:3:He began at a brass plate, since everything else stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:7:Her hands lay where they had lain for twenty minutes, the left under his neck and the right on the bone, over the very place that he was speaking of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:"Then it lit, late, with one word on it, and the word wasn't a Path. Karis found out what it is. It's what the form writes when it can't be filled in, and it stands where an older name used to be written, before somebody took the older name away. After that the light went out. It has been dark ever since, on a floor or a road or at your stone. When the five of you crossed that line, something stopped in each of you; and nothing changed in me, since nothing was lit to begin with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:25:"There's a Wind's, and that one is Lira's. There's an Iron Skin's, which is Brom's, and an Ember's, which came from Karis. You know those three better than I do, since you've had your hands in them. Then a Pressure's, from a Bronze at Ardenmere, and a Compression's, from a fighter on a circuit. A Storm's, from a Gold. An Anchor's, from a hand whose face I never saw. A Blade's, which a friend gave me in a bout that he had asked for." He drew one slow breath. "And a Shadow's. That one I keep shut, for the sake of the person it came from, and I'll tell you its Path and nothing more. I used it once on that hill, for ten strides, in the lane. I looked first to see where your eyes were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:51:She took her hands away then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:He had had them on him for so long that the cold came in where they had been, at his nape and on the bone, like two doors opened. She stepped back her stride and a half and shook both hands out from the wrist, twice, hard, as he had seen her shake them after a count that found nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:69:The grey of the yard came in, and the cold with it. Brom was sitting on the bench under the window, alone, with his hands on his knees. He had come down the ladder before it was light, Cael understood, and had put himself where a person could be sent from; he was on his feet before she had opened her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:75:He was back before she had moved from the sill, with Hesk's book held in both hands and a straw shut into it at the place. She did not take it at once. She looked at the hand that held it, as she looked at everything of hers first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:87:"That it was wrong." Brom's eyes stayed on her and kept away from the plank. "Each of us, one after another, in our own words. He said we were right. He said the right thing was to come across this yard and knock, or to tell you this morning before you'd washed your hands, and that he would do neither, and that he'd tell you the minute it was over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:111:"You let me walk in here with my purse open. I'd put a day on my round for this. I've wanted very few things for myself since I crossed the Line, and this was one of them, and you knew that it was; you watched me want it at a ford." Her voice had not risen. It had gone lower, and each word came out separately. "You used it. You lay there like a man asleep and you let me spend."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:129:He got down off the table. She came back to it and stood opposite him with her arms folded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:"Now what it did. You'll want that, for your book, and I want it for my hands. I'll tell you what I'd tell a second healer, and no more." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "I felt nothing go. I'd swear to that. Near the end there was something going along beside me, at my own pace. I took it for myself. You hear your own feet, in a long one, when you're tired. Then it wasn't beside me any longer. It was in you, going by itself, and you were different."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:"I've spent a mending. I knew that I would; a long one costs that, and I'm no emptier than a long one leaves me." She unfolded her arms and looked at her palms. "Past that I can't tell you, since I can't read myself. No Tide can. So I'll learn it as I learn everything. There are five on that bench after noon, and the first of them will tell me whether these are what they were. If they come back short, you'll hear it from me before you hear anything else today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:139:"Then it didn't touch my hands, and it's still what you did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:143:"There's a line in there about what it's to cost you. Whatever I like, you've written, down to my walking north without you on the road. I've been paid in a good many ways out here. I've had a coat, and a cold fowl, and a shoe on the mule. I never yet had a patient hand me his own fine, ready counted, before I'd said what was owing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:147:"I know what you meant. You meant to be square. You'd take whatever I gave you and call the account shut." She shook her head, once. "You don't set it. That's the first of what it costs. You're not the one who knows the price, and you'll go about not knowing it until I do, which won't be today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:161:"I don't know either. I'm a healer, not a theorist." Her palms went together, slowly, as they did when she had had them in cold water. "What I know is you've now got a piece of the thing I read with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:183:He lay down. She put the horn cup into his hand, with water in it, and waited until he had drunk. Then she took Hesk's book back and sat down on the three-legged stool herself, for the first time since he had come into that room, with the book on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:197:It was a careful hand, small and upright, and the pencil had been pressed hard. He had put down her three mendings, and that her round was late at the far end. He had put down that she could not say yes or no to a thing that she had never heard the name of, and in the next breath that he had decided not to let her hear it. He had written that he wanted it. She could not fault the page for a single thing that it left out, and she thought that she would have liked it better if she could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:Nobody had ever before told her the truth a night too late, in writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:203:She shut the book and laid her hands on its cover, palms down, and went over what they had found. She did it as her teacher had made her do it, for a second healer who was not in the room, with her knuckles ready to be struck for a guess.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:217:She put the question as she would have put it of a wound: where, and how deep, and what was standing behind it. She had felt nothing go. Her reserve was down by one mending, which was the price of the work and no more. She flexed her fingers on the leather. They were the hands that she had come in with, so far as hands could tell their owner anything, which was not far.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:221:It was a strange thing to be angry about, a theft with nothing missing. She was angry all the same, and she took the trouble to find out why, since anger that a healer does not understand gets into her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:225:That was the difference, and there was no other. Every mending of her life had been given, by a woman who knew the price and said yes. This had been taken off her while her hands were full, by a boy who had thought the price might be high and had kept his mouth shut. Her purse weighed the same. It was the asking that he had had from her; and she, who asked the dying before she laid a finger on them, knew to a hair what an asking was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:229:She had known it by the fourth line of his leaf, as she had told him, and she did not say it over now, even to herself, since a thing said over in an empty room is half given away. She turned the keeping of it over once instead, to be certain that she had her reasons straight and was not merely being hard. If the answer had been yes, then to tell him so was a pardon. If it had been no, it was a sentence, and a person can serve a sentence and be done. Either way he would have had from her, a day late, the very thing that he had gone round her to avoid, which was an end to not knowing. He had made her answer into a thing that never happened. It could stay so, and he could do his own carrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:235:She had eaten, and had her two hours on her back, with the door shut and the book on the sill. She came out and washed to the elbow in the bucket, and she washed more slowly than she ever did, as a carter walks who thinks that his axle may be cracked and would sooner not hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:239:She had put him first on purpose. It was the worst thing on the bench, and it was a thing that she knew: a scythe cut across the front of the leg, as old as the harvest, which had never closed because he had never once sat down. She had him on the plank where a boy had lain that morning. She put both hands round the shin, above the place and below it, and shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:243:It was all there. The banks came up under her palms before anything else, as they always did, broad and plain and a little silted, the bed of a man who had worked one piece of ground all his life; and in the bed his current stood behind the cut like water behind a board, patient, and rather tired of standing. She knew where to put her own. She opened the way, and his went through, and she held it open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:245:She could have wept, and she did nothing of the kind. She held a field-hand's leg for the better part of an hour, and paid for it at the rate that such a thing costs, and when she took her hands away there was a pink seam on his shin where a wet red mouth had been since the barley was cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:251:That made it no better, and she had told him that it would not. She was glad, though, in the plain way that a person is glad of her tools, and she rubbed her hands together for some time after the bench was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:275:He had done as he was told. He had put his shirt on and crossed the yard, and the steading's woman had met him at the long house door with a bowl before he could ask for one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:277:"She's had you since it was light," the woman said. "I've seen her keep a man an hour. I never saw her keep one two." She looked him over as she would have looked over a cart that had come home late, for what was broken on it, and found nothing that she could name. "Eat that standing, if you can't sit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:279:He ate it standing, and tasted none of it. Nobody came to him. Brom looked across once from the bench under the window, and got up, and climbed the ladder to the loft. On the platform by the western gate a lean man was watching the valley, as he did at every hour of every day. Cael had been left alone with a bowl by four people who knew when a person was not fit to be spoken to; and when the bowl was empty he went to break the ice for the geese, since it wanted doing, and since it could be done by somebody who was not thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:281:The trough stood by the barn wall, with a skin on it as thick as a slate. He put the heel of his right hand through it, and it was the left that he looked at afterward, turning it over in the cold: the feeling had come back as far as the knuckles in the night, and a hound's weight had dwindled to a yellow mark along the bone. That bill was nearly paid. He had come into this yard with a good many, and one by one a healer had told him that they were done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:283:He had both hands on the stone rim, and water running over his wrists, when he noticed that he was hearing something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:293:Nothing happened whatever. The beat went on, quick and slow, exactly as before; and he understood, with his wrists going numb, that a refusal must have something to refuse. The nine had each come to him wanting. One had wanted doorways, and one had asked about every edge in the world, and the heaviest had only wanted room, and had taken it. He had learned to say no to each of them, since each of them was always asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:301:He took his hands out of the water and dried them on his shirt. A healer was sitting on a stool across the yard, behind a shut door, with his leaf on her knee and an answer in her that he would never be given. He had nothing that he could do about either. He had four people in a loft who had told him the truth the night before and had not slept on it, and he owed them the morning, in order, with nothing left out.

