/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n -i "dead to|elbows|dead.*arm|arms.*dead|hand.*dead" editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:113:He could not dispute that, and he did not try. She had the book open before he had turned to go on; she walked the next mile with her nose a hand from a dead man's pencil, and went into a rut to the knee, and did not appear to notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:45:He was twelve feet from the stone when he made up his mind. He had refused Anchor, the thinnest of his ten, at these two irons the evening before, and again in the frost an hour ago. He let it look now, on purpose, with his eyes open; and since he had stopped dead to do it, he stood on a frozen road for the length of two counts while a friend with a bolt in him was being turned about by the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:He let himself go back to the corridor, which he allowed seldom. It was distempered green to the height of a man's shoulder, in a district seat three postings and twelve years behind him, and he had stopped dead in the middle of its floor with an armful of somebody's travel endorsements. A clerk had put his head out of a side door to ask whether he was unwell. He had not been unwell. He had understood, in the space between one step and the next, what a certain run of ledgers was that he had copied the year before as dull upkeep; and whose question it had been all along.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:263:Then there was rock under both feet, and she was standing on it, twenty feet back, with her arms out and her heart going like a mill. Behind her the ledge roared down into the gorge. She had not chosen where to come down, either. Her feet had found sound stone under the roof by themselves, and stopped dead on it, as she had spent a month on a river bar teaching them to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:9:Cael had her wrists. It was all that was left for him to do, and he could do it sitting, with his back against the pale rock. He drew, and she pushed with her elbows, and she came toward him over the plates a foot at a time with her face shut like a fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:347:Behind her nobody was eating any longer. Lira had come up on both elbows, and Brom sat with the bread halfway to his mouth; Seln's mild eyes were on the back of the healer's cropped head, and under his coat Karis had gone perfectly still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:91:She had asked nobody. She went round the inside of the posts, the whole quarter mile, flat out, with the geese scattering, and Cael saw her come down the last straight toward the gate with her head back and her arms going. Three strides short of the bowman's platform she did what she had spent a month on a gravel bar failing to do. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet, with nothing at the end of it but herself, breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:165:She did not walk and she did not run. She skipped. She went from plate to plate with her arms out and her coat flapping, a short quick hop to each, and never the same plate twice, and never two counts in one place; a square woman in a dead man's coat, coming across thirty yards of broken floor like a child on stepping-stones. It would have been comical on any other morning of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:137:Oryn walked out of that gate with her hands in the pockets of a dead man's coat, and she kept them there for the first mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:73:She turned her head. The boy was sitting up, a little way off, with his dead arm in his lap and his face to the east. He was not on watch, and he had not lain down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:133:"There," said Lira, who was up on her elbows. "Under the last one, at this end. Look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:"Because I cannot mend what I can't read." The healer said it without heat. "I read her leg at a count of ten and I knew what it was, and how she was built round it, and where the thing that knits had stopped; and then I knew where to put mine. I had my hands on him for three times as long and I got bone. I'd have got that much with my eyes shut, off a dead sheep." She showed her palms, briefly. "Past the bone there's nothing I can lay hold of. I'd be pushing in the dark, and I don't do that to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:323:There was an iron spike driven into the right-hand post, under the plank, where it could not be seen from the meadow, and Cael had noticed it before; finished slips were threaded on it, months of them, bark and hide together, like dead leaves on a twig.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:His left arm had gone dead to the elbow before the first quarter was out. He hung the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand. Something in his right arm had begun to ask about the lantern's brass rim. *Edge?* It had put that question to half the world for a season, patiently, and tonight for the first time it had been told yes; it asked now like a child that has been given one sweet. He told it no, and it asked again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:89:Brom said something to the dark that was not a word. Cael found that the lantern was shaking, and that it was not his dead arm that shook it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:195:*Mine: left calf open on the old seam. Left forearm struck; dead to the elbow. Ribs taped, three days to run; they held.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:265:He sat on beside her with a dead arm across his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:21:Karis could not close her fingers on a rung. She hooked her forearms behind each one and came down by her elbows, with her two white fists stuck out before her, in a silence so complete that she was plainly composing a letter of complaint and had not yet settled to whom it should be sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:*Fifty-fourth day. The ladder. Brom, one hand. Karis, elbows. Lira, seated, counting aloud. Mine, by the arms; the calf gives eleven strides on the flat and no rungs at all. Seln, as usual. The nine: idle, and asking. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:153:He untied the cuff with his right hand and his teeth. Under it the seam was pale and dry, from the heel of the hand to the middle of the forearm. He bent the wrist down and brought it back. Then he folded the linen in four and put it inside his shirt, on the left, where he kept a dead slip with six words on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:213:When he raised his head, she was sitting on the far side of the dead fire with her arms round her knees, watching him. She had her own coat on again, and he could not tell how long she had been awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:277:The head under his hands stopped pulling. There was no struggle in it: it was a weight that had been leaning against him, and then it was only a weight, and he was pressing a dead thing to the floor with all his strength.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:17:Brom set the kit on the table. Its case lay there in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid; inside were a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, and a mallet. The stake had never been driven. Beside it Karis put a dead man's ledger, shut. The three boxes stood against the warm wall, and each of them had one leaf in it that was the same as its fellows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:209:The path was a stride wide, and it stood a hand above the water. The reeds rose on either side of it higher than Brom's head, dry and rattling, so that they walked in a kind of lane, with a strip of grey sky over them and black water at both elbows. Cael went fourth. He had his ten fragments lying quiet in him, on the far side of a line, and he kept his eyes on Karis's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:271:He stood in a ditch with the cold in his elbows and looked again. The three courses came to the newer bar and touched it, and there was a point where each of them met the iron; but they did not end. They went through those points as a rope goes through a ring, and on, southward along the edge, out of his sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:285:He lay down in the rut on his left side, with the bar at the length of his arm. He put his right hand out toward it, and round it, and could not tell when he had touched it; he had to watch his own fingers close on the scale to learn that they had. Past the iron, his hand was on the far side of that edge, and he laid the dead palm against the outer face, low down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:145:He was the hold's boy, twelve or so and all elbows. He put his boot in the stirrup, and a hook on his belt over the string, and straightened his back with a grunt; and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch. Then he laid a bolt in the groove. It was a stubby thing with a blunt wooden head and three goose feathers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:7:He drew his arm back. Both his hands were dead to the elbow, with a cold that had nothing to do with the frost, and when he pushed himself up on them it was like leaning on two posts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:53:It was coming round at his head like a flail. He got his right arm up. It was a dead arm, and it was late, and for the first beat nothing in him answered at all; a pound of ash wood and horn met the bone of his forearm with the whole of a strong man's swing behind it. His knees began to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:*Shadow: once, from the fourth step to the old bar. Lira saw it go, from below. Seln turned his back. Three were round the door. The six were two hundred and sixty yards off, with a lip between; not one had a line to me. Let go in the rut. It holds. Dead to the elbows; I had to watch my own hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:215:Nobody had been told; the boy had gone out through the wicket with a mallet, and that had been enough. The steading's woman stood on her step with her arms folded. Her husband had come round the end of the long house with flour to his elbows, and was explaining to two carters from up the valley that the girl shot from a rail, since she had only the one arm to her, and that he had seen worse done with two. The healer had come as far as the door of the back room, and no farther, and leaned there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:279:The sun was on the river when he came out above the meadow. Four people were on the gravel bar. One of them ran, and stopped dead on a line, on her two feet, and flung up both arms; and a large one carrying a tree let it roll from his shoulder and stepped out from under it as neatly as a dancer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:41:It was over before he reached her. The hound had done the work itself; it had driven forward, as they drove at everything they could feel or remember, and the knife was only the length of a hand, and a hand had been enough. It lay across her with its head on her shoulder, and she lay under it with her elbows locked and her eyes wide open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:She was not sitting. She stood in the middle of the floor with her arms folded and her weight on her own boot, and her lips moved at each name. The five of them had the boards nearest the door, where they had sat on the forty-ninth night, and on the boards between Brom's elbows lay a sheet of cream paper with a lamp beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:73:Cael had seen the man before without knowing whose he was. He was small and spare, a head shorter than the steading's woman, with a bald brown crown and flour to both elbows; and in all his days at that hold Cael had never heard him say a word, since he had never been near enough to the hearth. Thornwater, it appeared, kept its talk in one place. The woman at the gate counted people with her lips and spent a word on every six of them, and the man on the platform had said perhaps forty in a hundred days. Here was the hold's whole share of speech, walking beside Brom with a wooden spoon in his belt and getting through it at a great rate.

